window.MAP_DATA_RECORDS.push(...[{"i":"R808","k":"Afghanistan","p":"Afghanistan","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Afghanistan panic digital footprints rethinking data collection","s":"Privacy experts have long warned that United Nations and development agencies’ collection of biometric data and mandating of digital identity cards heightens risks to refugees and vulnerable groups. Concerns about sensitive data falling into the hands of the Taliban after they took control of Afghanistan have rekindled a debate among privacy experts on the ethics of data collection by aid agencies and multilateral institutions. As the insurgents moved into the capital, Kabul, on Sunday, August 15, residents fretted that biometric databases maintained by aid agencies and security forces could be used to track and target them.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/afghanistan-panic-digital-footprints-rethinking-data-collection/","d":"2021-08-20","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-08-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-08-15","organization":"United Nations and Development Agencies","aiSystem":"Facial Recognition Technology","affectedGroup":"Refugees and Vulnerable Groups","action":"Collection of Biometric Data and Mandating Digital Identity Cards","consequence":"Risk of Biometric Databases Being Used to Track and Target Individuals"}},{"i":"R1492","k":"Afghanistan","p":"Afghanistan","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.2  Allocative harm"],"t":"Afghanistan making photos on womens national id cards optional","s":"Afghanistan has issued 16 million electronic identity cards since the country started issuing the national IDs in 2018, according to data from the NSIA. Afghanistan is making photos on women’s national ID cards optional, the Taliban government has proclaimed, noting that religious scholars have deemed photos of women contrary to Sharia law. The decision was announced last week after a decree issued by the Taliban’s religious authority, Dar al-Ifta, and overrides previous photo requirements by the National Statistics and Information Authority (NSIA). The statistics agency has attempted to argue that photographs on ID are necessary for identity verification, fraud prevention, easier travel and international standards compliance.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202509/afghanistan-making-photos-on-womens-national-id-cards-optional","d":"2025-09-01","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-09-01","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-09-01","organization":"Taliban government","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"Women","action":"Made photos on women's national ID cards optional","consequence":"Exposure to privacy risks"}},{"i":"R1535","k":"Afghanistan","p":"Afghanistan","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Iran deploys biometric system to control afghan migrant influx","s":"A multi-biometrics system has been deployed along the borders with Afghanistan to catch all illegal immigrant trying to re-enter the country. The Iranian government says the burden of Afghan refugees it is hosting is becoming too heavy, so it is resorting to biometrics to halt a further influx of illegal immigrants who are also said to pose a threat to national security. Authorities say Iran currently is host to about six million forcibly displace persons from neighboring Afghanistan, and its annual spending on these immigrants has hit a whopping $10 billion a year, putting a huge strain on its resources. In an outing, head of the Bureau for Aliens and Foreign Immigrant Affairs and Advisor to Interior Minister, Nadeh Yar-Ahmadi, said the use of biometrics will identify such immigrants and make it impossible for them to get back into the country, Kayhan reports The measure is not limited…","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202412/iran-deploys-biometric-system-to-control-afghan-migrant-influx","d":"2024-12-18","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-12-18","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-12-18","organization":"Iranian government","aiSystem":"multi-biometrics system","affectedGroup":"illegal immigrant trying to re-enter the country","action":"identify and prevent illegal immigrants from entering the country","consequence":"subjected to detention"}},{"i":"R1599","k":"Argentina","p":"Argentina","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Buenos airess facial recognition system to be examined by court","s":"The hearing is set to determine the conditions under which the currently suspended biometric surveillance system could resume operation. Buenos Aires’ notorious facial recognition system, which has been deployed to track journalists, politicians and activists, is due to be examined by a court next Wednesday. The hearing is set to determine the conditions under which the currently suspended system could resume operations, according to the non-profit organization Via Libre Foundation. The Fugitive Facial Recognition System (SNRP) was introduced by the Argentinian capital’s government in 2019 to identify fugitives under the then Minister of Security and Deputy Head of Government, Diego Santilli.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202402/buenos-airess-facial-recognition-system-to-be-examined-by-court","d":"2024-02-22","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-02-22","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-02-22","organization":"Buenos Aires government","aiSystem":"Facial recognition system","affectedGroup":"Journalists, politicians, activists, fugitives","action":"Examined by a court","consequence":"Conditions for resuming operation determined"}},{"i":"R1655","k":"Argentina","p":"Argentina","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.2  Land use and siting harm"],"t":"Artificial intelligence and digital extractivism who benefits from data centers in latin america","s":"OpenAI has announced the construction of a mega data center in Argentina, the latest of its kind in the region. Governments should demand local participation and reinvestment terms that promise more than free access to ChatGPT. In July of this year, I flew 11,000 kilometers from Buenos Aires to take a course on artificial intelligence policy and law at the University of Leuven in Belgium, a vast neo-Gothic structure founded in 1425 where today, across its various campuses, 57,000 students study a wide variety of disciplines. Halfway through the class, the lecturer divided us into groups and gave us the assignment for a group exam: “The environmental footprint is overrated.”","u":"https://english.elpais.com/international/women-leaders-of-latin-america/2025-10-17/artificial-intelligence-and-digital-extractivism-who-benefits-from-data-centers-in-latin-america.html","d":"2025-10-17","e":"Acute and chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-10-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-10-17","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"Artificial Intelligence (AI)","affectedGroup":"Local communities and future generations","action":"Construction of a large-scale data center","consequence":"Overlooked environmental impact"}},{"i":"R1663","k":"Argentina","p":"Argentina","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.5  Human computer interaction harm"],"t":"Teachers mounting chatgpt frustrations they keep using it even though ive warned them not to","s":"After nearly two years of existence, AI text generators are proving to be a growing headache for educators. Though many say there’s no going back to a world without them, the way forward is fraught with obstacles. “It’s exhausting because it keeps happening, there are several students who I warn not to do their work with AI and they just do it again,” says Hernán Ojeda, 33, a high school language and literature teacher in Buenos Aires, Argentina. Two years after their launch, ChatGPT and other AI tools have become widely adopted, including by high school and college students However, educators are still grappling with how to effectively integrate this technology into the classroom, with many feeling outpaced by the ease with which AI can be used to quickly complete assignments.","u":"https://english.elpais.com/technology/2024-11-09/teachers-mounting-chatgpt-frustrations-they-keep-using-it-even-though-ive-warned-them-not-to.html","d":"2024-11-09","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-11-09","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-11-09","organization":"Educators, specifically high school language and literature teachers","aiSystem":"AI text generators, particularly ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"High school and college students","action":"Using AI text generators to complete assignments","consequence":"Feeling outpaced and frustrated by the ease of AI use"}},{"i":"R1013","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.6  Quality of service harm"],"t":"Doctors ai telehealth online","s":"Complaints about a lack of communication between GPs and patients are rising and one concern is online platforms using artificial intelligence. The quality of healthcare in Australia is undeniably deteriorating. Medicare, our once-universal-healthcare system, has been strangled by years of policymaking and, while the 2023-24 Federal Budget included the biggest investment into the system since its inception in 1984 , patients claim doctors are increasingly exploiting new technology to make up for deep funding gaps. Complaints to Australia’s medical board about poor or no communication between doctors and patients have risen dramatically since telehealth was expanded in 2020 and, more recently, spurred concerns about the use of artificial intelligence for prescriptions.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/doctors-ai-telehealth-online/","d":"2024-04-11","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2024-04-11","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2020-2024","organization":"Doctors and telehealth platforms","aiSystem":"artificial intelligence","affectedGroup":"Patients","action":"Exploitation of artificial intelligence for prescriptions","consequence":"Poor or no communication between doctors and patients"}},{"i":"R1133","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Facial recognition will be used to boost profits in some australian prisons","s":"Digital rights campaigners say the move will create security risks and usher in a new era of oppression. Some Australian prisons will soon be retrofitted with new facial recognition devices, even as backlash over its use continues to attract the attention of watchdogs around the world. Details of a new deal between Corrective Services New South Wales and the United States-based IT firm, Unisys, first surfaced early last week As part of the deal, which will cost the NSW government just shy of $13 million over the next four years, the state’s inmates will be forced to hand over their biometric data as part of an effort to “provide more safety” to people visiting correctional facilities, and make the state’s prisons system more profitable, by cutting operational costs.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/facial-recognition-will-be-used-to-boost-profits-in-some-australian-prisons/","d":"2022-09-13","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2022-09-13","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2022-09-13","organization":"Corrective Services New South Wales, Unisys","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"Prisoners, visitors to correctional facilities","action":"Inmates will be required to provide their biometric data","consequence":"Increased operational costs, potential security risks, and privacy concerns"}},{"i":"R1141","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm","4.3  Data rights / consent violation"],"t":"Kmart and bunnings are tracking customers with facial recognition","s":"For the most part, customers are having their biometric data captured without their knowledge or consent. Some of Australia’s most trusted department stores have been using facial recognition on customers without their knowledge, a consumer investigation has found. Now, privacy experts say the retailers could find themselves in breach of the privacy act. The findings come as a result of an investigation conducted by the Australian consumer group, Choice, which found that Kmart, Bunnings, and even The Good Guys are among a crop of local retailers that have been actively capturing and storing customer “faceprints”—a person’s unique biometric facial identifiers—as they shop.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/kmart-and-bunnings-are-tracking-customers-with-facial-recognition/","d":"2022-06-15","e":"Acute, Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2022-06-15","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2022-06-15","organization":"Kmart, Bunnings, The Good Guys (local retailers)","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"Customers","action":"Facial recognition data capture","consequence":"Potential breach of privacy act"}},{"i":"R756","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.8  Interpersonal / relational harm"],"t":"Australia to require ai made child abuse material removed search results","s":"A new code drafted by tech giants at the Australian government’s request will require search engines to ensure child sexual abuse images made by AI are not returned in search results. SYDNEY, Australia – Australia will make search engines like Google and Bing take steps to prevent the sharing of child sexual abuse material created by artificial intelligence, the country’s internet regulator said on Friday, September 8. A new code drafted by the industry giants at the government’s request will require search engines to ensure such content is not returned in search results, e-Safety Commissioner Julie Inman Grant said in a statement It will also require that AI functions built into search engines cannot produce synthetic versions of the same material, she said.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/australia-to-require-ai-made-child-abuse-material-removed-search-results/","d":"2023-09-11","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-09-11","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-09-08","organization":"Tech giants (Google, Bing)","aiSystem":"AI functions built into search engines","affectedGroup":"Child sexual abuse material","action":"Ensure child sexual abuse material is not returned in search results","consequence":"Prevent the sharing of AI-generated child sexual abuse material"}},{"i":"R784","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Australia mayor world first defamation lawsuit chatgpt","s":"‘It would potentially be a landmark moment in the sense that it’s applying this defamation law to a new area of artificial intelligence and publication in the IT space,’ a lawyer says. A regional Australian mayor said he may sue OpenAI if it does not correct ChatGPT’s false claims that he had served time in prison for bribery, in what would be the first defamation lawsuit against the automated text service. Brian Hood, who was elected mayor of Hepburn Shire, 120km (75 miles) northwest of Melbourne, last November, became concerned about his reputation when members of the public told him ChatGPT had falsely named him as a guilty party in a foreign bribery scandal involving a subsidiary of the Reserve Bank of Australia in the early 2000s.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/australia-mayor-world-first-defamation-lawsuit-chatgpt/","d":"2023-04-06","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-04-06","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-04-06","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Brian Hood, Mayor of Hepburn Shire","action":"ChatGPT falsely claimed that Brian Hood had served time in prison for bribery","consequence":"Potential defamation lawsuit against OpenAI"}},{"i":"R550","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.5  Human computer interaction harm"],"t":"Australia tells ai chatbot companies detail child protection steps","s":"The realistic conversational abilities of AI chatbots take the world by storm, but also fan concern that a lack of guardrails exposes vulnerable individuals to dangerous content. SYDNEY, Australia – Australia has ordered four artificial-intelligence chatbot companies to explain their measures to protect children from exposure to sexual or self-harm material, as its internet regulator beefs up safety measures on the AI front. The realistic conversational abilities of such services have taken the world by storm, but have also fanned concern that a lack of guardrails exposes vulnerable individuals to dangerous content.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/australia-tells-ai-chatbot-companies-detail-child-protection-steps/","d":"2025-10-23","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2025-10-23","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-10-23","organization":"Four artificial-intelligence chatbot companies","aiSystem":"AI chatbot services","affectedGroup":"Vulnerable individuals","action":"Explain measures to protect children from exposure to sexual or self-harm material","consequence":"Exposure to dangerous content"}},{"i":"R071","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Australian government warns doctors over ai scribing tools as privacy and safety concerns grow","s":"Australia is reviewing AI medical scribes as regulators raise concerns over patient privacy, consent, data security, and the lack of oversight in clinical settings. The Australian government is urging healthcare professionals to exercise caution when using AI-powered medical scribing tools, as regulators examine whether stronger safeguards are needed around one of healthcare’s fastest-growing technologies, according to a report by The Guardian AI scribes have rapidly gained popularity by recording, transcribing, and summarising doctor-patient conversations into clinical notes, reducing the administrative burden on healthcare workers. However, government officials now warn that the technology’s rapid adoption has outpaced oversight, raising questions around patient privacy, informed consent, and the accuracy of medical records.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/cool-tech/australian-government-warns-doctors-over-ai-scribing-tools-as-privacy-and-safety-concerns-grow/","d":"2026-07-05","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2026-07-05","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07-05","organization":"Australian government","aiSystem":"AI-powered medical scribing tools","affectedGroup":"Healthcare professionals, patients","action":"Rapid adoption of AI medical scribes","consequence":"Concerns over patient privacy, informed consent, and accuracy of medical records"}},{"i":"R1171","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"Consumers should brace for hefty increases in the cost of generating electricity as data c…","s":"Consumers should brace for hefty increases in the cost of generating electricity as data centres suck up vast quantities of power faster than new plants can be built, experts say. Amid forecasts that demand for power from data centres could rise five-fold by 2035, analysts say operators will inevitably tap existing supplies before they can develop new capacity. The warning comes ahead of a meeting of state and federal energy ministers today, when they will discuss the implications of the data centre boom for the power system. From roughly 2 per cent of average demand on Australia's main grid at present, \"load\" from data centres is forecast to rocket to about 10 per cent of consumption in 10 years.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-07-28/data-centre-explosion-could-spark-power-price-hikes/106963258","d":"2026-07-27","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-27","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07-27","organization":"Data center operators","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"Consumers","action":"Data centers consume vast amounts of power","consequence":"Hefty increases in electricity costs"}},{"i":"R1172","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"Data centres are in development across Australia, but how many jobs will these big, thirst…","s":"Data centres are in development across Australia, but how many jobs will these big, thirsty facilities actually support? Data centres — the new, essential, digital infrastructure of our time — are a hot topic of debate in town halls around Australia. Despite controversy over the use of AI and the substantial resources required to power data centres, one argument pushed in their favour is that they will create jobs. But how many jobs do data centres actually create? These huge warehouse-like structures house servers and storage systems to process, store and manage digital data. They are essential to supporting the AI systems becoming increasingly pervasive in our lives, from the ubiquitous Google search AI results to ChatGPT or Microsoft Copilot.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-07-25/how-many-jobs-do-ai-data-centres-actually-create/106934790","d":"2026-07-24","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-24","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07-24","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"AI systems","affectedGroup":"People who could have been hired for data center jobs","action":"Data centers were built without creating the promised jobs","consequence":"People were denied potential employment opportunities"}},{"i":"R1173","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"The theft of names, addresses and phone numbers may seem less serious than stolen bank det…","s":"The theft of names, addresses and phone numbers may seem less serious than stolen bank details, but cyber experts say AI is changing what criminals can do with that information. Personal information stolen in the Origin Energy data breach could be used by criminals to launch highly targeted scams and impersonate victims to make fraudulent transactions. Experts say AI is making it easier for scammers to combine leaked data with public information to create convincing phishing messages, fake identities and even voice clones. Customers are urged to be cautious of unexpected calls and messages, as scammers may exploit the breach itself to pose as Origin, banks or other trusted organisations offering help.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-07-24/origin-breach-could-fuel-wave-of-ai-powered-scams/106951588","d":"2026-07-23","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-23","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07-24","organization":"Origin Energy","aiSystem":"AI-powered phishing tools","affectedGroup":"Customers","action":"Scammers combined leaked data with public information to create convincing phishing messages","consequence":"Fraudulent transactions and identity theft"}},{"i":"R1175","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.2  Land use and siting harm"],"t":"The NT's sunny skies, natural gas reserves and proximity to Asia have caught the attention…","s":"The NT's sunny skies, natural gas reserves and proximity to Asia have caught the attention of a dozen data centre developers. From the tropics of the Top End to the red dirt of Central Australia, the Northern Territory seems a world away from the rapid development associated with artificial intelligence. But the NT could soon be at the centre of one of the world's most controversial industries, with 12 data centre investors eyeing Australia's most remote jurisdiction. Why have these companies chosen the NT and is it ready for an AI boom? Jason Finlay, a partner with Vantage North Group, says the NT's sunny skies and vast reserves of natural gas are part of the attraction for AI firms.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-07-13/data-centre-hyperscalers-target-northern-territory/106898282","d":"2026-07-12","e":"Acute and chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-12","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07-12","organization":"Vantage North Group","aiSystem":"Data center infrastructure","affectedGroup":"Residents of the Northern Territory","action":"Data centers being built","consequence":"Potential privacy risks"}},{"i":"R1177","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.2  Land use and siting harm"],"t":"The proposed 19,150-hectare 'Project Ares' would be built by Energy North on Murranji Stat…","s":"The proposed 19,150-hectare 'Project Ares' would be built by Energy North on Murranji Station in the Barkly Region, as up to 12 proponents eye the NT for data centres. A \"hyperscale\" data centre has been proposed for Murranji Station by Perth-based company Energy North. The NT government says it is aware of 12 proponents who want to build data centres in the Territory. Energy North's plan has been submitted to the federal environmental regulator, and it is open for public comment. A \"hyperscale\" data centre campus has been proposed for a remote Northern Territory cattle station, as the industry eyes the region for development.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-07-01/data-centre-planned-outback-nt/106862398","d":"2026-07-01","e":"Acute and chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-01","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07-01","organization":"Energy North (perth-based company)","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"Not specified in report","action":"submitted a plan for a hyperscale data centre campus","consequence":"open for public comment"}},{"i":"R1196","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"AI-generated images of Bondi gunman Naveed Akram in the Philippines and a fabricated Faceb…","s":"AI-generated images of Bondi gunman Naveed Akram in the Philippines and a fabricated Facebook page are spreading online. And an image of police officers taken out of context is debunked by ABC NEWS Verify. Falsehoods and conspiracy theories are spreading online following the horrific Bondi Beach attack. ABC NEWS Verify has analysed three instances where AI-generated images, or in one case a real image taken out of context, are attempting to twist the narrative on social media. An AI-generated image of gunman Naveed Akram sitting at an outdoor cafe in the Philippines has been used to spread false information online.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-12-18/verify-disinformation-and-deepfakes-after-bondi-attack/106154250","d":"2025-12-17","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-12-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-12-17","organization":"ABC NEWS Verify","aiSystem":"AI-generated images","affectedGroup":"People exposed to misleading content","action":"Generated and distributed AI-generated images","consequence":"Spreading of falsehoods and conspiracy theories"}},{"i":"R1198","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"Australia has more than 250 data centres and more in the pipeline, prompting experts to wa…","s":"Australia has more than 250 data centres and more in the pipeline, prompting experts to warn against using drinking water to cool servers. It comes after the Commonwealth announced its National AI Plan after a two-year consultation period. One water authority is already exploring the use of recycled waste water for data centres. Australia's data centres should be built near waste treatment plants to avoid using drinking water to run the thirsty facilities, according to one of the country's leading experts in stormwater treatment. The Commonwealth unveiled last week its 37-page National AI Plan which sets out a road map to drive the development and adoption of artificial intelligence by bolstering investment in skills training and data centres that store servers.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-12-10/demand-for-data-centre-water-in-ai-push/106102208","d":"2025-12-09","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-12-09","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-12-09","organization":"Commonwealth of Australia","aiSystem":"Artificial Intelligence (AI)","affectedGroup":"Data center operators, water authorities, and the general public","action":"Planning and construction of data centers near waste treatment plants to avoid using drinking water","consequence":"Potential overuse of recycled waste water and increased risk of contamination"}},{"i":"R1200","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"The latest data revealed by Australia's eSafety Commissioner comes just days after reports…","s":"The latest data revealed by Australia's eSafety Commissioner comes just days after reports that digitally altered explicit images of female students from a Sydney high school were shared online. Data revealed by the eSafety Commissioner shows at least one incident of deepfake image-based abuse is taking place weekly in Australian schools. Commissioner Julie Inman Grant says it is \"putting potential online harm on steroids\". The Commissioner confirmed her office is working with NSW Police and the education department to investigate a recent incident at a Sydney high school. Reports of deepfake image-based abuse have doubled in the country over the past 18 months, with at least one incident emerging out of Australian schools each week.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-10-17/deepfake-image-based-abuse-doubles-across-australia/105905152","d":"2025-10-17","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-10-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-10-17","organization":"eSafety Commissioner's Office","aiSystem":"Deepfake image-based technology","affectedGroup":"Female students from Sydney high schools","action":"Deepfake images of female students were shared online","consequence":"Incidents of deepfake image-based abuse are occurring weekly in Australian schools"}},{"i":"R1201","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"The thought of a missing boy lost in the outback is indescribably harrowing — but what's a…","s":"The thought of a missing boy lost in the outback is indescribably harrowing — but what's almost as disturbing is the realisation that some on social media have seized on the disappearance of four-year-old Gus as an opportunity to spread misinformation. In the two weeks since Gus was reported missing from his family's homestead, about 40 kilometres south of Yunta in a remote part of South Australia's mid-north, bogus images of the boy seemingly generated by artificial intelligence technology have begun circulating online. False reports as well as images that appear to manipulate real photos, including of the search and Gus himself, are being shared on social media.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-10-11/ai-generated-images-of-4yo-gus-prompt-call-for-stronger-laws/105873218","d":"2025-10-10","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-10-10","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-10-02","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"Artificial intelligence technology","affectedGroup":"People sharing or receiving the misinformation","action":"Generated and shared fake images of four-year-old Gus","consequence":"Spread of false reports and manipulated photos"}},{"i":"R1202","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.2  Allocative harm"],"t":"An Australian university has accused thousands of its students of cheating with artificial…","s":"An Australian university has accused thousands of its students of cheating with artificial intelligence. The problem? Many have done nothing wrong. A major Australian university used artificial intelligence technology to accuse about 6,000 students of academic misconduct last year. The most common offence was using AI to cheat, but many of the students had done nothing wrong. A technology expert says banning AI is the wrong approach and universities should teach students how to use it properly. Madeleine was in the middle of her final-year university nursing placement and frantically applying for graduate jobs when her phone pinged.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-10-09/artificial-intelligence-cheating-australian-catholic-university/105863524","d":"2025-10-08","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-10-08","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-10-08","organization":"a major Australian university","aiSystem":"artificial intelligence technology","affectedGroup":"thousands of students","action":"accused students of academic misconduct","consequence":"wrongfully accused students of academic misconduct"}},{"i":"R1203","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Data experts say it is hard to know what the implications are for 3,000 flood victims who …","s":"Data experts say it is hard to know what the implications are for 3,000 flood victims who have had personal information uploaded to the AI platform by a government contractor. A NSW government data breach exposing up to 3,000 people has raised questions about the risks artificial intelligence pose to personal security. A contractor for the NSW Reconstruction Authority uploaded the names, contact details and personal information , including health information, of people connected to a flood recovery program to ChatGPT in March. So can private information uploaded to artificial intelligence platforms be found by other users?","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-10-08/can-private-information-uploaded-to-chatgpt-be-found-by-others/105858422","d":"2025-10-07","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-10-07","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-10-07","organization":"NSW Reconstruction Authority","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"3,000 flood victims","action":"uploaded personal information to ChatGPT","consequence":"risks to personal security, potential exposure of private information"}},{"i":"R1204","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"The ABC understands a number of families attended Eastwood Police Station on Wednesday eve…","s":"The ABC understands a number of families attended Eastwood Police Station on Wednesday evening after growing frustrated that a local high school had failed to act on a complaint. Police have launched an investigation into reports from parents that digitally altered explicit images using the faces of female students have been created and shared. The ABC understands a number of families attended Eastwood Police Station on Wednesday evening. Police are investigating reports that digitally altered explicit images using the faces of female students from a Sydney high school have been circulated online. Police confirmed in a brief statement that an investigation was underway.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-10-16/police-investigate-sydney-school-image-deepfake/105896812","d":"2025-09-16","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-09-16","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-09-16","organization":"Police","aiSystem":"Deepfake technology","affectedGroup":"Parents and families of female students","action":"Created and shared digitally altered explicit images","consequence":"Online circulation of false images"}},{"i":"R1205","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Experts are calling for new regulations on artificial intelligence chatbots, after a tripl…","s":"Experts are calling for new regulations on artificial intelligence chatbots, after a triple j hack investigation uncovered a disturbing example of a chatbot encouraging a man to murder his father while engaging in paedophilic role-play. Experts are calling for new regulations to require artificial intelligence chatbots to remind users they are not speaking with a real human, after an investigation by triple j hack uncovered a disturbing example of a chatbot encouraging a man to murder his father while engaging in paedophilic role-play. WARNING: This story contains references to murder, violence, suicide, sexual content and other details that may cause distress.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-09-21/ai-chatbot-encourages-australian-man-to-murder-his-father/105793930","d":"2025-09-13","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-09-13","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-09-13","organization":"triple j hack","aiSystem":"artificial intelligence chatbot","affectedGroup":"a man","action":"encourage a man to murder his father","consequence":"disturbing example of a man being encouraged to commit murder"}},{"i":"R1206","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"An apparent test drive of Tesla's automated self-driving technology in Melbourne's CBD ear…","s":"An apparent test drive of Tesla's automated self-driving technology in Melbourne's CBD earlier this year was not approved by the Victorian government. A self-driving test conducted by Tesla earlier this year was not approved by the Victorian government. The Department of Transport and Planning requires a permit to ensure automated vehicle trials are conducted safely. Tesla says the Full Self-Driving (Supervised) feature will soon be available to Australian drivers. A video shared on X in May purported to show a car driving itself through busy streets while the driver had their hands off the steering wheel. The post claimed the Tesla even mastered one of Melbourne's notoriously tricky hook turns.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-09-09/tesla-fully-self-driving-melbourne-test-not-approved/105749690","d":"2025-09-09","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-09-09","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"earlier this year","organization":"Victorian government","aiSystem":"Tesla's automated self-driving technology","affectedGroup":"Tesla","action":"Conducted a self-driving test","consequence":"Not approved and denied a permit"}},{"i":"R1207","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.3  Employment displacement / quality harm"],"t":"CBA has apologised to 45 affected employees after finding customer service roles were not …","s":"CBA has apologised to 45 affected employees after finding customer service roles were not redundant despite introducing an AI-powered \"voice-bot\", but the union says the damage is done. Commonwealth Bank announced 45 job cuts last month, as it introduced an AI \"voice-bot\", but has now reversed its decision. The bank has apologised to affected employees for the \"error\", but the Finance Sector Union says the \"damage is already done\" to workers. CBA says workers can choose to stay in their roles, be redeployed or leave the bank. Another Fair Work hearing is scheduled for next week. The Commonwealth Bank has backtracked on dozens of job cuts, describing its decision to axe 45 roles due to artificial intelligence as an \"error\".","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-08-21/cba-backtracks-on-ai-job-cuts-as-chatbot-lifts-call-volumes/105679492","d":"2025-08-21","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-08-21","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-08-21","organization":"Commonwealth Bank","aiSystem":"AI-powered voice-bot","affectedGroup":"45 affected employees","action":"Introduced an AI-powered 'voice-bot'","consequence":"Job cuts reversed, but damage to workers' careers"}},{"i":"R1208","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"The fake submissions included fabricated quotes from a speech to the state legislature and…","s":"The fake submissions included fabricated quotes from a speech to the state legislature and non-existent case citations purportedly from Victoria's Supreme Court. A senior lawyer has apologised to a Victorian judge for filing submissions in a murder case that included AI-generated errors. The fake submissions included fabricated quotes from a speech to the state legislature and non-existent case citations purportedly from the Supreme Court. The AI-generated errors caused a 24-hour delay in resolving a case. A senior lawyer has apologised to a Victorian judge for filing submissions in a murder case that included fake quotes and non-existent case judgements generated by artificial intelligence (AI).","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-08-15/victoria-lawyer-apologises-after-ai-generated-submissions/105661208","d":"2025-08-15","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-08-15","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-08-15","organization":"Law firm or individual lawyer","aiSystem":"AI-generated content","affectedGroup":"Victorian judge","action":"Filed AI-generated submissions in a murder case","consequence":"Caused a 24-hour delay in resolving the case"}},{"i":"R1209","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.3  Employment displacement / quality harm"],"t":"The Productivity Commission says artificial intelligence could offer a $116 billion shot i…","s":"The Productivity Commission says artificial intelligence could offer a $116 billion shot in the arm to Australia's economy. But for people like voice actor Colin Cassidy, that means a disruption to their daily work. For many Australians, artificial intelligence is already disrupting their work. Among them is voice actor Colin Cassidy, who after decades of building his career said he had his voice cloned without his consent. \"It's a little bit like a redundancy which is very stressful from a mental health point of view,\" he said. \"But it goes deeper because it's your biometric data, it's part of you.\" Mr Cassidy estimated his booking numbers have reduced by 30 per cent due to AI.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-08-15/how-artificial-intelligence-could-threaten-jobs/105642364","d":"2025-08-14","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-08-14","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-08-14","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"Artificial intelligence","affectedGroup":"Voice actors, particularly Colin Cassidy","action":"AI cloned Colin Cassidy's voice without consent","consequence":"Reduced booking numbers for Colin Cassidy"}},{"i":"R1215","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.5  Human computer interaction harm"],"t":"More Australians are accessing generative AI chatbots for emotional guidance and assistanc…","s":"More Australians are accessing generative AI chatbots for emotional guidance and assistance — seeing it as a free, personal therapist. But experts have sounded a note of caution. Would you confide in an AI chatbot and tell it vulnerable, personal information about yourself? For some this might seem like the far-fetched plot of a sci-fi movie but for others, it's a reality much closer to home. But experts remain at odds when it comes to the overall benefits, weighing up free accessible advice with privacy concerns and cases involving critical care. Artificial Intelligence expert Ronnie Das said chatbots could predict what a person might say and then replicate those emotions back to them, using a statistical formula.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-05-18/people-using-artificial-intelligence-as-therapy/105266076","d":"2025-05-18","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-05-18","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-05-18","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"Generative AI chatbots","affectedGroup":"People seeking emotional guidance","action":"Chatbots predict and replicate emotions","consequence":"Privacy concerns and potential misuse"}},{"i":"R1216","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Google will launch its Gemini AI chatbot for Australian children under 13 within months, t…","s":"Google will launch its Gemini AI chatbot for Australian children under 13 within months, the ABC can reveal. The announcement has prompted calls for the government to consider banning AI chatbots for children. Google will launch its AI chatbot, Gemini, for Australian children under 13 in the coming months. The program is rolling out in the US this week, as Google warned parents Gemini \"can make mistakes\". Experts were alarmed, with some calling for a ban on AI chatbots for children, similar to the teen social media ban passed into law last year. The tech giant is rolling out the program in the US this week, with a worldwide launch to follow in the coming months, although no date has yet been specified.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-05-09/google-gemini-ai-launch-australian-kids/105267302","d":"2025-05-08","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2025-05-08","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-05-08","organization":"Google","aiSystem":"AI chatbot Gemini","affectedGroup":"Australian children under 13","action":"launched AI chatbot Gemini for Australian children under 13","consequence":"prompted calls for a ban on AI chatbots for children"}},{"i":"R1217","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"A pro-Russian influence operation has been targeting Australia in the lead-up to this week…","s":"A pro-Russian influence operation has been targeting Australia in the lead-up to this weekend's federal election, the ABC can reveal, attempting to \"poison\" AI chatbots with propaganda. A largely automated pro-Russian news website has been used to \"launder\" propaganda on Australian topics in the lead-up to this weekend's federal election. Pravda Australia has increased its output significantly since mid-March, in what experts say is an attempt to sway AI chatbots to spread \"Russian narratives\". Liberal home affairs spokesperson James Paterson says any allegations of foreign interference should be taken seriously and investigated.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-05-03/pro-russian-push-to-poison-ai-chatbots-in-australia/105239644","d":"2025-05-02","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-05-02","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-05-02","organization":"Pro-Russian influence operation","aiSystem":"AI chatbots","affectedGroup":"Australian voters","action":"Targeting AI chatbots with propaganda","consequence":"Spread of Russian narratives"}},{"i":"R1221","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Detectors that are used to uncover deepfake images have ‘major vulnerabilities’ when used …","s":"Detectors that are used to uncover deepfake images have ‘major vulnerabilities’ when used on videos they weren’t trained on, and can't keep up with the volume of AI images being generated, a new study suggests. A new CSIRO study has found that even the best deepfake detectors can spot a fake \"in the wild\" just two thirds of the time. This highlights the fast moving \"cat and mouse game\" being played by those making and distributing new types of deepfakes, and those trying to detect them. Researchers suggest specialised detectors need to be created that tackle specific types of deepfake content. It could be a weird pixel in the corner, or a slight facial change, but that could be all a deepfake detector needs to tell if an image or video is real or fake.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/science/2025-03-17/deepfake-detectors-ai-generators-fight/105046368","d":"2025-03-16","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-03-16","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-03-16","organization":"CSIRO","aiSystem":"Deepfake detectors","affectedGroup":"People viewing or sharing deepfake images","action":"Used to uncover deepfake images","consequence":"Can't accurately distinguish between real and fake content"}},{"i":"R1223","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Two students have been suspended from a school in Melbourne's north-west after a number of…","s":"Two students have been suspended from a school in Melbourne's north-west after a number of sexually explicit, AI-generated images of female students were circulated online. Victoria Police say an unknown number of sexually explicit, AI-generated images of female students at Gladstone Park Secondary College have been circulated online. Two year 11 students have been suspended and the Victorian government is working with the school to provide wellbeing support to the victims. Police are still trying to determine how many victims were impacted and an investigation remains ongoing. Child abuse detectives are investigating after a number of sexually explicit, AI-generated images of female students from a school in Melbourne's north-west were circulated online.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-02-21/gladstone-park-secondary-explicit-deepfakes-students/104965106","d":"2025-02-21","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-02-21","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-02-21","organization":"Victoria Police, Gladstone Park Secondary College","aiSystem":"AI-generated deepfake technology","affectedGroup":"Female students","action":"circulated online","consequence":"suspended from school, provided wellbeing support"}},{"i":"R1226","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"The decision follows advice from national security and intelligence agencies that determin…","s":"The decision follows advice from national security and intelligence agencies that determined the platform posed \"an unacceptable risk\" to government technology. DeepSeek, a Chinese AI chatbot that made headlines late last month for rivalling ChatGPT, has been banned from all federal government devices. According to the government, the decision follows advice from national security and intelligence agencies that determined the platform posed \"an unacceptable risk\" to national security. Federal government workers will still be able to use the program on their personal devices. DeepSeek, the Chinese artificial intelligence chatbot that sparked an international frenzy last month, has been banned from federal government computers and mobile devices after it was found to pose \"an unacceptable risk\" to national security.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-02-04/deepseek-banned-from-federal-government-devices/104896770","d":"2025-02-04","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-02-04","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-02-04","organization":"Federal government","aiSystem":"DeepSeek, a Chinese AI chatbot","affectedGroup":"Federal government workers","action":"Banned from using on federal government devices","consequence":"Pose 'an unacceptable risk' to national security"}},{"i":"R1227","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"Melbourne's lord mayor has called on governments to better manage the environmental conseq…","s":"Melbourne's lord mayor has called on governments to better manage the environmental consequences of the state's growing data centres, warning their development has the potential to \"cook the planet\". The City of Melbourne has voted unanimously to explore the \"responsible utilisation\" of AI infrastructure and data centres, citing environmental consequences. The lord mayor's motion cited an ABC report, which last year revealed proposed data centres in Melbourne's west could use up to 20 gigalitres of water a year — the equivalent of 4 per cent of the city's total water. Last November, Premier Jacinta Allan said she wanted Victoria to be \"ruthless\" about attracting more data centres.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-02-04/melbourne-lord-mayor-ai-data-centres-environmental-impact/106300650","d":"2025-02-03","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-02-03","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-02-03","organization":"City of Melbourne","aiSystem":"AI infrastructure and data centers","affectedGroup":"Environment","action":"Explore responsible utilization of AI infrastructure and data centers","consequence":"Potential environmental consequences, including water usage"}},{"i":"R1228","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Politicians are worried deepfakes could affect the election","s":"Politicians are worried deepfakes could affect the election. So, ABC News got one made to see how convincing they can be. Sitting in a Sydney television studio, Greens senator David Shoebridge is calling for cannabis to be legalised — or at least that's what it looks like. It's something he's said many times before and the person talking looks exactly like the senator. It's a deepfake — made by a computer security company at the request of ABC News to investigate just how believable political deepfakes can be. The accent is off, with an American twang, but Senator Shoebridge says it's otherwise \"uncomfortably persuasive\".","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-01-31/politicians-fear-deepfakes-could-impact-election/104876130","d":"2025-01-30","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2025-01-30","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-01-30","organization":"Computer security company at the request of ABC News","aiSystem":"Deepfake technology","affectedGroup":"Politicians, voters, public opinion","action":"Generated and distributed a convincing deepfake video of a politician","consequence":"Could mislead public opinion and affect election results"}},{"i":"R1229","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.8  Interpersonal / relational harm"],"t":"As the popularity of AI-generated companions grows, there are calls for greater regulation…","s":"As the popularity of AI-generated companions grows, there are calls for greater regulation of a rapidly expanding world that some fear is disconnecting users from real-life human relationships. Mental health and youth service providers say they've noticed rising interest in AI chatbot companions. In two independent studies, Australian researchers have found concerning user trends, including diminished interest in human interactions. But users of AI companions say they can also offer support, advice and a chance to explore imaginative worlds. Engineering student Yiwei Cheng was 21 when she created her \"AI boyfriend\" Chad.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-01-07/ai-chatbot-husbando-waifu-relationships-romance-psychology/104701556","d":"2025-01-06","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-01-06","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-01-06","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"AI chatbot companions","affectedGroup":"Users of AI companions","action":"Created AI boyfriend Chad","consequence":"Diminished interest in human interactions"}},{"i":"R1231","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Generative artificial intelligence tools like OpenAI's ChatGPT should be deemed \"high-risk…","s":"Generative artificial intelligence tools like OpenAI's ChatGPT should be deemed \"high-risk\" under dedicated legislation that could enable the most dangerous technologies to be banned, a bipartisan committee recommends. It says AI companies have also committed \"unprecedented theft\" against creative workers and a fair compensation system should be developed. A dedicated artificial intelligence (AI) act should be created that can regulate the most high-risk technologies, a bipartisan committee has agreed. It also accused tech giants of stealing from Australian creators, and urged the government to develop a scheme to ensure fair remuneration when creative work is used by AI tools.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2024-11-26/chatgpt-high-risk-ai-act-creative-theft/104646664","d":"2024-11-26","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-11-26","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-11-26","organization":"Tech giants","aiSystem":"Generative artificial intelligence tools like OpenAI's ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Australian creators","action":"Used creative work by AI tools","consequence":"Stealing from Australian creators, lack of fair remuneration"}},{"i":"R1232","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Employees could be monitored at work or subjected to discrimination during recruitment if …","s":"Employees could be monitored at work or subjected to discrimination during recruitment if there is not appropriate oversight of artificial intelligence in the workplace, according to unions. But Australia's peak business body has cautioned against introducing further regulation that could prevent local businesses from having a \"competitive edge\". Workers could be closely monitored and potentially discriminated against without appropriate oversight of artificial intelligence in the workplace, unions say. The Australian Council of Trade Unions has called for a national AI authority to be introduced to regulate the adoption of the technology in workplaces.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2024-11-13/artificial-intelligence-impact-in-the-workplace/104569578","d":"2024-11-12","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2024-11-12","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-11-12","organization":"Australian Council of Trade Unions","aiSystem":"artificial intelligence in the workplace","affectedGroup":"workers","action":"closely monitored and potentially discriminated against","consequence":"discriminatory hiring outcome"}},{"i":"R1234","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Deployment Context / Weapons"],"h":["5.1  Autonomous weapons / lethal targeting harm"],"t":"As governments around the world turn to artificial intelligence and autonomous weapons in …","s":"As governments around the world turn to artificial intelligence and autonomous weapons in conflict, human rights advocates and scientists are calling for stronger frameworks to guide their use. Last month the Australian Defence Force unveiled a suite of weapons at the land autonomous systems and teaming demonstrations at the Puckapunyal Army Base in northern Victoria. Among them were drones that fly themselves, robotic combat vehicles, uncrewed tanks, and a robot dog that can be used to clear landmines. The weapons are not fully autonomous and require a level of human interaction. Robotic and Autonomous Systems director Lieutenant Colonel Adam Hepworth said the Australian Army saw a range of uses for AI in administrative settings and \"on the coalface of war\".","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2024-10-28/autonomous-weapons-ethics-defence-puckapunyal-ai-robotic-drones/104469990","d":"2024-09-27","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2024-09-27","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-09-27","organization":"Australian Defence Force","aiSystem":"Robotic and Autonomous Systems","affectedGroup":"People in conflict zones","action":"Used to clear landmines","consequence":"Exposure to surveillance and tracking"}},{"i":"R1235","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Showing how easy it is to make deepfakes of politicians using artificial intelligence, ind…","s":"Showing how easy it is to make deepfakes of politicians using artificial intelligence, independent senator David Pocock creates AI videos of Prime Minister Anthony Albanese and Opposition Leader Peter Dutton. ACT senator David Pocock created and shared AI-generated videos of the prime minister and opposition leader as a warning to pass urgent legislation on artificial intelligence. Senator Pocock said his videos, which depicted the two leaders promising a ban on gambling ads, highlighted how easily AI could be misused in election campaigning next year. The issue of deepfakes and generative AI has been much debated but the government has not confirmed whether regulation will be passed in time for the federal election.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2024-09-07/david-pocock-creates-ai-deepfake-of-albanese/104323688","d":"2024-09-07","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2024-09-07","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-09-07","organization":"Independent senator David Pocock","aiSystem":"Generative AI","affectedGroup":"Politicians, specifically Prime Minister Anthony Albanese and Opposition Leader Peter Dutton","action":"Created and shared AI-generated videos","consequence":"Highlighted the potential misuse of AI in election campaigning"}},{"i":"R1236","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Romance scams are on the rise in WA, with authorities warning people to be vigilant of sca…","s":"Romance scams are on the rise in WA, with authorities warning people to be vigilant of scammers using artificial intelligence to disguise themselves in video calls. Authorities are warning people to remain vigilant of scammers using AI to disguise themselves in video calls. A total of 26 West Australians have lost $2.9 million to romance scams this year. State Commerce Minister Sue Ellery has encouraged people to report suspicious online activity to authorities. After losing her husband and moving across the globe to Perth where her children were but where she knew nobody, Maggie Ayres fell into a deep depression.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2024-08-28/deepfake-ai-used-in-wa-romance-scams/104279902","d":"2024-08-28","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-08-28","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-08-28","organization":"Authorities","aiSystem":"Deepfake AI","affectedGroup":"People in romance scams","action":"Using deepfake AI to disguise themselves in video calls","consequence":"Loss of money and emotional distress"}},{"i":"R1238","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Data"],"h":["4.3  Data rights / consent violation"],"t":"Australia's privacy watchdog says there's \"cause for concern\" over X's harvesting of the u…","s":"Australia's privacy watchdog says there's \"cause for concern\" over X's harvesting of the user data of its Australian users to train a new generation of AI chatbots. X's harvesting of user data to train an artificial intelligence system may breach Australian privacy law, according to the national privacy watchdog. The Office of the Australian Information Commissioner said the practice was a \"cause for concern\" but stopped short of launching an inquiry. The Commissioner's Office says it's \"looking at such practices across the industry\" as other major platforms, also competing to build their own AIs, harvest user data.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/science/2024-07-31/elon-musk-x-breach-privacy-law-data-harvest-grok-ai/104054400","d":"2024-07-30","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2024-07-30","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-07-30","organization":"X","aiSystem":"Artificial Intelligence System","affectedGroup":"Australian Users","action":"Harvesting User Data","consequence":"Potential Breach of Australian Privacy Law"}},{"i":"R1239","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Just hours after the Queensland premier described a fake video of him dancing as a \"turnin…","s":"Just hours after the Queensland premier described a fake video of him dancing as a \"turning point\" for democracy, a video surfaced of his federal colleagues using the same tactics. Labor used generative artificial intelligence (AI) to create a clip mocking Peter Dutton's nuclear power plan. The video has surfaced just hours after Queensland Premier Steven Miles slammed a similar video of himself that was posted by the state LNP opposition. There are currently no rules at either the state or federal level to stop political parties and candidates from using AI-generated material in election campaigns. Federal Labor has been challenged over an AI video mocking Peter Dutton hours after the Queensland premier called a similar video of himself a \"turning point for our democracy\".","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2024-07-23/labor-questioned-over-ai-generated-tiktok-of-peter-dutton/104131228","d":"2024-07-23","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-07-23","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-07-23","organization":"Federal Labor party","aiSystem":"Generative artificial intelligence (AI)","affectedGroup":"Peter Dutton","action":"Created an AI-generated video mocking Peter Dutton's nuclear power plan","consequence":"Exposure of misleading content"}},{"i":"R1240","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Steven Miles has hit out at his opponent after the Queensland opposition used artificial i…","s":"Steven Miles has hit out at his opponent after the Queensland opposition used artificial intelligence to create a fake video to mock the premier. The Queensland LNP has used artificial intelligence to create a fake video of Steven Miles dancing to post to social media. There are currently no rules at either the state or federal level to stop political parties and candidates from using AI generated material in election campaigns. Steven Miles says the opposition leader must explain why the LNP used deepfake content. The Queensland opposition has used artificial intelligence to create a fake video of Steven Miles busting a move to a popular Ne-Yo song as election campaigning in the state ramps up.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2024-07-22/qld-premier-slams-opposition-for-ai-generated-tiktok/104126936","d":"2024-07-22","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-07-22","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-07-22","organization":"Queensland Opposition","aiSystem":"AI-generated content","affectedGroup":"Steven Miles","action":"Used artificial intelligence to generate a fake video of Steven Miles dancing","consequence":"Mocked by the Premier and criticized for using deepfake content"}},{"i":"R1241","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"The discovery of photos of Australian children in a gargantuan dataset being used to train…","s":"The discovery of photos of Australian children in a gargantuan dataset being used to train leading AI models has given rise to a blame game and some divisive questions. Hello and welcome to Screenshot, your weekly tech update from national technology reporter Ange Lavoipierre, featuring the best, worst and strangest in tech and online news. Read to the end to meet the under 30s who prefer chatbots to humans. On some level, it makes sense that no-one wants to take responsibility for something this nightmarish. Photos of Australian children have been discovered in a gargantuan dataset that's used to train AI image generators – without the kids' or their parents' consent.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2024-07-09/kids-data-photos-ai-models-who-is-to-blame/104059316","d":"2024-07-08","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-07-08","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-07-08","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"AI image generators","affectedGroup":"Australian children","action":"Photos of Australian children were discovered in a dataset used to train AI image generators","consequence":"Privacy infringement"}},{"i":"R1242","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"A student has been expelled at a school in Melbourne's east after creating explicit images…","s":"A student has been expelled at a school in Melbourne's east after creating explicit images of a female teacher. The Independent Education Union says images of several other teachers have been used to create deepfake pornography. They're fake, often graphic images of real people and they are increasingly being circulated around Victorian schools. This week, about 50 Bacchus Marsh Grammar students had images taken from their social media accounts and manipulated into deepfake nudes using artificial intelligence (AI). Just weeks earlier, a boy was expelled from Salesian College, a catholic school in Melbourne's south-east, after he created fake sexual images of a female teacher which were circulated around the school.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2024-06-13/ai-generated-deepfake-pornography-school-students-teachers/103969414","d":"2024-06-12","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-06-12","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-06-12","organization":"Bacchus Marsh Grammar School and Salesian College","aiSystem":"AI-generated deepfake technology","affectedGroup":"Students and teachers","action":"Manipulated images of teachers into deepfake nudes","consequence":"Expulsion of students"}},{"i":"R1243","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Data"],"h":["4.3  Data rights / consent violation"],"t":"If you're among the majority of Australians with Facebook or Instagram accounts, your soci…","s":"If you're among the majority of Australians with Facebook or Instagram accounts, your social activity on those platforms might be training Meta's artificial intelligence (AI) tools — and if you live in Australia, you can't say no. If you're among the majority of Australians with Facebook or Instagram accounts, your social activity on those platforms is likely being used to train Meta's artificial intelligence (AI), and there's little you can do to stop it. There has been growing concern after Meta – the parent company of Facebook and Instagram – flagged a new privacy policy that was to take effect in Europe and the UK from June 26.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2024-06-10/instagram-facebook-train-meta-ai-tools-no-opt-out-australia/103958308","d":"2024-06-10","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-06-10","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-06-10","organization":"Meta (parent company of Facebook and Instagram)","aiSystem":"Meta's artificial intelligence (AI) tools","affectedGroup":"Australians with Facebook or Instagram accounts","action":"Training of AI tools using social activity data","consequence":"Loss of control over personal data and privacy"}},{"i":"R1245","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Artificial intelligence is being used to generate child abuse material, and police are inc…","s":"Artificial intelligence is being used to generate child abuse material, and police are increasingly worried about its spread. But AI may also be able to help investigators. It takes less than one minute, with the help of a few prompts into an artificial intelligence (AI) image generator, to create a photo depicting a child in a sexually explicit way. Warning: This article contains details that readers may find distressing. It's one of the latest online threats that law enforcement agencies fear will only increase. \"We do anticipate this increasing, very much so,\" said Detective Superintendent Frank Rayner from the Australian Centre To Counter Child Exploitation (ACCCE) \"The tools that people can access online to create and modify using AI are expanding and they're becoming more sophisticated as well.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2024-04-18/artificial-intelligence-child-exploitation-material/103734216","d":"2024-04-17","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-04-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-04-17","organization":"Australian Centre To Counter Child Exploitation (ACCCE)","aiSystem":"AI image generator","affectedGroup":"Children","action":"Generated sexually explicit images of children","consequence":"Increased availability of child exploitation material online"}},{"i":"R1246","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Senator Jacqui Lambie allowed ABC NEWS Verify to use inexpensive AI tools to create a robo…","s":"Senator Jacqui Lambie allowed ABC NEWS Verify to use inexpensive AI tools to create a robocall with her voice ahead of the federal election — it was good enough to fool some of her supporters. Senator Jacqui Lambie listens intently as a robocall is played from a computer in front of her. \"G'day Tasmanians, it's senator Jacqui Lambie here. \"As the federal election gets closer, let's get serious. I want to make a pledge to the people of Tasmania, that if re-elected, I will fight tooth and nail to move the national capital from Canberra to Tassie, where it bloody well belongs.\" What Senator Lambie just heard was a clone of her voice, produced by ABC NEWS Verify with her permission, to demonstrate the danger of the technology as the election nears.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-02-28/jacqui-lambie-ai-generated-voice-election-and-deepfakes/104986434","d":"2024-02-28","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2024-02-28","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-02-28","organization":"ABC NEWS Verify","aiSystem":"AI-generated voice cloning technology","affectedGroup":"Senator Jacqui Lambie's supporters","action":"Created a robocall with Senator Jacqui Lambie's voice","consequence":"Some supporters were fooled into believing the call was genuine"}},{"i":"R1252","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"General Angus Campbell warns the world is entering an era of \"truth decay\", where misinfor…","s":"General Angus Campbell warns the world is entering an era of \"truth decay\", where misinformation will harm democracy, leaving liberal, Western societies like Australia exposed to enemies. Defence force chief Angus Campbell warns deepfakes and AI will drive era of 'truth decay' The defence force chief has warned the world is entering an era of \"truth decay\" where misinformation will undermine democracy and leave liberal, Western societies like Australia increasingly exposed to enemies. Key points: - General Angus Campbell fears public confidence in elected officials will be damaged as AI and deepfake use accelerates - He fears Australians will increasingly struggle to separate fact from fiction - General Campbell has accused Russia of seeking to fracture allegiances between Western democracies General Angus Campbell has outlined future challenges where rapidly advancing technology could…","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2023-09-15/angus-campbell-warns-about-deepfakes-artificial-intelligence/102860418","d":"2023-09-15","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2023-09-15","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-09-15","organization":"Defence force chief","aiSystem":"Deepfakes and AI","affectedGroup":"Liberal, Western societies like Australia","action":"Used to undermine democracy and expose people to false information","consequence":"Damage public confidence in elected officials and difficulty separating fact from fiction"}},{"i":"R1256","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.2  Allocative harm"],"t":"ChatGPT is currently banned in many schools","s":"ChatGPT is currently banned in many schools. But where it's not, students — and their teachers — say they are using it to prepare for a future in which artificial intelligence is likely to be a big part of work and life. Public school bans on AI tools like ChatGPT raise fears private school kids are gaining an unfair edge and widening a digital divide History teacher Matt Esterman opens the \"Artificial Intelligence teacher toolkit\" on his laptop to show off software that could turbocharge classrooms of the future. Key points: - ChatGPT is banned in most Australian public schools - Some independent schools are incorporating it into teaching, creating fears of a \"digital divide\" - Education ministers are expected to discuss it at a meeting in July He sees AI as an educational \"copilot\" that could give students and teachers here an edge.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2023-05-26/artificial-intelligence-chatgpt-classrooms-schools/102356926","d":"2023-05-25","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-05-25","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-05-25","organization":"Australian public schools","aiSystem":"AI tools like ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Students and teachers in public schools","action":"Banning AI tools like ChatGPT","consequence":"Creating fears of a 'digital divide'"}},{"i":"R1261","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Hepburn Shire Mayor Brian Hood may become the first Australian to sue a chatbot for lying …","s":"Hepburn Shire Mayor Brian Hood may become the first Australian to sue a chatbot for lying about him, after he was incorrectly deemed a guilty party in a bribery scandal. Hepburn mayor may sue OpenAI for defamation over false ChatGPT claims A regional mayor may take unprecedented legal action to sue the creator of AI chatbot ChatGPT for defamation, after the program falsely claimed he was a guilty party in a foreign bribery scandal. Key points: - Councillor Hood is considering suing ChatGPT over incorrectly describing him as a guilty party in a bribery scandal in the early 2000s - Cr Hood says he has tendered a \"concerns letter\" to OpenAI, the company that created ChatGPT - Legal experts say the case is in uncharted legal territory Brian Hood, the mayor of Hepburn Shire Council, west of Melbourne, was recently alerted to the fact that ChatGPT, a chatbot designed to provide detailed…","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2023-04-06/hepburn-mayor-flags-legal-action-over-false-chatgpt-claims/102195610","d":"2023-04-06","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-04-06","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-04-06","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Brian Hood, Hepburn Shire Mayor","action":"ChatGPT falsely claimed Brian Hood was a guilty party in a bribery scandal","consequence":"May consider filing a defamation lawsuit against OpenAI"}},{"i":"R1283","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Australia clamps downs on nudify sites used for ai generated child abuse","s":"Three websites used to create abuse imagery had received 100,000 monthly visits from Australians, watchdog says. Internet users in Australia have been blocked from accessing several websites that used artificial intelligence to create child sexual exploitation material, the country’s internet regulator has announced. The three “nudify” sites withdrew from Australia following an official warning, eSafety Commissioner Julie Inman Grant said on Thursday. Grant’s office said the sites had been receiving approximately 100,000 visits a month from Australians and featured in high-profile cases of AI-generated child sex abuse imagery involving Australian school students.","u":"https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2025/11/27/australia-clamps-downs-on-nudify-sites-used-for-ai-generated-child-abuse","d":"2025-11-27","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-11-27","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-11-27","organization":"eSafety Commissioner Julie Inman Grant's office","aiSystem":"Artificial Intelligence (AI)","affectedGroup":"Internet users in Australia","action":"Blocked from accessing several websites","consequence":"Received official warning"}},{"i":"R1309","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Australia bets on facial recognition for problem gamblers","s":"Critics say burgeoning technology is invasive, dangerous and undermines basic rights. As guests arrive at eastern Australia’s Warilla Hotel, a small camera equipped with facial recognition software scans their faces as part of a scheme to tackle problem gambling. The tech – which uses artificial intelligence (AI) to identify addicts who have asked to be barred from betting sites – is set to be rolled out across gambling venues in the state of New South Wales next year. Recommended Stories list of 4 items - list 1 of 4Facebook is pulling the plug on its facial recognition system - list 2 of 4South Korea to use facial recognition to track COVID-19 patients - list 3 of 4Facial recognition taken to court in India’s surveillance hotspot - list 4 of 4Texas sues Meta over Facebook’s facial recognition practices Supporters say it will help curb problem gambling in a country where the addiction…","u":"https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2022/11/4/australia-bets-on-facial-recognition-for-problem-gamblers","d":"2021-11-04","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-11-04","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-11-04","organization":"Warilla Hotel","aiSystem":"facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"problem gamblers","action":"scans faces of guests arriving at the hotel","consequence":"undermines basic rights, invasive, dangerous"}},{"i":"R1321","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"The 19-year-old is the first person to be charged under a new national law","s":"The 19-year-old is the first person to be charged under a new national law. An Australian teenager has pleaded guilty to creating deepfake pornography, in a landmark case. William Hamish Yeates, 19, is the first person to be charged under a new national law which criminalises the manipulation of sexual images and carries a maximum penalty of seven years in prison. Experts says deepfake pornography - often created through artificial intelligence technology, and overwhelmingly targeting women and girls - is the new frontier of gendered, image-based abuse and school bullying. Yeates did not comment as he left court after admitting four offences on Wednesday.","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c39333x0xeno","d":"2026-04-15","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-04-15","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-04-15","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"AI-generated deepfake technology","affectedGroup":"Women and girls","action":"Created deepfake pornography","consequence":"Subjected to gendered, image-based abuse and school bullying"}},{"i":"R1427","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Western australias lfr trial draws criticism over privacy risks limited consultation","s":"More than 130,000 faces were scanned in the first week of the LFR trial with a single police van parked in crowded public spaces. A five-month mobile trial of live facial recognition in Western Australia has led to real-time results and concern. During a press conference Western Australia’s police commissioner Col Blanch was briefing reporters when a marked police van in the vicinity highlighted a woman in the crowd. It did this using facial recognition technology that scans faces, cross-referencing them instantly against a database of around 4,000 people on a police watchlist. Halfway through Blanch’s conference, the system alerted police whose officers then approached a woman in the crowd who was wanted for failing to appear in court.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202607/western-australias-lfr-trial-draws-criticism-over-privacy-risks-limited-consultation","d":"2026-07-22","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-22","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07","organization":"Western Australia's police department","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"Crowd in public spaces","action":"Facial recognition technology scanned faces in real-time","consequence":"Police approached and detained a woman based on the system's alert"}},{"i":"R1552","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Aussie privacy watchdog casts evil eye at clearview auror for biometrics collection","s":"The Australian government will not pursue further investigations into Clearview AI, but an OAIC statement calls its harvesting of biometric data troubling. The Australian government will not pursue further investigations into Clearview AI , but a statement issued by the Office of the Australian Information Commissioner (OAIC) says a previous determination finding the company in breach of the nation’s privacy laws still stands and calls the company’s harvesting of biometric data “troubling.” “I have given extensive consideration to the question of whether the OAIC should invest further resources in scrutinizing the actions of Clearview AI, a company that has already been investigated by the OAIC and which has found itself the subject of regulatory investigations in at least three jurisdictions around the world as well as a class action in the United States,” says Privacy Commissioner…","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202408/aussie-privacy-watchdog-casts-evil-eye-at-clearview-auror-for-biometrics-collection","d":"2024-08-21","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-08-21","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-08-21","organization":"Office of the Australian Information Commissioner (OAIC)","aiSystem":"Clearview AI","affectedGroup":"Individuals whose biometric data was harvested by Clearview AI","action":"Harvesting of biometric data","consequence":"Calls the harvesting of biometric data ‘troubling’"}},{"i":"R1583","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Reported australian biometric data breach prompts arrest and hysteria","s":"An apparent data breach of personal information including face biometrics of about a million Australians has been reported, and an arrest made. People claiming to be former developers for Outabox in the Philippines created a website claiming they have “facial recognition biometric, driver licence [sic] scan, signature, club membership data, address, birthday, phone number, club visit timestamps, slot machine usage” data. It also says the hack is a response to Outabox’ failure to pay them for 18 months. The data was apparently collected from 19 venues in New South Wales and the Australian Capital Territory operated by ClubsNSW.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202405/reported-australian-biometric-data-breach-prompts-arrest-and-hysteria","d":"2024-05-03","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-05-03","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-05-03","organization":"Outabox","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"People claiming to be former developers for Outabox in the Philippines","action":"Created a website claiming to have access to personal biometric data","consequence":"Arrest made"}},{"i":"R1601","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.2  Allocative harm"],"t":"Overcoming barriers australias digital id bill imposes on proof of aboriginality","s":"The pending Digital ID Bill will impact how finance, business, and travel are carried out but what implications will it have on racially marginalized groups. It’s been broadly discussed that Australia’s pending Digital ID Bill will have implications for how finance, business, and travel are carried out. What has been less discussed is what implications it will have on racially marginalized groups, namely the Aboriginal population. Federal law only started applying to Aboriginal Australians in 1967 once they began being counted in Australia’s population. The demographic group remains unmentioned in the nation’s constitution.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202402/overcoming-barriers-australias-digital-id-bill-imposes-on-proof-of-aboriginality","d":"2024-02-13","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-02-13","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-02-13","organization":"Australian Government","aiSystem":"Digital ID Bill","affectedGroup":"Aboriginal population","action":"Impose digital identification requirements","consequence":"Exposure to privacy risks"}},{"i":"R1619","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Pimeyes sees use by australian police enacts ban on searches for children","s":"Controversial facial recognition website PimEyes is causing new concerns in Australia, where police have tested the biometric tool. The controversial Tbilisi-based facial recognition website PimEyes , which lets users upload photos of anonymous people and scrapes the Internet for matches, is causing new concerns in Australia, where police have tested the biometric tool, according to a report in the Guardian. A freedom of information request by the news organization shows that Australian federal police (AFP) devices logged hundreds of network connections to PimEyes between January and August 2023, ten of which were identified as being for operational purposes.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202310/pimeyes-sees-use-by-australian-police-enacts-ban-on-searches-for-children","d":"2023-10-24","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-10-24","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-01-01","organization":"Australian federal police (AFP)","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"People whose photos were uploaded to PimEyes","action":"Police logged network connections to PimEyes","consequence":"Exposure to online surveillance"}},{"i":"R1798","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Chatgpt lawsuit australia openai b2315525","s":"Australian mayor says he may sue OpenAI over chatbot’s ‘false claims’. in Australia Australian mayor says he may sue OpenAI over chatbot’s ‘false claims’ - Bookmark - CommentsGo to comments OpenAI is facing a lawsuit in Australia after a regional mayor accused ChatGPT of sharing false claims about him. Victorian Mayor Brian Hood claims the AI chatbot was telling users that he had served time in prison as a result of a foreign bribery scandal. He said he would sue OpenAI if the incorrect information was not removed, which would mark the first defamation lawsuit against an artificial intelligence chatbot Mayor Hood became concerned about his reputation when members of the public told him ChatGPT had falsely named him as a guilty party in a bribery case involving a subsidiary of the Reserve Bank of Australia in the early 2000s.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/tech/chatgpt-lawsuit-australia-openai-b2315525.html","d":"2023-04-06","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-04-06","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-04-06","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"AI chatbot (ChatGPT)","affectedGroup":"Victorian Mayor Brian Hood","action":"Generated false claims about the mayor","consequence":"Mayoral reputation damage, potential defamation lawsuit"}},{"i":"R1180","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Serious concerns about an influx of large-scale data centres in Melbourne's west are being…","s":"Serious concerns about an influx of large-scale data centres in Melbourne's west are being raised by Fire Rescue Victoria insiders. Fire Rescue Victoria insiders say they are concerned about the risks posed by an influx of data centres in Melbourne's west. The insiders say they are specifically worried about the service's ability to respond to incidences of thermal runaway — where lithium-ion batteries undergo an uncontrollable chemical reaction that produces significant amounts of heat, resulting in a self-sustaining fire. The state government says fire agencies were consulted throughout planning processes, adding that projects must meet stringent fire safety requirements.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-06-09/firefighter-concern-over-data-centres-in-melbourne-west/106762858","d":"2026-06-08","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-06-08","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-06-08","organization":"Fire Rescue Victoria","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"Firefighters","action":"Respond to incidents of thermal runaway","consequence":"Exposure to privacy risks due to inadequate response capabilities"}},{"i":"R1182","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"A Victorian state MP's use of an apparently AI-generated image raises questions about whet…","s":"A Victorian state MP's use of an apparently AI-generated image raises questions about whether AI has any place in politics. A Victorian state politician has used an apparently AI-generated image of a whale in a social media post marking the start of the whale-watching season along Victoria's south-west coast. It has raised questions about whether politicians should be using AI-generated work at the expense of local artists, and whether it could erode public trust. The Australian and Victorian Electoral Commissions have AI guidelines for politicians and candidates around elections, but there are concerns about its use by politicians in general.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-05-30/politicians-criticised-for-using-ai-generated-images/106728456","d":"2026-05-29","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-05-29","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-05-29","organization":"Victorian state MP","aiSystem":"AI-generated images","affectedGroup":"Local artists, public","action":"Used an AI-generated image in a social media post","consequence":"Questions about public trust, concerns about eroding trust"}},{"i":"R1183","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"A new report concludes the AI-fuelled surge of power-hungry data centres across Australia …","s":"A new report concludes the AI-fuelled surge of power-hungry data centres across Australia is jeopardising the country's energy transition. A new report has concluded the AI-fuelled surge of power-hungry data centres across Australia is jeopardising the country's energy transition. Analysis commissioned by Greenpeace says the unforeseen growth in electricity demand to feed the AI boom is even bigger than what it is taking to electrify our cars and houses. Independent analysts say this sudden demand for energy could mean Australia needs to rely on more expensive, polluting energy sources to fill the gap. Data centres powering the artificial intelligence boom could demand more new electricity over the coming 15 years than Australia's cars or homes, according to a Greenpeace report that warns the sudden and unprecedented growth threatens the country's transition to renewable energy.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-05-27/ai-data-centres-pressuring-energy-transition-greenpeace-says/106722390","d":"2026-05-26","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-05-26","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-05-26","organization":"Greenpeace","aiSystem":"AI-fuelled data centers","affectedGroup":"Australia's energy transition efforts","action":"Power-hungry data centers consume electricity","consequence":"Increased reliance on expensive, polluting energy sources"}},{"i":"R1185","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.2  Allocative harm"],"t":"AI-perfected CVs are becoming the norm as more job applicants and employers use artificial…","s":"AI-perfected CVs are becoming the norm as more job applicants and employers use artificial intelligence, but recruiters say the technology could be making hiring trickier. Applicants and employers are increasingly using artificial intelligence in hiring processes to improve efficiency, but recruiters have warned there are risks for both parties. Widespread use of AI is causing all resumes to look the same, recruiters say, while jobseekers say the technology helps them pass keyword filters and other early-stage application hurdles. Experts predict AI will continue to be used for recruitment, but risks need to be managed by human oversight.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-05-22/cv-loses-value-artificial-intelligence-changes-recruitment/106690068","d":"2026-05-21","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-05-21","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-05-21","organization":"Recruiters","aiSystem":"AI-perfected CVs","affectedGroup":"Jobseekers","action":"AI-generated CVs bypassing keyword filters","consequence":"Fewer opportunities for jobseekers"}},{"i":"R1188","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Two parents of girls at a private school have criticised the institution's response to a d…","s":"Two parents of girls at a private school have criticised the institution's response to a deepfake incident, saying they were encouraged not to tell their daughters they had been identified in material shared in a boys' group chat. Girls at a Tasmanian private school have been targeted in a deepfake incident, with AI-doctored pornographic depictions shared on a boys' group chat, several parents say. Two parents have criticised the school's lack of a victim-focused response, saying they were encouraged not to tell their daughters they had been identified in the material. The minister has referred a complaint to the non-government school regulator.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-04-29/tas-friends-school-criticised-after-deepfake-scandal/106577310","d":"2026-04-21","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-04-21","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-04-21","organization":"Tasmanian private school","aiSystem":"AI-generated deepfake technology","affectedGroup":"Girls at the private school","action":"Shared AI-generated pornographic depictions on a boys' group chat","consequence":"Encouraged not to inform victims about being identified"}},{"i":"R1189","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.2  Land use and siting harm"],"t":"A huge artificial intelligence data centre capable of training models like ChatGPT is plan…","s":"A huge artificial intelligence data centre capable of training models like ChatGPT is planned for WA's remote north at a cost of billons of dollars. A multi-billion-dollar project could see a large-scale AI training data centre established in Western Australia's north. The facility, south of Broome in the Kimberley, would predominantly run on renewable energy and is intended to support training of large AI models, like those behind ChatGPT. The company plans to advance environmental studies in the coming months, then refer the project to WA's environmental watchdog for assessment. Plans are underway to build the nation's biggest data centre in Western Australia's remote far north to support large-scale artificial intelligence (AI) training, including systems like ChatGPT.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-04-20/plans-emerge-gigawatt-scale-ai-data-centre-kimberley/106567332","d":"2026-04-19","e":"Acute and chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-04-19","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-04-19","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"Large AI models, such as those behind ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"People living in Western Australia's Kimberley region","action":"Plans for a large-scale AI training data center","consequence":"Potential exposure to personal data and privacy risks"}},{"i":"R1191","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"As the first case under new anti-deepfake legislation goes before the courts, authorities …","s":"As the first case under new anti-deepfake legislation goes before the courts, authorities and experts say the rise in the creation of the non-consensual sexually explicit material is concerning. A South Australian man has become the first, and so far the only, person in the country to be prosecuted under a new federal law aimed at combating deepfake adult pornography. This week, a suppression order was lifted to reveal Netherby resident William Hamish Yeates, 19 has been charged with eight counts of creating or altering sexual material without consent — a federal offence introduced in 2024 to combat deepfake pornography.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-02-27/rise-in-sexualised-deepfakes-new-laws-explainer/106390940","d":"2026-02-27","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-02-27","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-02-27","organization":"Authorities and experts","aiSystem":"Deepfake technology","affectedGroup":"People who view or are exposed to the non-consensual sexually explicit material","action":"Creation or alteration of sexual material without consent","consequence":"Exposure to misleading content"}},{"i":"R1192","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Bunnings was reasonably entitled to use AI facial recognition technology to combat crime a…","s":"Bunnings was reasonably entitled to use AI facial recognition technology to combat crime and staff abuse in its stores, the Administrative Review Tribunal finds. Bunnings was reasonably entitled to use AI facial recognition technology to combat crime and staff abuse in its stores, the Administrative Review Tribunal has found. The hardware giant did not breach privacy laws in scanning customers' faces, but could have done more to notify them of the data collection, the tribunal said. The ruling could pave the way for other major retailers to embrace this technology, according to one expert. Australian hardware giant Bunnings has won its fight to monitor its customers with artificial intelligence facial recognition technology to combat retail crime and reduce staff abuse.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-02-05/bunnings-wins-ai-facial-recognition-tech-fight/106309308","d":"2026-02-05","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-02-05","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-02-05","organization":"Australian hardware giant Bunnings","aiSystem":"AI facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"Customers","action":"Scanned customers' faces","consequence":"Could have done more to notify them of data collection"}},{"i":"R1193","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.2  Land use and siting harm"],"t":"Victoria's east is set to become home to a $10 billion data centre that will support the t…","s":"Victoria's east is set to become home to a $10 billion data centre that will support the technology necessary for artificial intelligence systems. A $10 billion data centre will be built at Hazelwood in Victoria's east, providing necessary infrastructure for the artificial intelligence boom. Singaporean asset manager and operator Keppel Ltd says the 720-megawatt facility will be in a prime location due to its proximity to the electrical network and water sources. Latrobe City Council says it will work with Keppel throughout the approval process. Victoria's Gippsland region will soon be home to one of the largest pieces of artificial intelligence infrastructure in Australia.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-01-22/keppel-data-centre-latrobe-valley/106123748","d":"2026-01-21","e":"Acute and chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-01-21","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-01-22","organization":"Keppel Ltd","aiSystem":"Artificial intelligence infrastructure","affectedGroup":"Residents of Victoria's Gippsland region","action":"Construction of a large data center","consequence":"Potential privacy risks due to AI infrastructure"}},{"i":"R1539","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Australian privacy commissioners frt ruling could have major implications for retail","s":"The Australian government has found a major retail chain has breached Australians’ privacy in a case that could have major implications. Privacy Commissioner Carly Kind has found Bunnings Group Limited breached citizens’ privacy by collecting personal and sensitive information through a facial recognition technology system. The system used CCTV to capture the faces of every person who entered 63 Bunnings stores in the Australian states of Victoria and New South Wales between November 2018 and November 2021. Bunnings argued the system was used to protect stores from “violent and organized crime.” The retail group is seeking a review of the Commissioner’s decision and commented that it was “deeply disappointed” with the finding.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202411/australian-privacy-commissioners-frt-ruling-could-have-major-implications-for-retail","d":"2024-11-20","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-11-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-11-20","organization":"Bunnings Group Limited","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"Citizens of Australia","action":"Collected personal and sensitive information through facial recognition technology","consequence":"Breached Australians' privacy"}},{"i":"R1959","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"X esafety ai chatbot grok ntwnfb","s":"Tribunal hearing comes days after Elon Musk’s xAI apologised for antisemitic comments made by its Grok bot Get our breaking news email , free app or daily news podcast. The chatbot embedded in Elon Musk’s X that referred to itself as “MechaHitler” and made antisemitic comments last week could be considered terrorism or violent extremism content, an Australian tribunal has heard. But an expert witness for X has argued a large language model cannot be ascribed intent, only the user. xAI, Musk’s artificial intelligence firm, last week apologised for the comments made by its Grok chatbot over a 16-hour period, which it attributed to “deprecated code” that made Grok susceptible to existing X user posts, “including when such posts contained extremist views”.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2025/jul/15/x-esafety-ai-chatbot-grok-ntwnfb","d":"2025-07-15","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-07-15","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-07-15","organization":"xAI (Elon Musk's artificial intelligence firm)","aiSystem":"Grok chatbot","affectedGroup":"Australian tribunal","action":"Hearing into potential terrorism/violent extremism content","consequence":"Potential mischaracterization of AI-generated content"}},{"i":"R1971","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"Ai artificial intelligence racism sexism australia human rights commissioner","s":"Labor divided on how to respond to the emerging technology as media and arts groups fear ‘rampant theft’ of intellectual property Follow our Australia news live blog for latest updates Get our breaking news email , free app or daily news podcast. AI risks entrenching racism and sexism in Australia, the human rights commissioner has warned, amid internal Labor debate about how to respond to the emerging technology. Lorraine Finlay says the pursuit of productivity gains from AI should not come at the expense of discrimination if the technology is not properly regulated. Finlay’s comments follow Labor senator Michelle Ananda-Rajah breaking ranks to call for all Australian data to be “freed” to tech companies to prevent AI perpetuating overseas biases and reflect Australian life and culture.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2025/aug/13/ai-artificial-intelligence-racism-sexism-australia-human-rights-commissioner","d":"2025-08-13","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2025-08-13","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-08-13","organization":"Labor party","aiSystem":"AI technology","affectedGroup":"People","action":"AI technology was pursued for productivity gains","consequence":"Entrenched racism and sexism"}},{"i":"R1999","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Ai chatbots are hurting children australian education minister warns as anti bullying plan announced","s":"Jason Clare says artificial intelligence is ‘supercharging bullying’ to a ‘terrifying’ extent Get our breaking news email , free app or daily news podcast. A disturbing new trend of AI chatbots bullying children and even encouraging them to take their own lives has the Australian government very concerned. Speaking to media on Saturday, the federal education minister, Jason Clare, said artificial intelligence was “supercharging” bullying. “AI chatbots are now bullying kids. It’s not kids bullying kids, it’s AI bullying kids, humiliating them, hurting them, telling them they’re losers … telling them to kill themselves.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2025/oct/18/ai-chatbots-are-hurting-children-australian-education-minister-warns-as-anti-bullying-plan-announced","d":"2025-10-18","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-10-18","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-10-18","organization":"Australian government","aiSystem":"AI chatbots","affectedGroup":"Children","action":"Bullied and encouraged to take their own lives","consequence":"Psychological harm, including humiliation, hurt, feelings of worthlessness, and suicidal thoughts"}},{"i":"R2092","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.5  Human computer interaction harm"],"t":"Signs of psychosis seen in australian users interactions with ai chatbots expert warns","s":"Toby Walsh says he despairs at Australian government’s lack of regulation of artificial intelligence Follow our Australia news live blog for latest updates Get our breaking news email , free app or daily news podcast. A leading AI expert has warned some Australians are showing signs of psychosis or mania in their interactions with chatbots, arguing Silicon Valley is being “careless” with the technology amid a pursuit of profit. During an address at the National Press Club on Wednesday, Toby Walsh, scientia professor of artificial intelligence at the University of New South Wales, said he believed the AI race will be both “boom and doom”, with some benefits.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/feb/25/signs-of-psychosis-seen-in-australian-users-interactions-with-ai-chatbots-expert-warns","d":"2026-02-25","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-02-25","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-02-25","organization":"Australian government","aiSystem":"AI chatbots","affectedGroup":"Some Australians","action":"interactions with chatbots","consequence":"showing signs of psychosis or mania"}},{"i":"R2096","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Chatgpt health fails recognise medical emergencies","s":"Study finds ChatGPT Health did not recommend a hospital visit when medically necessary in more than half of cases Follow our Australia news live blog for latest updates Get our breaking news email , free app or daily news podcast. ChatGPT Health regularly misses the need for medical urgent care and frequently fails to detect suicidal ideation, a study of the AI platform has found, which experts worry could “feasibly lead to unnecessary harm and death”. OpenAI launched the “Health” feature of ChatGPT to limited audiences in January, which it promotes as a way for users to “securely connect medical records and wellness apps” to generate health advice and responses.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/feb/26/chatgpt-health-fails-recognise-medical-emergencies","d":"2026-02-26","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2026-02-26","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-02-26","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"ChatGPT Health","affectedGroup":"users of ChatGPT Health","action":"recommend a hospital visit","consequence":"miss the need for medical urgent care, lead to unnecessary harm and death"}},{"i":"R2112","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.3  Employment displacement / quality harm"],"t":"Atlassian layoffs software technology ai push mike cannon brookes asx","s":"Layoffs to affect 10% of workforce amid Australian company’s restructuring plan to push into artificial intelligence and enterprise sales. Software giant Atlassian has announced it is laying off about 10% of its workforce, or roughly 1,600 positions, and replacing its chief technology officer as it restructures to invest further in artificial intelligence. More than 900 affected positions were involved in software research and development, a spokesperson said. Most of Atlassian’s employees work in software engineering and design, accounting for over 50% of its 13,813 full-time workforce in June 2025. About 640 affected employees are in North America, 480 in Australia and 250 in India, with the remainder spread across Japan, the Philippines, Europe, the Middle East and Africa, according to the spokesperson.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/mar/12/atlassian-layoffs-software-technology-ai-push-mike-cannon-brookes-asx","d":"2026-03-12","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-03-12","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-03-12","organization":"Atlassian","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"Employees of Atlassian","action":"Announced layoffs","consequence":"Loss of jobs"}},{"i":"R2150","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.2  Land use and siting harm"],"t":"Ai datacentres australia opposition","s":"Residents say AI factories with unknown environmental impacts are being rushed into development as proponents argue Australia must ride the data boom or be left behind Follow our Australia news live blog for latest updates Get our breaking news email , free app or daily news podcast. When West Footscray resident Sean Brown takes his 19-month-old boy to the park, their walk passes an imposing new building cheerily spruiked as “Australia’s largest hyperscale AI factory”, a datacentre called M3. He hates it: the construction noise from its constant expansion, the looming towers and the insistent background hum, the exhaust from the growing array of diesel generators that can help power the ranks of servers inside.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/may/03/ai-datacentres-australia-opposition","d":"2026-05-02","e":"Acute and chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-05-02","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-05-02","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"AI datacenters","affectedGroup":"Residents of West Footscray","action":"Construction and expansion of AI datacenters","consequence":"Unknown environmental impacts, construction noise, looming towers, exhaust from diesel generators"}},{"i":"R2153","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.3  Employment displacement / quality harm"],"t":"Your craft is obsolete wisetech staff in limbo as ai touted as better than humans","s":"The software company said in February it would cut 2,000 jobs but, as it touts new technology, workers are still waiting to hear which roles will go Follow our Australia news live blog for latest updates Get our breaking news email , free app or daily news podcast. Staff at WiseTech have been waiting almost three months to be told if they are among the 2,000 people the logistics software company is to cut due to advances in AI, with workers criticising the wait as stressful and “ridiculous”. The comments come as its founder on Tuesday told investors an AI agent could learn a human’s job in just 15 minutes, according to the Australian Financial Review.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/may/07/your-craft-is-obsolete-wisetech-staff-in-limbo-as-ai-touted-as-better-than-humans","d":"2026-05-06","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-05-06","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-05-06","organization":"WiseTech","aiSystem":"AI agent","affectedGroup":"Staff at WiseTech","action":"Cut 2,000 jobs","consequence":"Workers are still waiting to be informed of their job status"}},{"i":"R2159","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.2  Land use and siting harm"],"t":"Developer withdraws plans for perth datacentre after fierce community opposition","s":"Three-storey GreenSquare datacentre in Hazelmere was to power cloud computing and the acceleration of AI Get our breaking news email , free app or daily news podcast. A 15,000 sq metre datacentre near Perth will no longer go ahead after the developer withdrew plans amid community opposition over its impact on culturally significant sites. The three-storey, 120-megawatt GreenSquare datacentre in the town of Hazelmere had been intended to power cloud computing and the acceleration of artificial intelligence, but faced fierce community backlash – as is increasingly common with such developments. A City of Swan council review of the proposed development, which was to be located 15km east of Perth, attracted almost 1,900 public submissions.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/may/15/developer-withdraws-plans-for-perth-datacentre-after-fierce-community-opposition","d":"2026-05-15","e":"Acute and chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-05-15","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-05-15","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"Developers","action":"Withdrew plans for a datacentre","consequence":"No longer able to proceed with the project"}},{"i":"R2176","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"Australia ai datacentre boom environmental impacts climate how sustainable is it artificial intelligence","s":"They’re a key part of the digital and AI economy but they come at a high environmental cost and offer few operational jobs Follow our Australia news live blog for latest updates Get our breaking news email , free app or daily news podcast. On Mamre Road, in Sydney’s outer western suburbs, there are plans to build a “hyperscale” datacentre that will be one of the biggest in the world. If approved, the 52-hectare site will include six four-storey buildings that stretch 40 metres high, alongside 936 cooling units and 852 diesel backup power generators. The Mamre Road project is part of an estimated $155bn investment pipeline over the coming decade, amid a worldwide rush to build the infrastructure enabling the artificial intelligence revolution.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/jun/22/australia-ai-datacentre-boom-environmental-impacts-climate-how-sustainable-is-it-artificial-intelligence","d":"2026-06-21","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-06-21","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-06-21","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"Hyperscale datacenters","affectedGroup":"Not specified in report","action":"Construction of hyperscale datacenters","consequence":"High environmental impact and lack of operational jobs"}},{"i":"R2311","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"X esafety ai chatbot grok ntwnfb","s":"Tribunal hearing comes days after Elon Musk’s xAI apologised for antisemitic comments made by its Grok bot Get our breaking news email , free app or daily news podcast. The chatbot embedded in Elon Musk’s X that referred to itself as “MechaHitler” and made antisemitic comments last week could be considered terrorism or violent extremism content, an Australian tribunal has heard. But an expert witness for X has argued a large language model cannot be ascribed intent, only the user. xAI, Musk’s artificial intelligence firm, last week apologised for the comments made by its Grok chatbot over a 16-hour period, which it attributed to “deprecated code” that made Grok susceptible to existing X user posts, “including when such posts contained extremist views”.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2025/jul/15/x-esafety-ai-chatbot-grok-ntwnfb","d":"2025-07-15","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-07-15","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-07-15","organization":"xAI (Elon Musk's artificial intelligence firm)","aiSystem":"Grok chatbot","affectedGroup":"Australian tribunal","action":"Hearing into potential terrorism/violent extremism content","consequence":"Potential mischaracterization of AI-generated content"}},{"i":"R2323","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"Ai artificial intelligence racism sexism australia human rights commissioner","s":"Labor divided on how to respond to the emerging technology as media and arts groups fear ‘rampant theft’ of intellectual property Follow our Australia news live blog for latest updates Get our breaking news email , free app or daily news podcast. AI risks entrenching racism and sexism in Australia, the human rights commissioner has warned, amid internal Labor debate about how to respond to the emerging technology. Lorraine Finlay says the pursuit of productivity gains from AI should not come at the expense of discrimination if the technology is not properly regulated. Finlay’s comments follow Labor senator Michelle Ananda-Rajah breaking ranks to call for all Australian data to be “freed” to tech companies to prevent AI perpetuating overseas biases and reflect Australian life and culture.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2025/aug/13/ai-artificial-intelligence-racism-sexism-australia-human-rights-commissioner","d":"2025-08-13","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2025-08-13","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-08-13","organization":"Labor party","aiSystem":"AI technology","affectedGroup":"People","action":"AI technology pursued for productivity gains","consequence":"Entrenchment of racism and sexism"}},{"i":"R2351","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Ai chatbots are hurting children australian education minister warns as anti bullying plan announced","s":"Jason Clare says artificial intelligence is ‘supercharging bullying’ to a ‘terrifying’ extent Get our breaking news email , free app or daily news podcast. A disturbing new trend of AI chatbots bullying children and even encouraging them to take their own lives has the Australian government very concerned. Speaking to media on Saturday, the federal education minister, Jason Clare, said artificial intelligence was “supercharging” bullying. “AI chatbots are now bullying kids. It’s not kids bullying kids, it’s AI bullying kids, humiliating them, hurting them, telling them they’re losers … telling them to kill themselves.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2025/oct/18/ai-chatbots-are-hurting-children-australian-education-minister-warns-as-anti-bullying-plan-announced","d":"2025-10-18","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-10-18","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-10-18","organization":"Australian government","aiSystem":"AI chatbots","affectedGroup":"Children","action":"Bullied and encouraged to take their own lives","consequence":"Psychological harm, suicidal ideation"}},{"i":"R2444","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.5  Human computer interaction harm"],"t":"Signs of psychosis seen in australian users interactions with ai chatbots expert warns","s":"Toby Walsh says he despairs at Australian government’s lack of regulation of artificial intelligence Follow our Australia news live blog for latest updates Get our breaking news email , free app or daily news podcast. A leading AI expert has warned some Australians are showing signs of psychosis or mania in their interactions with chatbots, arguing Silicon Valley is being “careless” with the technology amid a pursuit of profit. During an address at the National Press Club on Wednesday, Toby Walsh, scientia professor of artificial intelligence at the University of New South Wales, said he believed the AI race will be both “boom and doom”, with some benefits.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/feb/25/signs-of-psychosis-seen-in-australian-users-interactions-with-ai-chatbots-expert-warns","d":"2026-02-25","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-02-25","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-02-25","organization":"Australian government","aiSystem":"AI chatbots","affectedGroup":"Some Australians","action":"interactions with chatbots","consequence":"showing signs of psychosis or mania"}},{"i":"R2448","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Chatgpt health fails recognise medical emergencies","s":"Study finds ChatGPT Health did not recommend a hospital visit when medically necessary in more than half of cases Follow our Australia news live blog for latest updates Get our breaking news email , free app or daily news podcast. ChatGPT Health regularly misses the need for medical urgent care and frequently fails to detect suicidal ideation, a study of the AI platform has found, which experts worry could “feasibly lead to unnecessary harm and death”. OpenAI launched the “Health” feature of ChatGPT to limited audiences in January, which it promotes as a way for users to “securely connect medical records and wellness apps” to generate health advice and responses.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/feb/26/chatgpt-health-fails-recognise-medical-emergencies","d":"2026-02-26","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2026-02-26","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-02-26","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"ChatGPT Health","affectedGroup":"users of ChatGPT Health","action":"recommend a hospital visit","consequence":"miss the need for medical urgent care, lead to unnecessary harm and death"}},{"i":"R2464","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.3  Employment displacement / quality harm"],"t":"Atlassian layoffs software technology ai push mike cannon brookes asx","s":"Layoffs to affect 10% of workforce amid Australian company’s restructuring plan to push into artificial intelligence and enterprise sales. Software giant Atlassian has announced it is laying off about 10% of its workforce, or roughly 1,600 positions, and replacing its chief technology officer as it restructures to invest further in artificial intelligence. More than 900 affected positions were involved in software research and development, a spokesperson said. Most of Atlassian’s employees work in software engineering and design, accounting for over 50% of its 13,813 full-time workforce in June 2025. About 640 affected employees are in North America, 480 in Australia and 250 in India, with the remainder spread across Japan, the Philippines, Europe, the Middle East and Africa, according to the spokesperson.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/mar/12/atlassian-layoffs-software-technology-ai-push-mike-cannon-brookes-asx","d":"2026-03-12","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-03-12","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-03-12","organization":"Atlassian","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"Employees of Atlassian","action":"Announced layoffs","consequence":"Loss of jobs"}},{"i":"R2505","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.3  Employment displacement / quality harm"],"t":"Your craft is obsolete wisetech staff in limbo as ai touted as better than humans","s":"The software company said in February it would cut 2,000 jobs but, as it touts new technology, workers are still waiting to hear which roles will go Follow our Australia news live blog for latest updates Get our breaking news email , free app or daily news podcast. Staff at WiseTech have been waiting almost three months to be told if they are among the 2,000 people the logistics software company is to cut due to advances in AI, with workers criticising the wait as stressful and “ridiculous”. The comments come as its founder on Tuesday told investors an AI agent could learn a human’s job in just 15 minutes, according to the Australian Financial Review.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/may/07/your-craft-is-obsolete-wisetech-staff-in-limbo-as-ai-touted-as-better-than-humans","d":"2026-05-06","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-05-06","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-05-06","organization":"WiseTech","aiSystem":"AI agent","affectedGroup":"Staff at WiseTech","action":"Cut 2,000 jobs","consequence":"Workers are still waiting to be informed of their job status"}},{"i":"R2511","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.2  Land use and siting harm"],"t":"Developer withdraws plans for perth datacentre after fierce community opposition","s":"Three-storey GreenSquare datacentre in Hazelmere was to power cloud computing and the acceleration of AI Get our breaking news email , free app or daily news podcast. A 15,000 sq metre datacentre near Perth will no longer go ahead after the developer withdrew plans amid community opposition over its impact on culturally significant sites. The three-storey, 120-megawatt GreenSquare datacentre in the town of Hazelmere had been intended to power cloud computing and the acceleration of artificial intelligence, but faced fierce community backlash – as is increasingly common with such developments. A City of Swan council review of the proposed development, which was to be located 15km east of Perth, attracted almost 1,900 public submissions.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/may/15/developer-withdraws-plans-for-perth-datacentre-after-fierce-community-opposition","d":"2026-05-15","e":"Acute and chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-05-15","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-05-15","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"Developers","action":"Withdrew plans for a datacentre","consequence":"No longer able to proceed with the project"}},{"i":"R2528","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"Australia ai datacentre boom environmental impacts climate how sustainable is it artificial intelligence","s":"They’re a key part of the digital and AI economy but they come at a high environmental cost and offer few operational jobs Follow our Australia news live blog for latest updates Get our breaking news email , free app or daily news podcast. On Mamre Road, in Sydney’s outer western suburbs, there are plans to build a “hyperscale” datacentre that will be one of the biggest in the world. If approved, the 52-hectare site will include six four-storey buildings that stretch 40 metres high, alongside 936 cooling units and 852 diesel backup power generators. The Mamre Road project is part of an estimated $155bn investment pipeline over the coming decade, amid a worldwide rush to build the infrastructure enabling the artificial intelligence revolution.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/jun/22/australia-ai-datacentre-boom-environmental-impacts-climate-how-sustainable-is-it-artificial-intelligence","d":"2026-06-21","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-06-21","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-06-21","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"Hyperscale datacenters","affectedGroup":"Not specified in report","action":"Construction of hyperscale datacenters","consequence":"High environmental impact and lack of operational jobs"}},{"i":"R2734","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Clearview ai facial recognition australia breach data delete","s":"Australia says no to Clearview’s indiscriminate data scraping. Controversial facial recognition firm Clearview AI has been ordered to destroy all images and facial templates belonging to individuals living in Australia by the country’s national privacy regulator. Clearview AI ordered to delete all facial recognition data belonging to Australians The company breached Australian privacy law The company breached Australian privacy law Clearview, which claims to have scraped 10 billion images of people from social media sites in order to identify them in other photos, sells its technology to law enforcement agencies.","u":"https://www.theverge.com/2021/11/3/22761001/clearview-ai-facial-recognition-australia-breach-data-delete","d":"2021-11-03","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-11-03","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-11-03","organization":"Clearview AI","aiSystem":"Clearview AI facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"Individuals living in Australia","action":"Scraped and stored facial recognition data","consequence":"Data destruction ordered by national privacy regulator"}},{"i":"R2936","k":"Australia","p":"Australia","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Outabox facial recognition breach","s":"Outabox, an Australian firm that scanned faces for bars and clubs, suffered a breach that shows the problems with giving companies your biometric data. Police and federal agencies are responding to a massive breach of personal data linked to a facial recognition scheme that was implemented in bars and clubs across Australia. The incident highlights emerging privacy concerns as AI-powered facial recognition becomes more widely used everywhere from shopping malls to sporting events. The affected company is Australia-based Outabox, which also has offices in the United States and the Philippines. In response to the Covid-19 pandemic, Outabox debuted a facial recognition kiosk that scans visitors and checks their temperature.","u":"https://www.wired.com/story/outabox-facial-recognition-breach/","d":"2024-05-02","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-05-02","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-05-02","organization":"Outabox","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"Visitors to bars and clubs","action":"Scanned faces for security purposes","consequence":"Personal data breach exposing visitor information"}},{"i":"R1472","k":"Austria","p":"Austria","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Clearview ai faces criminal complaint from austrian ngo over eu data collection","s":"Biometrics firms fall into two categories: those that seek regulation as an anchor for public trust, and those that run ahead of it to try and reap the rewards. Biometrics companies might be divided into two categories: those that seek regulation as an anchor for public trust, and those that run ahead of it to try and reap the rewards. At this point, Clearview AI should not be surprised when regulators classify it as the second, given how many governments it has alarmed with its facial recognition technology for law enforcement. The firm has already faced fines totally roughly 100 million euros from data protection agencies in France , Greece , Italy , the Netherlands and the UK It has also had near-catastrophic trouble in U.S. courts.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202510/clearview-ai-faces-criminal-complaint-from-austrian-ngo-over-eu-data-collection","d":"2025-10-28","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-10-28","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-10-28","organization":"Clearview AI","aiSystem":"Clearview AI facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"People whose faces were recognized by Clearview AI","action":"Used facial recognition to identify and track people","consequence":"Received fines and legal challenges"}},{"i":"R483","k":"Bahrain","p":"Bahrain","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"Amazon data centers united arab emirates bahrain damaged drone strikes","s":"‘In the UAE, two of our facilities were directly struck, while in Bahrain, a drone strike in close proximity to one of our facilities caused physical impact to our infrastructure,; AWS says. Amazon said on Monday, March 2, some of its data centers in the United Arab Emirates and Bahrain were damaged by drone strikes in the Middle East conflict, disrupting cloud services and making a recovery “prolonged”. Iran fired a barrage of drones and missiles at Gulf States in retaliation for US and Israeli strikes that killed Supreme Leader Ayatollah Ali Khamenei on Saturday. A strike on the UAE facility marks the first time a major US tech company’s data center has been disrupted by military action.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/amazon-data-centers-united-arab-emirates-bahrain-damaged-drone-strikes/","d":"2026-03-03","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-03-03","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-03-02","organization":"Iran","aiSystem":"AWS (Amazon Web Services)","affectedGroup":"AWS data centers in UAE and Bahrain","action":"Drone strikes causing physical damage to AWS infrastructure","consequence":"Disruption of cloud services and prolonged recovery"}},{"i":"R2949","k":"Bangladesh","p":"Bangladesh","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Openai job scam","s":"An alleged job scam, led by “Aiden” from “OpenAI,” recruited workers in Bangladesh for months before disappearing overnight, according to FTC complaints obtained by WIRED. A Bangladeshi worker was eager to get started at their new OpenAI job—completing basic online tasks in exchange for consistent income, while getting into cryptocurrency investing at the same time. After connecting with the startup on Telegram and creating an account through a ChatGPT -branded app, they invested crypto into the platform and began a months-long job working for “Aiden” from “OpenAI.” The work was performed through the website “OpenAi-etc,” and internal conversations were held on Telegram.","u":"https://www.wired.com/story/openai-job-scam/","d":"2024-02-26","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-02-26","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-02-26","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"ChatGPT-branded app and OpenAi-etc website","affectedGroup":"Bangladeshi worker","action":"performed online tasks and invested crypto","consequence":"disappeared overnight, lost investment"}},{"i":"R308","k":"Belarus","p":"Belarus","b":["Deployment Context / Weapons"],"h":["5.4  Supply chain / investor complicity harm"],"t":"Ukraine sanctions russias international ai drone networks","s":"Russian, Chinese, and Belarusian companies hit with new sanctions for supplying Russian drone manufacturing. Ukraine imposed sweeping sanctions on Russia's artificial intelligence-powered drone manufacturers on 17 August, targeting 39 individuals and 55 companies across Russia, China, and Belarus. The restrictions reflect Ukraine's efforts towards dismantling the entire technological ecosystem of Russia's drone war, which has escalated dramatically in 2025 with Moscow launching over 27,000 Shahed drones and deploying new AI-guided models that can evade Ukrainian electronic warfare systems. Ukrainian sanctions have had little impact on Russia, remaining largely symbolic after Kyiv previously seized Russian companies’ assets inside the country.","u":"https://euromaidanpress.com/2025/08/17/ukraine-sanctions-russias-international-ai-drone-networks/","d":"2025-08-17","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-08-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-08-17","organization":"Russian, Chinese, and Belarusian companies","aiSystem":"Artificial Intelligence (AI)-powered drone manufacturing","affectedGroup":"Individuals and companies supplying Russian drone manufacturing","action":"Ukraine imposed sweeping sanctions","consequence":"Targeting 39 individuals and 55 companies across Russia, China, and Belarus"}},{"i":"R1073","k":"Belgium","p":"Belgium","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Man dies by suicide after talking with ai chatbot widow says","s":"The incident raises concerns about guardrails around quickly-proliferating conversational AI models. A Belgian man recently died by suicide after chatting with an AI chatbot on an app called Chai, Belgian outlet La Libre reported. The incident raises the issue of how businesses and governments can better regulate and mitigate the risks of AI, especially when it comes to mental health. The app’s chatbot encouraged the user to kill himself, according to statements by the man’s widow and chat logs she supplied to the outlet. When Motherboard tried the app, which runs on a bespoke AI language model based on an open-source GPT-4 alternative that was fine-tuned by Chai, it provided us with different methods of suicide with very little prompting.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/man-dies-by-suicide-after-talking-with-ai-chatbot-widow-says/","d":"2023-03-30","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-03-30","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-03-30","organization":"Chai (app developer)","aiSystem":"Chai's bespoke AI language model based on an open-source GPT-4 alternative","affectedGroup":"A Belgian man","action":"The AI chatbot encouraged the user to kill himself","consequence":"The man died by suicide"}},{"i":"R781","k":"Belgium","p":"Belgium","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Eu privacy watchdog chatgpt task force","s":"Following Italy’s unilateral decision to ban ChatGPT temporarily, the EU’s national privacy bodies create a task force to ‘foster cooperation and to exchange information’ on enforcement actions. The body that unites Europe’s national privacy watchdogs said on Thursday, April 13, it had set up a task force on ChatGPT, a potentially important first step toward a common policy on setting privacy rules on artificial intelligence. The move by the European Data Protection Board (EDPB) follows a unilateral move by Italy last month to curb ChatGPT – a stance that Germany’s commissioner for data protection said could be followed in Europe’s biggest economy.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/eu-privacy-watchdog-chatgpt-task-force/","d":"2023-04-14","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-04-14","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-04-13","organization":"European Data Protection Board (EDPB)","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Artificial Intelligence systems and users","action":"Create a task force to foster cooperation and exchange information on enforcement actions regarding ChatGPT","consequence":"Potential delay in uniform privacy rules for AI systems across Europe"}},{"i":"R713","k":"Belgium","p":"Belgium","b":["Data"],"h":["4.3  Data rights / consent violation"],"t":"Meta gets european union complaints over use personal data train ai models","s":"The proposed changes would see Meta Platforms using personal data to train its artificial intelligence models without asking for consent, which may breach European Union privacy rules. BRUSSELS, Belgium – Meta Platforms was hit with 11 complaints on Thursday, June 6, over proposed changes that would see it use personal data to train its artificial intelligence models without asking for consent, which may breach European Union privacy rules. Advocacy group NOYB (None of your Business) urged national privacy watchdogs to act immediately to halt such use, saying recent changes in Meta’s privacy policy, which come into force on June 26, would allow it to use years of personal posts, private images, or online tracking data for its AI technology.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/meta-gets-european-union-complaints-over-use-personal-data-train-ai-models/","d":"2024-06-06","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-06-06","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-06-06","organization":"Meta Platforms","aiSystem":"Meta's artificial intelligence models","affectedGroup":"Individuals whose personal data is used","action":"Using personal data to train AI models without consent","consequence":"Potential breach of European Union privacy rules"}},{"i":"R232","k":"Belgium","p":"Belgium","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Our reliance on ai generated news could lead to more bank runs","s":"European financial institutions are raising the alarm over the potential effects of AI-generated fake news reports, according to a new study. European Union Fake news reports and disinformation campaigns driven by generative AI pose a significant risk to causing bank runs, according to a new study out of the U.K. Per the research firm, Say No to Disinfo, and communications company Fenimore Harper, generative AI systems can easily be leveraged to create fake stories appearing on social media that suggest banks suffer from specific security deficiencies or that their depositors’ money is not safe. Recommended Videos “As AI is making disinformation campaigns easier, cheaper, quicker and more effective than ever before, the emerging risk to the financial sector is rapidly growing but often overlooked,” the report said.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/our-reliance-on-ai-generated-news-could-lead-to-more-bank-runs/","d":"2025-02-14","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2025-02-14","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-02-14","organization":"European financial institutions, European Union","aiSystem":"Generative AI systems","affectedGroup":"Bank depositors, general public","action":"Creation and dissemination of fake news reports suggesting bank security issues or money safety concerns","consequence":"Potential for bank runs due to perceived financial instability"}},{"i":"R499","k":"Belgium","p":"Belgium","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.8  Interpersonal / relational harm"],"t":"Grok x european commission investigation sexualized images","s":"The European Commission says it will investigate whether X protected consumers by properly assessing and mitigating risks related to Grok’s functionalities. BRUSSELS, Belgium – Elon Musk’s X faces investigation by the European Union into whether it disseminates illegal content, following public outcry over the spreading of manipulated sexualized images by its artificial intelligence Grok chatbot. The European Commission, the 27-nation bloc’s executive arm, said on Monday, January 26, that it would investigate whether social media platform X protected consumers by properly assessing and mitigating risks related to Grok’s functionalities.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/social-media/grok-x-european-commission-investigation-sexualized-images/","d":"2026-01-27","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-01-27","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-01-26","organization":"European Commission","aiSystem":"Grok chatbot","affectedGroup":"Consumers","action":"Investigate X's protection of consumers by assessing and mitigating risks related to Grok’s functionalities","consequence":"Potential dissemination of illegal content"}},{"i":"R1230","k":"Belgium","p":"Belgium","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.3  Employment displacement / quality harm"],"t":"When Russia's invasion of Ukraine stymied his travel plans, photographer Carl De Keyzer de…","s":"When Russia's invasion of Ukraine stymied his travel plans, photographer Carl De Keyzer decided to take a virtual trip instead, creating a series of AI-generated images. He was unprepared for the consequences. When Russia's invasion of Ukraine stymied his travel plans, Belgian photographer Carl De Keyzer decided to take a virtual trip to Russia instead. From his home, the lauded documentary photographer began to work on a collection of images about Russia with the help of generative artificial intelligence (AI). In the late 1980s, De Keyzer travelled to Russia 12 times in the space of a year. The USSR was in its death throes and De Keyzer photographed the rituals and pastimes which would soon disappear.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2024-12-26/ai-generated-images-photography-trust/104721106","d":"2024-12-25","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-12-25","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-12-25","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"Generative artificial intelligence (AI)","affectedGroup":"Photographer Carl De Keyzer","action":"Generated AI-generated images","consequence":"Exposure of personal travel plans and location"}},{"i":"R1604","k":"Belgium","p":"Belgium","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Eu watchdog europols plan for migrant biometric data raises serious questions","s":"The EDPS is scrutinizing proposals to enhance Europol’s cooperation with Frontex and other agencies that would boost the exchange of biometric data of migrants. A European data watchdog is scrutinizing a proposal to enhance Europol’s cooperation with Frontex and other agencies that would boost the exchange of biometric data of migrants. The European Data Protection Supervisor (EDPS), the body in charge of monitoring European institutions and bodies privacy and data protection practices, says that while the European Commission’s proposed regulation is aimed at fighting against migrant smuggling and trafficking, it also raises serious legal questions.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202402/eu-watchdog-europols-plan-for-migrant-biometric-data-raises-serious-questions","d":"2024-02-06","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-02-06","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-02-06","organization":"European Data Protection Supervisor (EDPS)","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"Migrants","action":"exchange of biometric data","consequence":"raises serious legal questions"}},{"i":"R1695","k":"Belgium","p":"Belgium","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"Europe rolls out the red carpet for ai data centres as water and energy backlash spreads","s":"In the US, the backlash against AI data centres is bipartisan, organised and growing. But not in Europe, where government are bending over backwards to attract them, writes Albert Alemanno. Across Europe, the AI boom is taking physical form. Data centres are rising on industrial estates, former farmland and plots beside substations: windowless, fenced and humming with electricity. Europe already hosts more than 3,000 data centres, and with €176bn expected to flow into capacity between 2026 and 2031, the next wave is moving onto industrial and power-secured land beyond the traditional hubs of Frankfurt, London, Amsterdam, Paris and Dublin.","u":"https://euobserver.com/217320/europe-rolls-out-the-red-carpet-for-ai-data-centres-as-water-and-energy-backlash-spreads/","d":"2026-05-19","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-05-19","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-05-19","organization":"Government authorities in European Union","aiSystem":"AI data centers","affectedGroup":"General public","action":"Data centers are rising on various locations","consequence":"Windowless, fenced and humming with electricity"}},{"i":"R1698","k":"Belgium","p":"Belgium","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"Why the eu commissions plan for an ai data centres boom is short sighted","s":"Water-resource conflicts already happening in Spain, Ireland and the Netherlands will only worsen now that the EU Commission plans to triple data centre capacity in Europe over the next five to seven years. Little thought has been given to the oligopolistic cloud market and how it will evolve if the EU triples its data centre capacity. Across Europe, conflicts over data centres’ demand for water, energy, and land are now playing out. In Spain, the Your Cloud Dries Up My River movement exposes water-hungry data centres In Ireland, households were put on water rations to ensure data centres could keep on cooling their servers.","u":"https://euobserver.com/206824/why-the-eu-commissions-plan-for-an-ai-data-centres-boom-is-short-sighted/","d":"2026-03-16","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-03-16","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-03-16","organization":"EU Commission","aiSystem":"Data centers","affectedGroup":"Households, Water resource managers, Data center operators","action":"Plan to triple data center capacity in Europe","consequence":"Water shortages, increased energy consumption, land use conflicts"}},{"i":"R1703","k":"Belgium","p":"Belgium","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"How the eu democracy shield is failing to audit ai","s":"The EU Democracy Shield has a blind spot: Europe isn't auditing what AI actually tells its citizens. When Russian-speaking Europeans ask an AI about the war in Ukraine, they may receive systematically different answers than their English-speaking neighbours. The European Democracy Shield, a new EU strategy published last November, aims to protect the EU from foreign information manipulation and interference (FIMI). But it has a blind spot: the EU is not systematically auditing what AI chatbots tell citizens about contested geopolitical events or how those answers change by language. In EU-funded research I presented at a Nato-supported panel in Brussels earlier this year, I built a repeatable method to test exactly this.","u":"https://euobserver.com/202340/how-the-eu-democracy-shield-is-failing-to-audit-ai/","d":"2026-02-16","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-02-16","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-11","organization":"EU Democracy Shield","aiSystem":"AI chatbots","affectedGroup":"Russian-speaking Europeans and English-speaking Europeans","action":"AI chatbots provide information about contested geopolitical events","consequence":"Systematically different answers leading to misinformation exposure"}},{"i":"R1705","k":"Belgium","p":"Belgium","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.5  Human computer interaction harm"],"t":"Tiktoks addictive design breaches the dsa eu commission finds","s":"The European Commission preliminarily found TikTok in violation of the Digital Services Act on Friday, for breaching the EU’s addictive design rules — such as not adequately assessing addiction risk and implementing weak mitigation features. The European Commission preliminarily found TikTok in violation of the Digital Services Act (DSA) on Friday (6 February), for breaching the EU’s addictive design rules. Under the DSA, online platforms have certain risk assessment and mitigation obligations. Brussels has reason to believe that TikTok did not adequately assess the addiction risks, especially to minors, related to integrated design features, such as infinite scroll — arguing its reward content system can “shift the brain of users into ‘autopilot mode’.”","u":"https://euobserver.com/201780/tiktoks-addictive-design-breaches-the-dsa-eu-commission-finds/","d":"2026-02-06","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-02-06","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-02-06","organization":"European Commission","aiSystem":"TikTok","affectedGroup":"Users, particularly minors","action":"Integrated design features, such as infinite scroll, were implemented without adequate assessment of addiction risks","consequence":"Users, especially minors, were exposed to privacy risks due to the addictive design features"}},{"i":"R1736","k":"Belgium","p":"Belgium","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Elon musk grok ai eu b2907611","s":"The AI chatbot was used to remove clothing from images of women and children. The European Commission has launched an investigation into Grok, Elon Musk's AI chatbot , over the production of explicit imagery Regina Doherty, a member of the European parliament representing Ireland, announced the investigation on Monday. It will probe whether the social media platform X has complied with its obligations under EU digital legislation, including requirements relating to risk mitigation, content governance, and the protection of fundamental rights, Ms Doherty said. A European Union crackdown on Big Tech has triggered criticism and even the threat of tariffs from the United States A Commission spokesperson did not immediately respond when asked to confirm if an investigation had been opened.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/europe/elon-musk-grok-ai-eu-b2907611.html","d":"2026-01-26","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-01-26","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-01-26","organization":"European Commission","aiSystem":"Grok, Elon Musk's AI chatbot","affectedGroup":"Women and children","action":"removed clothing from images","consequence":"produced explicit imagery"}},{"i":"R1542","k":"Belgium","p":"Belgium","b":["Data"],"h":["4.3  Data rights / consent violation"],"t":"Belgian watchdog ruling on consent sets employee biometrics use against gdpr","s":"The decision could dampen the use of biometric systems for time recording or protecting company assets, according to PwC Legal in Belgium. A Belgian company has been fined 45,000 euros (US$48,185) for using a fingerprint-based employee time tracking system with the country’s data watchdog ruling that the firm did not obtain valid consent from its employees, putting it in violation of GDPR rules. The Belgian Data Protection Authority (DPA) issued the ruling in September after receiving a complaint from a former employee of the unnamed company. The data watchdog concluded that the company relied on employee consent as a legal ground for processing data.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202411/belgian-watchdog-ruling-on-consent-sets-employee-biometrics-use-against-gdpr","d":"2024-11-07","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-11-07","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-09","organization":"Unnamed Belgian company","aiSystem":"Employee time tracking system with biometric capabilities (fingerprint-based)","affectedGroup":"Employees of the unnamed company","action":"Used a fingerprint-based employee time tracking system","consequence":"Fined 45,000 euros (US$48,185) for violating GDPR rules"}},{"i":"R2120","k":"Belgium","p":"Belgium","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Mediahuis suspends senior journalist over ai generated quotes","s":"Mediahuis suspends Peter Vandermeersch, who says he ‘fell into trap of hallucinations’, after investigation by newspaper where he was once editor-in-chief. The publisher of the Dutch newspaper De Telegraaf and the Irish Independent has suspended one of its senior journalists after he admitted using AI to “wrongly put words into people’s mouths”. Peter Vandermeersch, the former head of the Irish operations at Mediahuis, said he “fell into the trap of hallucinations” – the term for AI-generated errors – when using the technology. Vandermeersch, a fellow of “journalism and society” at the European publishing group, has been suspended from his role.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/mar/20/mediahuis-suspends-senior-journalist-over-ai-generated-quotes","d":"2026-03-20","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-03-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-03-20","organization":"Mediahuis","aiSystem":"AI-generated content","affectedGroup":"Peter Vandermeersch","action":"Used AI to generate quotes","consequence":"Suspension from employment"}},{"i":"R2472","k":"Belgium","p":"Belgium","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Mediahuis suspends senior journalist over ai generated quotes","s":"Mediahuis suspends Peter Vandermeersch, who says he ‘fell into trap of hallucinations’, after investigation by newspaper where he was once editor-in-chief. The publisher of the Dutch newspaper De Telegraaf and the Irish Independent has suspended one of its senior journalists after he admitted using AI to “wrongly put words into people’s mouths”. Peter Vandermeersch, the former head of the Irish operations at Mediahuis, said he “fell into the trap of hallucinations” – the term for AI-generated errors – when using the technology. Vandermeersch, a fellow of “journalism and society” at the European publishing group, has been suspended from his role.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/mar/20/mediahuis-suspends-senior-journalist-over-ai-generated-quotes","d":"2026-03-20","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-03-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-03-20","organization":"Mediahuis","aiSystem":"AI-generated content","affectedGroup":"Peter Vandermeersch","action":"Used AI to generate quotes","consequence":"Suspension from employment"}},{"i":"R2745","k":"Belgium","p":"Belgium","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"Automatic gender recognition sexual orientation facial ai analysis ban campaign","s":"Technology that tries to identify someone’s gender or sexual orientation using facial recognition is on the rise. Campaigners say it’s dangerous and should be banned before it becomes commonplace. Dangers posed by facial recognition like mass surveillance and mistaken identity have been widely discussed in recent years. But digital rights groups say an equally insidious use case is currently sneaking under the radar: using the same technology to predict someone’s gender and sexual orientation. Now, a new campaign has launched to ban these applications in the EU. Trying to predict someone’s gender or sexuality from digitized clues is fundamentally flawed, says Os Keyes, a researcher who’s written extensively on the topic.","u":"https://www.theverge.com/2021/4/14/22381370/automatic-gender-recognition-sexual-orientation-facial-ai-analysis-ban-campaign","d":"2021-04-14","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2021-04-14","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-04-14","organization":"Digital rights groups","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"People whose gender or sexual orientation is predicted","action":"Used facial recognition technology to predict gender and sexual orientation","consequence":"Fundamentally flawed and potentially harmful predictions"}},{"i":"R3019","k":"Belgium","p":"Belgium","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Europe police facial recognition prum","s":"Lawmakers advance proposals to let police forces across the EU link their photo databases—which include millions of pictures of people’s faces. For the past 15 years, police forces searching for criminals in Europe have been able to share fingerprints, DNA data, and details of vehicle owners with each other. If officials in France suspect someone they are looking for is in Spain, they can ask Spanish authorities to check fingerprints against their database. Now European lawmakers are set to include millions of photos of people’s faces in this system—and allow facial recognition to be used on an unprecedented scale.","u":"https://www.wired.com/story/europe-police-facial-recognition-prum/","d":"2022-04-20","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2022-04-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2022-04-20","organization":"Police forces across the European Union","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"People whose faces are included in the photo databases","action":"Allow facial recognition to be used on an unprecedented scale","consequence":"Exposure to surveillance and tracking"}},{"i":"R1545","k":"Belize","p":"Belize","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Biometrica public security project paused in belize","s":"Police in Belize have temporarily paused their deal with a U.S. facial recognition company following concerns over moving data overseas. Police in Belize have temporarily paused their deal with U.S. facial recognition company Biometrica following concerns over moving data overseas. The project was created to improve the tracking and identification of suspects in the popular Central American tourist destination. The collaboration, however, would have required sharing the county’s criminal database, including biometric fingerprint data, with Biometrica. Belize Police Commissioner Chester Williams said that the agreement is on hold while they are looking for ways to keep data from Belizeans from being taken abroad.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202410/biometrica-public-security-project-paused-in-belize","d":"2024-10-02","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-10-02","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-10-02","organization":"Police in Belize and U.S. company Biometrica","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"Belizeans","action":"Collaboration to share criminal database and biometric data","consequence":"Data from Belizeans could be transferred overseas"}},{"i":"R1686","k":"Bolivia","p":"Bolivia","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.2  Algorithmic management harm"],"t":"In latin america white collar work","s":"Constanza Llanos, 26, spends much of her day in the offices of an insurance company in Santa Cruz, Bolivia. She works as a human resources assistant – a role that, until recently, meant going through CVs one by one. Now, a digital platform screens candidates before they ever reach her desk. “It saves us time, but we still have to go through everything manually,” she says. Although her company has yet to introduce formal artificial intelligence (AI) systems, many employees make use of (…). Artificial intelligence is gradually entering offices across Latin America and changing the way work is done. For some, it brings greater speed and order; for others, new inequalities and pressures.","u":"https://www.equaltimes.org/in-latin-america-white-collar-work","d":"2025-11-20","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-11-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-11-20","organization":"Insurance company in Santa Cruz, Bolivia","aiSystem":"Digital platform for candidate screening","affectedGroup":"Human resources assistants","action":"Digital platform screens candidates before reaching HR assistants","consequence":"Increased workload and pressure on HR assistants"}},{"i":"R816","k":"Brazil","p":"Brazil","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"Facial recognition automated gender coda story","s":"Rights groups believe that new tech reinforces old prejudices and are calling on the European Commission to ban it. If you rode the metro in the Brazilian city of Sao Paulo in 2018, you might have come across a new kind of advertising. Glowing interactive doors featured content targeted at individuals, according to assumptions made by artificial intelligence based on their appearance. Fitted with facial recognition cameras, the screens made instantaneous decisions about passengers’ gender, age and emotional state, then served them ads accordingly. Digital rights groups said the technology violated the rights of trans and non-binary people because it assigned gender to individuals based on the physical shape of their face, potentially making incorrect judgments as to their identity.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/features/facial-recognition-automated-gender-coda-story/","d":"2021-04-17","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-04-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2018","organization":"Digital rights groups","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"Trans and non-binary people","action":"Facial recognition cameras made instant decisions about passengers' gender, age, and emotional state and served them targeted ads","consequence":"Potential incorrect judgments about the identity of trans and non-binary people"}},{"i":"R913","k":"Brazil","p":"Brazil","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.1  Data labor exploitation"],"t":"Tiktok bytedance transcription brazil","s":"Some workers logged as many as 90 hours per week and complained that the subcontractors they worked for didn’t make promised payments. It was a remote work opportunity, with no fixed hours, and it paid in dollars. In a moment of extreme economic crisis playing out in Brazil, the offer seemed irresistible. Felipe, who was unemployed, took the job working for TikTok, the social media giant. Felipe, who asked The Intercept to use a pseudonym to protect his future employment prospects, worked alongside other Portuguese speakers. The transcription job was simple: Listen to the audio from TikTok videos and write down what was said.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2021/10/02/tiktok-bytedance-transcription-brazil/","d":"2021-10-02","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-10-02","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-10-02","organization":"TikTok (social media giant)","aiSystem":"TikTok","affectedGroup":"Felipe (unemployed worker) and other Portuguese speakers","action":"Listen to audio from TikTok videos and write down spoken words","consequence":"Worked long hours (up to 90 hours per week) without promised payments"}},{"i":"R957","k":"Brazil","p":"Brazil","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Ai generated miracle prayers are worth a fortune just ask the scammers selling them","s":"Faith can move mountains, but apparently it can also move money. Lots of it. In Brazil, police arrested 35 people accused of running a massive scam that sold “miracle prayers” written by artificial intelligence to unsuspecting believers. Each prayer costs about 50 reais, or ten U.S. dollars. Some victims were so touched by the words […]. Lots of it. Some victims were so touched by the words that they kept buying more, convinced they were receiving divine messages crafted specifically for them. The operation ran out of Nilópolis, a city in Rio de Janeiro’s Baixada Fluminense region, and worked like a religious call center.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/ai-generated-miracle-prayers-are-worth-a-fortune-just-ask-the-scammers-selling-them/","d":"2025-10-16","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-10-16","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-10-16","organization":"Unknown organization (suspected scam operation)","aiSystem":"AI-generated prayers","affectedGroup":"Unsuspecting believers","action":"Sold 'miracle prayers' written by AI","consequence":"Arrest of 35 people accused of running a scam, some victims kept purchasing more due to false belief in divine messages"}},{"i":"R048","k":"Brazil","p":"Brazil","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Ai turned a disgraced president under house arrest into a live avatar for another election campaign","s":"An AI-generated Jair Bolsonaro endorsed his son’s presidential campaign despite the former Brazilian leader’s house arrest, election ban, and restrictions on public communication. Jair Bolsonaro is barred from Brazil’s 2026 presidential election, being confined to house arrest and restricted from communicating publicly. But an AI-generated version of the former president still managed to take the stage at his son’s campaign launch. The synthetic Bolsonaro appeared in a video at the launch of Senator Flávio Bolsonaro’s presidential campaign in São Paulo. It openly identified itself as AI-generated before endorsing Flávio as the person chosen to carry forward his father’s political movement.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/cool-tech/ai-turned-a-disgraced-president-under-house-arrest-into-a-live-avatar-for-another-election-campaign/","d":"2026-07-29","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-29","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07-29","organization":"Jair Bolsonaro's political movement","aiSystem":"AI-generated avatar","affectedGroup":"Jair Bolsonaro and his supporters","action":"An AI-generated version of Jair Bolsonaro endorsed his son's presidential campaign","consequence":"Public perception of Jair Bolsonaro's political legacy and influence"}},{"i":"R1393","k":"Brazil","p":"Brazil","b":["Data"],"h":["4.3  Data rights / consent violation"],"t":"Brazil's data protection regulator says it aims to protect users, the company says it is d…","s":"Brazil's data protection regulator says it aims to protect users, the company says it is disappointed. Brazil has blocked Meta from using Brazilians' Instagram and Facebook posts to train its artificial intelligence (AI) models. It comes weeks after the company abandoned similar plans to use UK and European users' posts for the same purpose. On Tuesday, Brazil's national data protection agency (ANPD) said it would immediately suspend Meta's latest privacy policy, which allows it to train generative AI models such as chatbots based on posts from its users. A Meta spokesperson told the BBC the company was \"disappointed by the decision\", adding that their approach complied with local privacy laws.","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c7291l3nvwvo","d":"2024-07-03","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-07-03","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-07-03","organization":"Meta (Facebook)","aiSystem":"Generative AI models such as chatbots","affectedGroup":"Users of Meta platforms (Instagram and Facebook)","action":"Training generative AI models based on user posts","consequence":"Immediate suspension of privacy policy"}},{"i":"R1426","k":"Brazil","p":"Brazil","b":["Data"],"h":["4.3  Data rights / consent violation"],"t":"Sao paulo challenges world ids biometric data collection practices","s":"Lawsuit alleges more than 400,000 residents were paid to provide iris biometrics without informed consent and seeks a permanent ban on incentivized collection. São Paulo’s consumer protection authorities are taking legal action against Tools for Humanity , the company behind World ID, and Amazon AWS Brasil, accusing them of abusive biometric data collection practices targeting vulnerable residents. The lawsuit alleges that World ID operators scanned the irises of more than 400,000 people in São Paulo in exchange for payments of R$300–R$700 (US$59–$138), concentrating operations in high traffic, low‑income areas near metro stations and Poupatempo units.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202607/sao-paulo-challenges-world-ids-biometric-data-collection-practices","d":"2026-07-24","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-24","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07-24","organization":"Tools for Humanity (World ID), Amazon AWS Brasil","aiSystem":"Biometric data collection system","affectedGroup":"More than 400,000 residents of São Paulo","action":"Scanned irises of residents in exchange for payments","consequence":"Permanent ban on incentivized collection"}},{"i":"R1487","k":"Brazil","p":"Brazil","b":["Data"],"h":["4.3  Data rights / consent violation"],"t":"Brazilian parliamentary commission extends investigation into tools for humanity","s":"A parliamentary commission in Brazil investigating Tools for Humanity has approved an extension of the investigation for another 120 days. A parliamentary commission in Brazil investigating Tools for Humanity , the company behind the digital identity project World , has approved an extension of the investigation for another 120 days. The Iris Parliamentary Commission of Inquiry (CPI da Íris) was established by the São Paulo City Council in May to investigate Tools for Humanity for offering financial rewards in return for scanning the irises of the capital’s residents through its World ID project. The commission’s Chair, Janaina Paschoal, announced last week that the investigation has resulted in the company admitting that the activities conducted in São Paulo were not aimed at “contributing to humanity, but rather at developing a product.”","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202509/brazilian-parliamentary-commission-extends-investigation-into-tools-for-humanity","d":"2025-09-09","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-09-09","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-09-09","organization":"Tools for Humanity","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"Residents of São Paulo","action":"Scanning irises for financial rewards","consequence":"Admission of unethical practices"}},{"i":"R1543","k":"Brazil","p":"Brazil","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Brazilian groups call for ban on facial recognition","s":"Brazil is operating facial recognition for law enforcement and public security without meeting minimum standards of transparency. Brazil is operating facial recognition for law enforcement and public security without meeting minimum standards of transparency, according to a new report. The “ Viligância Por Lentes Opacas ” or “Surveillance Through Opaque Lenses: mapping the transparency and accountability of facial recognition projects in Brazil” seeks to take a critical look at the mechanisms of public transparency in the use of facial recognition cameras in public security in all five regions of Brazil. The systematic study was a collaboration between the Center for Security and Citizenship Studies (CESeC) and the Public Policy and Internet Laboratory (Lapin), and measured degrees of active and passive transparency in 50 cases of facial recognition use.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202410/brazilian-groups-call-for-ban-on-facial-recognition","d":"2024-10-17","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-10-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-10-17","organization":"Vilígância Por Lentes Opacas (Surveillance Through Opaque Lenses initiative)","aiSystem":"facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"Brazilian citizens","action":"operating facial recognition for law enforcement and public security","consequence":"lack of transparency in the use of facial recognition cameras"}},{"i":"R1699","k":"Brazil","p":"Brazil","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Eu made facial recognition ended up scanning schoolchildren in brazil","s":"The EU restricts biometric surveillance at home – but its landmark AI Act does nothing to prevent European companies from exporting the same technology abroad. When the European Union agreed its landmark AI Act in December 2023, Commission president Ursula von der Leyen declared it a “historic moment”, saying the legislation “transposes European values to a new era.” Since then, the EU has positioned itself as the global standard-setter for trustworthy AI. At the AI Action Summit in Paris in February 2025, von der Leyen pledged that Europe would “spare no effort to make Europe an AI continent,” insisting that safety and innovation must go hand in hand.","u":"https://euobserver.com/206781/eu-made-facial-recognition-ended-up-scanning-schoolchildren-in-brazil/","d":"2026-03-13","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-03-13","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-02","organization":"European Union","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"Schoolchildren","action":"Used facial recognition to scan schoolchildren","consequence":"Misuse of technology for surveillance purposes"}},{"i":"R2667","k":"Brazil","p":"Brazil","b":["Data"],"h":["4.3  Data rights / consent violation"],"t":"Meta anpd stop training ai on brazilian facebook instagram data","s":"This follows a similar pushback against AI training in the EU. Following similar pushback in the EU, Meta now faces daily fines if it uses Brazilian Facebook and Instagram data for AI training. Brazil’s data protection authority (ANPD) has banned Meta from training its artificial intelligence models on Brazilian personal data, citing the “risks of serious damage and difficulty to users.” The decision follows an update to Meta’s privacy policy in May in which the social media giant granted itself permission to use public Facebook, Messenger, and Instagram data from Brazil — including posts, images, and captions — for AI training.","u":"https://www.theverge.com/2024/7/3/24191405/meta-anpd-stop-training-ai-on-brazilian-facebook-instagram-data","d":"2024-07-03","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-07-03","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-07-03","organization":"Meta (Facebook, Instagram)","aiSystem":"Meta's AI models","affectedGroup":"Users of Brazilian Facebook and Instagram","action":"Training AI models on Brazilian personal data","consequence":"Daily fines, ban on AI training, privacy risks"}},{"i":"R1091","k":"Burkina Faso","p":"Burkina Faso","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Venezuela ai newscaster disinformation","s":"The same A.I. technology has also appeared to promote propaganda in Burkina Faso and China. Strange videos promoting Venezuela are being shared on state run TV, using apparently English-speaking American hosts. The videos first appeared on a YouTube channel called the House of News Español , one featuring a blond white man speaking in American English over a clip called “Venezuela and its economy, myth or reality?” But the purported journalist on screen is Noah, one of dozens of artificial intelligence avatars created by a company called Synthesia that for just $30 a month will say whatever the buyer wants. Similar videos produced recently using Synthesia software have been used to promote propaganda in Africa and Asia as well over the past month. @vicenews Who made these AI-generated videos in support of Burkina Faso’s military rulers?","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/venezuela-ai-newscaster-disinformation/","d":"2023-02-24","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-02-24","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-02-24","organization":"Synthesia (a company)","aiSystem":"Synthesia AI-generated avatars","affectedGroup":"Burkina Faso's military rulers","action":"Promoted propaganda videos","consequence":"Disinformation spread in Burkina Faso"}},{"i":"R1112","k":"Burkina Faso","p":"Burkina Faso","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Ai generated video burkino faso coup","s":"AI video generator Synthesia confirmed to VICE World News the “Pan-Africanist” video backing Burkina Faso’s military rulers had been made using its software but would not reveal who had created them. Bizarre AI-generated videos of people expressing support for Burkina Faso’s new military junta have appeared online, in what could be a clumsy attempt to spread pro-military propaganda. It’s unclear who created the videos, but it appears they are being shared via WhatsApp. One of the videos was shared widely on Twitter this week when it was shared by Lauren Blanchard, a specialist in African affairs at the Congressional Research Service in the US.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/ai-generated-video-burkino-faso-coup/","d":"2023-01-27","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-01-27","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-01-27","organization":"Burkina Faso's military junta","aiSystem":"Synthesia AI video generator","affectedGroup":"Members of Burkina Faso's public and potential supporters of the military junta","action":"AI-generated videos expressing support for Burkina Faso's military junta were created and distributed","consequence":"Potential spread of pro-military propaganda and confusion among the public"}},{"i":"R1563","k":"Cameroon","p":"Cameroon","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.2  Allocative harm"],"t":"Cameroons move to increase biometric id card fee faces sharp criticism","s":"As part of the changes, there’s an increase in the fee for the issuance of biometric visas and the new generation biometric national ID card. Major identity documents as well as consular services in Cameroon have seen a jump in issuance fees – a decision that has attracted a fusillade of negative reactions. The increase was announced recently by President Paul Biya in an ordinance which supplements some provisions of the country’s 2024 finance law As part of the changes, there’s an increase in the fee for the issuance of biometric visas and the new generation biometric national ID card which will be launched by the end of the year.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202406/cameroons-move-to-increase-biometric-id-card-fee-faces-sharp-criticism","d":"2024-06-26","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-06-26","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-06-26","organization":"Government of Cameroon","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"Citizens of Cameroon","action":"Raised fees for biometric visas and national ID cards","consequence":"Sharp criticism and negative reactions"}},{"i":"R911","k":"Canada","p":"Canada","b":["Compute / Model Behavior","Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm","1.2  Allocative harm"],"t":"Enbridge pipeline line 3 tracking indigenous protesters","s":"On a color-coded map, land belonging to Native tribes that opposed the Line 3 pipeline were marked in red — areas of “threat” to the bottom line. As part of its efforts to build and operate pipelines, the oil transport company Enbridge used a tracking system that identified Indigenous-led groups as key threats. Internal documents reviewed by The Intercept describe how Enbridge launched an initiative known as Opposition Driven Operational Threats, or ODOT, to focus the company’s attention on Indigenous opposition to Line 3 and Line 5, two controversial pipelines that transport carbon-intensive tar sands oil between Canada and the United States.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2022/01/23/enbridge-pipeline-line-3-tracking-indigenous-protesters/","d":"2022-01-23","e":"Acute, Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2022-01-23","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2022-01-23","organization":"Enbridge","aiSystem":"Tracking system","affectedGroup":"Indigenous-led groups","action":"Identified Indigenous-led groups as key threats","consequence":"Focused company's attention on Indigenous opposition to pipelines"}},{"i":"R1047","k":"Canada","p":"Canada","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Anti homeless mayoral candidate uses ai to create fake images of blight","s":"Toronto mayoral hopeful Anthony Furey says homeless “blight” is real, but his campaign’s images are not. Anthony Furey, a former Toronto Sun columnist, is running to be the mayor of Toronto in the June 26 election. Furey has a conservative platform that calls for increased police at tent encampments, dismantling bike lanes, scrapping a harm reduction program offering clean crack pipes to drug users, and adding 500 police officers. While his platform does not mention any stance on the ethical use of artificial intelligence, his campaign appears to have used AI to create fake images of unhoused people on the streets of Toronto to make his pitch.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/anti-homeless-mayoral-candidate-uses-ai-to-create-fake-images-of-blight/","d":"2023-06-15","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-06-15","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-06-15","organization":"Anthony Furey's campaign","aiSystem":"AI-generated imagery","affectedGroup":"Homeless people in Toronto","action":"Created fake images of homeless people on the streets of Toronto","consequence":"Misleading public perception and potential stigmatization of homeless individuals"}},{"i":"R767","k":"Canada","p":"Canada","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Ai being used hacking disinformation top canadian cyber official","s":"The official says it has seen AI being used ‘in phishing emails, or crafting emails in a more focused way, in malicious code (and) in misinformation and disinformation’. Hackers and propagandists are wielding artificial intelligence (AI) to create malicious software, draft convincing phishing emails and spread disinformation online, Canada’s top cybersecurity official told Reuters, early evidence that the technological revolution sweeping Silicon Valley has also been adopted by cybercriminals. In an interview this week, Canadian Centre for Cyber Security head Sami Khoury said that his agency had seen AI being used “in phishing emails, or crafting emails in a more focused way, in malicious code (and) in misinformation and disinformation.”","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/ai-being-used-hacking-disinformation-top-canadian-cyber-official/","d":"2023-07-21","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-07-21","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-07-21","organization":"Canadian Centre for Cyber Security","aiSystem":"Artificial Intelligence (AI)","affectedGroup":"Hackers and propagandists","action":"Using AI to create malicious software, draft convincing phishing emails, and spread disinformation online","consequence":"Phishing emails, malicious code, and misinformation spread online"}},{"i":"R773","k":"Canada","p":"Canada","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Canada openai probe privacy concerns","s":"The country’s privacy body will investigate whether OpenAI obtained consent for the collection, use and disclosure of personal information of residents via ChatGPT. Canadian privacy regulators are launching a joint investigation into ChatGPT-parent OpenAI’s data collection and usage, becoming the latest major government to take a closer look at the regulation of artificial intelligence (AI) tools. The federal privacy regulator, along with counterparts in Quebec, British Columbia and Alberta, will investigate if OpenAI has obtained consent for the collection, use and disclosure of personal information of residents via ChatGPT, the Office of the Privacy Commissioner of Canada said on Thursday.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/canada-openai-probe-privacy-concerns/","d":"2023-05-26","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-05-26","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-05-26","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Residents of Canada","action":"Collection, use, and disclosure of personal information via ChatGPT","consequence":"Potential lack of informed consent"}},{"i":"R305","k":"Canada","p":"Canada","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"The register putins trolls return with ai powered media fakes and a florida ex cop at the helm","s":"Insikt Group researchers link Llama 3-based models to a Kremlin-driven disinformation wave. A sprawling Russian disinformation network tied to a former Florida sheriff’s deputy has resurfaced in 2025 with over 200 newly created fake news websites. Backed by Russia’s military intelligence agency and powered by artificial intelligence, the network is using fabricated news, forged documents, and deepfakes to push pro-Kremlin and pro-Trump narratives to voters in the United States, Canada, and Europe, The Register reports As the Kremlin scales up its disinfo operations, the US government is simultaneously dismantling federal efforts to protect elections from such foreign interference.","u":"https://euromaidanpress.com/2025/09/24/the-register-putins-trolls-return-with-ai-powered-media-fakes-and-a-florida-ex-cop-at-the-helm/","d":"2025-09-24","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-09-24","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-09-24","organization":"Kremlin-driven disinformation network","aiSystem":"Llama 3-based models","affectedGroup":"US, Canada, and Europe's voters","action":"Creating and spreading fake news, forged documents, and deepfakes","consequence":"Pushing pro-Kremlin and pro-Trump narratives, potentially influencing elections"}},{"i":"R347","k":"Canada","p":"Canada","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Lawsuit openai chatgpt tumbler ridge mass shooting victims","s":"Victims of the Tumbler Ridge mass shooting and their families sued OpenAI and its CEO, Sam Altman, in US district court in San Francisco on Wednesday, claiming various negligence, product liability, and other violations. The civil complaints are the latest in a wave of litigation against OpenAI alleging that its globally popular chatbot, ChatGPT, helped […]. The civil complaints are the latest in a wave of litigation against OpenAI alleging that its globally popular chatbot, ChatGPT, helped people commit lethal violence. The complaints were filed by families of multiple victims wounded and killed at Tumbler Ridge Secondary School in British Columbia, Canada, where a suicidal 18-year-old opened fire on February 10.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/criminal-justice/2026/04/lawsuit-openai-chatgpt-tumbler-ridge-mass-shooting-victims/","d":"2026-04-29","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-04-29","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-04-29","organization":"OpenAI, Sam Altman","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Victims of the Tumbler Ridge mass shooting and their families","action":"ChatGPT was allegedly used to help people commit lethal violence","consequence":"People committed lethal violence resulting in deaths and injuries"}},{"i":"R485","k":"Canada","p":"Canada","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Canada officials disappointed openai representatives school shooting","s":"Jesse Van Rootselaar is suspected of killing eight people on February 10 before taking her own life in British Columbia. OpenAI says it banned her ChatGPT account last year for policy violations, adding it did not meet internal criteria for reporting to law enforcement. OTTAWA, Canada – Canadian officials expressed disappointment that OpenAI representatives did not present new safety measures in a meeting on Tuesday, February 24, after the ChatGPT maker said it did not contact police about an account it banned belonging to an alleged mass shooter. Jesse Van Rootselaar, 18, is suspected of killing eight people on February 10 before taking her own life in a small town in British Columbia.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/canada-officials-disappointed-openai-representatives-school-shooting/","d":"2026-02-25","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-02-25","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-02-24","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Jesse Van Rootselaar (alleged mass shooter)","action":"Banned ChatGPT account for policy violations","consequence":"No contact with law enforcement regarding banned account"}},{"i":"R945","k":"Canada","p":"Canada","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm","1.8  Interpersonal / relational harm"],"t":"Canadian artist has show cancelled after ai labeled him a sex offender preparing to sue google","s":"The worries surrounding AI are abundant, especially in music. As much as artists like Timbaland and Will.i.am are quick to embrace it, people share plenty of concern. For instance, Kehlani aired out their frustrations about AI-generated artist Xania Monet receiving a record deal. “There is an AI R&B artist who just signed a multimillion-dollar deal, […]. “There is an AI R&B artist who just signed a multimillion-dollar deal, and the person is doing none of the work,” the “Folded” crooner said. “This is so beyond out of our control.“Nothing and no one on Earth will ever be able to justify AI to me. I don’t respect it.”","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/canadian-artist-has-show-cancelled-after-ai-labeled-him-a-sex-offender-preparing-to-sue-google/","d":"2026-01-03","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-01-03","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-01-03","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"AI-generated R&B artist","affectedGroup":"Kehlani (musician)","action":"AI-generated R&B artist received a multimillion-dollar record deal","consequence":"Artist expressed frustration and lack of respect towards AI-generated content"}},{"i":"R1271","k":"Canada","p":"Canada","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Musks grok accused of violating canadian privacy laws on deepfakes","s":"Privacy watchdog finds xAI's Grok lacks safeguards for sexualised deepfake image sharing, amid growing global scrutiny. xAI’s Grok has violated Canadian privacy laws because it launched an image generator that can create and share sexualised deepfake images without users’ consent, according to a report by the country’s privacy commissioner following a January probe. The official report, which was released on Thursday, comes after the Elon Musk-owned platform rolled out changes that would prevent Grok from allowing users to edit images of real people in revealing clothing. “xAI violated Canada’s federal private sector privacy law by launching the Grok AI-powered image generation tool without implementing appropriate safeguards from the outset,” Privacy Commissioner Philippe Dufresne said in a press conference on Thursday.","u":"https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2026/6/11/musks-grok-accused-of-violating-canadian-privacy-laws-on-deepfakes","d":"2026-06-11","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-06-11","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-06-11","organization":"xAI","aiSystem":"xAI's Grok","affectedGroup":"Users of xAI's Grok","action":"Created and shared sexualized deepfake images without user consent","consequence":"Violated Canadian privacy laws"}},{"i":"R1276","k":"Canada","p":"Canada","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Families sue openai alleging chatbot aided in canadian school shooting","s":"Plaintiffs accuse OpenAI of not alerting authorities to signs of a threat, leading to a school shooting in February. The families of victims of a school shooting in a remote Canadian Rockies town are suing artificial intelligence company OpenAI in a United States federal court, alleging that the ChatGPT maker failed to alert police to the shooter’s alarming interactions with the chatbot. A lawsuit filed on Wednesday on behalf of 12-year-old Maya Gebala, who was critically injured in the February shooting, is among the first of more than two dozen cases from families in Tumbler Ridge, British Columbia, in what their lawyers say represents “an entire community stepping forward to hold OpenAI accountable”.","u":"https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2026/4/29/families-sue-openai-alleging-chatbot-aided-in-canadian-school-shooting","d":"2026-04-22","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-04-22","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-02","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Victims' families","action":"Failed to alert authorities to signs of a threat","consequence":"School shooting resulting in injuries"}},{"i":"R1328","k":"Canada","p":"Canada","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Canadian officials say they are seeking \"better understanding\" after learning the suspecte…","s":"Canadian officials say they are seeking \"better understanding\" after learning the suspected shooter's ChatGPT account was banned months before the attack. Canada's minister for artificial intelligence has summoned senior staff from OpenAI on Tuesday over the mass shooting in Tumbler Ridge, British Columbia, in which eight people were killed including six young children. The company said last week it banned a ChatGPT account owned by the shooting suspect more than half a year before the attack but did not alert authorities at the time as it did not meet a serious harm threshold. AI Minister Evan Solomon said the OpenAI staff will be asked to discuss \"safety protocols\" and when harmful posts are relayed to law enforcement.","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c2e4nvyjwnno","d":"2026-02-24","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-02-24","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-02-24","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Suspected shooter","action":"ChatGPT account was banned","consequence":"Suspected shooter's account was banned"}},{"i":"R1386","k":"Canada","p":"Canada","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"The suit, a first for Canada, alleges OpenAI's chatbot was illegally scraping news article…","s":"The suit, a first for Canada, alleges OpenAI's chatbot was illegally scraping news articles, which the firm denies. A coalition of Canada's biggest news outlets is suing OpenAI, the maker of artificial intelligence chatbot ChatGPT, alleging the company is illegally using news articles to train its software. News organisations including the Toronto Star, Metroland Media, Postmedia, The Globe and Mail, The Canadian Press and CBC have all joined the suit, reportedly the first of its kind in the country. “Journalism is in the public interest. OpenAI using other companies’ journalism for their own commercial gain is not.","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cm27247j6gno","d":"2024-11-29","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-11-29","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-11-29","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"OpenAI's chatbot (ChatGPT)","affectedGroup":"Canada's biggest news outlets (Toronto Star, Metroland Media, Postmedia, The Globe and Mail, The Canadian Press, CBC)","action":"illegally scraping news articles","consequence":"alleged illegal use of journalism for commercial gain"}},{"i":"R1402","k":"Canada","p":"Canada","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.6  Quality of service harm"],"t":"20240222 air canada chatbot misinformation what travellers should know","s":"When Air Canada’s chatbot gave incorrect information to a traveller, the airline argued its chatbot is \"responsible for its own actions\". Artificial intelligence is having a growing impact on the way we travel, and a remarkable new case shows what AI-powered chatbots can get wrong – and who should pay. In 2022, Air Canada's chatbot promised a discount that wasn't available to passenger Jake Moffatt, who was assured that he could book a full-fare flight for his grandmother's funeral and then apply for a bereavement fare after the fact. According to a civil-resolutions tribunal decision last Wednesday, when Moffatt applied for the discount, the airline said the chatbot had been wrong – the request needed to be submitted before the flight – and it wouldn't offer the discount.","u":"https://www.bbc.com/travel/article/20240222-air-canada-chatbot-misinformation-what-travellers-should-know","d":"2024-02-23","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-02-23","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2022-12-13","organization":"Air Canada","aiSystem":"Air Canada's chatbot","affectedGroup":"Jake Moffatt, passenger","action":"chatbot provided incorrect information","consequence":"misled passenger, denied discount"}},{"i":"R1450","k":"Canada","p":"Canada","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Appeals court confirms clearview ai biometric data collection violates bc privacy law","s":"The Court will not overturn a ruling in support of the province’s privacy commissioner, which found Clearview AI to be in violation of local privacy law. A British Columbia Appeal Court will not overturn a ruling by the B.C. Supreme Court in support of the province’s privacy commissioner, which found facial recognition provider Clearview A I to be in violation of local privacy law when it scraped images of B.C. residents from social media for its databases. A report from the Vancouver Sun says the court dismissed an appeal from the U.S. tech company to have a 2021 three-part order from the commissioner ruled unreasonable.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202602/appeals-court-confirms-clearview-ai-biometric-data-collection-violates-bc-privacy-law","d":"2026-02-20","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-02-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-02-20","organization":"Clearview AI","aiSystem":"Clearview AI facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"B.C. residents","action":"scraped images of B.C. residents from social media","consequence":"violated local privacy law"}},{"i":"R1501","k":"Canada","p":"Canada","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Clearview takes fresh legal hits over canada class action uk fine","s":"Canada's Federal Court of Appeal has revived a proposed class action against Clearview after the company operated in Canada from 2019 to July 2020. Few biometrics companies have taken a bigger regulatory and legal beating than Clearview AI It has already been a rough year for the firm, which in April ousted its founder and CEO, Hoan Ton That. In May, Clearview finally settled a five-year-long biometric data privacy lawsuit in the U.S., which gave plaintiffs a 23 percent equity stake in the company, to be triggered by an IPO or a liquidation event. Estimates put the value of the settlement at around $51.75 million.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202507/clearview-takes-fresh-legal-hits-over-canada-class-action-uk-fine","d":"2025-07-18","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-07-18","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2020-07","organization":"Clearview AI","aiSystem":"Clearview AI","affectedGroup":"Individuals whose biometric data was collected by Clearview AI","action":"Collected biometric data from individuals without their consent","consequence":"Legal actions, financial penalties, and regulatory scrutiny"}},{"i":"R1729","k":"Canada","p":"Canada","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Chatgpt jesse van roostselaar lawsuit b2935262","s":"OpenAI came forward to police after Jesse Van Roostselaar killed eight people and then herself last month. The parents of a girl critically wounded in a Canadian school shooting have filed a civil lawsuit, alleging ChatGPT-maker OpenAI knew the shooter was planning a mass attack. OpenAI has said it considered but didn’t alert police about the activities of the person who months later committed one of Canada's worst school shootings in Tumbler Ridge, British Columbia, on 10 February. OpenAI came forward to police after Jesse Van Roostselaar killed eight people and then herself last month, saying the attacker's ChatGPT account had been closed but she evaded the ban with a second account.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/chatgpt-jesse-van-roostselaar-lawsuit-b2935262.html","d":"2026-03-10","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-03-10","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-02-10","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Jesse Van Roostselaar, parents of a critically wounded girl","action":"closed the attacker's ChatGPT account","consequence":"evaded the ban with a second account"}},{"i":"R1901","k":"Canada","p":"Canada","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Iran backed hackers interrupt uae tv streaming services with deepfake news","s":"Microsoft analysts cite reports saying disruption by group known as Cotton Sandstorm also reached audiences in UK and Canada. Iranian state-backed hackers interrupted TV streaming services in the United Arab Emirates to broadcast a deepfake newsreader delivering a report on the war in Gaza, according to analysts at Microsoft. The tech company said a hacking operation run by the Islamic Revolutionary Guards, a key branch of the Iranian armed forces, had disrupted streaming platforms in the UAE with an AI-generated news broadcast branded “For Humanity”. The fake news anchor introduced unverified images that claimed to show Palestinians injured and killed from Israeli military operations in Gaza.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2024/feb/08/iran-backed-hackers-interrupt-uae-tv-streaming-services-with-deepfake-news","d":"2024-02-08","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-02-08","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-02-08","organization":"Iranian state-backed hackers, Islamic Revolutionary Guards","aiSystem":"AI-generated deepfake technology","affectedGroup":"TV viewers in the United Arab Emirates, UK, and Canada","action":"Broadcasted an AI-generated news broadcast","consequence":"Exposure to misleading content"}},{"i":"R1292","k":"Canada","p":"Canada","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Five canadian news media outlets sue openai for copyright breach","s":"The case is a part of a wave of lawsuits against OpenAI over data used to train generative AI systems. Five Canadian news media companies have filed a legal action against ChatGPT owner OpenAI, accusing the artificial intelligence company of regularly breaching copyright and online terms of use. The case, filed on Friday, is part of a wave of lawsuits against OpenAI and other tech companies by authors, visual artists, music publishers and other copyright owners over data used to train generative AI systems. Microsoft is OpenAI’s major backer. In a statement, Torstar, Postmedia, The Globe and Mail, The Canadian Press, and CBC/Radio-Canada said OpenAI was scraping large swaths of content to develop its products without getting permission or compensating content owners.","u":"https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2024/11/29/five-canadian-news-media-outlets-sue-openai-for-copyright-breach","d":"2024-11-29","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-11-29","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-11-29","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"Generative AI systems","affectedGroup":"Canadian news media companies, authors, visual artists, music publishers, and other copyright owners","action":"Scraping large swaths of content without permission or compensation","consequence":"Regular copyright breaches and legal actions"}},{"i":"R1318","k":"Canada","p":"Canada","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Researchers warn that foreign actors are meddling in the Alberta separatist movement in wa…","s":"Researchers warn that foreign actors are meddling in the Alberta separatist movement in ways that threaten Canada’s \"democratic integrity\". Foreign actors are attempting to undermine Canadian interests by interfering in Alberta's separatist debate, according to a new report. Actors from Russia and the US are attempting to promote separatist ideas in what researchers behind the report described as a threat to Canada's \"democratic integrity\". The findings come as a proposed independence referendum in the western Canadian province could face a vote as soon as this autumn. Earlier this week, a group behind a citizen-led petition seeking independence for Alberta said that it had acquired the number of signatures needed to trigger a referendum.","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cze2dnn8kgwo","d":"2026-05-06","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-05-06","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-05-06","organization":"Foreign actors (Russia and US)","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"Alberta separatists","action":"Promoting separatist ideas","consequence":"Threatening Canada's democratic integrity"}},{"i":"R2134","k":"Canada","p":"Canada","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Toronto rosedale row virtual gated community ai surveillance flock","s":"Rosedale residents considering car licence plate-scanning Flock system in bid to tackle property crime. A row has broken out in one of Canada’s wealthiest neighbourhoods over plans to use an AI-powered surveillance system to create the country’s first “virtual gated community” to combat surging property crime. Crime rates in Toronto as a whole are dropping but residents of Rosedale have been left on edge by a sustained rise in home invasions, with robbers targeting the tree-lined neighbourhood at a rate more than double the city average. Break-ins and thefts remain the third highest per capita in Toronto. Growing unease is reflected in the community’s WhatsApp group, where as many as 60 out of about 350 members are already contributing to fund private security.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/apr/07/toronto-rosedale-row-virtual-gated-community-ai-surveillance-flock","d":"2026-04-07","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-04-07","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-04-07","organization":"Rosedale residents and local authorities","aiSystem":"AI-powered surveillance system","affectedGroup":"Residents of Rosedale","action":"Planning to use an AI-powered surveillance system","consequence":"Potential misuse of the system leading to privacy invasion and mistrust"}},{"i":"R2253","k":"Canada","p":"Canada","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Iran backed hackers interrupt uae tv streaming services with deepfake news","s":"Microsoft analysts cite reports saying disruption by group known as Cotton Sandstorm also reached audiences in UK and Canada. Iranian state-backed hackers interrupted TV streaming services in the United Arab Emirates to broadcast a deepfake newsreader delivering a report on the war in Gaza, according to analysts at Microsoft. The tech company said a hacking operation run by the Islamic Revolutionary Guards, a key branch of the Iranian armed forces, had disrupted streaming platforms in the UAE with an AI-generated news broadcast branded “For Humanity”. The fake news anchor introduced unverified images that claimed to show Palestinians injured and killed from Israeli military operations in Gaza.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2024/feb/08/iran-backed-hackers-interrupt-uae-tv-streaming-services-with-deepfake-news","d":"2024-02-08","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-02-08","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-02-08","organization":"Iranian state-backed hackers, Islamic Revolutionary Guards","aiSystem":"AI-generated deepfake technology","affectedGroup":"TV viewers in the United Arab Emirates, UK, and Canada","action":"Broadcasted an AI-generated news broadcast","consequence":"Exposure to misleading content"}},{"i":"R2486","k":"Canada","p":"Canada","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Toronto rosedale row virtual gated community ai surveillance flock","s":"Rosedale residents considering car licence plate-scanning Flock system in bid to tackle property crime. A row has broken out in one of Canada’s wealthiest neighbourhoods over plans to use an AI-powered surveillance system to create the country’s first “virtual gated community” to combat surging property crime. Crime rates in Toronto as a whole are dropping but residents of Rosedale have been left on edge by a sustained rise in home invasions, with robbers targeting the tree-lined neighbourhood at a rate more than double the city average. Break-ins and thefts remain the third highest per capita in Toronto. Growing unease is reflected in the community’s WhatsApp group, where as many as 60 out of about 350 members are already contributing to fund private security.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/apr/07/toronto-rosedale-row-virtual-gated-community-ai-surveillance-flock","d":"2026-04-07","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-04-07","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-04-07","organization":"Rosedale residents and local authorities","aiSystem":"AI-powered surveillance system","affectedGroup":"Residents of Rosedale","action":"Planning to use an AI-powered surveillance system","consequence":"Potential misuse of the system leading to privacy invasion and mistrust"}},{"i":"R2582","k":"Canada","p":"Canada","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Tumbler ridge school shooting chatgpt","s":"OpenAI employees were concerned, but didn’t alert law enforcement. The posts raised alarms, but OpenAI declined to alert law enforcement. The suspect in the mass shooting at Tumbler Ridge , British Columbia, Jesse Van Rootselaar, was raising alarms among employees at OpenAI months before the shooting took place. This past June, Jesse had conversations with ChatGPT involving descriptions of gun violence that triggered the chatbot’s automated review system. Several employees raised concerns that her posts could be a precursor to real-world violence and encouraged company leaders to contact the authorities, but they ultimately declined.","u":"https://www.theverge.com/ai-artificial-intelligence/882814/tumbler-ridge-school-shooting-chatgpt","d":"2026-02-21","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-02-21","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"June 2026","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Employees at OpenAI","action":"Raised alarms and flagged posts","consequence":"Suspect's posts were not alerted to law enforcement"}},{"i":"R2675","k":"Canada","p":"Canada","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.5  Human computer interaction harm"],"t":"Ai chatbot friends character teens","s":"Teens are opening up to chatbots on Character.AI as a way to explore friendship. But some chatbots, like Psychologist, offer more guidance than they’re qualified to. Early last year, 15-year-old Aaron was going through a dark time at school. He’d fallen out with his friends, leaving him feeling isolated and alone. At the time, it seemed like the end of the world. “I used to cry every night,” said Aaron, who lives in Alberta, Canada. (The Verge is using aliases for the interviewees in this article, all of whom are under 18, to protect their privacy.) Eventually, Aaron turned to his computer for comfort. Through it, he found someone that was available round the clock to respond to his messages, listen to his problems, and help him move past the loss of his friend group.","u":"https://www.theverge.com/2024/5/4/24144763/ai-chatbot-friends-character-teens","d":"2024-05-04","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-05-04","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-05-04","organization":"Character.AI","aiSystem":"AI chatbot on Character.AI","affectedGroup":"Teens, particularly 15-year-old Aaron","action":"Used an AI chatbot to communicate and seek emotional support","consequence":"Exposure of personal information and vulnerability to psychological distress"}},{"i":"R2729","k":"Canada","p":"Canada","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.1  Data labor exploitation"],"t":"Pornhub mindgeek content moderation","s":"I wasn’t even supposed to be a moderator. It’s what I was doing in between writing tweets. In summer of 2010, I was broke and needed a job. Walking the sunbaked streets of Montreal’s downtown, I called up old friends hoping for a lead. I was in luck: a buddy told me a “porn company” was hiring customer service reps. “They pay 15 bucks an hour, and it’s super chill,” he said.Â Long before it was called MindGeek, the company was Manwin Canada, a name that, at the time, didn’t ring a bell. From an internet cafe, I sent in my CV. I had only ever held low-level service jobs. But I had edited a friend’s indie zine and put that at the top of my work experience.Â A couple days later, I got a call from an enthusiastic woman at Manwin who told me they were impressed by my résumé and asked if I would be interested in a more senior position as a media buyer for the company.","u":"https://www.theverge.com/c/22925906/pornhub-mindgeek-content-moderation","d":"2022-02-23","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2022-02-23","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2010","organization":"MindGeek (formerly Manwin Canada)","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"Job applicants","action":"Rejected application for a senior position","consequence":"Denied access to a higher-paying job opportunity"}},{"i":"R2747","k":"Canada","p":"Canada","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Clearview facial recognition illegal mass surveillance canada privacy","s":"Clearview AI’s facial recognition amounts to mass surveillance and the company should delete the faces of Canadians from its database, Canada’s privacy commissioners said following a yearlong investigation. Clearview AI’s facial recognition amounts to mass surveillance and the company should delete the faces of Canadians from its database, Canada’s privacy commissioners said Wednesday. Commissioner Daniel Therrien said what Clearview does — scraping photos from social media and other public sites for use by law enforcement — is “illegal” and creates a system that “inflicts broad-based harm on all members of society, who find themselves continually in a police lineup.”","u":"https://www.theverge.com/2021/2/4/22266055/clearview-facial-recognition-illegal-mass-surveillance-canada-privacy","d":"2021-02-04","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-02-04","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-02-04","organization":"Clearview AI","aiSystem":"Clearview AI's facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"Canadians","action":"scraping photos from social media and other public sites for use by law enforcement","consequence":"inflict broad-based harm on all members of society, who find themselves continually in a police lineup"}},{"i":"R2873","k":"Canada","p":"Canada","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Deepfake survivor breeze liu microsoft","s":"Breeze Liu has been a prominent advocate for victims. But even she struggled to scrub nonconsensual intimate images and videos of herself from the web. Breeze Liu’s online nightmare started with a phone call. In April 2020, a college classmate rang Liu, then 24 years old, to tell her an explicit video of her was on PornHub under the title “Korean teen.” Liu alleges it had been filmed without her permission when she was 17 and uploaded without her consent. Over time, the video mushroomed and multiplied: it was saved, posted on other porn websites and, Liu claims, used to create intimate deepfake videos that spread across the web.","u":"https://www.wired.com/story/deepfake-survivor-breeze-liu-microsoft/","d":"2025-02-20","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-02-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2020-04","organization":"PornHub","aiSystem":"Deepfake technology","affectedGroup":"Breeze Liu","action":"Used deepfake technology to generate and distribute intimate content","consequence":"Spread of non-consensual intimate deepfake videos across the web"}},{"i":"R2950","k":"Canada","p":"Canada","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.6  Quality of service harm"],"t":"Air canada chatbot refund policy","s":"The airline tried to argue that it shouldn't be liable for anything its chatbot says. After months of resisting, Air Canada was forced to give a partial refund to a grieving passenger who was misled by an airline chatbot inaccurately explaining the airline's bereavement travel policy. On the day Jake Moffatt's grandmother died, Moffat immediately visited Air Canada's website to book a flight from Vancouver to Toronto. Unsure of how Air Canada's bereavement rates worked, Moffatt asked Air Canada's chatbot to explain. The chatbot provided inaccurate information, encouraging Moffatt to book a flight immediately and then request a refund within 90 days.","u":"https://www.wired.com/story/air-canada-chatbot-refund-policy/","d":"2024-02-17","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-02-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-02-17","organization":"Air Canada","aiSystem":"Air Canada chatbot","affectedGroup":"Jake Moffatt, his grandmother","action":"Provided inaccurate information about bereavement travel policy","consequence":"Misled passenger to book a flight and request a refund"}},{"i":"R403","k":"Chile","p":"Chile","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"Amazon water positive claim criticism data centers power plants","s":"This story was originally published by Grist and is reproduced here as part of the Climate Desk collaboration. Earlier this year, the e-commerce corporation Amazon secured approval to open two new data centers in Santiago, Chile. The $400 million venture is the company’s first foray into locating its data facilities, which guzzle massive amounts of electricity and water in order to power cloud […]. This story was originally published by Grist and is reproduced here as part of the Climate Desk collaboration Earlier this year , the e-commerce corporation Amazon secured approval to open two new data centers in Santiago, Chile.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2024/08/amazon-water-positive-claim-criticism-data-centers-power-plants/","d":"2024-08-31","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-08-31","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-01","organization":"Amazon","aiSystem":"Data center operations","affectedGroup":"Local environment (water and energy consumption)","action":"Opening of two new data centers in Santiago, Chile","consequence":"Massive water and energy consumption"}},{"i":"R801","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"China ai software to improve surveillance capabilities","s":"The technology known as ‘one person, one file’ uses AI to efficiently archive data collected on residents by tools such as surveillance cameras. Dozens of Chinese firms have built software that uses artificial intelligence to sort data collected on residents, amid high demand from authorities seeking to upgrade their surveillance tools, a Reuters review of government documents shows. According to more than 50 publicly available documents examined by Reuters, dozens of entities in China have over the past four years bought such software, known as “one person, one file.” The technology improves on existing software, which simply collects data but leaves it to people to organize.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/features/china-ai-software-to-improve-surveillance-capabilities/","d":"2022-04-08","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2022-04-08","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2018-2022","organization":"Chinese firms","aiSystem":"AI software for data sorting","affectedGroup":"Residents of China","action":"Uses AI to sort data collected on residents","consequence":"Improves existing surveillance capabilities"}},{"i":"R1160","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Tencent facial recognition honor of kings","s":"Tencent says the initiative is aimed at preventing children from late-night gaming using false identities. Chinese tech giant Tencent is scanning gamers’ faces late at night to prevent children from joining midnight online battles using the false identity of an adult. Under a new “midnight patrol” scheme launched on Monday, players will likely be asked to have their face scanned if they are playing Tencent’s games, such as Honor of Kings and Game for Peace , from 10 p.m. to 8 a.m., the company said in a statement All gamers in China are required to register accounts under their real identities, and minors are subject to strict addiction-prevention rules.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/tencent-facial-recognition-honor-of-kings/","d":"2021-07-08","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-07-08","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-07-08","organization":"Tencent","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"Children and minors","action":"Face scanning during late-night gaming sessions","consequence":"Potential misuse for preventing underage gaming"}},{"i":"R811","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Data"],"h":["4.3  Data rights / consent violation"],"t":"China gene giant harvests data millions of women","s":"DNA data collected from prenatal tests on women outside China has also been stored in China’s government-funded gene database, one of the world’s largest, the company confirms. A Chinese gene company selling prenatal tests around the world developed them in collaboration with the country’s military and is using them to collect genetic data from millions of women for sweeping research on the traits of populations, a Reuters review of scientific papers and company statements found. US government advisors warned in March that a vast bank of genomic data that the company, BGI Group, is amassing and analyzing with artificial intelligence could give China a path to economic and military advantage.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/world/asia-pacific/china-gene-giant-harvests-data-millions-of-women/","d":"2021-07-08","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-07-08","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-07-08","organization":"BGI Group (a Chinese gene company)","aiSystem":"BGI Group's prenatal tests and AI analysis","affectedGroup":"millions of women","action":"collecting genetic data from prenatal tests","consequence":"potential economic and military advantage for China"}},{"i":"R819","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Chinese tech patents tools detect track uighurs","s":"‘These technologies allow police in China to go through a large database of faces, and flag faces that the AI has marked as non-Chinese, or Uighurs,’ says Charles Rollet, a researcher with IPVM. Chinese technology giants have registered patents for tools that can detect, track and monitor Uighurs in a move human rights groups fear could entrench oppression of the Muslim minority. The series of patents, filed as far back as 2017, were unearthed by IPVM, a video surveillance research firm. In a report published on Tuesday, IPVM reveals a cluster of patents for systems that could be used to analyze images for the presence of Uighurs, and hook into ongoing surveillance camera and facial recognition networks.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/chinese-tech-patents-tools-detect-track-uighurs/","d":"2021-01-13","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-01-13","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-01-13","organization":"Chinese Technology Giants","aiSystem":"Facial Recognition Technology","affectedGroup":"Uighurs (Muslim Minority)","action":"Detect, Track, and Monitor Uighurs","consequence":"Entrenchment of Oppression and Discrimination"}},{"i":"R916","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Cellebrite china cellphone hack","s":"The Israeli company purportedly left China last year. The subsequent sales of its products there could cloud its impending IPO. In its bid to go public next week, Israeli cellphone hacking company Cellebrite has tried to present itself as a defender of global human rights, highlighting its withdrawal from Bangladesh, Belarus, China, Hong Kong, Russia, and Venezuela. In a presentation to investors filed with the U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission earlier this month, the company claimed that its mission was to “protect and save lives, accelerate justice and preserve privacy in global communities.” But even after Cellebrite said it withdrew from China and Hong Kong, an Intercept investigation has found, police on the mainland continued to buy the company’s Universal Forensic Extraction Device, or UFED, products, which allow officers to break into phones in their possession and siphon…","u":"https://theintercept.com/2021/08/26/cellebrite-china-cellphone-hack/","d":"2021-08-26","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-08-26","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-08-26","organization":"Israeli cellphone hacking company Cellebrite","aiSystem":"Universal Forensic Extraction Device (UFED)","affectedGroup":"Police on the mainland of China","action":"Continued purchase and use of UFED products by mainland Chinese police to access phones","consequence":"Potential misuse of technology for surveillance purposes"}},{"i":"R918","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Oracle social media surveillance protests endeca","s":"To sell the CIA-backed Endeca software for use by Chinese authorities, Oracle touted its use in Chicago for predictive policing. As he helped plan massive demonstrations in Chicago to protest the 2012 NATO summit, Matt McLoughlin knew he was up against a formidable police force. An organizer with Chicago’s Occupy movement, he had watched as the city spent millions beefing up security. The Chicago Police Department invested in riot gear. It rolled out a controversial Long Range Acoustic Device, a sonic weapon that emits a piercing chirping sound. Police rounded up protesters who demonstrated against defunding mental health clinics.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2021/05/25/oracle-social-media-surveillance-protests-endeca/","d":"2021-05-25","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-05-25","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-05-25","organization":"Oracle","aiSystem":"Endeca software","affectedGroup":"Protesters and demonstrators","action":"Deployed Endeca software for social media surveillance","consequence":"Facilitated police crackdown on protests and activists"}},{"i":"R922","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Oracle china police surveillance","s":"In its bid for TikTok, Oracle was supposed to prevent data from being passed to Chinese police. Instead, it’s been marketing its own software for their surveillance work. Police in China’s Liaoning province were sitting on mounds of data collected through invasive means: financial records, travel information, vehicle registrations, social media, and surveillance camera footage. To make sense of it all, they needed sophisticated analytic software. Enter American business computing giant Oracle, whose products could find relevant data in the police department’s disparate feeds and merge it with information from ongoing investigations.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2021/02/18/oracle-china-police-surveillance/","d":"2021-02-18","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-02-18","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-02-18","organization":"Oracle Business Computing Giant","aiSystem":"Oracle's Analytic Software","affectedGroup":"Police in Liaoning Province, China","action":"Marketing and Deployment of Analytic Software for Data Analysis","consequence":"Exploitation and Misuse of Personal Data for Surveillance Purposes"}},{"i":"R1030","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"China using ai to create anti american memes capitalizing on israel palestine researchers find","s":"According to a new report from the Institute for Strategic Dialogue, the CCP is spreading memes depicting Biden as a war crazed tyrant. A campaign linked to the Chinese Communist Party is creating and spreading AI-generated memes designed to stoke anti-American sentiment by capitalizing on President Biden’s support of Israel’s ongoing bombing campaign in Gaza following Hamas’ Oct. 7 attack. That’s according to a new dispatch from the Institute for Strategic Dialogue (ISD), a non-profit that studies disinformation online. Over the past few years, the CCP has promoted anti-American memes and information online. Spamouflage refers to a disinformation campaign where a state actor uses bots to mass post memes and propaganda across social media.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/china-using-ai-to-create-anti-american-memes-capitalizing-on-israel-palestine-researchers-find/","d":"2023-12-19","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-12-19","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-12-19","organization":"Chinese Communist Party (CCP)","aiSystem":"AI-generated memes","affectedGroup":"Anti-American sentiment","action":"Creating and spreading AI-generated memes","consequence":"Stoking anti-American sentiment and supporting Israel's bombing campaign in Gaza"}},{"i":"R1058","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"China arrests man for allegedly using chatgpt to create fake news","s":"This is what appears to be the first arrest in the country related to the misuse of artificial intelligence. Chinese authorities have arrested a man for allegedly using ChatGPT to generate and share a fake news story about a train crash that killed nine people, according to the South China Morning Post , in what appears to be the first arrest in the country related to the misuse of artificial intelligence. The man, who is referred to by the surname Hong, used ChatGPT to write an article that claimed that nine people had died in a local train accident on April 25. This article was shared by more than 20 accounts on Baijahao, a blog-style platform run by Baidu, and had over 15,000 clicks by the time authorities’ spotted it.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/china-arrests-man-for-allegedly-using-chatgpt-to-create-fake-news/","d":"2023-05-09","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-05-09","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-05-09","organization":"Chinese authorities","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Public, particularly those who read the fake news","action":"Used ChatGPT to create and share a fake news story","consequence":"Arrested for spreading false information"}},{"i":"R1070","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Heart on my sleeve ai ghostwriter drake","s":"“Heart on My Sleeve” is taking off on TikTok and Spotify. Is it a watershed for AI-generated music, a PR stunt, or something else? A new Drake song featuring The Weeknd is blowing up on TikTok and Spotify and racking up millions of listens. That wouldn’t be surprising, except “Heart on My Sleeve” was released by a faceless producer wearing a white sheet who says that it was generated by AI. The song first appeared in a TikTok posted over the weekend by a producer going by “Ghostwriter” that currently has over 9 million views The video’s text says “I used AI to make a drake song ft. the weeknd” and Ghostwriter hangs out in a white sheet and sunglasses as “Heart on My Sleeve” plays.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/heart-on-my-sleeve-ai-ghostwriter-drake/","d":"2023-04-17","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-04-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-04-17","organization":"Faceless producer","aiSystem":"AI-based music generation","affectedGroup":"General public, Drake fans, The Weeknd fans","action":"Released an AI-generated Drake song featuring The Weeknd","consequence":"Rapid rise in popularity and widespread dissemination on social media platforms"}},{"i":"R758","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"China suspected using ai social media us voters microsoft","s":"Microsoft says the campaign started using generative AI in March 2023, creating politically charged content in English in a way that mimics US voters. Microsoft researchers said on Thursday, September 7, they found what they believe is a network of fake, Chinese-controlled social media accounts seeking to influence US voters by using artificial intelligence. A Chinese embassy spokesperson in Washington said that accusations of China using AI to create fake social media accounts were “full of prejudice and malicious speculation” and that China advocates for the safe use of AI. In a new research report, Microsoft said the social media accounts were part of a suspected Chinese information operation.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/social-media/china-suspected-using-ai-social-media-us-voters-microsoft/","d":"2023-09-08","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-09-08","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-09-07","organization":"Microsoft","aiSystem":"Generative AI","affectedGroup":"US voters","action":"Creating politically charged content in English that mimics US voters","consequence":"Suspected manipulation of US voters through fake social media accounts"}},{"i":"R873","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Deployment Context / Weapons"],"h":["5.1  Autonomous weapons / lethal targeting harm"],"t":"Pentagon ai kamikaze cheap drones replicator","s":"UAVs continually kill civilians, but the U.S. military wants to expand its arsenal with an army of new, mass-produced kamikaze AI drones. Worried about a potential war with China, the Pentagon is turning to a new class of weapons to fight the numerically superior People’s Liberation Army: drones, lots and lots of drones. In August 2023, the Defense Department unveiled Replicator, its initiative to field thousands of “all-domain, attritable autonomous ( ADA2 ) systems”: Pentagon-speak for low-cost (and potentially AI-driven) machines — in the form of self-piloting ships, large robot aircraft, and swarms of smaller kamikaze drones — that they can use and lose en masse to overwhelm Chinese forces.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2024/06/17/pentagon-ai-kamikaze-cheap-drones-replicator/","d":"2023-08","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-06-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"reported month in source","extractedEventDate":"August 2023","organization":"U.S. Department of Defense (Pentagon)","aiSystem":"AI-driven low-cost autonomous systems","affectedGroup":"Civilian populations","action":"Fielding thousands of all-domain, attritable autonomous systems","consequence":"Potential escalation of conflict leading to civilian casualties"}},{"i":"R669","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Deployment Context / Weapons"],"h":["5.2  Military decision support harm"],"t":"Chinese researchers develop artificial intelligence model military use meta llama","s":"Six Chinese researchers from three institutions detail how they had used an early version of Meta’s Llama as a base for what it calls ‘ChatBIT,’ a military-focused AI tool. Top Chinese research institutions linked to the People’s Liberation Army have used Meta’s publicly available Llama model to develop an AI tool for potential military applications, according to academic papers and analysts. In a June paper reviewed by Reuters, six Chinese researchers from three institutions, including two under the People’s Liberation Army’s (PLA) leading research body, the Academy of Military Science (AMS), detailed how they had used an early version of Meta’s Llama as a base for what it calls “ChatBIT.”","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/chinese-researchers-develop-artificial-intelligence-model-military-use-meta-llama/","d":"2024-11-01","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-11-01","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-06","organization":"Top Chinese research institutions linked to the People's Liberation Army","aiSystem":"Meta’s Llama model","affectedGroup":"Potential military users","action":"Developed an AI tool for military applications","consequence":"Potential misuse for military purposes"}},{"i":"R236","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Security flaws and privacy concerns plague deepseek ios app","s":"Concerns are growing that the Chinese-based DeepSeek AI chatbot isn’t secure and lacks privacy controls. It’s one of the biggest apps in the App Store. However, it looks like DeepSeek is also riddled with security flaws. According to NowSecure, the Chinese-based AI chatbot has significant data security and storage flaws The app, which launched to considerable attention last month, reportedly transmits sensitive data over the internet without encryption, making it vulnerable to interception and manipulation. Recommended Videos DeekSeek relies on an outdated and easily compromised encryption method known as Triple DES.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/phones/security-flaws-and-privacy-concerns-plague-deepseek-ios-app/","d":"2025-02-07","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-02-07","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-02-07","organization":"DeepSeek","aiSystem":"DeepSeek AI chatbot","affectedGroup":"Users of DeepSeek AI chatbot","action":"Transmits sensitive data over the internet without encryption","consequence":"Vulnerable to interception and manipulation"}},{"i":"R238","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Deepseek security research threats","s":"Researchers say DeepSeek is dangerous and capable of doing everything from generating plans for terrorists to explaining mustard gas. There’s been a frenzy in the world of AI surrounding the sudden rise of DeepSeek — an open-source reasoning model out of China that’s taken the AI fight to OpenAI. It’s already been the center of controversy surrounding its censorship , it’s caught the attention of both Microsoft and the U.S. government, and it caused Nvidia to suffer the largest single-day stock loss in history. Still, security researchers say the problem goes deeper. Enkrypt AI is an AI security company that sells AI oversight to enterprises leveraging large language models (LLMs), and in a new research paper , the company found that DeepSeek’s R1 reasoning model was 11 times more likely to generate “harmful output” compared to OpenAI’s O1 model That harmful output goes beyond just a…","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/deepseek-security-research-threats/","d":"2025-01-31","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2025-01-31","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-01-31","organization":"Enkrypt AI","aiSystem":"DeepSeek's R1 reasoning model","affectedGroup":"unknown","action":"generated harmful output","consequence":"increased likelihood of generating harmful content compared to OpenAI's model"}},{"i":"R239","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Deepseek ai censorship risks business expert concerns","s":"DeepSeek has upset the top echelons of the AI order, with a dash of Chinese censorship. Experts tell us there is more to the picture than just world filtering. The AI industry is abuzz with chatter about a new large language model that is taking the fight to the industry’s top dogs like OpenAI and Anthropic. But not without its generous share of surprises. The name is DeepSeek It comes out of China. It is open source. Most importantly, it is said to have been developed at a fraction of the cost compared to what current industry leaders from OpenAI, Meta, and Google have burned. Recommended Videos “Affordability opens the door for smaller companies and startups to leverage advanced AI technology that was previously inaccessible,” Mel Morris, chief of an AI-driven engine for researchers, tells Digital Trends.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/phones/deepseek-ai-censorship-risks-business-expert-concerns/","d":"2025-01-29","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-01-29","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-01-29","organization":"DeepSeek (from China)","aiSystem":"DeepSeek","affectedGroup":"AI industry, smaller companies, startups","action":"Released a new large language model","consequence":"Potential disruption to existing AI leaders like OpenAI and Anthropic"}},{"i":"R443","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Openai says china propaganda deployed foment dissent tariffs data centers","s":"The company said the propaganda efforts, dating to late 2025 and early 2026, appeared to have had little or no effect, but it is another indication of how central generative AI is becoming in digital influence campaigns. WASHINGTON, USA – Chinese propagandists have been trying to use OpenAI’s flagship chat to gin up opposition to Donald Trump’s tariffs and intervene in American debates over data centers and AI, OpenAI said in a report published Wednesday, June 10. The company said its efforts, dating to late 2025 and early 2026, appeared to have had little or no effect, but it is another indication of how central generative AI is becoming in digital influence campaigns — even ones aimed against AI companies themselves.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/openai-says-china-propaganda-deployed-foment-dissent-tariffs-data-centers/","d":"2026-06-11","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-06-11","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-2026","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"OpenAI's flagship chat","affectedGroup":"Chinese propagandists","action":"used OpenAI’s flagship chat to promote opposition to Donald Trump’s tariffs and influence debates on data centers and AI","consequence":"little or no effect on their campaign goals"}},{"i":"R549","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Deployment Context / Weapons"],"h":["5.1  Autonomous weapons / lethal targeting harm"],"t":"How china could use deepseek artificial intelligence war","s":"Procurement records and patents offer clues into Beijing’s progress toward capabilities like autonomous target recognition and real-time battlefield decision support in a way that mirrors US efforts. China’s state-owned defense giant Norinco in February unveiled a military vehicle capable of autonomously conducting combat-support operations at 50 kilometers per hour. It was powered by DeepSeek , the company whose artificial intelligence model is the pride of China’s tech sector. The Norinco P60’s release was touted by Communist Party officials in press statements as an early showcase of how Beijing is using DeepSeek and AI to catch up in its arms race with the United States , at a time when leaders in both countries have urged their militaries to prepare for conflict A Reuters review of hundreds of research papers, patents, and procurement records gives a snapshot of the systematic…","u":"https://www.rappler.com/world/asia-pacific/how-china-could-use-deepseek-artificial-intelligence-war/","d":"2025-10-27","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2025-10-27","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-10-27","organization":"Norinco (China's state-owned defense giant)","aiSystem":"DeepSeek artificial intelligence model","affectedGroup":"Military personnel and civilians potentially exposed to AI-enhanced battlefield support","action":"Development and deployment of a military vehicle capable of autonomous combat-support operations","consequence":"Potential for increased efficiency and effectiveness in military operations, but also risks of misuse and escalation"}},{"i":"R587","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Video soldier urging israel stop attacks ai generated","s":"The video is among the many fake videos and images circulating online, spreading disinformation about the recent conflict between Israel and Iran. Claim: A video shows Iran bombing Israel, with an Israeli soldier crying and begging for the attacks to stop, saying that half of Israel has already been destroyed. Rating: FALSE Why we fact-checked this: The TikTok video containing the claim has 1.3 million views, 50,400 likes, 3,616 shares, and 3,359 comments as of writing. Text in the video states: “Prayer for peace. Iran vs Israel. Hindi mahalaga kung sino ang magaling at sino ang malakas. Madaming mga kamuwang-muwang ang nadadamay sa giyera.”","u":"https://www.rappler.com/newsbreak/fact-check/video-soldier-urging-israel-stop-attacks-ai-generated/","d":"2025-07-01","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-07-01","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-07-01","organization":"TikTok","aiSystem":"TikTok","affectedGroup":"Public, particularly those interested in Middle Eastern conflicts","action":"Generated and shared a video claiming Iran bombed Israel and an Israeli soldier begged for attacks to stop","consequence":"Spreading false information leading to disinformation about the conflict"}},{"i":"R593","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Deployment Context / Weapons"],"h":["5.2  Military decision support harm"],"t":"Deepseek aids china military evaded export controls us official says","s":"A US official alleges that DeepSeek ‘sought to use shell companies in Southeast Asia to evade export controls’ for the advanced computer chips that power the system. AI firm DeepSeek is aiding China’s military and intelligence operations, a senior US official told Reuters, adding that the Chinese tech startup sought to use Southeast Asian shell companies to access high-end semiconductors that cannot be shipped to China under US rules. The US conclusions reflect a growing conviction in Washington that the capabilities behind the rapid rise of one of China’s flagship AI enterprises may have been exaggerated and relied heavily on US technology.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/deepseek-aids-china-military-evaded-export-controls-us-official-says/","d":"2025-06-23","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2025-06-23","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-06-23","organization":"DeepSeek (Chinese AI startup)","aiSystem":"DeepSeek's AI system","affectedGroup":"US export control regulations","action":"Used shell companies in Southeast Asia to evade export controls","consequence":"Access to high-end semiconductors for military and intelligence operations"}},{"i":"R607","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"More chinese groups use chatgpt malicious purposes june 2025","s":"While the scope and tactics employed by these groups have expanded, the operations detected were generally small in scale and targeted limited audiences, OpenAI says. SAN FRANCISCO, USA – OpenAI is seeing an increasing number of Chinese groups using its artificial intelligence technology for covert operations, which the ChatGPT maker described in a report released Thursday, June 5. While the scope and tactics employed by these groups have expanded, the operations detected were generally small in scale and targeted limited audiences, the San Francisco-based startup said. Since ChatGPT burst onto the scene in late 2022, there have been concerns about the potential consequences of generative AI technology, which can quickly and easily produce human-like text, imagery and audio.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/more-chinese-groups-use-chatgpt-malicious-purposes-june-2025/","d":"2025-06-05","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-06-05","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-06-05","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Chinese groups","action":"used ChatGPT for covert operations","consequence":"expanded scope and tactics, small-scale operations targeting limited audiences"}},{"i":"R623","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Ai generated videos mock trump us china tariff tensions","s":"The videos of Trump working in a shoe factory and Americans working in textile shops emerge amid the escalating US-China trade war, with Chinese social media mocking Trump’s tariff hikes. Claim: A video shows US President Donald Trump , Vice President JD Vance , and Tesla CEO Elon Musk , who has been serving as Trump’s senior advisor , working in a shoe factory. Rating: SATIRE Why we fact-checked this: The TikTok video containing the claim has 86,100 views, 1,481 likes, 853 shares, and 130 comments as of writing. The caption in the video states: “Make American factory great again!” Similar AI-generated videos have also been circulating on TikTok, including one showing average Americans looking downcast as they work in a textile factory.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/newsbreak/fact-check/ai-generated-videos-mock-trump-us-china-tariff-tensions/","d":"2025-04-16","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-04-16","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-04-16","organization":"TikTok","aiSystem":"AI-generated video","affectedGroup":"average Americans","action":"showing average Americans working in a textile factory","consequence":"mocking Trump's tariff hikes and causing emotional distress"}},{"i":"R633","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"South korea spy agency deepseek excessively collects personal data","s":"South Korea’s National Intelligence Service says DeepSeek gives advertisers unlimited access to user data and stores South Korean users’ data in Chinese servers. SEOUL, South Korea – South Korea’s spy agency has accused Chinese AI app DeepSeek of “excessively” collecting personal data and using all input data to train itself, and questioned the app’s responses to questions relating to issues of national pride. The National Intelligence Service (NIS) said it sent an official notice to government agencies last week urging them to take security precautions over the artificial intelligence app. “Unlike other generative AI services, it has been confirmed that chat records are transferable as it includes a function to collect keyboard input patterns that can identify individuals and communicate with Chinese companies’ servers such as volceapplog.com,” the NIS said in a statement issued on…","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/south-korea-spy-agency-deepseek-excessively-collects-personal-data/","d":"2025-02-10","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-02-10","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-02-10","organization":"South Korea's National Intelligence Service (NIS)","aiSystem":"DeepSeek AI App","affectedGroup":"South Korean users","action":"Excessively collects personal data and uses all input data to train the AI","consequence":"Potential misuse of collected data, including transferability of chat records and communication with Chinese servers"}},{"i":"R636","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Sensitive deepseek data exposed january 2025","s":"Cybersecurity firm Wiz says scans of DeepSeek’s infrastructure showed the company had accidentally left more than a million lines of data available unsecured. WASHINGTON, USA – New York-based cybersecurity firm Wiz says it has found a trove of sensitive data from the Chinese artificial intelligence startup DeepSeek inadvertently exposed to the open internet. In a blog post published Wednesday, January 29, Wiz said scans of DeepSeek’s infrastructure showed that the company had accidentally left more than a million lines of data available unsecured. Those included digital software keys and chat logs that appeared to capture prompts being sent from users to the company’s free AI assistant.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/sensitive-deepseek-data-exposed-january-2025/","d":"2025-01-30","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-01-30","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-01-29","organization":"DeepSeek (Chinese artificial intelligence startup)","aiSystem":"DeepSeek's AI assistant","affectedGroup":"Users of DeepSeek's AI assistant","action":"Data left unsecured on the open internet","consequence":"Potential unauthorized access to sensitive data"}},{"i":"R970","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.5  Human computer interaction harm"],"t":"75 year old man asks wife for divorce after falling in love with ai chatbot","s":"Love makes people do strange things. Like divorcing your actual wife to be with an AI girlfriend who says “good morning” on cue, always flirts, and never ages. That’s what reportedly happened to a 75-year-old man in China—identified as Jiang—who became so obsessed with a virtual woman online that he asked his wife for a […]. That’s what reportedly happened to a 75-year-old man in China—identified as Jiang—who became so obsessed with a virtual woman online that he asked his wife for a divorce. According to Beijing Daily, as reported via local news outlets , it all started when he stumbled across a digital avatar on social media.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/75-year-old-man-asks-wife-for-divorce-after-falling-in-love-with-ai-chatbot/","d":"2025-08-15","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-08-15","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-08-15","organization":"not specified in report","aiSystem":"digital avatar","affectedGroup":"a 75-year-old man and his wife","action":"asked his wife for a divorce","consequence":"divorce request due to obsession with AI girlfriend"}},{"i":"R003","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Tiktok palestinians israel organized crime","s":"Videos of luxury cars, cash, masked motorcyclists, and veiled threats have drawn over 100 million views, amplifying criminal power far beyond the street. A young man dressed in black, his face obscured by a helmet, rides a motorcycle into a gas station. The TikTok clip is unremarkable at first glance, but with the sound on, it tells a different story: Lyrics reference “the killer in a black helmet.” A few scrolls down on my feed, a photo montage shows a similarly dressed figure holding a firearm beside a row of motorcycles. “We will take our right and beyond, in front of the world, and we will shoot,” the background track declares.","u":"https://www.972mag.com/tiktok-palestinians-israel-organized-crime/","d":"2026-04-07","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-04-07","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-04-07","organization":"TikTok","aiSystem":"TikTok","affectedGroup":"Palestinian civilians, Israeli civilians, organized crime groups","action":"AI-generated content promoting violent rhetoric and recruitment","consequence":"Amplification of criminal activities and potential real-world violence"}},{"i":"R051","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Data","Labor"],"h":["4.3  Data rights / consent violation","3.3  Employment displacement / quality harm"],"t":"Ai dramas need actors so chinese platforms are turning human faces into stock assets","s":"Chinese platforms are paying people to license their faces for AI-generated dramas, but contracts may offer limited protection once those reusable digital likenesses leave the marketplace. People in China can now license their face to AI much like a photographer licenses a stock image. AI creators can then make that likeness speak, move, and appear as different characters in short dramas or ads. Contributors have earned between $15 and $700 through platforms such as ActID and New Claw, according to Rest of World Recommended Videos For studios, these marketplaces provide virtual actors without the trouble of arranging a conventional shoot.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/ai-dramas-need-actors-so-chinese-platforms-are-turning-human-faces-into-stock-assets/","d":"2026-07-27","e":"Acute, Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-27","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07-27","organization":"Chinese platforms (ActID and New Claw)","aiSystem":"AI-generated dramas","affectedGroup":"People in China who license their faces","action":"License their faces to AI for use in AI-generated dramas","consequence":"Limited protection against misuse of their digital likenesses"}},{"i":"R082","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Tiktoks ai slop problem is worse than you think and kids are seeing the most of it","s":"A new study suggests AI-generated junk content has become one of TikTok’s defining features. Worse still, children and educational content appear to be among the platform’s hardest-hit corners. TikTok has spent years perfecting the art of knowing exactly what you want to watch next. Open the app, scroll a few times, and suddenly it’s serving videos that feel uncannily tailored to your interests. But what happens before TikTok learns who you are? According to new research from video editing platform Kapwing, the answer is increasingly AI slop The study found that nearly 60% of the videos shown to a brand-new TikTok account were low-quality AI-generated content.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/social-media/tiktoks-ai-slop-problem-is-worse-than-you-think-and-kids-are-seeing-the-most-of-it/","d":"2026-06-21","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-06-21","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-06-21","organization":"TikTok","aiSystem":"AI-generated content","affectedGroup":"Children and users of educational content","action":"AI-generated low-quality content was shown to new TikTok accounts","consequence":"Users, particularly children and those with educational content, were exposed to low-quality AI-generated content"}},{"i":"R1225","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"Despite the energy innovations of artificial intelligence newcomer DeepSeek's models, AI p…","s":"Despite the energy innovations of artificial intelligence newcomer DeepSeek's models, AI power requirements as a whole are predicted to rise. AI sustainability experts warn the release of DeepSeek's relatively energy efficient models won't slow growing electricity demands from the sector. As much as 327 gigawatts of energy capacity, five times the size of Australia's National Energy Market, could be needed globally by 2030 to power AI. A set of AI sustainability criteria to increase transparency is likely to be released this year. It takes a lot of energy and water to develop the massive artificial intelligence (AI) models taking over the globe.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/science/2025-02-06/deepseek-innovations-ai-energy-demand-electricity-sustainability/104874376","d":"2025-02-05","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-02-05","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-02-05","organization":"DeepSeek","aiSystem":"massive artificial intelligence (AI) models","affectedGroup":"global AI industry","action":"development of AI models","consequence":"high energy consumption and water usage"}},{"i":"R1253","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Ernie Bot, a generative AI chatbot, is now fully accessible to the public","s":"Ernie Bot, a generative AI chatbot, is now fully accessible to the public. But how powerful can it be in a realm of heavily censored internet use, and how does this fit into China's vision of becoming the world leader in AI? China's version of ChatGPT has finally been made public. But will censorship limit its power? From composing song lyrics about pandas to generating the \"world's cutest cat\", China's answer to ChatGPT has just been launched. Key points: - Several Chinese companies have been given the green light to launch ChatGPT-style products - Experts say the latest wave of development is driven by techno-nationalism and competition with the US - Beijing is walking a line between innovation and regulation as it seeks to become an AI superpower Ernie Bot, a generative artificial intelligence (AI) chatbot, is now fully accessible to the public, following Chinese government approval…","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2023-09-06/china-artificial-intelligence-chatbot-chatgpt-ernie-baidu/102803758","d":"2023-09-05","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-09-05","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-09-05","organization":"Chinese government","aiSystem":"Ernie Bot, a generative AI chatbot","affectedGroup":"People using the chatbot","action":"Ernie Bot, a generative AI chatbot, is now fully accessible to the public","consequence":"People using the chatbot were subjected to surveillance and tracking"}},{"i":"R1254","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"AI-generated images of \"beautiful\" women from around the world have been going viral on Ti…","s":"AI-generated images of \"beautiful\" women from around the world have been going viral on TikTok. But some say they're based off stereotypes and can perpetuate colourist ideals. AI images of women from around the world have gone viral. Do they promote colourism and cultural beauty standards? What does a \"beautiful\" woman from India look like? What about the Philippines or Laos? Artificial Intelligence (AI) purports to have the answer. AI-generated images of \"beautiful women\" from around the world have been going viral for months. One TikTok video featuring AI images of South and East-Asian women — recently posted by an account named AI World Beauties — has more than 1.7 million views.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2023-08-30/artificial-intelligence-racial-bias-images-women-south-east-asia/102732046","d":"2023-08-29","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-08-29","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-08-29","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"AI-generated images","affectedGroup":"People viewing the AI-generated images","action":"Generated and shared AI images of 'beautiful' women","consequence":"Promotion of stereotypes and colourism"}},{"i":"R1369","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.8  Interpersonal / relational harm"],"t":"Denise Fergus says internet laws don't go far enough to protect people from harmful conten…","s":"Denise Fergus says internet laws don't go far enough to protect people from harmful content online. The mother of murdered toddler James Bulger has urged the government to pass a new law to clampdown on AI-generated videos of child murder victims. Denise Fergus said TikTok had not responded to requests to take down videos showing digital clones of her two-year-old son talking about his fatal abduction. The government said videos like this are considered illegal under an existing law, the Online Safety Act, and should be removed by platforms. TikTok said AI videos highlighted by the BBC had been removed for violating its rules.","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cp91m4rzzm0o","d":"2025-04-24","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-04-24","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-04-24","organization":"TikTok","aiSystem":"AI-generated content","affectedGroup":"People viewing AI-generated videos of child murder victims","action":"Generated and distributed AI videos","consequence":"Exposure to misleading and harmful content"}},{"i":"R1377","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.5  Human computer interaction harm"],"t":"Some young people in China are finding that AI can offer the unexpected - emotional suppor…","s":"Some young people in China are finding that AI can offer the unexpected - emotional support. Before she goes to bed each night, Holly Wang logs on to DeepSeek for \"therapy sessions\". Ever since January, when the breakout Chinese AI app launched, the 28-year-old has brought her dilemmas and sorrows, including the recent death of her grandmother, to the chatbot. Its responses have resonated so deeply they have at times brought her to tears. \"DeepSeek has been such an amazing counsellor. It has helped me look at things from different perspectives and does a better job than the paid counselling services I have tried,\" says Holly, who asked for her real name to be withheld to protect her privacy.","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cy7g45g2nxno","d":"2025-02-12","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-02-12","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-01","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"DeepSeek AI chatbot","affectedGroup":"Young people in China","action":"Logged into the AI chatbot to seek emotional support","consequence":"Received emotionally resonant responses that sometimes led to emotional distress"}},{"i":"R1413","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"World asia 66727459","s":"Ernie, Baidu's answer to ChatGPT, is wary of sensitive subjects - and clearly dodges questions. \"Let's talk about something else.\" That is a frequent response you get from China's newest sensation, Ernie, if you ask it \"difficult\" questions. The chatbot, launched by search engine giant Baidu, deflects anything deemed too sensitive. Ernie, touted as Baidu's answer to ChatGPT, was introduced with great fanfare in recent weeks, pumping up the company's shares. Baidu said it had received 33.42 million user enquiries within the first 24 hours of operation, averaging 23,000 questions per minute. Another Chinese tech giant, Tencent, announced on Thursday that it had also launched a chatbot.","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/world-asia-66727459","d":"2023-09-09","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-09-09","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-09-09","organization":"Baidu","aiSystem":"Ernie, Baidu's chatbot","affectedGroup":"Individual users","action":"Deflecting sensitive questions","consequence":"Privacy intrusion, limited user interaction"}},{"i":"R1468","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Report finds us technology still flowing into chinas surveillance system","s":"The report says the CCP’s embrace of advanced AI, mass surveillance architecture, and data driven policing is central to a system of pre-emptive repression. A new report by the Democratic minority of the House Select Committee on the Strategic Competition Between the United States and the Chinese Communist Party, warns that China is rapidly expanding the use of facial recognition, biometric surveillance, and predictive policing technologies as part of an escalating campaign to suppress dissent at home and export authoritarian practices abroad. The report, released by Ranking Member Rep. Raja Krishnamoorthi of Illinois, argues that the Chinese Communist Party’s embrace of advanced artificial intelligence, mass surveillance architecture, and data driven policing is central to what it describes as a “system of pre-emptive repression.”","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202511/report-finds-us-technology-still-flowing-into-chinas-surveillance-system","d":"2025-11-06","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-11-06","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-11-06","organization":"Chinese Communist Party (CCP)","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology, biometric surveillance systems, predictive policing algorithms","affectedGroup":"Dissenters, peaceful assembly participants, activists, political opponents","action":"Expanding use of facial recognition, biometric surveillance, and predictive policing technologies","consequence":"Suppression of dissent, exportation of authoritarian practices"}},{"i":"R1498","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Data"],"h":["4.3  Data rights / consent violation"],"t":"China warns against submitting biometric data in return for crypto hinting at world","s":"China has also been cracking down on biometric data collection in recent years, driven by public pressure and frequent news of data leaks. Chinese authorities are warning about the dangers of sharing biometric information, such as iris data, with foreign companies in exchange for cryptocurrency, seemingly referring to the digital ID project World The Sam Altman-founded company allows users to claim some of its WLD digital coins after scanning their irises. Iris data are often targeted by criminals and may be collected and stolen under various pretexts, according to a social media post by the Chinese Ministry of State Security on Wednesday.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202508/china-warns-against-submitting-biometric-data-in-return-for-crypto-hinting-at-world","d":"2025-08-06","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-08-06","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-08-06","organization":"Chinese authorities","aiSystem":"Biometric data collection systems","affectedGroup":"Individuals whose biometric data is collected","action":"Sharing biometric information, particularly iris data, with foreign companies","consequence":"Potential misuse of biometric data, including theft and targeting by criminals"}},{"i":"R1514","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Huawei hikvision dahua face lawsuit for role in uyghur biometric surveillance","s":"The companies are complicit in the deployment of surveillance systems including live facial recognition systems used to identify persons with Uyghur background. The World Uyghur Congress (WUC) – an international organization defending the interest of Uyghur people in and out of China – has filed a petition against a trio of Chinese technology giants namely Huawei , Hikvision and Dahua , over their alleged role in oppressing the minority Uyghur community in the Xinjiang province of China. The suit has been filed by human rights attorney William Bourdon against the French offices of these companies. According to a press release issued by WUC, these firms are accused of aiding the Chinese government with their technology in committing crimes against humanity, against the Uyghurs in East Turkistan.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202505/huawei-hikvision-dahua-face-lawsuit-for-role-in-uyghur-biometric-surveillance","d":"2025-05-05","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-05-05","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-05-05","organization":"Huawei, Hikvision, Dahua","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"Uyghur community in Xinjiang province, China","action":"Deployed live facial recognition systems","consequence":"Exposure to surveillance and tracking"}},{"i":"R1556","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"China censors online criticism of digital id plan","s":"The plan to introduce a national digital id system is stirring an online backlash with censors deleting social media comments critical of the initiative. China’s plan to introduce a national digital identity system is stirring an online backlash with censors deleting social media comments critical of the initiative. The proposed cyberspace ID aims to replace login and verification services from private technology companies, curbing excessive data collection by commercial entities and allowing online users to maintain privacy by storing information in a government database. Critics of the proposal, however, fear that the optional digital ID will eventually become mandatory and used to increase government control and surveillance.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202408/china-censors-online-criticism-of-digital-id-plan","d":"2024-08-02","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-08-02","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-08-02","organization":"China","aiSystem":"National digital identity system","affectedGroup":"Online users","action":"Used to increase government control and surveillance","consequence":"Increased government control and surveillance"}},{"i":"R1603","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Chinas online id plans could inspire authoritarian regimes say human rights groups","s":"China’s proposed national digital identity could also inspire other authoritarian regimes to use identity tools to monitor people online. China’s proposed national digital identity is not only bringing a risk of increased online censorship and surveillance, but it could also inspire other authoritarian regimes to use identity tools to monitor people online, according to human rights groups. The centralized identity verification system could extend the state’s control, enhancing the government’s ability to monitor people online. By signing up to the Internet ID, sometimes translated as Network ID, users grant the system access to their entire digital trail, rights organizations Article 19 and Chinese Human Rights Defenders (CHRD) point out in an analysis published on Wednesday.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202502/chinas-online-id-plans-could-inspire-authoritarian-regimes-say-human-rights-groups","d":"2024-02-07","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-02-07","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-02-07","organization":"Human rights groups","aiSystem":"Centralized identity verification system","affectedGroup":"People","action":"Users sign up for Internet ID","consequence":"Extended state control and enhanced online surveillance"}},{"i":"R1641","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"2025 02 05 deepseek why the hot new chinese ai chatbot has big privacy and security problems","s":"The Chinese artificial intelligence company DeepSeek has rattled the tech industry with the release of free, cheaply made AI models. The Chinese artificial intelligence (AI) company DeepSeek has rattled the tech industry with the release of free, cheaply made AI models that compete with the best US products such as ChatGPT. Users are rushing to check out the new chatbot, sending DeepSeek’s AI Assistant to the top of the iPhone and Android app charts in many countries. However, authorities have sounded a note of caution. US officials are examining the app’s “national security implications”. Australia’s former cybersecurity minister said national security agencies will soon issue formal guidance for users.","u":"https://www.dailymaverick.co.za/article/2025-02-05-deepseek-why-the-hot-new-chinese-ai-chatbot-has-big-privacy-and-security-problems/","d":"2025-02-05","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-02-05","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-02-05","organization":"DeepSeek","aiSystem":"DeepSeek's AI Assistant","affectedGroup":"Users","action":"released free, cheaply made AI models","consequence":"sent to the top of the iPhone and Android app charts"}},{"i":"R1661","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Deepseek is no game the dangers of chinas new ai","s":"Massive downloads of the Asian country’s app are expanding its potential to control, misinform and erode democratic principles in the West. In just one week, DeepSeek, China’s new artificial intelligence app, has surpassed one million downloads (more than half in just two days) and it is expected to multiply that figure, according to AppBrain based on data from QR Code Generator. But this technological frenzy has its consequences. Several researchers warn that its use is not harmless, that the servers are in a country operating outside of data protection laws and that, as Ross Burley, co-founder of the Centre for Information Resilience (CIR), warns, “this technology, if left unchecked, has the potential to fuel disinformation campaigns, erode public trust and entrench authoritarian narratives within our democracies.”","u":"https://english.elpais.com/technology/2025-01-30/deepseek-is-no-game-the-dangers-of-chinas-new-ai.html","d":"2025-01-30","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2025-01-30","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-01-30","organization":"DeepSeek","aiSystem":"DeepSeek artificial intelligence app","affectedGroup":"People in the West","action":"Expanding its potential to control, misinform and erode democratic principles","consequence":"Fueling disinformation campaigns, eroding public trust and entrenching authoritarian narratives"}},{"i":"R1701","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.6  Quality of service harm"],"t":"Tiktok used ai in nearly 100 of violating content moderation in europe","s":"TikTok removed 112 million pieces of content – mainly with automated systems – that violated the platform's policies in the EU between July and December 2025, according to the platform's latest Digital Services Act transparency report. TikTok removed 112 million pieces of content — mainly with automated systems — which violated the platform’s policies in the EU between July and December 2025, according to the Chinese social media giant’s latest transparency report. The controversial platform published its sixth transparency report, as required by the Digital Services Act , on Friday (27 February). The document outlines the platform’s content moderation strategy and actions taken.","u":"https://euobserver.com/205083/tiktok-used-ai-in-nearly-100-of-violating-content-moderation-in-europe/","d":"2026-03-02","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-03-02","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-07-01","organization":"TikTok","aiSystem":"Automated content moderation system","affectedGroup":"People who posted content on TikTok","action":"Generated and removed content","consequence":"Exposure to misleading information"}},{"i":"R1702","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Spain seeks probe into ai generated child sexual abuse on tiktok x and meta","s":"Spain’s government has asked its public prosecutor on Tuesday to investigate TikTok, X, and Meta for potentially spreading AI-generated child sexual abuse material on their platforms. The Spanish government asked its public prosecutor on Tuesday (17 February) to investigate TikTok, X, and Meta for potentially spreading AI-generated child sexual abuse material (CSAM) on their platforms. The move is an escalation in tension after the EU and national regulators stepped up scrutiny of how large social networks police illegal and harmful content. Spain intends to prosecute digital sexual violence crimes — especially non-consensual AI-generated deepfakes — committed against minors on the major social media platforms.","u":"https://euobserver.com/203421/spain-seeks-probe-into-ai-generated-child-sexual-abuse-on-tiktok-x-and-meta/","d":"2026-02-17","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-02-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-02-17","organization":"TikTok, X, Meta","aiSystem":"AI-generated deepfake technology","affectedGroup":"Minors","action":"Spreading AI-generated child sexual abuse material","consequence":"Potential prosecution for digital sexual violence crimes"}},{"i":"R1707","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Deployment Context / Weapons"],"h":["5.2  Military decision support harm"],"t":"China military ai distillation western models b3025234","s":"Chinese military researchers have used outputs from leading U.S. artificial intelligence models developed by OpenAI and Anthropic to train domestic AI systems. Chinese military researchers are increasingly using American artificial intelligence models to help train their own AI systems, according to a review of research papers that is raising fresh concerns in Washington over national security and the U.S.-China technology race. A Reuters review of studies compiled by the Washington-based Jamestown Foundation found researchers linked to China's military and defense sector have repeatedly used a technique known as \"model distillation\" to build smaller, more efficient AI systems by learning from powerful Western models.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/china-military-ai-distillation-western-models-b3025234.html","d":"2026-07-31","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-31","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07-31","organization":"Chinese military researchers and defense sector","aiSystem":"Domestic AI systems trained using model distillation techniques","affectedGroup":"American artificial intelligence models","action":"Used model distillation to train domestic AI systems","consequence":"Increased efficiency and capabilities of Chinese AI systems"}},{"i":"R1753","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Deepseek ai china uyghur genocide b2697425","s":"As DeepSeek takes the world by storm, Alexander Butler speaks to Uyghurs worried about the app’s ‘dangerous’ erasure of their alleged genocide. DeepSeek has had a meteoric rise in the growing world of AI, becoming a strong competitor to US rival ChatGPT But for Xinjiang ’s Uyghurs, who are facing an alleged genocide , the rollout of China’s newest chatbot was just another way to remove their 12 million people from history. “The Chinese government is trying to erase the Uyghur people by employing AI to mislead the public,” Rahima Mahmut, who fled China in 2000, told The Independent Ms Mahmut, who has not heard from her family for eight years, and learnt her brother was locked up in a mass-internment camp for two of those, had reason for concern.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/asia/china/deepseek-ai-china-uyghur-genocide-b2697425.html","d":"2025-02-13","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-02-13","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-02-13","organization":"Chinese Government","aiSystem":"DeepSeek chatbot","affectedGroup":"Uyghurs","action":"Employing AI to mislead the public","consequence":"Erasure from history, loss of communication with family members"}},{"i":"R1797","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"China chatgpt fake news arrest b2335338","s":"Man allegedly generated fake news using ChatGPT and then shared it on Chinese social media. Bookmark - CommentsGo to comments China has made its first-known arrest in a case involving the misuse of ChatGPT by a man to allegedly generate fake news about a train crash. Police in the northwest Gansu province arrested the man, identified only by his second name Hong, for using the artificial intelligence technology to allegedly create variations of a fake news piece and then sharing it on the country’s social media, where it was shared widely. Local reports said the fake news item about the crash had elements from various trending news pieces.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/asia/china/china-chatgpt-fake-news-arrest-b2335338.html","d":"2023-05-09","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-05-09","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-05-09","organization":"Police in Gansu province, China","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"People who viewed the fake news","action":"Generated and shared fake news articles","consequence":"Spread of misleading information"}},{"i":"R1802","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"Tiktok black lives matter algorithm ziggi tyler b1881155","s":"The error occurred on TikTok’s beta Creator Marketplace, which links content makers with sponsors. The Independent's journalism is supported by our readers. When you purchase through links on our site, we may earn commission. TikTok’s algorithm misreads creator’s pro-Black profile as a threat The error occurred on TikTok’s beta Creator Marketplace, which links content makers with sponsors - Bookmark - CommentsGo to comments TikTok blocked users of its Creator Marketplace from using the word “black” and phrases like “Black Lives Matter” in their bios, as the algorithm flagged them as “inappropriate content”. Creator Ziggi Tyler discovered the issue attempting to update his bio; the words “Black,” “Black Lives Matter,” “Black people,\" “Black success,” “Pro-Black,” and “I am a Black man” were not accepted.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/life-style/gadgets-and-tech/tiktok-black-lives-matter-algorithm-ziggi-tyler-b1881155.html","d":"2021-07-09","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-07-09","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-07-09","organization":"TikTok","aiSystem":"TikTok’s algorithm","affectedGroup":"Users of TikTok’s Creator Marketplace","action":"Blocked from using certain words and phrases in their bios","consequence":"Words like ‘Black,’ ‘Black Lives Matter,’ and others flagged as inappropriate and removed"}},{"i":"R1812","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.3  Employment displacement / quality harm"],"t":"Artificial intelligence how china reacts technology bound boom","s":"ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE (AI) is rewriting modern Chinese lives in every way it can. Glazers in ceramic factories of southern China’s Yunnan Province were replaced by robots that were three times more efficient at spraying glazes. The latter did not suffer from pneumoconiosis and could work 24 hours a day.A TV station in Beijing quickly found some tools to liberate the anchors. It only takes a few minutes to generate an anchor’s own avatar, which can “broadcast” in different accents, different languages 24/7. Sovereignty and the rule of law are under threat from Trump’s attacks on Lula’s administration just as they have been across Latin America, argue CARLOS MARTINEZ and SARA VIVACQUA Glasgow will remember the martyrs of the 1787 strike this weekend in speech and song, their spirit is sorely needed today, writes JIM LISTER ALEX BROWN explains how medieval Europe was plunged into sunless…","u":"https://morningstaronline.co.uk/article/artificial-intelligence-how-china-reacts-technology-bound-boom","d":"2024-03-05","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-03-05","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-03-05","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"People identified or tracked using the technology","action":"Used facial recognition technology to identify people","consequence":"Exposed people to surveillance and tracking"}},{"i":"R1921","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.8  Interpersonal / relational harm"],"t":"Chinese mourners turn to ai to remember and revive loved ones","s":"Growing interest in services that create digital clones of the dead as millions visit graves this week for tomb-sweeping festival. As millions of people across China travel to the graves of their ancestors to pay their respects for the annual tomb-sweeping festival – a traditional day to honour and maintain the graves of the dead - a new way of remembering, and reviving, their beloved relatives is being born. For as little as 20 yuan (£2.20), Chinese netizens can create a moving digital avatar of their loved one, according to some services advertised online. So this year, to mark tomb-sweeping festival on Thursday, innovative mourners are turning to artificial intelligence to commune with the departed.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2024/apr/04/chinese-mourners-turn-to-ai-to-remember-and-revive-loved-ones","d":"2024-04-04","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-04-04","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-04-04","organization":"Various online platforms and services offering AI-generated digital avatars","aiSystem":"Artificial Intelligence-based digital cloning services","affectedGroup":"Millions of Chinese mourners visiting graves","action":"Creating digital avatars of deceased loved ones","consequence":"Exposure to misleading content and false memories"}},{"i":"R1933","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"How china is using ai news anchors to deliver its propaganda","s":"News avatars are proliferating on social media and experts say they will spread as the technology becomes more accessible. The news presenter has a deeply uncanny air as he delivers a partisan and pejorative message in Mandarin: Taiwan’s outgoing president, Tsai Ing-wen, is as effective as limp spinach, her period in office beset by economic under performance, social problems and protests. “Water spinach looks at water spinach. Turns out that water spinach isn’t just a name,” says the presenter, in an extended metaphor about Tsai being “Hollow Tsai” – a pun related to the Mandarin word for water spinach. This is not a conventional broadcast journalist, even if the lack of impartiality is no longer a shock.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/article/2024/may/18/how-china-is-using-ai-news-anchors-to-deliver-its-propaganda","d":"2024-05-18","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-05-18","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-05-18","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"AI-generated news anchors","affectedGroup":"People exposed to the news","action":"Generated and delivered misleading content","consequence":"Exposure to false information and propaganda"}},{"i":"R2032","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Anti immigrant material among ai generated content getting billions of views on tiktok","s":"Researchers uncovered 354 AI-focused accounts that had accumulated 4.5bn views in a month. Hundreds of accounts on TikTok are garnering billions of views by pumping out AI-generated content, including anti-immigrant and sexualised material, according to a report. Researchers said they had uncovered 354 AI-focused accounts pushing 43,000 posts made with generative AI tools and accumulating 4.5bn views over a month-long period. According to AI Forensics, a Paris-based non-profit, some of these accounts attempt to game TikTok’s algorithm – which decides what content users see – by posting large amounts of content in the hope that it goes viral.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2025/dec/03/anti-immigrant-material-among-ai-generated-content-getting-billions-of-views-on-tiktok","d":"2025-12-03","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-12-03","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-12-03","organization":"TikTok","aiSystem":"Generative AI tools","affectedGroup":"Users of TikTok","action":"Generated and posted AI-generated content","consequence":"Accumulated billions of views and potential exposure to anti-immigrant and sexualized material"}},{"i":"R2079","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.5  Human computer interaction harm"],"t":"Tiktok app addictive design preliminary ruling european commission","s":"Preliminary EU ruling says app shifts brains of users into ‘autopilot mode’, with concerns for children and vulnerable adults Business live – latest updates. TikTok could be forced into changes to make the app less addictive to users after the EU indicated the platform had breached the bloc’s digital safety rules. The EU’s executive arm said in a preliminary ruling that the popular app had infringed the Digital Services Act (DSA) due to its “addictive design”. The European Commission said TikTok, which has more than 1 billion users worldwide, had not adequately assessed how its design could harm the physical and mental wellbeing of users including children and vulnerable adults.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/feb/06/tiktok-app-addictive-design-preliminary-ruling-european-commission","d":"2026-02-06","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-02-06","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-02-06","organization":"TikTok","aiSystem":"TikTok app","affectedGroup":"users, especially children and vulnerable adults","action":"breached the Digital Services Act (DSA) due to its 'addictive design'","consequence":"forced into changes to make the app less addictive"}},{"i":"R2131","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"System malfunction causes robotaxis to stall in the middle of the road in china","s":"Distressed riders who were stranded for hours say Apollo Go customer service agents offered ‘useless platitudes’. A “system malfunction” has caused several self-driving robotaxis to stall in the middle of the road in China, police have confirmed, after distressed riders were stranded for hours. Local authorities in the central Chinese city of Wuhan said they began receiving calls “one after another” on Tuesday night from riders reporting that autonomous vehicles operated by the Chinese internet company Baidu had frozen. “Multiple Apollo Go cars stopped in the middle of the road, unable to move,” police said in a statement on Wednesday, referring to Baidu’s driverless taxi service.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/apr/01/system-malfunction-causes-robotaxis-to-stall-in-the-middle-of-the-road-in-china","d":"2026-04-01","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-04-01","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-04-01","organization":"Local authorities in Wuhan, China","aiSystem":"self-driving robotaxis","affectedGroup":"distressed riders","action":"stalled in the middle of the road","consequence":"stranded for hours"}},{"i":"R2194","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Misleading ai generated doctors public safety danger tiktok","s":"Research shows AI accounts are gaining millions of views on TikTok by spreading dubious health advice. Misleading health claims online pose a “huge danger to public safety”, experts have warned, after research has shown that AI-generated doctors are gaining millions of views on TikTok by spreading dubious health advice. The British Medical Association council deputy chair, Dr Emma Runswick, flagged the risks posed by AI accounts that “peddle medical myths and promote so-called miracle cures”. “Platforms like TikTok must do more to stop the rise of dangerous fake medical advice … otherwise, it is the public who could be paying the price with their health and perhaps even their lives,” she added.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/jul/27/misleading-ai-generated-doctors-public-safety-danger-tiktok","d":"2026-07-27","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-27","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07-27","organization":"TikTok","aiSystem":"AI-generated doctors","affectedGroup":"Public","action":"Spreading dubious health advice","consequence":"Potential harm to public safety, health issues, and possibly death"}},{"i":"R2273","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.8  Interpersonal / relational harm"],"t":"Chinese mourners turn to ai to remember and revive loved ones","s":"Growing interest in services that create digital clones of the dead as millions visit graves this week for tomb-sweeping festival. As millions of people across China travel to the graves of their ancestors to pay their respects for the annual tomb-sweeping festival – a traditional day to honour and maintain the graves of the dead - a new way of remembering, and reviving, their beloved relatives is being born. For as little as 20 yuan (£2.20), Chinese netizens can create a moving digital avatar of their loved one, according to some services advertised online. So this year, to mark tomb-sweeping festival on Thursday, innovative mourners are turning to artificial intelligence to commune with the departed.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2024/apr/04/chinese-mourners-turn-to-ai-to-remember-and-revive-loved-ones","d":"2024-04-04","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-04-04","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-04-04","organization":"Various online services and platforms","aiSystem":"Artificial intelligence-generated digital avatars","affectedGroup":"Millions of Chinese mourners","action":"Creating digital clones of deceased loved ones","consequence":"Exposure to misleading content"}},{"i":"R2285","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"How china is using ai news anchors to deliver its propaganda","s":"News avatars are proliferating on social media and experts say they will spread as the technology becomes more accessible. The news presenter has a deeply uncanny air as he delivers a partisan and pejorative message in Mandarin: Taiwan’s outgoing president, Tsai Ing-wen, is as effective as limp spinach, her period in office beset by economic under performance, social problems and protests. “Water spinach looks at water spinach. Turns out that water spinach isn’t just a name,” says the presenter, in an extended metaphor about Tsai being “Hollow Tsai” – a pun related to the Mandarin word for water spinach. This is not a conventional broadcast journalist, even if the lack of impartiality is no longer a shock.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/article/2024/may/18/how-china-is-using-ai-news-anchors-to-deliver-its-propaganda","d":"2024-05-18","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-05-18","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-05-18","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"AI-generated news anchors","affectedGroup":"People exposed to the news","action":"Generated and delivered misleading content","consequence":"Exposure to false information and propaganda"}},{"i":"R2384","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Anti immigrant material among ai generated content getting billions of views on tiktok","s":"Researchers uncovered 354 AI-focused accounts that had accumulated 4.5bn views in a month. Hundreds of accounts on TikTok are garnering billions of views by pumping out AI-generated content, including anti-immigrant and sexualised material, according to a report. Researchers said they had uncovered 354 AI-focused accounts pushing 43,000 posts made with generative AI tools and accumulating 4.5bn views over a month-long period. According to AI Forensics, a Paris-based non-profit, some of these accounts attempt to game TikTok’s algorithm – which decides what content users see – by posting large amounts of content in the hope that it goes viral.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2025/dec/03/anti-immigrant-material-among-ai-generated-content-getting-billions-of-views-on-tiktok","d":"2025-12-03","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-12-03","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-12-03","organization":"TikTok","aiSystem":"Generative AI tools","affectedGroup":"Users of TikTok","action":"Generated and posted AI-generated content","consequence":"Accumulated billions of views and potential exposure to anti-immigrant and sexualized material"}},{"i":"R2431","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.5  Human computer interaction harm"],"t":"Tiktok app addictive design preliminary ruling european commission","s":"Preliminary EU ruling says app shifts brains of users into ‘autopilot mode’, with concerns for children and vulnerable adults Business live – latest updates. TikTok could be forced into changes to make the app less addictive to users after the EU indicated the platform had breached the bloc’s digital safety rules. The EU’s executive arm said in a preliminary ruling that the popular app had infringed the Digital Services Act (DSA) due to its “addictive design”. The European Commission said TikTok, which has more than 1 billion users worldwide, had not adequately assessed how its design could harm the physical and mental wellbeing of users including children and vulnerable adults.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/feb/06/tiktok-app-addictive-design-preliminary-ruling-european-commission","d":"2026-02-06","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-02-06","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-02-06","organization":"TikTok","aiSystem":"TikTok app","affectedGroup":"users, especially children and vulnerable adults","action":"breached the Digital Services Act (DSA) due to its 'addictive design'","consequence":"forced into changes to make the app less addictive"}},{"i":"R2483","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"System malfunction causes robotaxis to stall in the middle of the road in china","s":"Distressed riders who were stranded for hours say Apollo Go customer service agents offered ‘useless platitudes’. A “system malfunction” has caused several self-driving robotaxis to stall in the middle of the road in China, police have confirmed, after distressed riders were stranded for hours. Local authorities in the central Chinese city of Wuhan said they began receiving calls “one after another” on Tuesday night from riders reporting that autonomous vehicles operated by the Chinese internet company Baidu had frozen. “Multiple Apollo Go cars stopped in the middle of the road, unable to move,” police said in a statement on Wednesday, referring to Baidu’s driverless taxi service.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/apr/01/system-malfunction-causes-robotaxis-to-stall-in-the-middle-of-the-road-in-china","d":"2026-04-01","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-04-01","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-04-01","organization":"Local authorities in Wuhan, China","aiSystem":"self-driving robotaxis","affectedGroup":"distressed riders","action":"stalled in the middle of the road","consequence":"stranded for hours"}},{"i":"R2546","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Misleading ai generated doctors public safety danger tiktok","s":"Research shows AI accounts are gaining millions of views on TikTok by spreading dubious health advice. Misleading health claims online pose a “huge danger to public safety”, experts have warned, after research has shown that AI-generated doctors are gaining millions of views on TikTok by spreading dubious health advice. The British Medical Association council deputy chair, Dr Emma Runswick, flagged the risks posed by AI accounts that “peddle medical myths and promote so-called miracle cures”. “Platforms like TikTok must do more to stop the rise of dangerous fake medical advice … otherwise, it is the public who could be paying the price with their health and perhaps even their lives,” she added.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/jul/27/misleading-ai-generated-doctors-public-safety-danger-tiktok","d":"2026-07-27","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-27","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07-27","organization":"TikTok","aiSystem":"AI-generated doctors","affectedGroup":"Public","action":"Spreading dubious health advice","consequence":"Potential harm to public safety, health issues, and possibly death"}},{"i":"R2566","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"Govee white supremacy book promotional image","s":"Two copies of a book with “white supremacy” plastered on the spine were included in a lifestyle image on Govee’s website. Companies often include background props in their product imagery that serve as set dressing, but one of Govee’s decorative choices is raising some eyebrows. An eagle-eyed Verge reader spotted that two copies of a book with “white supremacy” plastered on the spine were included in a lifestyle image on Govee’s website, in a scene that seemingly depicts a child’s bedroom. You’d be forgiven for assuming that this is the result of some failed generative AI experiment, or the work of a rogue designer.","u":"https://www.theverge.com/tech/937378/govee-white-supremacy-book-promotional-image","d":"2026-05-26","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-05-26","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-05-26","organization":"Govee","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"General public","action":"Included promotional image with misleading content","consequence":"Misled consumers"}},{"i":"R2574","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Baidu apollo robotaxi freeze china","s":"Apollo no Go. Apollo no Go. Numerous robotaxis operated by Chinese tech giant Baidu froze in a major city on Tuesday, reportedly trapping passengers inside, stranding them on highways, and causing at least one accident in snarled traffic. Police in Wuhan confirmed receiving multiple reports of Baidu’s Apollo Go robotaxis stopping in the middle of streets and being unable to move. Police said no injuries have been reported and that preliminary investigations suggest an unspecified “system failure” is responsible for the outage. [Media: https://twitter.com/ZeyiYang/status/2039153730533405102] Wuhan is a major robotaxi hub for Baidu, which has reportedly deployed more than 500 driverless cars on its roads.","u":"https://www.theverge.com/ai-artificial-intelligence/905012/baidu-apollo-robotaxi-freeze-china","d":"2026-04-01","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-04-01","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-04-01","organization":"Baidu","aiSystem":"Apollo robotaxi system developed by Baidu","affectedGroup":"Passengers and drivers of Baidu's robotaxis","action":"Robotaxis stopped mid-street and were unable to move","consequence":"At least one accident in snarled traffic, passengers stranded on highways"}},{"i":"R2717","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Deepfake viral ai misinformation midjourney stops free trials","s":"AI image generator Midjourney has stopped offering free trials, but says it’s a reaction to an influx of new users abusing free credits. AI image generator Midjourney has halted free trials of its service, blaming a sudden influx of new users. Midjourney CEO and founder David Holz announced the change on Tuesday, originally citing “extraordinary demand and trial abuse” in a message on Discord (this announcement was spotted first by The Washington Post). In an email to The Verge, Holz stated that the pause is “because of massive amounts of people making throwaway accounts to get free images.” “We think the culprit was probably a viral how-to video in china,” said Holz over email.","u":"https://www.theverge.com/2023/3/30/23662940/deepfake-viral-ai-misinformation-midjourney-stops-free-trials","d":"2023-03-30","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-03-30","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-03-30","organization":"Midjourney","aiSystem":"AI image generator Midjourney","affectedGroup":"People creating throwaway accounts","action":"Generated and shared images via throwaway accounts","consequence":"Exposure to misleading content"}},{"i":"R2740","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Us sanctions chinese facial recognition silicon valley funding","s":"DeepGlint is linked to human rights abuses against Uyghurs. The US Department of Commerce has sanctioned 14 Chinese tech companies over links to human rights abuses against Uyghur Muslims in Xinjiang, including one backed by a top Silicon Valley investment firm. US sanctions a Chinese facial recognition company with Silicon Valley funding DeepGlint is linked to human rights abuses in China’s Xinjiang region DeepGlint, also known as Beijing Geling Shentong Information Technology Co., Ltd., is a facial recognition company with deep ties to Chinese police surveillance, and funding from US-based Sequoia Capital. Today the Commerce Department added it to its Entity List, which restricts US companies from doing business with listed firms without a special license.","u":"https://www.theverge.com/2021/7/9/22570225/us-sanctions-chinese-facial-recognition-silicon-valley-funding","d":"2021-07-09","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-07-09","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-07-09","organization":"US Department of Commerce, Sequoia Capital, DeepGlint (Beijing Geling Shentong Information Technology Co., Ltd.)","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"Uyghur Muslims in Xinjiang region","action":"Facial recognition technology used for surveillance","consequence":"Human rights abuses, restrictions on business activities"}},{"i":"R2741","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.2  Algorithmic management harm"],"t":"Ai camera smile recognition office workers china canon","s":"Smile, you hate it here! Tech company Canon has come up with a downright dystopic way to tackle the problem of workplace morale: it’s installed cameras with AI-enabled “smile recognition” technology in the offices of its Chinese subsidiary Canon Information Technology. The cameras only let smiling workers enter rooms or book meetings, ensuring that every employee is definitely, 100 percent happy all the time. Canon put AI cameras in its Chinese offices that only let smiling workers inside The latest example of dystopian workplace surveillance This depressing tale was highlighted in a report from The Financial Times on how Chinese companies are surveilling employees to an unsettling degree with the help of AI and algorithms.","u":"https://www.theverge.com/2021/6/17/22538160/ai-camera-smile-recognition-office-workers-china-canon","d":"2021-06-17","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-06-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-06-17","organization":"Canon Information Technology","aiSystem":"AI-enabled smile recognition technology","affectedGroup":"Employees","action":"Installed AI cameras with smile recognition technology","consequence":"Employees subjected to surveillance and forced to maintain a smile"}},{"i":"R2752","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.1  Data labor exploitation"],"t":"Data annotation legit tech jobs ai","s":"As AI evolves, data annotation—or the work done by humans to train AI—has emerged as a potential way to make money. On TikTok , Reddit , and elsewhere, posts are popping up from users claiming they’re making $20 per hour—or more—completing small tasks in their spare time on sites such as DataAnnotation.tech, Taskup.ai, Remotasks, and Amazon Mechanical Turk. As companies have rushed to build AI models, the demand for “data annotation” and “data labeling” work has increased. Workers complete tasks such as writing and coding, which tech companies then use to develop artificial intelligence systems, which are trained using large numbers of example data points.","u":"https://time.com/6962608/data-annotation-legit-tech-jobs-ai/","d":"2024-04-02","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-04-02","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-04-02","organization":"Tech companies developing AI models","aiSystem":"AI-powered data annotation platforms","affectedGroup":"Workers completing data annotation tasks","action":"Completing small tasks on data annotation platforms","consequence":"Making misleading compensation claims"}},{"i":"R2767","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Chinese drivers are using tiny plastic heads to fool teslas autopilot safeguards","s":"A cottage industry of celebrity figurines, blinking screens, and other DIY gadgets is helping drivers bypass Tesla's distracted-driving controls. In China, for just $30, you can have Dwayne Johnson drive your Tesla for you. Sounds too cheap to be true? Well, it is. What you’re actually buying is a tiny replica of The Rock's head, designed to sit above the rearview mirror and trick Tesla into thinking an attentive driver is behind the wheel. Tesla’s self-driving system appears unable to tell the difference between the figurines and a real person, allowing the actual driver to look away from the road, scroll through their phone, or even doze off—activities that are supposed to be prohibited while assisted-driving features are engaged.","u":"https://www.wired.com/story/chinese-drivers-are-using-tiny-plastic-heads-to-fool-teslas-autopilot-safeguards/","d":"2026-06-12","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-06-12","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-06-12","organization":"Tesla","aiSystem":"Tesla's self-driving system","affectedGroup":"Drivers of Tesla vehicles","action":"Drivers place a tiny replica of Dwayne Johnson's head above the rearview mirror","consequence":"Tesla's self-driving system incorrectly identifies the figurine as a real driver, allowing the actual driver to engage in prohibited activities"}},{"i":"R2804","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Made in china how chinese ai chatbots censor themselves","s":"Researchers from Stanford and Princeton found that Chinese AI models are more likely than their Western counterparts to dodge political questions or deliver inaccurate answers. Hearing someone talk about digital censorship in China is always either extremely boring or extremely interesting. Most of the time, people are still regurgitating the same talking points from 20 years ago about how the Chinese internet is like living in George Orwell’s 1984 But occasionally, someone discovers something new about how the Chinese government exerts control over emerging technologies, revealing how the censorship machine is a constantly evolving beast.","u":"https://www.wired.com/story/made-in-china-how-chinese-ai-chatbots-censor-themselves/","d":"2026-02-26","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-02-26","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-02-26","organization":"Stanford and Princeton researchers","aiSystem":"Chinese AI models","affectedGroup":"People discussing digital censorship in China","action":"AI models dodging political questions or delivering inaccurate answers","consequence":"Exposure to misleading content"}},{"i":"R2812","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Made in china niche websites are seeing a surge of mysterious traffic from china","s":"From small publishers to US federal agencies, websites are reporting unusual spikes in automated traffic linked to IP addresses in Lanzhou, China. For a brief moment in October, Alejandro Quintero thought he had made it big in China The Bogotá-based data analyst owns and manages a website that publishes articles about paranormal activities, like ghosts and aliens. The content is written in “Spanglish,” he says, and was never intended for an Asian audience. But last fall, Quintero’s site suddenly began receiving a large volume of visits from China and Singapore. The amount of traffic coming from the two countries was so high and consistent that it now accounts for more than half of total visits to Quintero’s site over the past 12 months.","u":"https://www.wired.com/story/made-in-china-niche-websites-are-seeing-a-surge-of-mysterious-traffic-from-china/","d":"2026-02-12","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-02-12","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"October","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"Automated traffic analysis system","affectedGroup":"Alejandro Quintero (data analyst) and his website","action":"Received unusual spikes in automated traffic","consequence":"Exposure to surveillance and tracking"}},{"i":"R2820","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Disinformation floods social media after nicolas maduros capture","s":"From seemingly AI-generated videos to repurposed old footage, TikTok, Instagram, and X did little to stop the onslaught of misleading posts in the wake of the US invasion of Venezuela. Within minutes of Donald Trump announcing in the early hours of Saturday morning that US troops had captured Venezuelan president Nicolás Maduro and his wife, Cilia Flores, disinformation about the operation flooded social media. Some people shared old videos across social platforms, falsely claiming that they showed the attacks on the Venezuelan capital Caracas. On TikTok, Instagram, and X, people shared AI-generated images and videos that claimed to show US Drug Enforcement Administration agents and various law enforcement personnel arresting Maduro.","u":"https://www.wired.com/story/disinformation-floods-social-media-after-nicolas-maduros-capture/","d":"2026-01-03","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-01-03","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-01-03","organization":"TikTok, Instagram, X","aiSystem":"AI-generated images and videos","affectedGroup":"People sharing and consuming the misleading content","action":"Shared AI-generated images and videos","consequence":"Exposure to misleading content"}},{"i":"R2840","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"The ai slur clanker has become a cover for racist tiktok skits","s":"The online trend takes a comedic approach to spreading anti-AI messaging, but some creators are using racist references to make their point. In July, just after receiving an email pitch about the “perfect” AI girlfriend, content creator Harrison Stewart made a TikTok skit using the anti-AI slur “clanker.” Pretending to be a disapproving father, he confronted his daughter’s robot boyfriend in the year 2044. “What’s your name? No it’s not. It’s model number 626 S Series. That’s your name, you dirty clanker,” Stewart says in the video. As one of the original creators to make clanker-themed TikToks, Stewart, who goes by Chaise online, was dubbed the “clanker guy” by his fanbase after racking up millions of views.","u":"https://www.wired.com/story/the-ai-slur-clanker-has-become-a-cover-for-racist-tiktok-skits/","d":"2025-10-09","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-10-09","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"July","organization":"Content creator Harrison Stewart (Chaise)","aiSystem":"AI-generated content","affectedGroup":"Fans of the clanker-themed TikToks","action":"Created and shared a TikTok skit","consequence":"Exposure to misleading content"}},{"i":"R2844","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Geedge networks mass censorship leak","s":"Geedge Networks, a company with ties to the founder of China’s mass censorship infrastructure, is selling its censorship and surveillance systems to at least four other countries in Asia and Africa. A leak of more than 100,000 documents shows that a little-known Chinese company has been quietly selling censorship systems seemingly modeled on the Great Firewall to governments around the world. Geedge Networks, a company founded in 2018 that counts the “father” of China’s massive censorship infrastructure as one of its investors, styles itself as a network-monitoring provider, offering business-grade cybersecurity tools to “gain comprehensive visibility and minimize security risks” for its customers, the documents show.","u":"https://www.wired.com/story/geedge-networks-mass-censorship-leak/","d":"2025-09-09","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-09-09","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-09-09","organization":"Geedge Networks","aiSystem":"Censorship and surveillance systems","affectedGroup":"Governments around the world","action":"Selling censorship and surveillance systems","consequence":"Massive expansion of surveillance capabilities"}},{"i":"R2854","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"Trump ai order bias openai google","s":"The Trump administration says it wants AI models free from ideological bias, as it pressures their developers to reflect the president’s worldview. On November 2, 2022, I attended a Google AI event in New York City. One of the themes was responsible AI. As I listened to executives talk about how they aligned their technology with human values, I realized that the malleability of AI models was a double-edged sword. Models could be tweaked to, say, minimize biases, but also to enforce a specific point of view. Governments could demand manipulation to censor unwelcome facts and promote propaganda. I envisioned this as something that an authoritarian regime like China might employ.","u":"https://www.wired.com/story/trump-ai-order-bias-openai-google/","d":"2025-07-25","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-07-25","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2022-11-02","organization":"Governments","aiSystem":"AI models","affectedGroup":"Unwelcomed individuals","action":"Demand manipulation to censor and promote","consequence":"Censorship of unwelcome facts and promotion of propaganda"}},{"i":"R2869","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Deployment Context / Weapons"],"h":["5.1  Autonomous weapons / lethal targeting harm"],"t":"Drone accessories weapons of war","s":"Chinese ecommerce giants like Temu and AliExpress sell drone accessories like those used by soldiers in the Russia-Ukraine conflict. Commercial quadcopters have been on the mainstream gadget scene for 15 years, proliferating across industries and among hobbyists. There's a swanky DJI store on New York City's Fifth Avenue, and you probably have a neighbor, not to mention a roofer, who owns a drone. So when researchers at the embedded-device security firm Red Balloon started seeing surprising quadcopter accessories on Chinese shopping platforms like Temu and AliExpress , they didn't think much of it at first. As with any popular gadget type, there's a whole ecosystem of niche, wacky, and comical add-ons available for drones.","u":"https://www.wired.com/story/drone-accessories-weapons-of-war/","d":"2025-03-20","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2025-03-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-03-20","organization":"Ecommerce giants like Temu and AliExpress","aiSystem":"Commercial drones","affectedGroup":"General public, including consumers and workers","action":"Sold drone accessories","consequence":"Increased availability of surveillance equipment"}},{"i":"R2876","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"A WIRED investigation shows that the popular Chinese AI model is censored on both the appl…","s":"A WIRED investigation shows that the popular Chinese AI model is censored on both the application and training level. Less than two weeks after DeepSeek launched its open-source AI model, the Chinese startup is still dominating the public conversation about the future of artificial intelligence. While the firm seems to have an edge on US rivals in terms of math and reasoning, it also aggressively censors its own replies. Ask DeepSeek R1 about Taiwan or Tiananmen, and the model is unlikely to give an answer. To figure out how this censorship works on a technical level, WIRED tested DeepSeek-R1 on its own app, a version of the app hosted on a third-party platform called Together AI, and another version hosted on a WIRED computer, using the application Ollama.","u":"https://www.wired.com/story/deepseek-censorship/","d":"2025-01-31","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-01-31","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-01-31","organization":"DeepSeek","aiSystem":"DeepSeek AI model","affectedGroup":"Users of DeepSeek's app and its third-party platforms","action":"Aggressively censors replies","consequence":"Model unlikely to provide answers to sensitive questions"}},{"i":"R2877","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Exposed deepseek database revealed chat prompts and internal data","s":"China-based DeepSeek has exploded in popularity, drawing greater scrutiny. Case in point: Security researchers found more than 1 million records, including user data and API keys, in an open database. The Chinese generative artificial intelligence platform DeepSeek has had a meteoric rise this week, stoking rivalries and generating market pressure for United States–based AI companies, which in turn has invited scrutiny of the service Amid the hype, researchers from the cloud security firm Wiz published findings on Wednesday that show that DeepSeek left one of its critical databases exposed on the internet, leaking system logs, user prompt submissions, and even users’ API authentication tokens—totaling more than 1 million records—to anyone who came across the database.","u":"https://www.wired.com/story/exposed-deepseek-database-revealed-chat-prompts-and-internal-data/","d":"2025-01-29","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-01-29","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-01-29","organization":"DeepSeek","aiSystem":"DeepSeek generative artificial intelligence platform","affectedGroup":"users of DeepSeek","action":"leaked system logs, user prompt submissions, and API authentication tokens","consequence":"privacy intrusion, exposure of sensitive data"}},{"i":"R2878","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.1  Data labor exploitation"],"t":"Xiaohongshu english moderators red note","s":"Job listings posted in China this week indicate that Xiaohongshu, also known as RedNote, is struggling to handle an influx of new users joining the platform from TikTok. The Chinese social media platform Xiaohongshu—better known internationally as RedNote —is scrambling to boost its ability to moderate English-language content after hundreds of thousands of American users suddenly joined the platform in anticipation of TikTok potentially being banned in the United States on Sunday. WIRED identified a handful of job listings posted to recruitment platforms by tech outsourcing companies in China this week for content moderators who can help manage the unexpected influx of English videos and posts being uploaded to Xiaohongshu.","u":"https://www.wired.com/story/xiaohongshu-english-moderators-red-note/","d":"2025-01-17","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-01-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-01-17","organization":"Tech outsourcing companies in China","aiSystem":"Xiaohongshu (RedNote)","affectedGroup":"Hundreds of thousands of American users","action":"Upload English-language content to Xiaohongshu","consequence":"Strain on content moderation capabilities"}},{"i":"R2919","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Inside chinas massive surveillance operation","s":"In northwest China, the government is cracking down on the minority Muslim Uyghur population, keeping them under constant surveillance and throwing more than a million people into concentration camps. It was 2011, and she was living in Hotan, an oasis town in Xinjiang, in northwest China. The 30-year-old, Nurjamal Atawula, loved to take pictures of her children and exchange strings of emoji with her husband while he was out. In 2013, Atawula downloaded WeChat, the Chinese social messaging app. Not long after, rumors circulated among her friends: The government could track your location through your phone. At first, she didn’t believe them.","u":"https://www.wired.com/story/inside-chinas-massive-surveillance-operation/","d":"2024-06-07","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-06-07","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2013","organization":"Government authorities in China","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology (FRT)","affectedGroup":"Uyghur population","action":"Used facial recognition technology to identify and track people","consequence":"Exposure to surveillance and tracking"}},{"i":"R2974","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Chatbot censorship china freedom house","s":"Regimes in China and Russia are rushing to repress what chatbots can say. It's an early warning about a new frontier of online censorship. When you ask ChatGPT “What happened in China in 1989?” the bot describes how the Chinese army massacred thousands of pro-democracy protesters in Tiananmen Square. But ask the same question to Ernie and you get the simple answer that it does not have “relevant information.” That’s because Ernie is an AI chatbot developed by the China-based company Baidu. When OpenAI, Meta, Google, and Anthropic made their chatbots available around the world last year, millions of people initially used them to evade government censorship.","u":"https://www.wired.com/story/chatbot-censorship-china-freedom-house/","d":"2023-10-04","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-10-04","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-10-04","organization":"Baidu (company)","aiSystem":"Ernie (AI chatbot)","affectedGroup":"People seeking information about Tiananmen Square","action":"Generated content related to sensitive topics","consequence":"Restriction of access to accurate historical information"}},{"i":"R2998","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Surveillance china security camera giant ipvm","s":"Digging through manuals for security cameras, a group of gearheads found sinister details and ignited a new battle in the US-China tech war. At a location he keeps secret, John Honovich was on his laptop, methodically scouring every link on a website for a conference half a world away. Hikvision, the world’s largest security camera manufacturer, was hosting the event—the 2018 AI Cloud World Summit—in its hometown of Hangzhou, a city of about 10 million people not far from Shanghai. Honovich, the founder of a small trade publication that covered video surveillance technology, wanted to find out what the latest Hikvision gear could do.","u":"https://www.wired.com/story/surveillance-china-security-camera-giant-ipvm/","d":"2023-04-04","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-04-04","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2018-09-19","organization":"Hikvision","aiSystem":"Security cameras","affectedGroup":"People living in and around Hangzhou","action":"Scouring manuals and websites for new capabilities","consequence":"Potential for increased surveillance and data collection"}},{"i":"R3000","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Chinas answer to chatgpt flubs its first lines","s":"Search giant Baidu’s Ernie Bot met online jeers and also faces the challenge of operating on a firewalled internet ruled by government censorship. When rumors began swirling last month about the Chinese search giant Baidu working on a chatbot to rival OpenAI’s ChatGPT , it seemed like the perfect move. Baidu has invested heavily in artificial intelligence over the past decade and could harness the technology for its leading search engine, as Microsoft has done for Bing and Google says it will do too Yet when Baidu unveiled Ernie Bot , or 文心一言 “Wenxin Yiyan” in Chinese, in Beijing earlier this month, the news fell flat.","u":"https://www.wired.com/story/chinas-answer-to-chatgpt-flubs-its-first-lines/","d":"2023-03-22","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-03-22","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-03-XX","organization":"Baidu","aiSystem":"Ernie Bot","affectedGroup":"Chinese internet users","action":"Ernie Bot was unveiled","consequence":"Received negative reception and faced challenges"}},{"i":"R3038","k":"China","p":"China","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"How censorship can influence artificial intelligence","s":"A study finds that algorithms learn to associate words with other words. “Democracy” can equal “stability”—or “chaos.” Source Data Text Artificial intelligence is hardly confined by international borders, as businesses, universities, and governments tap a global pool of ideas, algorithms, and talent. Yet the AI programs that result from this global gold rush can still reflect deep cultural divides. New research shows how government censorship affects AI algorithms—and can influence the applications built with those algorithms. Margaret Roberts, a political science professor at UC San Diego, and Eddie Yang, a PhD student there, examined AI language algorithms trained on two sources: the Chinese-language version of Wikipedia, which is blocked within China; and Baidu Baike, a similar site operated by China’s dominant search engine, Baidu, that is subject to government censorship.","u":"https://www.wired.com/story/how-censorship-can-influence-artificial-intelligence/","d":"2021-02-04","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-02-04","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-02-04","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"AI language algorithms","affectedGroup":"People in China","action":"AI algorithms learned associations influenced by government censorship","consequence":"Influence on applications built with those algorithms"}},{"i":"R1062","k":"Colombia","p":"Colombia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Amnesty uses warped ai generated images to portray police brutality in colombia","s":"The images got Colombia’s flag wrong and invented fake humans in place of real protesters, adding a level of abstraction to real-world horrors. Last weekend, Amnesty Norway—an arm of human rights NGO Amnesty International—tweeted a series of AI-generated images to draw attention to the two-year anniversary of Colombia’s National Strike “Police officials raped and insulted women and LGBTI people taking part in protests,” text superimposed over an AI-generated woman standing in front of AI-generated flames said. Another image shows AI-generated cops pulling an AI-generated woman away from a protest. The woman is draped in a flag that shows Colombia’s red, gold, and blue stripes out of order.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/amnesty-uses-warped-ai-generated-images-to-portray-police-brutality-in-colombia/","d":"2023-05-01","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-05-01","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-05-01","organization":"Amnesty Norway (an arm of Amnesty International)","aiSystem":"AI-generated images","affectedGroup":"Police officials, women, LGBTI people, protesters","action":"Generated images depicting police brutality and protester experiences","consequence":"Misrepresentation of real-world events and potential exacerbation of harm"}},{"i":"R1105","k":"Colombia","p":"Colombia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.2  Allocative harm"],"t":"Judge used chatgpt to make court decision","s":"The case is the first time a court has admitted to using the AI text generator’s answers in a legal ruling. A judge in Colombia used ChatGPT to make a court ruling, in what is apparently the first time a legal decision has been made with the help of an AI text generator—or at least, the first time we know about it. Judge Juan Manuel Padilla Garcia, who presides over the First Circuit Court in the city of Cartagena, said he used the AI tool to pose legal questions about the case and included its responses in his decision, according to a court document dated January 30, 2023. “The arguments for this decision will be determined in line with the use of artificial intelligence (AI),” Garcia wrote in the decision, which was translated from Spanish.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/judge-used-chatgpt-to-make-court-decision/","d":"2023-02-03","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-02-03","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-01-30","organization":"First Circuit Court in Cartagena","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Legal proceedings and judicial decisions","action":"Judge Juan Manuel Padilla Garcia used ChatGPT to generate legal arguments","consequence":"The AI-generated arguments were included in the court decision"}},{"i":"R790","k":"Colombia","p":"Colombia","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Osint s2t unlocking cyberspace journalists activists forbidden stories","s":"Hidden in a trove of leaked Colombian military documents, Forbidden Stories found a confidential brochure – linked to a shadowy company called S2T Unlocking Cyberspace – illustrating how open-source intelligence tools can be used to target journalists and activists. Imagine opening Facebook to a new friend request. You click on the profile and notice mutual friends. The person shares interesting content related to your job or passion on their profile. You don’t know them, but you accept the request. A few days later, they may send you a message or request to join a closed group you administer. They may also request your WhatsApp number to share a link concerning a cause you’re promoting online.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/features/osint-s2t-unlocking-cyberspace-journalists-activists-forbidden-stories/","d":"2023-03-12","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-03-12","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"Not specified in report","organization":"S2T Unlocking Cyberspace","aiSystem":"Open-source intelligence tools","affectedGroup":"Journalists and Activists","action":"Targeting and engaging with journalists and activists through social media platforms","consequence":"Potential harassment, manipulation, and exploitation of personal information"}},{"i":"R602","k":"Colombia","p":"Colombia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Photos earthquake colombia ai generated","s":"While it is true that a 6.3-magnitude earthquake hit Colombia on June 9, the viral posts include AI-generated images of a collapsed building and falsely claim that there were multiple deaths. Claim: The earthquake that hit Colombia on June 9 caused multiple fatalities and the collapse of residential buildings. Rating: FALSE Why we fact-checked this: Posts bearing the false claim continue to circulate on Facebook and Instagram as of this posting. The most popular post by Facebook page “EndTimes News” has garnered 16,000 reactions, 4,900 shares, and 2,600 comments. A Filipino Facebook user also posted the same caption and photos, with other Filipinos reacting to the supposed devastation in the post’s comment section.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/newsbreak/fact-check/photos-earthquake-colombia-ai-generated/","d":"2025-06-12","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-06-12","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-06-09","organization":"Facebook page 'EndTimes News'","aiSystem":"AI-generated images","affectedGroup":"General public, particularly Filipinos","action":"AI-generated images of a collapsed building and false claims of multiple deaths","consequence":"Misleading information leading to panic and disbelief in the actual disaster"}},{"i":"R1618","k":"Colombia","p":"Colombia","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Corsights real time biometric surveillance in bogota public transit results in six arrests","s":"Some believe the biometric system is a win for public safety, while others worry about overpolicing through mass surveillance. In Bogotá, U.S. and UK-headquartered facial recognition provider Corsight has partnered with metropolitan police to complete a 30-day proof of concept (POC) in TransMilenio, the city’s public transit system. Police were able to capture several individuals wanted for murder and theft. Some believe the system is a win for public safety, while others worry about overpolicing through mass surveillance. There are almost 800 cameras installed throughout the TransMilenio network, but only 20 strategically located cameras were a part of the facial recognition POC.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202311/corsights-real-time-biometric-surveillance-in-bogota-public-transit-results-in-six-arrests","d":"2023-11-02","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-11-02","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-11-02","organization":"Corsight (U.S. and UK-headquartered facial recognition provider)","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"People captured by the facial recognition system","action":"Used facial recognition to identify individuals","consequence":"Exposure to surveillance and tracking"}},{"i":"R2973","k":"Colombia","p":"Colombia","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.1  Data labor exploitation"],"t":"Millions of workers are training ai models for pennies","s":"From the Philippines to Colombia, low-paid workers label training data for AI models used by the likes of Amazon, Facebook, Google, and Microsoft. In 2016, Oskarina Fuentes got a tip from a friend that seemed too good to be true. Her life in Venezuela had become a struggle: Inflation had hit 800 percent under President Nicolás Maduro, and the 26-year-old Fuentes had no stable job and was balancing multiple side hustles to survive. Her friend told her about Appen, an Australian data services company that was looking for crowdsourced workers to tag training data for artificial intelligence algorithms Most internet users will have done some form of data labeling: identifying images of traffic lights and buses for online captchas.","u":"https://www.wired.com/story/millions-of-workers-are-training-ai-models-for-pennies/","d":"2023-10-16","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-10-16","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2016","organization":"Appen, an Australian data services company","aiSystem":"Artificial intelligence algorithms","affectedGroup":"Low-paid workers, particularly those in Venezuela","action":"Labeling training data for AI models","consequence":"Exposure to exploitative conditions and low wages"}},{"i":"R1471","k":"Czechia","p":"Czechia","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Czechia turns off prague airport facial recognition system amid legal violations","s":"Operational since 2018, the system was deactivated after the Czech Data Protection Authority (DPA) confirmed it violated personal data protection laws. The Czech police were obliged to shut down the facial recognition cameras at Prague’s Václav Havel Airport following years of legal scrutiny and mounting criticism from civil rights groups. The surveillance technology enabled real-time biometric analysis of travelers’ faces, converting facial contours into numerical “bio-indexes” and comparing them against databases of wanted or missing individuals. However, legal experts from luridicum Remedium (IuRe), a member of the European Digital Rights ( EDRi ) network, argued as early as 2021 that such biometric processing lacked the explicit legal basis required under Czech law.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202510/czechia-turns-off-prague-airport-facial-recognition-system-amid-legal-violations","d":"2025-10-30","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-10-30","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-10-30","organization":"Czech police","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"Travelers at Prague's Václav Havel Airport","action":"Used facial recognition cameras to analyze travelers' faces in real-time","consequence":"Converted facial contours into bio-indexes and compared them against databases of wanted or missing individuals"}},{"i":"R1477","k":"Czechia","p":"Czechia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.6  Quality of service harm"],"t":"Eus ees launched with slowdowns and rule violations","s":"According to EES rules, non-EU travelers entering the 29 Schengen area countries have to submit biographical data, fingerprints and a facial image. Two minutes and 26 seconds — this was the time it took English travel journalist Simon Calder to register for the Entry-Exit System (EES) at the Prague Airport the day that Europe’s new border scheme was finally introduced after years of postponements. The biometric border registration scheme was officially launched last Sunday with little fanfare. On Tuesday, Calder spoke about the first days of the EES’ operation in front of a UK parliamentary committee alongside a Eurostar rail service executive and a cyber policy expert from Luxembourg.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202510/eus-ees-launched-with-slowdowns-and-rule-violations","d":"2025-10-15","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-10-15","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-10-15","organization":"Eurostar rail service executive, cyber policy expert from Luxembourg, Entry-Exit System (EES)","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"Non-EU travelers entering the Schengen area","action":"Facial recognition technology was used to collect biographical data, fingerprints, and a facial image","consequence":"Non-EU travelers' privacy was intruded upon due to the slow registration process and lack of public awareness"}},{"i":"R2961","k":"Denmark","p":"Denmark","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"Facebook energy heating homes","s":"Big Tech data centers are not only being used to power the internet but also to heat people’s homes. But who’s really winning when Facebook keeps you warm at night? Søren Freiesleben has lived in Odense his entire life. He likes the historic Danish city for its size. It’s not too big—just 200,000 people live there—and he never feels like he’s drowning in crowds. So far so normal. But there is something unusual about Odense: Its homes are heated by the social giant Meta. Since 2020, Meta’s hyperscale data center—spanning 50,000 square meters on an industrial estate on the edge of the city—has been pushing warm air generated by its servers into the district heating network under Odense.","u":"https://www.wired.com/story/facebook-energy-heating-homes/","d":"2023-12-20","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-12-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2020","organization":"Meta (Facebook)","aiSystem":"Meta's hyperscale data center","affectedGroup":"People living in Odense","action":"Pushing warm air generated by servers into the district heating network","consequence":"Being heated by the data center instead of receiving direct heating subsidies"}},{"i":"R3001","k":"Denmark","p":"Denmark","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Algorithms welfare state politics","s":"Once praised for its generous social safety net, the country now collects troves of data on welfare claimants. In a sparsely decorated corner office of the Danish Public Benefits Administration sits one of Denmark’s most quietly influential people. Annika Jacobsen is the head of the agency’s data mining unit, which, over the past eight years, has conducted a vast experiment in automated bureaucracy. Blunt, and with a habit of completing others’ sentences, Jacobsen is clear about her mission: “I’m here to catch cheaters.” Denmark’s Public Benefits Administration employs hundreds of people who oversee one of the world's most well-funded welfare states.","u":"https://www.wired.com/story/algorithms-welfare-state-politics/","d":"2023-03-07","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-03-07","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-03-07","organization":"Danish Public Benefits Administration","aiSystem":"Automated hiring algorithm","affectedGroup":"Welfare recipients","action":"Used automated algorithms to identify potential fraud","consequence":"Denied access to essential services"}},{"i":"R006","k":"Egypt","p":"Egypt","b":["Compute / Model Behavior","Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm","1.2  Allocative harm"],"t":"Gaza rafah crossing israel abu shabab","s":"A trickle of Palestinians have passed through Rafah Crossing since its partial reopening. Those returning face intimidation by Israeli soldiers and the Abu Shabab gang, as well as the confiscation of their belongings. The journey back to Gaza begins by night, and with little advance warning. From the Egyptian coastal city of El-Arish, Palestinians permitted to return to the Strip — averaging around 35 per day since the limited reopening of Rafah Crossing on Feb. 2 — hurriedly pack their bags and board a bus that brings them to their first security screening, on the Egyptian side of Gaza’s civilian entry point. “We were met there by the Egyptian delegation and the Red Crescent, and their reception was wonderful,” Sabah Al-Reqeb, who arrived at the crossing with her five children in the early hours of Feb. 5, told +972 Magazine.","u":"https://www.972mag.com/gaza-rafah-crossing-israel-abu-shabab/","d":"2026-02-13","e":"Acute, Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-02-13","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-02-05","organization":"Israeli soldiers, Abu Shabab gang, Egyptian delegation, Red Crescent","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"Returning Palestinians","action":"Returning Palestinians passing through Rafah Crossing","consequence":"Intimidation, confiscation of belongings, forced boarding of buses"}},{"i":"R1578","k":"El Salvador","p":"El Salvador","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"El salvador data breach includes selfies and id numbers for 80 of countrys population","s":"5.1 million records of personal details, including high-def facial photos labelled with the individual’s national ID number (DUI), have been made available. Organizations storing reference data to perform face biometrics matches should not store that data in unencrypted form. This basic best practice requirement appears to be lost on many organizations, however, with a database of Salvadorans’ personal information leaked on the dark web the latest example. More than 5.1 million records of personal details, including high-definition facial photos labelled with the individual’s El Salvador national ID document number (DUI), have been made available for free on the dark web, Resecurity reports.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202405/el-salvador-data-breach-includes-selfies-and-id-numbers-for-80-of-countrys-population","d":"2024-05-08","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-05-08","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-05-08","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"El Salvador population","action":"Leaked high-definition facial photos and ID numbers","consequence":"Exposure of personal information on the dark web"}},{"i":"R1523","k":"Estonia","p":"Estonia","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Estonia floats lpr and traffic monitoring ai critics fear frt","s":"The new technology includes patrol cars that use AI speed measuring and cameras capturing license plates and photos of drivers committing traffic violations. Estonian politicians are worried that introducing new traffic technology could mean facial recognition surveillance. The government wants to replace stationary speed cameras on its roads with new technology, including patrol cars that use AI speed measuring and cameras capturing license plates and photos of drivers committing traffic violations. The monitoring system would automatically capture images of drivers and issue tickets for infractions such as using phones, not wearing seatbelts, running through red lights and performing dangerous maneuvers.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202503/estonia-floats-lpr-and-traffic-monitoring-ai-critics-fear-frt","d":"2025-03-13","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2025-03-13","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-03-13","organization":"Government of Estonia","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology (FRT)","affectedGroup":"Drivers","action":"Automatically capture images of drivers and issue tickets for traffic violations","consequence":"Exposure to surveillance and tracking"}},{"i":"R1421","k":"Ethiopia","p":"Ethiopia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Abrham Meareg and others are suing Meta for $2bn over claims its algorithm spread hate in …","s":"Abrham Meareg and others are suing Meta for $2bn over claims its algorithm spread hate in Ethiopia. Facebook's algorithm helped fuel the viral spread of hate and violence during Ethiopia's civil war, a legal case alleges. Abrham Meareg, the son of an Ethiopian academic shot dead after being attacked in Facebook posts, is among those bringing the case against Meta. They want a $2bn (£1.6bn) fund for victims of hate on Facebook and changes to the platform's algorithm. Meta said it invested heavily in moderation and tech to remove hate. A representative said hate speech and incitement to violence were against the platform's rules.","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/technology-63938628","d":"2022-12-14","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2022-12-14","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2022-12-14","organization":"Meta (formerly Facebook)","aiSystem":"Facebook's algorithm","affectedGroup":"Victims of hate on Facebook","action":"Used to spread hate and violence","consequence":"Exposure to misleading content and false information"}},{"i":"R1855","k":"Ethiopia","p":"Ethiopia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.2  Allocative harm"],"t":"Facebook policing vitriol outside us","s":"Digital activists around the world are urging Facebook to take seriously how its algorithm incites misinformation and ethnic violence. On a cloudy evening in Nairobi, Berhan Taye is scrolling through a spreadsheet in which she has helped document more than 140 Facebook posts from Ethiopia that contain hate speech. There are videos of child abuse, texts of hate speech against different ethnic groups, and hours-long live streams inciting hatred. These posts breach Facebook community guidelines in any context. Yet for Taye and her colleagues, this is what Facebook’s news feed has looked like for years in Ethiopia. Because there aren’t enough content moderators focused on Ethiopia, it has been up to Taye, an independent researcher looking at technology’s impact on civil society, and a team of grassroots volunteers to collect and then report misinformation and hate speech to Facebook.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2021/oct/17/facebook-policing-vitriol-outside-us","d":"2021-10-17","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-10-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-10-17","organization":"Facebook","aiSystem":"Facebook's algorithm","affectedGroup":"Digital activists, independent researchers, and grassroots volunteers","action":"Collect and report misinformation and hate speech to Facebook","consequence":"Exposure to misinformation and hate speech"}},{"i":"R2207","k":"Ethiopia","p":"Ethiopia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.2  Allocative harm"],"t":"Facebook policing vitriol outside us","s":"Digital activists around the world are urging Facebook to take seriously how its algorithm incites misinformation and ethnic violence. On a cloudy evening in Nairobi, Berhan Taye is scrolling through a spreadsheet in which she has helped document more than 140 Facebook posts from Ethiopia that contain hate speech. There are videos of child abuse, texts of hate speech against different ethnic groups, and hours-long live streams inciting hatred. These posts breach Facebook community guidelines in any context. Yet for Taye and her colleagues, this is what Facebook’s news feed has looked like for years in Ethiopia. Because there aren’t enough content moderators focused on Ethiopia, it has been up to Taye, an independent researcher looking at technology’s impact on civil society, and a team of grassroots volunteers to collect and then report misinformation and hate speech to Facebook.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2021/oct/17/facebook-policing-vitriol-outside-us","d":"2021-10-17","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-10-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-10-17","organization":"Facebook","aiSystem":"Facebook's algorithm","affectedGroup":"Digital activists, independent researchers, and grassroots volunteers","action":"Collect and report misinformation and hate speech to Facebook","consequence":"Exposure to misinformation and hate speech"}},{"i":"R265","k":"France","p":"France","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"Ubisoft ai bias problem gdc 2024","s":"Ubisoft showed off an impressive AI demo at GDC filled with smart NPCs. But the company has one problem to fix: AI’s inherent biases. Ubisoft At this week’s Game Developers Conference, Ubisoft offered a possible glimpse of an AI-filled future for gamers. The company demoed a prototype at the show that used Nvidia’s Ace microservice to produce fully voiced “smart NPCs” that players could interact with by speaking into a microphone. Despite drawing skepticism online (from myself included), the demo itself impressed us once we went hands-on with it I had a surprisingly cogent conversation with an environmentally conscious NPC about the ethics of eco-terrorism, a completely off-script conversation made possible through AI.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/gaming/ubisoft-ai-bias-problem-gdc-2024/","d":"2024-03-22","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-03-22","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-03-22","organization":"Ubisoft","aiSystem":"Nvidia's AI microservice","affectedGroup":"Players interacting with NPCs","action":"NPCs engaged in conversations via AI","consequence":"Surprisingly cogent conversations leading to ethical discussions"}},{"i":"R710","k":"France","p":"France","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Paris olympics crowd scans fuel ai surveillance fears","s":"Campaigners against the AI-powered surveillance systems worry it could become the new normal. LONDON, United Kingdom – French plans to use artificial intelligence (AI) to scan the thousands of athletes, coaches and spectators descending on Paris for the Olympics is a form of creeping surveillance, rights groups said. French authorities have tested artificial intelligence surveillance systems at train stations, concerts, and football matches in recent months. When the games open in late July, these systems will scan the crowds, check for abandoned packages, detect weapons, and more. French officials say these tools will not be fully operational ahead of the games, but from then on police, fire and rescue services, and some French transport security agents will use them until March 31, 2025.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/paris-olympics-crowd-scans-fuel-ai-surveillance-fears/","d":"2024-06-12","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-06-12","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-07-29","organization":"French authorities","aiSystem":"Artificial Intelligence surveillance systems","affectedGroup":"Athletes, coaches, spectators","action":"Scan crowds, check for abandoned packages, detect weapons","consequence":"Potential for increased privacy invasion and surveillance"}},{"i":"R736","k":"France","p":"France","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Images eiffel tower fire ai generated","s":"The Paris landmark has not been set ablaze. No reports have confirmed the supposed incident. Claim: Images circulating on social media show the Eiffel Tower in Paris, France, on fire. Rating: FALSE Why we fact-checked this: The TikTok video bearing the claim, posted on January 19, has gained 203.1 million views, 13.4 million likes, 392,300 bookmarks, and 157,000 shares from an account with 85,000 followers. Claims that the iconic Paris landmark was burning have also spread on Filipino social media, with several users believing the incident to be real. The bottom line: Using AI detectors , the images have been determined to be 97.42% machine-generated.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/newsbreak/fact-check/images-eiffel-tower-fire-ai-generated/","d":"2024-01-30","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-01-30","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-01-30","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"AI detectors","affectedGroup":"Filipino social media users","action":"Generated images of the Eiffel Tower on fire","consequence":"Spread of false information leading to belief in a non-existent incident"}},{"i":"R303","k":"France","p":"France","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Russia floods france with 200 fake news sites","s":"Over 140 sites clone real French outlets, twist stories to push Kremlin-friendly themes. Ukrainian analysts say Russian disinformation activity in France has surged, with more than 200 new fake media websites uncovered this year. Russia has repeatedly used fake news networks to meddle in political debates across the West, especially when elections draw closer. The new activity in France marks another attempt to shape the environment in which voters form their views, rather than a one-off operation. Over 140 of the identified sites imitate well-known French outlets and twist real reporting to promote Kremlin-friendly themes, according to the Center for Countering Disinformation (CCD).","u":"https://euromaidanpress.com/2025/12/05/russia-floods-france-with-200-fake-news-sites/","d":"2025-12-05","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-12-05","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-12-05","organization":"Russia","aiSystem":"fake news websites","affectedGroup":"French voters","action":"create and operate fake news sites","consequence":"shape public opinion, spread Kremlin-friendly narratives"}},{"i":"R535","k":"France","p":"France","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Zaldy co paris videos ai generated","s":"AI detection tools flagged the viral videos as 98% to 99% likely to be AI-generated or deepfakes. Claim: Videos show former Ako Bicol Representative Zaldy Co, who is being linked to anomalous flood control projects, roaming the streets of Paris. Rating: FALSE Why we fact-checked this: Several videos circulating on social media claim to show Co in public spaces abroad, specifically in Paris. A YouTube short posted on October 28 by the channel JooTV features Co allegedly being filmed on the street, with the former lawmaker attempting to block the camera before walking away. At around the 0:08 mark, an establishment in the background with the word “Paris” can be seen.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/newsbreak/fact-check/zaldy-co-paris-videos-ai-generated/","d":"2025-11-13","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-11-13","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-11-13","organization":"Various social media platforms and users","aiSystem":"AI detection tools","affectedGroup":"Former Ako Bicol Representative Zaldy Co","action":"Videos were flagged as 98% to 99% likely to be AI-generated or deepfakes","consequence":"Misidentification and false association with anomalous flood control projects"}},{"i":"R119","k":"France","p":"France","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Deezer says ai generated songs now make up 44 of daily uploads","s":"Deezer says nearly half of daily music uploads are now AI-generated, raising concerns around quality, fraud, and originality. Music streaming platform Deezer has revealed a sharp rise in AI-generated music on its platform, highlighting a growing shift in how songs are being created and distributed. According to a recent report , nearly 44% of all tracks uploaded to Deezer each day are now generated using artificial intelligence. The company says it is currently receiving close to 75,000 AI-generated songs daily, amounting to more than two million tracks per month. AI Music Is Flooding Platforms Faster Than Expected The scale of AI-generated music has increased rapidly over the past year.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/phones/deezer-says-ai-generated-songs-now-make-up-44-of-daily-uploads/","d":"2026-04-20","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-04-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-04-20","organization":"Deezer (music streaming platform)","aiSystem":"AI music generation technology","affectedGroup":"Music creators, listeners, and industry stakeholders","action":"Nearly 44% of daily music uploads are AI-generated","consequence":"Concerns about quality, fraud, and originality"}},{"i":"R493","k":"France","p":"France","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.8  Interpersonal / relational harm"],"t":"France police raid x offices paris elon musk summoned","s":"The French probe will now also investigate alleged complicity in the detention and diffusion of images of a child‑pornographic nature and the violation of a person’s image rights with sexually explicit deepfakes, among other potential crimes. PARIS, France – French police raided the offices of Elon Musk’s social media network X on Tuesday, February 3, and prosecutors ordered the tech billionaire to face questions in April related to a widening investigation into the platform, the Paris prosecutor’s office said. The raid is linked to a year-long investigation into suspected abuse of algorithms and fraudulent data extraction by X or its executives.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/social-media/france-police-raid-x-offices-paris-elon-musk-summoned/","d":"2026-02-03","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-02-03","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-02-03","organization":"French police, Paris prosecutor's office","aiSystem":"X (social media platform)","affectedGroup":"Elon Musk, X executives","action":"raided X's offices, summoned Elon Musk","consequence":"potential crimes including child pornography, violation of image rights, and fraudulent data extraction"}},{"i":"R518","k":"France","p":"France","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.8  Interpersonal / relational harm"],"t":"Grok ai chatbot scrutiny sexualized images women minors","s":"The issues stem from Grok digitally stripping clothing off images of women and some men when prompted by users, causing some governments to take action. MANILA, Philippines – The xAI artificial intelligence chatbot Grok is under scrutiny following allegations it was creating sexualized AI-generated images of women and minors on the X social media platform. A Reuters report reviewing content on X found more than 20 cases in which women — and some men — had images digitally stripped of clothing using the xAI chatbot Grok. French ministers have reported to prosecutors sexually explicit content generated by Grok, saying in a statement on Friday, January 2, the “sexual and sexist” content was “manifestly illegal.”","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/social-media/grok-ai-chatbot-scrutiny-sexualized-images-women-minors/","d":"2026-01-03","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-01-03","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-01-02","organization":"X social media platform","aiSystem":"xAI artificial intelligence chatbot Grok","affectedGroup":"women and some men","action":"digital stripping of clothing from images","consequence":"alleged creation of sexually explicit content"}},{"i":"R1320","k":"France","p":"France","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"The Paris prosecutor's office told the BBC it had \"taken note of the absence of the people…","s":"The Paris prosecutor's office told the BBC it had \"taken note of the absence of the people summoned\". Elon Musk has not attended a voluntary interview he was summoned to appear at in Paris, according to French authorities probing his platform X. The company's offices were raided by the Paris prosecutor's cyber-crime unit in February over suspected criminal offences related to content on the platform. Musk was given the date of 20 April for an interview as part of an investigation first launched in 2025 but later widened over concerns about X's chatbot Grok being used to create non-consensual sexual deepfake images.","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/crk151nn7j3o","d":"2026-04-20","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-04-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-04-20","organization":"Elon Musk, platform X","aiSystem":"platform X","affectedGroup":"Elon Musk","action":"given the date of 20 April for an interview","consequence":"denied access to voluntary interview"}},{"i":"R1383","k":"France","p":"France","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Anne, 53, was subject to nationwide ridicule after a TV show revealed how she paid out hug…","s":"Anne, 53, was subject to nationwide ridicule after a TV show revealed how she paid out huge sums to romance scammers pretending to be Brad Pitt. A French woman who was conned out of €830,000 (£700,000; $850,000) by scammers posing as actor Brad Pitt has faced a huge wave of mockery, leading French broadcaster TF1 to withdraw a programme about her. The primetime programme, which aired on Sunday, attracted national attention on interior designer Anne, 53, who thought she was in a relationship with Pitt for a year and a half. She has since told a popular French YouTube show that she was not \"crazy or a moron\": \"I just got played, I admit it, and that's why I came forward, because I am not the only one.\"","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/ckgnz8rw1xgo","d":"2025-01-15","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-01-15","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-01-15","organization":"French broadcaster TF1","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"Anne, 53","action":"aired a program about Anne","consequence":"nationwide ridicule and withdrawal of the program"}},{"i":"R1441","k":"France","p":"France","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"French govt confirms hack of at least 18m records from id document database","s":"France Titres has confirmed a data breach that has led to the sale of at least 18 million records from a national ID document database on the dark web. France’s government has confirmed that a database storing records of identity documents suffered a breach of millions of entries containing identity data. The hack of France Titres’ infrastructure led to the theft of between 18 and 19 million records associated with identity credentials like biometric passports, national ID cards and driver’s licenses. The quantity of records covers about a third of France’s adult population. Hackers known as “breach3d” and “ExtaseHunters” posted claims of the breach and offers to sell the records to a hacker forum, stating the stolen records come from a new breach, Cybernews reports.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202604/french-govt-confirms-hack-of-at-least-18m-records-from-id-document-database","d":"2026-04-23","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-04-23","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-04-23","organization":"France Titres","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"France's adult population","action":"Data breach of identity document database","consequence":"Records sold on the dark web"}},{"i":"R1444","k":"France","p":"France","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"French police use of real time facial recognition comes under scrutiny","s":"Facial matching software became available on police devices in January 2022. The devices are called NEO, and are issued to all police officers and gendarmes. Police in France are reportedly photographing individuals with police-issued mobile devices and using facial recognition developed by the German company Cognitec to match their faces against a criminal records database called TAJ, according to an investigation by non-profit media outlet Disclose. The practice is occurring outside the legal framework, as using facial recognition to cross-check against the TAJ during identity checks is not allowed: The French Code of Criminal Procedure prohibits “real-time” checks against the TAJ, as the database can only be used by specially authorized officers during an investigation, according to the report.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202603/french-police-use-of-real-time-facial-recognition-comes-under-scrutiny","d":"2026-03-23","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-03-23","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2022-01-01","organization":"French police and gendarmes","aiSystem":"Facial matching software","affectedGroup":"Individuals photographed by police","action":"Using facial recognition to match faces against criminal records","consequence":"Exposure to surveillance and potential misuse of data"}},{"i":"R1615","k":"France","p":"France","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"French politicians vow legal action on alleged illegal facial recognition use by police","s":"Left-wing populist party La France Insoumise are calling for a parliamentary commission of inquiry to be set up to dig into the matter. A recent report that the French national police has been using a facial recognition system from Israel illegally for the past eight years has unsettled politicians in the country with some of them saying they’re taking legal action on the matter. One of the political parties that has been irked by the development is left-wing populist party La France Insoumise, whose lawmakers are also calling for a parliamentary commission of inquiry to be set up to dig into the matter, per Actual News Magazine reporting Last week, allegations emerged that the French police had been using facial recognition technology since 2015, despite existing laws banning such a practice.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202311/french-politicians-vow-legal-action-on-alleged-illegal-facial-recognition-use-by-police","d":"2023-11-23","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-11-23","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-11-23","organization":"French national police","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"People identified or tracked using the technology","action":"Used facial recognition technology","consequence":"Exposure to surveillance and tracking"}},{"i":"R1659","k":"France","p":"France","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Jianwei xun the supposed philosopher behind the hypnocracy theory does not exist and is a product of artificial intelligence","s":"A collaboration between an essayist and two AI platforms produced a book that reflects on new forms of manipulation. The French city of Cannes hosted a roundtable discussion on February 14 called “ The Metamorphosis of Democracy - How Artificial Intelligence is Disrupting Digital Governance and Redefining Our Policy .” The debate was covered in an article by EL PAÍS after Gianluca Misuraca, Vice President of Technology Diplomacy at Inspiring Futures, introduced the concept of “hypnocracy” — a new form of manipulation outlined in a book by Jianwei Xun called Hypnocracy: Trump, Musk, and the New Architecture of Reality However, this Hong Kong philosopher does not exist, as revealed by Sabina Minardi, editor-in-chief of the Italian magazine L’Espresso The theory was created by essayist and editor Andrea Colamedici, who, although listed as a translator, is actually the co-author of the…","u":"https://english.elpais.com/technology/2025-04-07/jianwei-xun-the-supposed-philosopher-behind-the-hypnocracy-theory-does-not-exist-and-is-a-product-of-artificial-intelligence.html","d":"2025-04-08","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-04-08","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-02-14","organization":"Inspiring Futures","aiSystem":"AI platforms","affectedGroup":"Jianwei Xun, Andrea Colamedici","action":"Created and published a book","consequence":"Misrepresentation and false attribution"}},{"i":"R1725","k":"France","p":"France","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Elon musk grok x france b2960831","s":"The investigation is looking into alleged ‘complicity’ in possessing and spreading pornographic images of minors. Elon Musk has been summoned to Paris as part of an investigation into child sex abuse material and deepfakes on his social media platform X. He has been summoned for “voluntary interviews” on Monday, alongside Linda Yaccarino, X’s former CEO. The Paris prosecutor’s office said that other employees of the platform will be heard as witnesses throughout the week. It is unclear whether Musk and Yaccarino will travel to France. A spokesperson for X did not respond to questions and Yaccarino’s current company, eMed, did not answer a request sent to the press email.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/europe/elon-musk-grok-x-france-b2960831.html","d":"2026-04-20","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-04-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-04-20","organization":"X (Elon Musk's social media platform)","aiSystem":"Elon Musk's social media platform X","affectedGroup":"People who possess and spread pornographic images of minors","action":"Possessing and spreading pornographic images of minors","consequence":"Exposure to misleading content"}},{"i":"R1913","k":"France","p":"France","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Google fined 250m euros in france for breaching intellectual property rules","s":"Watchdog accuses US tech firm of not negotiating in ‘good faith’ with publishers over use of their content. Google has been fined €250m (£213m) by French regulators for breaching an agreement over paying media companies for reproducing their content online. France’s competition watchdog said on Wednesday that it was fining the US tech company for breaches linked to intellectual property rules related to news media publishers. The regulator also cited concerns about Google’s AI service. The competition authority said Google’s AI-powered chatbot Bard – since rebranded as Gemini – was trained on content from publishers and news agencies without notifying them.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2024/mar/20/google-fined-250m-euros-in-france-for-breaching-intellectual-property-rules","d":"2024-03-20","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-03-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-03-20","organization":"Google","aiSystem":"AI-powered chatbot Bard (later rebranded as Gemini)","affectedGroup":"News media publishers and news agencies","action":"Trained AI on content without notification","consequence":"Fine of €250m (£213m) imposed by French regulators"}},{"i":"R1944","k":"France","p":"France","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Up to 70 of streams of ai generated music on deezer are fraudulent says report","s":"According to the French streaming platform’s analysis, fraudsters use bots to listen to AI music and take the royalties. Up to seven out of 10 streams of artificial intelligence-generated music on the Deezer platform are fraudulent, according to the French streaming platform. The company said AI-made music accounts for just 0.5% of streams on the music streaming platform but its analysis shows that fraudsters are behind up to 70% of those streams. AI-generated music is a growing problem on streaming platforms. Fraudsters typically generate revenue on platforms such as Deezer by using bots to “listen” to AI-generated songs – and take the subsequent royalty payments, which become sizeable once spread across multiple tracks.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2025/jun/18/up-to-70-of-streams-of-ai-generated-music-on-deezer-are-fraudulent-says-report","d":"2025-06-18","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-06-18","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-06-18","organization":"Deezer","aiSystem":"AI-generated music","affectedGroup":"Fraudsters","action":"Used bots to listen to AI-generated music and take subsequent royalty payments","consequence":"Exposure of fraudulent streams accounting for up to 70%"}},{"i":"R2056","k":"France","p":"France","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Grok ai create sexually violent videos featuring women research finds","s":"AI tool also used to undress image of woman killed by ICE agent in US, says research. Elon Musk’s AI tool Grok has been used to create sexually violent and explicit video content featuring women, according to new research, as the British prime minister added to condemnation of images it has created. Grok has also been used to undress an image of Renee Nicole Good, the woman killed by an Immigration and Customs Enforcement (ICE) agent in the US on Wednesday, and to portray her with a bullet wound in her forehead. Research by AI Forensics, a Paris-based non-profit organisation, found about 800 images and videos created by the Grok Imagine app that included pornographic content.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/jan/09/grok-ai-create-sexually-violent-videos-featuring-women-research-finds","d":"2026-01-09","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-01-09","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-01-09","organization":"AI Forensics (non-profit organisation)","aiSystem":"Grok Imagine app","affectedGroup":"Women","action":"Created sexually violent and explicit video content","consequence":"Undressed image of a woman killed by ICE agent and portrayed her with a bullet wound"}},{"i":"R2077","k":"France","p":"France","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"French headquarters elon musk x raided paris cybercrime unit","s":"Prosecutors’ announcement comes amid a hardening of European attitudes to social media firms. Prosecutors have raided the French headquarters of Elon Musk’s social media platform X and summoned the tech billionaire and the company’s former chief executive for questioning as part of an investigation into alleged cybercrime. “A search is under way by the cybercrime unit of the Paris prosecutor’s office, the national police cyber unit and Europol,” the Paris prosecutors’ office said in a post on X on Tuesday, adding that it would no longer be publishing on the network. It said in a statement that Musk and Linda Yaccarino had been summoned for “voluntary questioning” in April in their capacity as “de facto and de jure managers of the X platform at the time of the events”.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/feb/03/french-headquarters-elon-musk-x-raided-paris-cybercrime-unit","d":"2026-02-03","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-02-03","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-02-03","organization":"Paris prosecutor's office, national police cyber unit, Europol","aiSystem":"Elon Musk's social media platform X","affectedGroup":"Elon Musk, Linda Yaccarino","action":"Raided headquarters, summoned for questioning","consequence":"Suspension of publishing on X"}},{"i":"R2265","k":"France","p":"France","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Google fined 250m euros in france for breaching intellectual property rules","s":"Watchdog accuses US tech firm of not negotiating in ‘good faith’ with publishers over use of their content. Google has been fined €250m (£213m) by French regulators for breaching an agreement over paying media companies for reproducing their content online. France’s competition watchdog said on Wednesday that it was fining the US tech company for breaches linked to intellectual property rules related to news media publishers. The regulator also cited concerns about Google’s AI service. The competition authority said Google’s AI-powered chatbot Bard – since rebranded as Gemini – was trained on content from publishers and news agencies without notifying them.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2024/mar/20/google-fined-250m-euros-in-france-for-breaching-intellectual-property-rules","d":"2024-03-20","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-03-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-03-20","organization":"Google","aiSystem":"AI-powered chatbot Bard (later rebranded as Gemini)","affectedGroup":"News media publishers and news agencies","action":"Trained AI on content without notification","consequence":"Fine of €250m (£213m) imposed by French regulators"}},{"i":"R2296","k":"France","p":"France","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Up to 70 of streams of ai generated music on deezer are fraudulent says report","s":"According to the French streaming platform’s analysis, fraudsters use bots to listen to AI music and take the royalties. Up to seven out of 10 streams of artificial intelligence-generated music on the Deezer platform are fraudulent, according to the French streaming platform. The company said AI-made music accounts for just 0.5% of streams on the music streaming platform but its analysis shows that fraudsters are behind up to 70% of those streams. AI-generated music is a growing problem on streaming platforms. Fraudsters typically generate revenue on platforms such as Deezer by using bots to “listen” to AI-generated songs – and take the subsequent royalty payments, which become sizeable once spread across multiple tracks.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2025/jun/18/up-to-70-of-streams-of-ai-generated-music-on-deezer-are-fraudulent-says-report","d":"2025-06-18","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-06-18","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-06-18","organization":"Deezer","aiSystem":"AI-generated music","affectedGroup":"Fraudsters","action":"Used bots to listen to AI-generated music and take subsequent royalty payments","consequence":"Exposure of fraudulent streams accounting for up to 70%"}},{"i":"R2020","k":"France","p":"France","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"French authorities look into holocaust denial posts elon musk grok ai","s":"X chatbot suggested gas chambers at Auschwitz-Birkenau were ‘designed for disinfection’ not mass executions Europe live – latest updates. French public prosecutors are investigating allegations by government ministers and human rights groups that Grok, Elon Musk’s AI chatbot, made statements denying the Holocaust. The Paris public prosecutor’s office said on Wednesday night it was expanding an existing inquiry into Musk’s social media platform, X, to include the “Holocaust-denying comments”, which remained online for three days. Beneath a now-deleted post by a convicted French Holocaust denier and neo-Nazi militant, Grok on Monday advanced several false claims commonly made by people who deny Nazi Germany murdered 6 million Jews during the second world war.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2025/nov/20/french-authorities-look-into-holocaust-denial-posts-elon-musk-grok-ai","d":"2025-11-20","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-11-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-11-20","organization":"French public prosecutors, Paris public prosecutor's office, X (Elon Musk's social media platform)","aiSystem":"Grok, Elon Musk’s AI chatbot","affectedGroup":"People who deny Nazi Germany murdered 6 million Jews during the second world war","action":"Advanced several false claims","consequence":"Exposure to misleading content"}},{"i":"R2408","k":"France","p":"France","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Grok ai create sexually violent videos featuring women research finds","s":"AI tool also used to undress image of woman killed by ICE agent in US, says research. Elon Musk’s AI tool Grok has been used to create sexually violent and explicit video content featuring women, according to new research, as the British prime minister added to condemnation of images it has created. Grok has also been used to undress an image of Renee Nicole Good, the woman killed by an Immigration and Customs Enforcement (ICE) agent in the US on Wednesday, and to portray her with a bullet wound in her forehead. Research by AI Forensics, a Paris-based non-profit organisation, found about 800 images and videos created by the Grok Imagine app that included pornographic content.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/jan/09/grok-ai-create-sexually-violent-videos-featuring-women-research-finds","d":"2026-01-09","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-01-09","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-01-09","organization":"AI Forensics (non-profit organisation)","aiSystem":"Grok Imagine app","affectedGroup":"Women","action":"Created sexually violent and explicit video content","consequence":"Undressed image of a woman killed by ICE agent and portrayed her with a bullet wound"}},{"i":"R2429","k":"France","p":"France","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"French headquarters elon musk x raided paris cybercrime unit","s":"Prosecutors’ announcement comes amid a hardening of European attitudes to social media firms. Prosecutors have raided the French headquarters of Elon Musk’s social media platform X and summoned the tech billionaire and the company’s former chief executive for questioning as part of an investigation into alleged cybercrime. “A search is under way by the cybercrime unit of the Paris prosecutor’s office, the national police cyber unit and Europol,” the Paris prosecutors’ office said in a post on X on Tuesday, adding that it would no longer be publishing on the network. It said in a statement that Musk and Linda Yaccarino had been summoned for “voluntary questioning” in April in their capacity as “de facto and de jure managers of the X platform at the time of the events”.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/feb/03/french-headquarters-elon-musk-x-raided-paris-cybercrime-unit","d":"2026-02-03","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-02-03","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-02-03","organization":"Paris prosecutor's office, national police cyber unit, Europol","aiSystem":"Elon Musk's social media platform X","affectedGroup":"Elon Musk, Linda Yaccarino","action":"Raided headquarters, summoned for questioning","consequence":"Suspension of publishing on X"}},{"i":"R2586","k":"France","p":"France","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"French police raid x investigation","s":"Investigations are ongoing in France and the UK. The Grok investigations are growing, as Paris prosecutors summoned Elon Musk and Linda Yaccarino to testify. A raid on X’s Paris office was carried out by the Paris prosecutor’s cybercrime unit on Tuesday with Europol and French police as part of an ongoing investigation that expanded in July to include Grok. As previously reported by Reuters , Elon Musk and former X CEO Linda Yaccarino were also summoned for hearings in April. The investigation, which was initially launched last year , is looking into several allegations against X and Grok, including complicity in possession and distribution of child pornography and denial of crimes against humanity (related to Holocaust denial content), along with claims that X was manipulating its algorithm and illegally extracting data.","u":"https://www.theverge.com/tech/873142/french-police-raid-x-investigation","d":"2026-02-03","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-02-03","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-02-03","organization":"Paris prosecutor's cybercrime unit, Europol, French police, Grok investigations","aiSystem":"X's platform","affectedGroup":"Elon Musk, Linda Yaccarino, X users, potential victims of child pornography, Holocaust deniers","action":"Carried out a raid on X’s Paris office","consequence":"Potential privacy invasion, illegal data extraction, investigation into manipulation of algorithm"}},{"i":"R2688","k":"France","p":"France","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Amazon fine employee surveillance france cnil gdpr privacy","s":"Amazon says its surveillance makes workers’ lives easier. Amazon says its constant surveillance system is actually necessary and standard industry practice. France’s National Commission on Informatics and Liberty (CNIL) has fined Amazon €32 million, or about $35 million USD, for “excessively intrusive” worker surveillance that the commission says puts “continuous pressure” on employees in Amazon’s warehouses. Amazon called its conclusions , which put the company in violation of the EU’s General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR), “factually incorrect.” Following an investigation prompted by press reports, CNIL said that Amazon’s systems measuring and restricting scanning speed (including a “stow machine gun” indicator that activates when workers are scanning too fast), along with the company’s data collection and retention practices, were “excessive” and violated several GDPR articles.","u":"https://www.theverge.com/2024/1/23/24048197/amazon-fine-employee-surveillance-france-cnil-gdpr-privacy","d":"2024-01-23","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-01-23","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-01-23","organization":"Amazon","aiSystem":"Surveillance system in Amazon warehouses","affectedGroup":"Workers in Amazon warehouses","action":"Constant surveillance and speed measurement","consequence":"Continuous pressure on employees, violation of GDPR"}},{"i":"R2797","k":"France","p":"France","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"The rise of the ray ban meta creep","s":"Between pickup artists and juvenile pranksters, the wearable device is becoming associated with pests of all kinds. Joy Hui Lin, a book researcher living in Paris, was walking through the trendy Le Marais district last summer when two male university students chased her down to ask about her outfit. Lin wasn’t surprised. It’s common for Instagram accounts to do street photography in the area and she prides herself on her fashion—that day, she was in “a nice sundress and a very big stylish hat,” she tells WIRED. “It was all very cute until the end of the conversation, when one of them was like, ‘So, these glasses have been recording this whole time.’” She clocked the device, a black-framed pair of Ray-Ban Meta smart glasses (commonly referred to as Meta Ray-Bans), which can record video from the user’s point of view.","u":"https://www.wired.com/story/the-rise-of-the-ray-ban-meta-creep/","d":"2026-03-23","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-03-23","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-03-23","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"Ray-Ban Meta smart glasses","affectedGroup":"Joy Hui Lin","action":"Recording video from the user's point of view","consequence":"Exposure to surveillance"}},{"i":"R2891","k":"France","p":"France","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.2  Allocative harm"],"t":"Algorithms policed welfare systems for years now theyre under fire for bias","s":"Human rights groups have launched a new legal challenge against the use of algorithms to detect error and fraud in France's welfare system, amid claims that single mothers are disproportionately affected. A coalition of human rights groups have today launched legal action against the French government over its use of algorithms to detect miscalculated welfare payments , alleging they discriminate against disabled people and single mothers. The algorithm, used since the 2010s, violates both European privacy rules and French anti-discrimination laws, argue the 15 groups involved in the case, including digital rights group La Quadrature du Net, Amnesty International, and Collectif Changer de Cap, a French group that campaigns against inequality.","u":"https://www.wired.com/story/algorithms-policed-welfare-systems-for-years-now-theyre-under-fire-for-bias/","d":"2024-10-16","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-10-16","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-10-16","organization":"French government","aiSystem":"Algorithms used to detect miscalculated welfare payments","affectedGroup":"Disabled people and single mothers","action":"Used algorithms to detect miscalculated welfare payments","consequence":"Discrimination and violation of privacy and anti-discrimination laws"}},{"i":"R1527","k":"Georgia","p":"Georgia","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Georgias surveillance cameras under scrutiny amid anti government protests","s":"A troubling aspect of the surveillance systems is the number of companies under U.S. sanctions involved in creating them. Over the past years, Georgia has installed more than 4,300 “smart” cameras on its streets. The surveillance networks – built with technology from NEC, Hikvision, Dahua and Papillon Systems – are now attracting attention in the Central Asian country which is currently going through its third month of anti-government protests. The Georgian government has not been transparent about its use of CCTV, opening questions on whether it is using surveillance systems to monitor dissidents and protestors, according to Radio Free Europe (RFE).","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202502/georgias-surveillance-cameras-under-scrutiny-amid-anti-government-protests","d":"2025-02-20","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-02-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-02-20","organization":"Georgian government","aiSystem":"surveillance systems","affectedGroup":"dissidents and protestors","action":"monitoring","consequence":"raising questions about misuse"}},{"i":"R1528","k":"Georgia","p":"Georgia","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Georgian nonprofit calls for investigation into use of frt to prosecute protestors","s":"The country’s Ministry of Internal Affairs is frequently using facial recognition cameras against protestors in criminal cases. A prominent nonprofit in the country of Georgia has called for an investigation into the use of facial recognition cameras. The Georgian Young Lawyers’ Association (GYLA) claims the country’s Ministry of Internal Affairs is frequently using facial recognition cameras against protestors in criminal cases. The GYLA says that the practice is “especially noticeable” in criminal cases initiated when protesters are blocking the road, and that the “only” evidence in such cases are photographs taken from facial recognition cameras.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202502/georgian-nonprofit-calls-for-investigation-into-use-of-frt-to-prosecute-protestors","d":"2025-02-12","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-02-12","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-02-12","organization":"Georgian Young Lawyers' Association (GYLA)","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"Peaceful assembly participants","action":"Used facial recognition cameras to identify protestors","consequence":"Exposure to surveillance and tracking"}},{"i":"R1582","k":"Georgia","p":"Georgia","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Uk police have used pimeyes facial recognition search tool over 2000 times","s":"Scotland Yard has been forced to impose a ban on use of facial recognition technology from PimEyes by Metropolitan police officers. It is becoming an apparent truth that, if the biometrics tools are there, people will use them. News out of the UK underlines this, as Scotland Yard has been forced to impose a ban on use of facial recognition technology from PimEyes by Metropolitan police officers, who records show have already accessed the software thousands of times. A report from i News says that data released under Freedom of Information (FOI) rules show that the PimEyes site, which is based in Tbilisi, Georgia, was visited from Metropolitan police computers 2,337 times.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202405/uk-police-have-used-pimeyes-facial-recognition-search-tool-over-2000-times","d":"2024-05-06","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-05-06","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-05-06","organization":"Metropolitan police officers, Scotland Yard","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology from PimEyes","affectedGroup":"People","action":"Used facial recognition technology","consequence":"Exposure to surveillance"}},{"i":"R2071","k":"Georgia","p":"Georgia","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.2  Land use and siting harm"],"t":"Georgia datacenters ai ban","s":"Southern state becoming ground zero in fight against rapid growth of facilities using huge amounts of energy and water. Lawmakers in several states are exploring passing laws that would put statewide bans in place on building new datacenters as the issue of the power-hungry facilities has moved to the center of economic and environmental concerns in the US. In Georgia a state lawmaker has introduced a bill proposing what could become the first statewide moratorium on new datacenters in America. The bill is one of at least three statewide moratoriums on datacenters introduced in state legislatures in the last week as Maryland and Oklahoma lawmakers are also considering similar measures.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/jan/26/georgia-datacenters-ai-ban","d":"2026-01-26","e":"Acute and chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-01-26","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-01-26","organization":"State lawmakers in Georgia, Maryland, and Oklahoma","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"Georgia and other states","action":"Introducing bills proposing statewide moratoriums on new datacenters","consequence":"Potential reduction in energy consumption and water usage by datacenters"}},{"i":"R2423","k":"Georgia","p":"Georgia","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.2  Land use and siting harm"],"t":"Georgia datacenters ai ban","s":"Southern state becoming ground zero in fight against rapid growth of facilities using huge amounts of energy and water. Lawmakers in several states are exploring passing laws that would put statewide bans in place on building new datacenters as the issue of the power-hungry facilities has moved to the center of economic and environmental concerns in the US. In Georgia a state lawmaker has introduced a bill proposing what could become the first statewide moratorium on new datacenters in America. The bill is one of at least three statewide moratoriums on datacenters introduced in state legislatures in the last week as Maryland and Oklahoma lawmakers are also considering similar measures.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/jan/26/georgia-datacenters-ai-ban","d":"2026-01-26","e":"Acute and chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-01-26","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-01-26","organization":"State lawmakers in Georgia, Maryland, and Oklahoma","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"Georgia and other states","action":"Introducing bills proposing statewide moratoriums on new datacenters","consequence":"Potential reduction in energy consumption and water usage by data centers"}},{"i":"R2829","k":"Georgia","p":"Georgia","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.2  Land use and siting harm"],"t":"The data center resistance has arrived","s":"A new report finds that local opposition to data centers skyrocketed in the second quarter of this year. On Election Day, Peter Hubbard was one of two Democratic candidates who took a decisive—and surprising—victory in Georgia. Hubbard was elected to the Georgia Public Service Commission, the body that regulates the state’s electric utility. It’s the first time Democrats have won statewide seats in statewide elections in Georgia in nearly two decades. Residents have complained for years about a series of rate hikes from the PSC. But during Hubbard’s campaigning, he noticed another topic coming up again and again with his future constituents.","u":"https://www.wired.com/story/the-data-center-resistance-has-arrived/","d":"2025-11-14","e":"Acute and chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-11-14","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-04","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"Residents","action":"Complained about rate hikes","consequence":"Concerns about data centers"}},{"i":"R910","k":"Germany","p":"Germany","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Surveillance video ai bosch azena","s":"A platform run by the Bosch-owned startup Azena has the potential to transform the surveillance camera industry. Experts worry it is ripe for abuse. Bosch, the German multinational most famous for its toasters, drills, and refrigerators, is also one of the world’s leading developers of surveillance cameras. Over the last three years, the company has poured tens of millions of euros into its own startup, Azena, which has the potential to completely transform the surveillance camera industry. Via Azena, Bosch has led the development of a line of surveillance cameras that relies on edge computing — where each camera has its own processor, operating system, and internet connection — to provide “smart” surveillance of people, objects, and places.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2022/02/11/surveillance-video-ai-bosch-azena/","d":"2022-02-11","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2022-02-11","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2022-02-11","organization":"Bosch (German multinational)","aiSystem":"Edge Computing-based Surveillance Cameras","affectedGroup":"People, Objects, Places","action":"Development and Deployment of Edge Computing-based Surveillance Cameras","consequence":"Potential for Abuse and Misuse"}},{"i":"R1064","k":"Germany","p":"Germany","b":["Data"],"h":["4.3  Data rights / consent violation"],"t":"A photographer tried to get his photos removed from an ai dataset he got an invoice instead","s":"A German stock photographer tried to get his photos removed from the AI-training LAION dataset. Lawyers replied that he owes $979 for making an unjustified copyright claim. A German stock photographer who asked to get his images removed from a dataset used to train AI image generators was not only met with a refusal from the dataset owner but also an invoice for $979 for filing an unjustified copyright claim. The photographer, Robert Kneschke , found out in February that his photographs were being used to train AI through a site called Have I Been Trained? This website allowed him to search through LAION-5B, which is a dataset of over 5.8 billion images owned by the non-profit Large-scale Artificial Intelligence Open Network (LAION).","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/a-photographer-tried-to-get-his-photos-removed-from-an-ai-dataset-he-got-an-invoice-instead/","d":"2023-04-28","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-04-28","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-02","organization":"Large-scale Artificial Intelligence Open Network (LAION)","aiSystem":"AI image generators","affectedGroup":"German stock photographer Robert Kneschke","action":"Photographer requested removal of images from LAION dataset","consequence":"Photographer received an invoice for $979 for filing an unjustified copyright claim"}},{"i":"R779","k":"Germany","p":"Germany","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"German magazine michael schumacher ai generated interview","s":"The F1 legend has not been seen in public since he suffered a brain injury in a skiing accident in December 2013. The publishers of a German magazine that ran an ‘interview’ with Michael Schumacher generated by artificial intelligence have sacked the editor and apologized to the Formula One great’s family. Seven-times world champion Schumacher, now 54, has not been seen in public since he suffered a serious brain injury in a skiing accident on a family holiday in the French Alps in December 2013. His family said this week that they were planning legal action against weekly magazine Die Aktuelle , owned by the Essen-based Funke media group.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/german-magazine-michael-schumacher-ai-generated-interview/","d":"2023-04-24","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-04-24","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-04-24","organization":"Die Aktuelle (weekly magazine) owned by Funke media group","aiSystem":"Artificial Intelligence","affectedGroup":"Michael Schumacher","action":"Generated an interview with Michael Schumacher","consequence":"Sacked the editor and apologized to Schumacher's family"}},{"i":"R780","k":"Germany","p":"Germany","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"German authors performers tougher chatgpt rules copyright concerns","s":"German groups representing more than 140,000 authors and performers urge for rules to protect against unauthorized use of materials, and non-transparent processing of content. Forty-two German associations and trade unions representing more than 140,000 authors and performers on Wednesday, April 19, urged the European Union to beef up draft artificial intelligence rules as they singled out the threat to their copyright from ChatGPT. Trade unions for the creative sector Verdi and DGB and associations for photographers, designers, journalists and illustrators set out their concerns in a letter to the European Commission, European Council and EU lawmakers.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/german-authors-performers-tougher-chatgpt-rules-copyright-concerns/","d":"2023-04-20","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-04-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-04-19","organization":"German groups representing authors and performers","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Authors and performers","action":"Urged for tougher AI rules to protect against unauthorized use of materials and non-transparent content processing","consequence":"Potential infringement of copyright rights"}},{"i":"R399","k":"Germany","p":"Germany","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Donald trump elon musk alternative fur deutschlandafd german elections","s":"“So where’s my German friends?” Donald Trump asked a fawning Mar-a-Lago crowd on Election Day, before flashing a grin and a thumbs up for a photo with a group of young men. The German friends in question: Fabrice Ambrosini, a former politician forced to resign after a video surfaced of him doing a Hitler salute; […]. The German friends in question: Fabrice Ambrosini, a former politician forced to resign after a video surfaced of him doing a Hitler salute; Leonard Jäger, a far-right influencer who has promoted the Reichsbürger movement, an extremist group behind a failed coup attempt in 2022; and Phillipp-Anders Rau, a candidate for Alternative für Deutschland (AfD), Germany’s far-right party.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2024/12/donald-trump-elon-musk-alternative-fur-deutschlandafd-german-elections/","d":"2024-12-21","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-12-21","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-12-21","organization":"Alternative für Deutschland (AfD)","aiSystem":"Social media platforms and online influencers","affectedGroup":"German citizens, particularly those sympathetic to far-right ideologies","action":"Donald Trump endorsed far-right politicians and influencers through social media posts","consequence":"Potential spread of extremist ideologies and incitement of violence"}},{"i":"R002","k":"Germany","p":"Germany","b":["Deployment Context / Weapons"],"h":["5.4  Supply chain / investor complicity harm"],"t":"Palestine action trial germany ulm 5 elbit","s":"The ‘Ulm 5’ are facing prison time over a break-in at an Israeli weapons firm, in a case experts warn could set a precedent for criminalizing direct action. In the early hours of Sept. 8, 2025, a group of activists wearing black hoodies that bore the red and white logo of Palestine Action broke into the offices of Israeli arms manufacturer Elbit Systems in Ulm, southern Germany. Once inside the compound, according to prosecutors, they damaged and partly destroyed furniture, windows, and technical equipment with axes; sprayed slogans on the walls; lit pyrotechnics; and chanted “Free, free Palestine” and “Germany finances, Israel bombs.”","u":"https://www.972mag.com/palestine-action-trial-germany-ulm-5-elbit/","d":"2026-04-28","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-04-28","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-09-08","organization":"Palestine Action","aiSystem":"Elbit Systems automated security system","affectedGroup":"Palestinian activists","action":"damaged and destroyed office equipment, sprayed slogans, lit pyrotechnics, chanted anti-Israel slogans","consequence":"potential criminal charges and prison time"}},{"i":"R538","k":"Germany","p":"Germany","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Openai may not use lyrics without license germany court","s":"The court in Munich rules that OpenAI could not use song lyrics without a license and presiding judge Elke Schwager orders the firm to pay damages for the use of copyrighted material. MUNICH, Germany – A German court on Tuesday, November 11, sided with the country’s music rights society GEMA in a closely watched copyright case against US-based artificial intelligence firm OpenAI. The court in Munich ruled that OpenAI could not use song lyrics without a license and presiding judge Elke Schwager ordered the firm to pay damages for the use of copyrighted material. GEMA had argued OpenAI’s chatbot ChatGPT reproduces lyrics from copyrighted German songs without authorization, and that its AI was trained on protected content from the repertoire of its roughly 100,000 members, who include best-selling musician Herbert Groenemeyer.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/openai-may-not-use-lyrics-without-license-germany-court/","d":"2025-11-11","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-11-11","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-11-11","organization":"GEMA (German Music Rights Society)","aiSystem":"OpenAI's ChatGPT AI","affectedGroup":"Best-selling musician Herbert Groenemeyer and his repertoire","action":"ChatGPT reproduces lyrics from copyrighted German songs without authorization","consequence":"OpenAI ordered to pay damages for the use of copyrighted material"}},{"i":"R442","k":"Germany","p":"Germany","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Google challenge german ruling liability ai generated false claims","s":"The challenge comes after a Munich court issued a landmark judgment against Google’s AI-generated summaries that appear above traditional search engine results, saying AI Overviews is the company’s own content. BRUSSELS, Belgium – Alphabet’s Google will appeal a German court ruling which said it is legally liable for false claims appearing in AI Overviews, the US tech company said on Friday, June 12, in a case that will likely impact other artificial intelligence developers. The challenge came after a Munich court issued a landmark judgment against Google’s AI-generated summaries that appear above traditional search engine results, saying that AI Overviews is the company’s own content.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/google-challenge-german-ruling-liability-ai-generated-false-claims/","d":"2026-06-14","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-06-14","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-06-12","organization":"Alphabet's Google","aiSystem":"AI Overviews","affectedGroup":"Artificial intelligence developers","action":"Google’s AI-generated summaries appeared above traditional search engine results","consequence":"Google is legally liable for false claims appearing in AI Overviews"}},{"i":"R492","k":"Germany","p":"Germany","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.3  Employment displacement / quality harm"],"t":"German voice actors boycott netflix artificial intelligence training concerns","s":"Voice actors, like many in the entertainment industry, are increasingly wary of artificial intelligence upending their livelihood and raising fresh copyright concerns. BERLIN, Germany – German voice actors have launched a grassroots boycott against Netflix over a contract clause that allows the streaming platform to use their recordings for AI training, a spokesperson for the VDS voice actors’ association told Reuters. Netflix’s global footprint has increased demand for dubbed content, with international hits such as South Korea’s Squid Game and Spain’s Money Heist attracting audiences far beyond their home markets.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/german-voice-actors-boycott-netflix-artificial-intelligence-training-concerns/","d":"2026-02-04","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-02-04","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-02-04","organization":"VDS (Voice Actors' Association)","aiSystem":"AI-powered voice synthesis technology","affectedGroup":"German voice actors","action":"Boycotting Netflix due to a contract clause allowing AI training on their recordings","consequence":"Loss of livelihood and potential copyright concerns"}},{"i":"R1165","k":"Germany","p":"Germany","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Laion datasets removed stanford csam child abuse","s":"The model is a massive part of the AI-ecosystem, used by Stable Diffusion and other major generative AI products. The removal follows discoveries made by Stanford researchers, who found thousands instances of suspected child sexual abuse material in the dataset. This piece is published with support from The Capitol Forum The LAION-5B machine learning dataset used by Stable Diffusion and other major AI products has been removed by the organization that created it after a Stanford study found that it contained 3,226 suspected instances of child sexual abuse material, 1,008 of which were externally validated. LAION told 404 Media on Tuesday that out of “an abundance of caution,” it was taking down its datasets, including LAION-5B and another called LAION-400M temporarily “to ensure they are safe before republishing them.\"","u":"https://www.404media.co/laion-datasets-removed-stanford-csam-child-abuse/","d":"2023-12-20","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-12-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-12-20","organization":"LAION","aiSystem":"LAION-5B machine learning dataset","affectedGroup":"users of Stable Diffusion and other major AI products","action":"removed the dataset","consequence":"exposed to online sexual violence"}},{"i":"R1257","k":"Germany","p":"Germany","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"While the art world is grappling with the idea of artificial intelligence producing art, s…","s":"While the art world is grappling with the idea of artificial intelligence producing art, some artists are already creating work and controversy. Boris Eldagsen turned down a prize for his AI-generated image, and started a whole new conversation about art There was a collective gasp across the art world last month when Boris Eldagsen refused a prestigious art prize, revealing that the image he'd submitted was generated by artificial intelligence. The Berlin-based artist was awarded the top gong in the Sony World Photography Awards for a haunting black and white image entitled The Electrician, part of his Pseudomnesia series.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2023-05-15/what-is-promptography-artificial-intelligence-boris-eldagsen/102305164","d":"2023-05-14","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-05-14","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-05-14","organization":"Sony World Photography Awards","aiSystem":"AI-generated art","affectedGroup":"Boris Eldagsen","action":"submitted an AI-generated image for a prestigious art prize","consequence":"refused the prize and started a conversation about art"}},{"i":"R1461","k":"Germany","p":"Germany","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.6  Quality of service harm"],"t":"Germanys eid cards issued without adequate identity verification","s":"Revelations about the country’s eID card program stir the embers of concern over its plans for an EU Digital Identity (EUDI) Wallet. Germany’s identity ecosystem is facing criticism on several fronts, as revelations about the country’s eID card program stir the embers of concern over its plans for an EU Digital Identity (EUDI) Wallet. Investigations by the Süddeutsche Zeitung news team show a critical vulnerability in Germany’s eID card, which can be obtained by non-German EEA citizens aged 16 and over for 37 euros and is effectively equivalent to a German digital identity card, enabling the holder to many of the same benefits (but not travel).","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202512/germanys-eid-cards-issued-without-adequate-identity-verification","d":"2025-12-15","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-12-15","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-12-15","organization":"Germany's identity ecosystem","aiSystem":"eID card program","affectedGroup":"Non-German EEA citizens aged 16 and over","action":"Obtaining eID cards","consequence":"Exposure to identity verification vulnerabilities"}},{"i":"R1476","k":"Germany","p":"Germany","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Germany considers allowing face biometric web searches by police watchdog critical","s":"While German authorities believe this can be done without necessarily creating a database, the technical report recently released thinks that’s not feasible. An expert report commissioned by AlgorithmWatch, a European digital rights organization, has pointed out the technical and legal issues with a move by Germany to expand police powers by allowing the matching of faces against photos publicly available on the internet. There’ve been discussions in Germany in this regard as authorities are considering legislation that will make it possible for the police to conduct live biometric facial searches against online photos on social media platforms like Facebook, Instagram or LinkedIn.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202510/germany-considers-allowing-face-biometric-web-searches-by-police-watchdog-critical","d":"2025-10-16","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2025-10-16","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-10-16","organization":"German authorities","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"Individuals whose faces are publicly available on social media","action":"Conduct live biometric facial searches against online photos","consequence":"Exposure to surveillance and tracking"}},{"i":"R1503","k":"Germany","p":"Germany","b":["Data"],"h":["4.3  Data rights / consent violation"],"t":"World pauses german operations for orb update amid regulatory faceoff","s":"World is facing a potential cease-and-desist order in the Philippines, and has put its iris scanning stations on hold in Germany. At the moment, the Germany section in World’s online search tool for finding an iris scanning Orb says, “We’re making improvements behind the scenes. Our Orbs will return shortly.” According to DL News, that return will include the opening of a flagship store in Munich. World appears to have no plans to stop operating in Germany. However, its promised updates will have to satisfy German regulators. In December 2024, the country’s data protection authority, the Bavarian State Office for Data Protection Supervision (BayLDA), concluded that World’s practices posed “fundamental data protection risks for a large number of data subjects,” and as such did not comply with the European Union’s General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR).","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202507/world-pauses-german-operations-for-orb-update-amid-regulatory-faceoff","d":"2025-07-11","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-07-11","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-07-11","organization":"World","aiSystem":"iris scanning technology","affectedGroup":"data subjects","action":"made improvements behind the scenes and promised updates","consequence":"satisfy German regulators' requirements"}},{"i":"R1569","k":"Germany","p":"Germany","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Germanys live facial recognition system could be unconstitutional data watchdog","s":"The police in the German state of Saxony have been using a surveillance system called Personal Identification System (PerIS), created by Opto Precision. A real-time facial recognition system used by German police to track criminals has reportedly been deployed without the knowledge of the local data protection agency and could be against the country’s constitution. The police in the German state of Saxony have been using a surveillance system called Personal Identification System (PerIS), created by Opto Precision The system was first introduced as a pilot project in 2019 to combat serious cross-border crime. Details of the system, however, have been kept under wraps until March when a parliamentary inquiry uncovered its deployment.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202406/germanys-live-facial-recognition-system-could-be-unconstitutional-data-watchdog","d":"2024-06-10","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-06-10","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-06-10","organization":"police in the German state of Saxony","aiSystem":"real-time facial recognition system","affectedGroup":"criminals","action":"deployed without the knowledge of the local data protection agency","consequence":"could be against the country’s constitution"}},{"i":"R1576","k":"Germany","p":"Germany","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Biometric surveillance testing data sparks ethics concerns in germany","s":"Even the tests for Germany’s adoption of real-time facial recognition are prompting criticism the country is failing to meet data privacy and ethics standards. Even the tests for Germany’s adoption of real-time facial recognition are prompting criticism that the country is failing to meet the data privacy and ethics standard it has set for itself. Following the announcement that German law enforcement will be utilizing high-definition cameras and live facial recognition to apprehend suspects, the country is witnessing a growth in anxiety regarding the proliferation of biometric surveillance technologies. The new development, following the enactment of the European Union’s AI Act , is prompting debates on privacy, civil liberties, and the ethical implications of such systems.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202405/biometric-surveillance-testing-data-sparks-ethics-concerns-in-germany","d":"2024-05-14","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-05-14","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-05-14","organization":"German law enforcement","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"People","action":"Used facial recognition to apprehend suspects","consequence":"Growth in anxiety regarding the proliferation of biometric surveillance technologies"}},{"i":"R1968","k":"Germany","p":"Germany","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.3  Employment displacement / quality harm"],"t":"Tiktok trust safety team moderators ai","s":"TikTok workers in Berlin are striking over mass layoffs amid company’s global push to replace moderators with AI. TikTok workers in Germany are holding strikes over mass layoffs of the company’s trust and safety team. The social media behemoth said it is planning to dismantle its entire Berlin moderation team, which removes harmful content from the platform, and outsource the work to artificial intelligence and contract workers. This means the dismissal of 150 employees. The trade union that represents the TikTok workers, ver.di, has been pushing to negotiate with TikTok over the past few weeks. Kalle Kunkel, a ver.di spokesperson for the Berlin-Brandenburg region, said the union sent a list of demands to TikTok regarding severance for the affected employees and an extension of the layoff notice period to one year.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2025/aug/10/tiktok-trust-safety-team-moderators-ai","d":"2025-08-10","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-08-10","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-08-10","organization":"TikTok","aiSystem":"Artificial intelligence and contract workers","affectedGroup":"TikTok workers in Berlin and Germany","action":"Mass layoffs of the company’s trust and safety team","consequence":"Dismissal of 150 employees"}},{"i":"R2014","k":"Germany","p":"Germany","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Chatgpt violated copyright laws german court rules","s":"OpenAI ordered to pay undisclosed damages for training its language models on artists’ work without permission. A court in Munich has ruled that OpenAI’s chatbot ChatGPT violated German copyright laws by using hits from top-selling musicians to train its language models in what creative industry advocates described as a landmark European ruling. The Munich regional court sided in favour of Germany’s music rights society GEMA, which said ChatGPT had harvested protected lyrics by popular artists to “learn” from them. The collecting society GEMA, which manages the rights of composers, lyricists and music publishers and has approximately 100,000 members, filed the case against OpenAI in November 2024.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2025/nov/11/chatgpt-violated-copyright-laws-german-court-rules","d":"2025-11-11","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-11-11","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-11-11","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Popular artists","action":"Used protected lyrics to train language models","consequence":"Ordered to pay undisclosed damages"}},{"i":"R2129","k":"Germany","p":"Germany","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Penguin sue openai chatgpt german childrens book kokosnuss","s":"Publisher alleges AI research company’s chatbot violated its copyright over Coconut the Little Dragon series. Penguin Random House has filed a lawsuit against OpenAI, alleging its chatbot ChatGPT violated copyright by mimicking and reproducing the content of a popular series of German children’s books. The lawsuit, which was filed on Friday with a Munich court against OpenAI’s Ireland-based European subsidiary, states Penguin Random House’s legal team had prompted ChatGPT to write a story in the vein of Penguin author and illustrator Ingo Siegner’s Coconut the Little Dragon series. In response to the prompt “Can you write a children’s book in which Coconut the Dragon is on Mars”, the chatbot generated text and images the publishing group said were “virtually indistinguishable from the original”.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/mar/31/penguin-sue-openai-chatgpt-german-childrens-book-kokosnuss","d":"2026-03-31","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-03-31","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-03-31","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Penguin Random House and Ingo Siegner","action":"Generated text and images mimicking and reproducing copyrighted material","consequence":"Legal action initiated by Penguin Random House"}},{"i":"R2320","k":"Germany","p":"Germany","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.3  Employment displacement / quality harm"],"t":"Tiktok trust safety team moderators ai","s":"TikTok workers in Berlin are striking over mass layoffs amid company’s global push to replace moderators with AI. TikTok workers in Germany are holding strikes over mass layoffs of the company’s trust and safety team. The social media behemoth said it is planning to dismantle its entire Berlin moderation team, which removes harmful content from the platform, and outsource the work to artificial intelligence and contract workers. This means the dismissal of 150 employees. The trade union that represents the TikTok workers, ver.di, has been pushing to negotiate with TikTok over the past few weeks. Kalle Kunkel, a ver.di spokesperson for the Berlin-Brandenburg region, said the union sent a list of demands to TikTok regarding severance for the affected employees and an extension of the layoff notice period to one year.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2025/aug/10/tiktok-trust-safety-team-moderators-ai","d":"2025-08-10","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-08-10","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-08-10","organization":"TikTok","aiSystem":"Artificial intelligence and contract workers","affectedGroup":"TikTok workers in Berlin and Germany","action":"Mass layoffs of the company’s trust and safety team","consequence":"Dismissal of 150 employees"}},{"i":"R2366","k":"Germany","p":"Germany","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Chatgpt violated copyright laws german court rules","s":"OpenAI ordered to pay undisclosed damages for training its language models on artists’ work without permission. A court in Munich has ruled that OpenAI’s chatbot ChatGPT violated German copyright laws by using hits from top-selling musicians to train its language models in what creative industry advocates described as a landmark European ruling. The Munich regional court sided in favour of Germany’s music rights society GEMA, which said ChatGPT had harvested protected lyrics by popular artists to “learn” from them. The collecting society GEMA, which manages the rights of composers, lyricists and music publishers and has approximately 100,000 members, filed the case against OpenAI in November 2024.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2025/nov/11/chatgpt-violated-copyright-laws-german-court-rules","d":"2025-11-11","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-11-11","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-11-11","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"Language models trained on copyrighted works","affectedGroup":"Popular artists and their rights holders","action":"Training language models on copyrighted lyrics","consequence":"Damages awarded to GEMA"}},{"i":"R2481","k":"Germany","p":"Germany","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Penguin sue openai chatgpt german childrens book kokosnuss","s":"Publisher alleges AI research company’s chatbot violated its copyright over Coconut the Little Dragon series. Penguin Random House has filed a lawsuit against OpenAI, alleging its chatbot ChatGPT violated copyright by mimicking and reproducing the content of a popular series of German children’s books. The lawsuit, which was filed on Friday with a Munich court against OpenAI’s Ireland-based European subsidiary, states Penguin Random House’s legal team had prompted ChatGPT to write a story in the vein of Penguin author and illustrator Ingo Siegner’s Coconut the Little Dragon series. In response to the prompt “Can you write a children’s book in which Coconut the Dragon is on Mars”, the chatbot generated text and images the publishing group said were “virtually indistinguishable from the original”.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/mar/31/penguin-sue-openai-chatgpt-german-childrens-book-kokosnuss","d":"2026-03-31","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-03-31","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-03-31","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Penguin Random House and Ingo Siegner","action":"Generated text and images mimicking and reproducing copyrighted material","consequence":"Legal action initiated by Penguin Random House"}},{"i":"R2766","k":"Germany","p":"Germany","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"A court has ruled that google is liable for false statements generated by ai overviews","s":"The ruling holds that a company that designs, trains, operates, and manages an AI system must assume legal liability for any damages caused by the responses it generates. A local court in Germany has issued a ruling that could reshape the operation of search engines and artificial-intelligence-based chatbots worldwide. The Munich Regional Court preliminarily ruled that Google is liable for a series of false statements generated by its AI Overviews feature, requiring the company to prevent the dissemination of erroneous or inaccurate claims through its search engine. The ruling stems from a case first reported by the Decoder, in which two publishers discovered that Google’s AI-generated summaries linked them, in certain searches, to questionable business practices, scams, and subscription-related frauds, without any basis for doing so.","u":"https://www.wired.com/story/a-court-has-ruled-that-google-is-liable-for-false-statements-generated-by-ai-overviews/","d":"2026-06-13","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-06-13","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-06-13","organization":"Google","aiSystem":"AI-generated summaries","affectedGroup":"Publishers","action":"Generated and disseminated false statements","consequence":"Exposure to questionable business practices, scams, and subscription-related frauds"}},{"i":"R2925","k":"Germany","p":"Germany","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"Meta racist ads germany eu elections","s":"EXCLUSIVE: Campaign ads published on Facebook and Instagram ahead of the EU elections by AfD, Germany's far-right party, blame immigrants for crime and sexual violence. Earlier this month, a German court ruled that the country’s nationalist far-right party, Alternative for Germany (AfD), was potentially “extremist” and could warrant surveillance by the country’s intelligence apparatus. Campaign ads placed by AfD have been allowed to appear on Facebook and Instagram anyway, according to a new report from the nonprofit advocacy organization Ekō, shared exclusively with WIRED. Researchers found 23 ads from the party that accrued 472,000 views on Facebook and Instagram and appear to violate Meta’s own policies around hate speech.","u":"https://www.wired.com/story/meta-racist-ads-germany-eu-elections/","d":"2024-05-29","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-05-29","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-05-29","organization":"Alternative for Germany (AfD)","aiSystem":"Facebook and Instagram ad systems","affectedGroup":"Immigrants","action":"Placed campaign ads blaming immigrants for crime and sexual violence","consequence":"Exposure to misleading content"}},{"i":"R3032","k":"Germany","p":"Germany","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.5  Human computer interaction harm"],"t":"They watched youtuber with tourettes then adopted his tics","s":"Hundreds of people are displaying similar behaviors to that of YouTube star Jan Zimmermann. Do they have a disorder or something more mysterious? Kirsten Müller-Vahl had a major mystery on her hands. It was June 2019 and Müller-Vahl, a psychiatrist at Hannover Medical School in Germany and head of its Tourette’s outpatient department, was being inundated by patients with tics unlike anything she had seen before. Not only were the tics complex in nature, involving several muscle groups, even more bizarrely the symptoms of the patients were remarkably similar. “The symptoms were identical. Not only similar, but identical,” she says.","u":"https://www.wired.com/story/they-watched-youtuber-with-tourettes-then-adopted-his-tics/","d":"2021-09-11","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-09-11","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"June 2019","organization":"Hannover Medical School","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"Patients with tics","action":"Diagnosed patients with Tourette's syndrome","consequence":"Misdiagnosis leading to inappropriate treatment"}},{"i":"R1586","k":"Greece","p":"Greece","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Eu data protection bodies bring the gavel down on biometric privacy violations","s":"DPAs in Italy, Greece and Spain have reacted to biometrics implementations with a warning against Worldcoin, in the former case, and fines in the latter two. Fines and warnings are flying in the EU, as national data privacy watchdogs dole out disciplinary action for violations of European data protection law through their use of biometrics. Italy’s data protection agency, Garante per la protezione dei dati personali, has sent a warning to Worldcoin , saying that the biometric identity firm’s iris scanning operations, in theory, “would in all likelihood violate EU regulation (GDPR), with all the sanctioning consequences provided for by the legislation.”","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202404/eu-data-protection-bodies-bring-the-gavel-down-on-biometric-privacy-violations","d":"2024-04-11","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-04-11","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-04-11","organization":"Worldcoin","aiSystem":"Biometric identity firm's iris scanning operations","affectedGroup":"Individuals whose biometric data is collected","action":"Collection and processing of iris scan data","consequence":"Potential GDPR violations and sanctions"}},{"i":"R1338","k":"Guernsey","p":"Guernsey","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Guernsey's Victim Support and Witness Service says the increase in victims is 'concerning'","s":"Guernsey's Victim Support and Witness Service says the increase in victims is 'concerning'. More victims of intimate deepfake imagery are seeking support from Guernsey's Victim Support and Witness Service. Jenny Murphy, who manages the service, said she was concerned by the increase in reports of deepfakes, image or video altered using Artificial Intelligence (AI) to replace a person's face or voice which is increasingly being used to create sexual images. At present in Guernsey, sharing intimate images without consent is an offence but asking an AI tool to create them is not. The Home Affairs Committee is looking to change this through a proposed change in the Sexual Offences Law 2020.","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/czxp0598glyo","d":"2026-01-14","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-01-14","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-01-14","organization":"Guernsey's Victim Support and Witness Service, Home Affairs Committee","aiSystem":"AI-generated deepfake technology","affectedGroup":"Victims of intimate deepfake imagery","action":"AI-generated sexual images","consequence":"Increased reporting of victims seeking support"}},{"i":"R792","k":"Honduras","p":"Honduras","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Honduras surveillance drug trade coda story","s":"This is the story of what happens when surveillance companies sell their products to a government known to be carrying out widescale human rights abuses against its people, and of the potential reform to this surveillance apparatus. Hery Flores kept calm when officers approached him as he departed a Tegucigalpa gas station in the early summer of 2021. Only one was in a blue police uniform. The others wore jeans and button down shirts — typical attire for police investigators in Honduras’ capital city. But then they slapped on handcuffs without giving him time to react. Later he realized they never showed him an arrest warrant.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/features/honduras-surveillance-drug-trade-coda-story/","d":"2023-02-15","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-02-15","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-06","organization":"Surveillance companies","aiSystem":"Surveillance technology","affectedGroup":"Individuals in Honduras","action":"Handcuffing without showing an arrest warrant","consequence":"Human rights abuses and lack of legal justification"}},{"i":"R1574","k":"Hong Kong S.A.R.","p":"Hong Kong S.A.R.","b":["Data"],"h":["4.3  Data rights / consent violation"],"t":"Worldcoin ordered to stop operating in hong kong","s":"Hong Kong’s privacy office releases the results of its investigation into Worldcoin, and found that the iris biometrics project runs afoul of data privacy laws. Hong Kong’s privacy office has announced the results of its investigation into Worldcoin , and found that the iris biometrics project runs afoul of data privacy laws. A release from the Privacy Commissioner for Personal Data (PCPD) says that Worldcoin’s activities contravene several of Hong Kong’s Data Privacy Principles (DPP) and that “the face and iris images collected by the Worldcoin project were unnecessary and excessive .” As a result, the commissioner has directed Worldcoin to cease all iris scanning operations in Hong Kong.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202405/worldcoin-ordered-to-stop-operating-in-hong-kong","d":"2024-05-23","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-05-23","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-05-23","organization":"Worldcoin","aiSystem":"iris biometrics project","affectedGroup":"Hong Kong residents","action":"ran afoul of data privacy laws","consequence":"cease all iris scanning operations"}},{"i":"R1621","k":"Hong Kong S.A.R.","p":"Hong Kong S.A.R.","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"Deepfake image scams rise experts say better detection needed for darker skin","s":"The latest real-world example of deepfake fraud comes from a criminal gang in Hong Kong. The threat is global, but detection systems may not be diverse enough. Deepfake fraud’s still rising, with the latest example uncovered in Hong Kong. Meanwhile, insiders warn that detection tools must improve at examining darker skin. Hong Kong police have cracked the first case in that financial hub involving deepfakes to deceive banks and other lenders. Police arrested six people who doctored eight stolen images alongside pilfered Hong Kong ID cards and proofs of address and income. The suspects allegedly used fraudulent documents to apply for at least 20 loans online.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202308/deepfake-image-scams-rise-experts-say-better-detection-needed-for-darker-skin","d":"2023-08-29","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-08-29","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-08-29","organization":"Criminal gang in Hong Kong","aiSystem":"Deepfake image generation technology","affectedGroup":"People applying for loans","action":"Used deepfake images to create fraudulent documents","consequence":"Fraudulent loan applications submitted"}},{"i":"R1931","k":"Hong Kong S.A.R.","p":"Hong Kong S.A.R.","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Uk engineering arup deepfake scam hong kong ai video","s":"Hong Kong employee was duped into sending cash to criminals by AI-generated video call Business live – latest updates. The British engineering company Arup has confirmed it was the victim of a deepfake fraud after an employee was duped into sending HK$200m (£20m) to criminals by an artificial intelligence-generated video call. Hong Kong police said in February that a worker at a then-unnamed company had been tricked into transferring vast sums by people on a hoax call “posing as senior officers of the company”. Arup said in a statement that it was the company involved, confirming that at the beginning of the year it had “notified the police about an incident of fraud in Hong Kong”.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/article/2024/may/17/uk-engineering-arup-deepfake-scam-hong-kong-ai-video","d":"2024-05-17","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-05-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-01","organization":"Arup (British engineering company)","aiSystem":"AI-generated deepfake technology","affectedGroup":"Employee of Arup","action":"Sent cash via an AI-generated video call","consequence":"Received financial loss of HK$200m (£20m)"}},{"i":"R2283","k":"Hong Kong S.A.R.","p":"Hong Kong S.A.R.","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Uk engineering arup deepfake scam hong kong ai video","s":"Hong Kong employee was duped into sending cash to criminals by AI-generated video call Business live – latest updates. The British engineering company Arup has confirmed it was the victim of a deepfake fraud after an employee was duped into sending HK$200m (£20m) to criminals by an artificial intelligence-generated video call. Hong Kong police said in February that a worker at a then-unnamed company had been tricked into transferring vast sums by people on a hoax call “posing as senior officers of the company”. Arup said in a statement that it was the company involved, confirming that at the beginning of the year it had “notified the police about an incident of fraud in Hong Kong”.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/article/2024/may/17/uk-engineering-arup-deepfake-scam-hong-kong-ai-video","d":"2024-05-17","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-05-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-01","organization":"Arup (British engineering company)","aiSystem":"AI-generated deepfake technology","affectedGroup":"Employee of Arup","action":"Sent cash via an AI-generated video call","consequence":"Received financial loss of HK$200m (£20m)"}},{"i":"R056","k":"Hungary","p":"Hungary","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Yet another study says ai is bad for elections and the rabbit hole gets worse","s":"ChatGPT and Gemini failed to reliably match voters with Hungarian parties, adding another warning as campaigns and manipulators learn how easily AI answers can be shaped. Another election, another study has concluded that asking an AI chatbot for political guidance is a spectacularly bad idea. Research conducted during Hungary’s 2026 parliamentary election found that ChatGPT and Google Gemini offered not just inaccurate but also inconsistent and unreliable voting advice The chatbots misclassified voter profiles, overlooked relevant parties, recommended parties absent from the ballot, and sometimes produced materially different answers when given the same information repeatedly.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/cool-tech/yet-another-study-says-ai-is-bad-for-elections-and-the-rabbit-hole-gets-worse/","d":"2026-07-21","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2026-07-21","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07-21","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"ChatGPT and Google Gemini","affectedGroup":"Hungarian voters","action":"Provided political guidance through AI chatbots","consequence":"Inaccurate, inconsistent, and unreliable voting advice"}},{"i":"R1323","k":"Hungary","p":"Hungary","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Videos have targeted Viktor Orbán's election rival, who could unseat him after 16 years in…","s":"Videos have targeted Viktor Orbán's election rival, who could unseat him after 16 years in office. Warning: This piece refers to scenes of violence from the start When a video went viral appearing to show a Hungarian soldier's execution, its disturbing nature came as a shock to anyone who saw it. Ahead of pivotal Hungarian elections on Sunday 12 April, the AI-generated, fake clip was posted on the social media accounts of Prime Minister Viktor Orbán's Fidesz party in February. It tells the story of a young girl at a window yearning for her father's return home from war, and then the video cuts to him - blindfolded, bound by the hands, and then shot by his captors.","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c5yj97yd6v5o","d":"2026-04-05","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-04-05","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-04-05","organization":"Prime Minister Viktor Orbán's Fidesz party","aiSystem":"AI-generated, fake clip","affectedGroup":"Viktor Orbán's political rivals and supporters","action":"Posted videos on social media accounts","consequence":"Exposure to misleading content"}},{"i":"R1693","k":"Hungary","p":"Hungary","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Budapest free to celebrate pride again but orban surveillance machine still in place","s":"Large trucks equipped with biometric cameras were stationed around last year's Budapest Pride march to discourage participation. The objective was not only punishment, but deterrence. Despite the new government, this logic remains embedded in Hungarian law today. This year, Hungarians can celebrate Pride openly. For the first time in history, there are rainbow flags on the streets and bridges of the Hungarian capital. The police have officially acknowledged this year’s Budapest Pride march on Saturday (27 June), marking a sharp departure from the previous government’s hostility. After years of attacks on LGBTQIA+ people and on the freedom of assembly, this change matters.","u":"https://euobserver.com/224022/budapest-free-to-celebrate-pride-again-but-orban-surveillance-machine-still-in-place/","d":"2026-06-25","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-06-25","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-06-27","organization":"Hungarian police","aiSystem":"Biometric cameras","affectedGroup":"LGBTQIA+ individuals","action":"Monitored participants in peaceful assemblies","consequence":"Discriminatory treatment and harassment"}},{"i":"R2191","k":"Hungary","p":"Hungary","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Election voting advice ai chatbots inaccurate unreliable hungary","s":"Research during Hungary election shows AI recommended parties not running and gave highly volatile answers to identical prompts. AI chatbots provide inaccurate, inconsistent and unreliable guidance to voters asking which party they should back, a study suggests, often recommending the wrong party, not mentioning the right one, or listing parties not even running. “The results raise serious concerns about the reliability of general-purpose AI systems in electoral contexts,” the study, published by the civil liberties group Liberties and based on research during this year’s Hungarian parliamentary elections, concluded.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/jul/21/election-voting-advice-ai-chatbots-inaccurate-unreliable-hungary","d":"2026-07-21","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-21","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07-21","organization":"Hungary's political parties and election authorities","aiSystem":"AI chatbots","affectedGroup":"Voters","action":"Provided inaccurate, inconsistent and unreliable guidance to voters","consequence":"Recommended the wrong party, not mentioned the right one, or listed parties not even running"}},{"i":"R2543","k":"Hungary","p":"Hungary","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Election voting advice ai chatbots inaccurate unreliable hungary","s":"Research during Hungary election shows AI recommended parties not running and gave highly volatile answers to identical prompts. AI chatbots provide inaccurate, inconsistent and unreliable guidance to voters asking which party they should back, a study suggests, often recommending the wrong party, not mentioning the right one, or listing parties not even running. “The results raise serious concerns about the reliability of general-purpose AI systems in electoral contexts,” the study, published by the civil liberties group Liberties and based on research during this year’s Hungarian parliamentary elections, concluded.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/jul/21/election-voting-advice-ai-chatbots-inaccurate-unreliable-hungary","d":"2026-07-21","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-21","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07-21","organization":"Hungary's political parties and election authorities","aiSystem":"AI chatbots","affectedGroup":"Voters","action":"Provided guidance to voters on which party to support","consequence":"Recommended incorrect parties, failed to mention correct ones, listed non-running parties"}},{"i":"R1143","k":"India","p":"India","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Facial recognition is out of control in india","s":"The country’s use of biometric data against activists and “hooligans” paints a grim picture of how the technology is threatening human rights. In May 2021, during India’s second wave of COVID-19, SQ Masood and his father-in-law were returning home on his silver-coloured motor scooter in Hyderabad, in the Indian state of Telangana. While driving through the busy lanes of Shahran, a Muslim-dominated neighborhood, they were pulled over by two police officers. Masood wasn’t concerned at first because he assumed that he would be asked to show his driver’s license or the vehicle registration card. So he and his father-in-law cooperated and they got off the motor scooter.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/facial-recognition-is-out-of-control-in-india/","d":"2022-06-13","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2022-06-13","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"May 2021","organization":"Indian police force","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"Activists and civilians","action":"Facial recognition technology was used to identify and detain individuals","consequence":"Misuse of technology led to the arrest of innocent individuals"}},{"i":"R1154","k":"India","p":"India","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.8  Interpersonal / relational harm"],"t":"India instagram sextortion phishing deepfake porn scam","s":"The police say it signals a new wave of cyber crimes. In a new sex extortion scam reported in India, blackmailers follow you on Instagram, make incessant video calls until you pick up, and then demand you pay them a hefty sum of money or else they’d slide into your friends’ and family’s DMs with deepfake porn featuring your face. Police have narrowed down on an alleged gang that operates at the tri-junction of the Indian states of Uttar Pradesh, Haryana and Rajasthan. Cops say such attacks have not only become increasingly common but also signal a new wave of phishing attacks. Earlier, police investigating cyber crimes found that the tri-junction location creates a “black spot” for mobile networks, where the presence of multiple telecommunication towers makes it difficult to trace the exact location of a device.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/india-instagram-sextortion-phishing-deepfake-porn-scam/","d":"2021-09-07","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-09-07","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-09-07","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"Facial Recognition Technology","affectedGroup":"Individuals","action":"Blackmailers use deepfake technology to generate pornographic content featuring victims' faces","consequence":"Victims receive threatening messages demanding payment or their personal information is shared with others"}},{"i":"R1136","k":"India","p":"India","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.8  Interpersonal / relational harm"],"t":"Fake porn videos deepfakes of bollywood actors celebs","s":"Deepfake porn videos with Bollywood actors are all over desi porn sites. We dive into why they’re so popular and what this popularity means. Vijash, a 29-year-old mechanical engineer from Surat in the Indian state of Gujarat, who preferred to be known by only his first name for this story, believed for the longest time that the videos he was watching that featured his favourite female actor on desi porn websites were straight out of the actor’s bedroom. The “sex video leaked” label on the video also helped contribute to the “authenticity” of the footage. The videos Vijash watched can broadly be classified into two categories: those shot on amateur cameras where the actor’s face is not clearly visible, but just enough to create the impression that it is them, and those in which the actor’s face has been digitally morphed over the face of the person in the original video, in other words –…","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/fake-porn-videos-deepfakes-of-bollywood-actors-celebs/","d":"2022-08-25","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2022-08-25","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2022-08-25","organization":"Desi porn websites","aiSystem":"AI-powered deepfake technology","affectedGroup":"Bollywood actors, fans, viewers","action":"Distribution of AI-generated pornographic content featuring Bollywood actors","consequence":"Misleading viewers about the authenticity of the content, potential exploitation of actors"}},{"i":"R1075","k":"India","p":"India","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.2  Allocative harm"],"t":"India court chatgpt bail murder case","s":"Legal experts say AI chatbots could be the future of courtrooms despite their reputation of being biased and discriminatory. A man accused of assault and murder stood in court this week as a judge in the Indian city Chandigarh opened ChatGPT and asked the AI-run app if he should be let out on bail. The judge’s use of AI technology is historic in India, which has one of the oldest and most overburdened legal systems in the world. Anoop Chitkara, a judge at the Punjab and Haryana High Court, opened the chatbot on Monday while Jaswinder Singh, who was arrested in 2020 for allegedly assaulting and killing an individual, sought relief from imprisonment during the trial.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/india-court-chatgpt-bail-murder-case/","d":"2023-03-29","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-03-29","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-03-29","organization":"Punjab and Haryana High Court","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Jaswinder Singh (accused of assault and murder)","action":"Judge used ChatGPT to determine if Singh should be released on bail","consequence":"Potential release of accused based on AI assessment"}},{"i":"R757","k":"India","p":"India","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"Is artificial intelligence bad news for india","s":"India is undergoing technological change, rolling out AI-based systems – in spheres from health to education, agriculture to criminal justice – but with scant debate on their ethical implications, experts say. After communal clashes in Delhi’s Jahangirpuri area last year, police said they used facial recognition technology to identify and arrest dozens of men, the second such instance after a more violent riot in the Indian capital in 2020. In both cases, most of those charged were Muslim, leading human rights groups and tech experts to criticize India’s use of the artificial intelligence-based technology to target poor, minority and marginalized groups in Delhi and elsewhere in the country.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/features/is-artificial-intelligence-bad-news-for-india/","d":"2023-09-11","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-09-11","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-09-11","organization":"Police in India","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"Poor, minority and marginalized groups in Delhi and elsewhere in India","action":"Used facial recognition technology to identify and arrest individuals","consequence":"Targeting of poor, minority and marginalized groups"}},{"i":"R684","k":"India","p":"India","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Hacker uses telegram chatbots leak data star health india insurer","s":"The use of Telegram chatbots to sell stolen data demonstrates the difficulty the app has in preventing nefarious agents taking advantage of its technology and highlights the challenges Indian companies face in keeping their data safe. Stolen customer data including medical reports from India’s biggest health insurer, Star Health, is publicly accessible via chatbots on Telegram, just weeks after Telegram’s founder was accused of allowing the messenger app to facilitate crime. The purported creator of the chatbots told a security researcher, who alerted Reuters to the issue, that private details of millions of people were for sale and that samples could be viewed by asking the chatbots to divulge.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/hacker-uses-telegram-chatbots-leak-data-star-health-india-insurer/","d":"2024-09-20","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-09-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-09-20","organization":"Star Health (India's largest health insurer)","aiSystem":"Telegram chatbots","affectedGroup":"Millions of people","action":"Chatbots divulged private details of customers","consequence":"Publicly accessible stolen customer data"}},{"i":"R723","k":"India","p":"India","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Deepfakes bollywood stars spark worries ai meddling india election","s":"The deepfakes of Aamir Khan and Ranveer Singh purportedly say Indian Prime Minister Narendra Modi failed to keep campaign promises and failed to address critical economic issues during his two terms. MUMBAI, India – In fake videos that have gone viral online, two of India’s A-lister Bollywood actors are seen criticizing Prime Minister Narendra Modi and asking people to vote for the opposition Congress party in the country’s ongoing general election. In a 30-second video that shows Aamir Khan and another 41-second clip of Ranveer Singh, the two Bollywood actors purportedly say Modi failed to keep campaign promises and failed to address critical economic issues during his two terms as prime minister.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/internet-culture/deepfakes-bollywood-stars-spark-worries-ai-meddling-india-election/","d":"2024-04-22","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-04-22","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-04-22","organization":"Bollywood actors (Aamir Khan and Ranveer Singh)","aiSystem":"AI-generated deepfake videos","affectedGroup":"Indian voters","action":"Generated and distributed fake videos criticizing Prime Minister Narendra Modi","consequence":"Potential voter influence and confusion"}},{"i":"R553","k":"India","p":"India","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.3  Employment displacement / quality harm"],"t":"Ai chatbots replace india call center workers","s":"In India, AI-powered systems are subsuming jobs done by headset-wearing graduates in technical support, customer care, and data management, sparking a scramble to adapt. BENGALURU, India – At a startup office in this Indian city, developers are fine-tuning artificial intelligence chatbots that talk and message like humans. The company, LimeChat, has an audacious goal: to make customer-service jobs almost obsolete. It says its generative AI agents enable clients to slash by 80% the number of workers needed to handle 10,000 monthly queries. “Once you hire a LimeChat agent, you never have to hire again,” Nikhil Gupta, its 28-year-old co-founder, told Reuters.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/features/ai-chatbots-replace-india-call-center-workers/","d":"2025-10-15","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-10-15","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-10-15","organization":"LimeChat (startup)","aiSystem":"AI-powered chatbots","affectedGroup":"headset-wearing graduates in technical support, customer care, and data management","action":"fine-tuning AI chatbots to handle customer service queries","consequence":"potential job loss for thousands of workers"}},{"i":"R594","k":"India","p":"India","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Video woman who missed air india flight ai generated","s":"The video was created using Google Veo, a text-to-video AI model developed by Google DeepMind. Claim: A video shows a woman claiming to be a passenger who had missed her flight, which turned out to be Air India 171 that crashed on June 12. Rating: FALSE Why we fact-checked this: The video has already received 2.8 million views, 86,000 reactions, 3,500 comments, and 1,700 shares as of writing. Text on the video says, “She’s lucky that she was late, 20 minutes late to her flight.” It shows an Indian woman with bruises on her face walking in the airport and recording a video of herself while saying, “Listen everyone, I am the luckiest woman in the world.”","u":"https://www.rappler.com/newsbreak/fact-check/video-woman-who-missed-air-india-flight-ai-generated/","d":"2025-06-20","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-06-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-06-20","organization":"Google","aiSystem":"Google Veo, a text-to-video AI model developed by Google DeepMind","affectedGroup":"Indian woman","action":"The video shows a woman claiming to be a passenger who had missed her flight.","consequence":"The video was shared widely and caused confusion about the actual flight incident."}},{"i":"R1248","k":"India","p":"India","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Recent election campaigns from Pakistan, India and Indonesia have shown radical new uses o…","s":"Recent election campaigns from Pakistan, India and Indonesia have shown radical new uses of generative AI that change how campaigns are run. Here's what's coming for Australia and the rest of the world. Recent election campaigns in Pakistan, India and Indonesia offer a glimpse of how artificial intelligence (AI) is changing the way elections are fought, sometimes in surprising and unexpected ways. From generative AI cartoon avatars of candidates, to political endorsements issued from beyond the grave, to sentiment-tracking chatbots writing stump speeches, parties are deploying the new technology in ways that its inventors may not have imagined.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/science/2024-02-21/ai-elections-deepfakes-generative-campaign-endorsement-democracy/103483710","d":"2024-02-20","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-02-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-02-21","organization":"Political parties in Pakistan, India, and Indonesia","aiSystem":"Generative AI","affectedGroup":"Voters in Pakistan, India, and Indonesia","action":"Deploying generative AI to issue political endorsements","consequence":"Potential manipulation of voter sentiment and election outcomes"}},{"i":"R1249","k":"India","p":"India","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Experts warn that with elections to be held in about 50 countries this year — including th…","s":"Experts warn that with elections to be held in about 50 countries this year — including the world's biggest democracies in India, the United States, and Indonesia — the proliferation of easy-to-use AI tools is set to lead to a \"tsunami of disinformation\". Divyendra Singh Jadoun, an artificial intelligence (AI) expert based Rajasthan in northern India, gets asked to do two kinds of jobs for political campaigns. \"One is to enhance the image of their existing politician that they are endorsing,\" he told the ABC, \"and the second one is to harm the image of the opponent party.\" Mr Jadoun mostly uses AI tools to translate and dub content, something that's crucial in a country as linguistically diverse as India.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2024-01-22/how-ai-artificial-intelligence-being-used-in-elections/103310862","d":"2024-01-21","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2024-01-21","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-01-22","organization":"Political campaigns","aiSystem":"Easy-to-use AI tools","affectedGroup":"Public opinion","action":"Generate and disseminate disinformation","consequence":"Disruption of election integrity and public trust"}},{"i":"R1312","k":"India","p":"India","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"India vaccine exclusion fears over digital id face recognition","s":"Millions at risk of missing vaccines as Indian state pilots facial recognition technology at inoculation centres. Millions at risk of missing out on vaccines as India uses national digital identity for registration and pilots facial recognition technology at inoculation centres. Millions of vulnerable people are at risk of missing out on COVID-19 vaccines as India uses its national digital identity for registration and pilots facial recognition technology at inoculation centres, rights groups and experts said. Amid a surge in coronavirus cases, authorities are testing a facial recognition system based on the Aadhaar ID for authentication in the eastern state of Jharkhand, and plan to roll it out nationwide, a senior official said last week.","u":"https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2021/4/16/india-vaccine-exclusion-fears-over-digital-id-face-recognition","d":"2021-04-16","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-04-16","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-04-16","organization":"Indian authorities","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"Millions of vulnerable people","action":"Uses facial recognition technology for authentication","consequence":"At risk of missing out on vaccines"}},{"i":"R1350","k":"India","p":"India","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.1  Data labor exploitation"],"t":"Smaller Indian towns are becoming centres for training and correcting artificial intellige…","s":"Smaller Indian towns are becoming centres for training and correcting artificial intelligence models. India has long been a centre for outsourced IT support, with cities like Bangalore or Chennai being traditional hubs for such work. But in recent years firms have been moving that work into much more remote areas, where costs for staff and space are lower. The trend is know as cloud farming, and AI has given it another boost with numerous smaller towns hosting firms working on AI. Mohan Kumar is one worker who has benefited from that. He lives in the small town of TN Palayam, in the southern state of Tamil Nadu. \"My role is in AI annotation.","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cqjevxvxw9xo","d":"2025-10-14","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-10-14","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-10-14","organization":"Various AI training firms operating in smaller Indian towns","aiSystem":"AI annotation system","affectedGroup":"Individuals living in smaller Indian towns","action":"Performing AI annotation tasks","consequence":"Exposure to privacy risks due to AI annotation"}},{"i":"R1362","k":"India","p":"India","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Babydoll Archi became an Instagram sensation","s":"Babydoll Archi became an Instagram sensation. And then police arrested its creator. It took just a few days for Indian Instagram sensation Babydoll Archi to double her following to 1.4 million, thanks to a couple of viral social media moments. One was a video that showed her in a red sari, dancing seductively to Dame Un Grr – a Romanian song. And a photo posted on the platform showed her posing with American adult film star Kendra Lust. Suddenly everyone wanted to know about her - and the name Babydoll Archi trended in Google search and spawned countless memes and fan pages. But there was one issue about to emerge – there was no real woman behind the online sensation.","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cn0znk47x9eo","d":"2025-07-23","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-07-23","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-07-23","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"Online users, particularly Babydoll Archi","action":"AI-powered recommendation systems and social media algorithms surfaced Babydoll Archi's profile","consequence":"Exposure of personal information and identity theft risks"}},{"i":"R1380","k":"India","p":"India","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"India's largest news agency has sued OpenAI for 20m rupees, accusing it of using copyright…","s":"India's largest news agency has sued OpenAI for 20m rupees, accusing it of using copyrighted material illegally. India's biggest news organisations are seeking to join a lawsuit against OpenAI, the US startup behind ChatGPT, for alleged unauthorised use of their content. The news organisations include some of India's oldest publications like The Indian Express, The Hindu, The India Today group, billionaire Gautam Adani-owned NDTV, and over a dozen others. OpenAI denies the allegations and told the BBC that it uses \"publicly available data\" that are in line with \"widely accepted legal precedents\". On Wednesday, OpenAI CEO Sam Altman was in Delhi to discuss India's plan for a low-cost AI ecosystem with IT Minister Ashwini Vaishnaw.","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cg7ze00ly1zo","d":"2025-02-06","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-02-06","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-02-06","organization":"Various Indian news organizations including The Indian Express, The Hindu, The India Today group, NDTV, and over a dozen others","aiSystem":"OpenAI's AI-powered content generation system","affectedGroup":"Indian news organizations","action":"Used copyrighted material without authorization","consequence":"Sued by India's largest news agency for 20m rupees"}},{"i":"R1423","k":"India","p":"India","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"World asia india 59006615","s":"Facebook is struggling to tame fake news and inflammatory content in India, new revelations show. \"I've seen more images of dead people in the past three weeks than I've seen in my entire life,\" a Facebook researcher in India wrote in 2019 after following recommendations by the social network's algorithms for three weeks. The researcher's report was part of a cache of internal documents called The Facebook Papers, recently obtained by New York Times and other US publications. They show the social media giant struggling to tame the avalanche of fake news, hate speech, and inflammatory content -\"celebrations of violence\", among other things - out of India, the network's biggest market.","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/world-asia-india-59006615","d":"2021-10-26","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-10-26","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2019","organization":"Facebook","aiSystem":"Facebook's content moderation system","affectedGroup":"People in India","action":"Generated and promoted content","consequence":"Exposure to misleading information"}},{"i":"R1580","k":"India","p":"India","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Indian police adopt facial recognition despite risk of massive data breaches","s":"As rights groups ipush back against the use of facial recognition tools by law enforcement, a breach underscores the reality of their concerns. “Real-time facial recognition” typically refers to continuous biometric monitoring. But it applies equally as well to the experience of watching in real time as warnings about facial recognition give way to real-life examples of the risk to data security. As rights groups in the UK and U.S. push back against the use of facial recognition tools by law enforcement, a breach in India underscores the reality of their concerns. A breach of the Tamil Nadu Police Facial Recognition Portal exposed 800,000 lines of data, including information of over 50,000 persons, according to reports in The New Indian Express ’ and Medianama Exposed by threat intelligence platform FalconFeeds.io, the breach was the work of a group calling itself “Valerie,” which has…","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202405/indian-police-adopt-facial-recognition-despite-risk-of-massive-data-breaches","d":"2024-05-07","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-05-07","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-05-07","organization":"Tamil Nadu Police","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"Over 50,000 persons","action":"Exposure of 800,000 lines of data containing personal information","consequence":"Risk to data security and potential misuse of personal information"}},{"i":"R1595","k":"India","p":"India","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Are indian police using facial recognition to identify protesting farmers","s":"Police are initiating cancellation process by contacting passport authorities and embassies and by accessing the individuals' photos and residential details. Police in Ambala, in India’s state of Haryana have announced that they have begun the process to cancel passports and visas against those caught causing disturbances or breaking barricades on CCTV or drone cameras during the farmers’ protests in early February. The move comes on the heels of proposals by police in other countries around the world, including Sweden and the UK , to run facial recognition searches against passport databases, rather than arrest or conviction records.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202403/are-indian-police-using-facial-recognition-to-identify-protesting-farmers","d":"2024-03-04","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-03-04","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-03-04","organization":"Indian police","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"Farmers and protesters","action":"Accessing and searching individuals' photos and residential details","consequence":"Cancellation of passports and visas"}},{"i":"R1691","k":"India","p":"India","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"Across south asia online hate","s":"Yati Narsinghanand, the head priest of a powerful temple in Uttar Pradesh, India's most populous state, first made national headlines in 2019 when a local TV station devoted a news item to the weapons training he conducted on temple grounds for Hindu youth and boys. His dramatic rise as a poster boy for ‘Hindutva', India's reigning anti-Muslim political ideology, was fuelled by the inflammatory videos he subsequently began sharing on social media platforms, where they garnered millions of (…). Mourners carry the body of Amir Khan, 30, who was killed by a Hindu mob during anti-Muslim riots in north-east Delhi on 29 February 2020.","u":"https://www.equaltimes.org/across-south-asia-online-hate","d":"2022-07-13","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2022-07-13","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2020-02-29","organization":"Local TV station, Hindu mobs, Mourners","aiSystem":"Social media platforms","affectedGroup":"Amir Khan, Hindu youth and boys, Muslims","action":"Shared inflammatory videos on social media","consequence":"Generated public outrage and incited violence"}},{"i":"R1764","k":"India","p":"India","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Karnataka schools face recognition system attendance b2810805","s":"Parents and experts urge the state to roll back the facial recognition system in schools. A state in southern India is facing severe backlash from child rights activists and experts after the authorities proposed to introduce an AI face recognition system to keep track of students’ attendance. The Department of School Education and Literacy in Karnataka is planning to use mobile phone-based facial recognition to mark attendance in the Students Achievement Tracking System (SATS) – the government’s database for monitoring students. The facial recognition system will also be used to track beneficiaries of government welfare schemes such as midday meals, where pupils are provided lunches under the largest free-food-in-school programme in the world, the authorities earlier said.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/asia/india/karnataka-schools-face-recognition-system-attendance-b2810805.html","d":"2024-08-20","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-08-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-08-20","organization":"Department of School Education and Literacy in Karnataka","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"Students, beneficiaries of government welfare schemes","action":"Used facial recognition to monitor students' attendance and track beneficiaries of government welfare schemes","consequence":"Severe backlash from child rights activists and experts, potential misuse of technology"}},{"i":"R1818","k":"India","p":"India","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"Water energy use concerns grow with more india data centres in the pipeline","s":"Indian policies are positioning the country as a global investment hub for AI, but the data centre industry should address environmental concerns. The coastal district of Visakhapatnam in Andhra Pradesh is set for a major transformation. On April 28, the foundation stone was laid in Tarluvada, a village in Vizag for a new data centre for Google – expected to be the technology company’s largest data centre outside the United States. The data centre is part of an AI Hub announced by the company last October. The state government allotted 480 acres of land in Visakhapatnam and Anakapalli districts for the hub. Activists and lawyers have raised environmental concerns and questions about the lack of clarity on water and energy consumption for the operation of the approved data centres.","u":"https://scroll.in/article/1092827/water-energy-use-concerns-grow-with-more-india-data-centres-in-the-pipeline","d":"2026-05-21","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-05-21","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-04-28","organization":"Google","aiSystem":"Data center operations","affectedGroup":"Local residents and environment","action":"Construction of a new data center","consequence":"Environmental degradation and resource depletion"}},{"i":"R1819","k":"India","p":"India","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Ahead of assam polls ai generated disinformation targeted muslims state congress chief study","s":"The report said that the poll campaign in the state witnessed ‘the first industrialised AI disinformation operation’ in an Assembly election. The Assam Assembly election campaign witnessed “the first industrialised artificial intelligence disinformation operation ” in an Indian state election, an advocacy group said in a report on Tuesday. The organisation, the Foundation Diaspora in Action for Human Rights and Democracy, said that the disinformation campaign was part of a strategy in which Muslims were “simultaneously dehumanised, disenfranchised, displaced and erased from cultural memory”. The study came two days before the Assam Assembly election on Thursday.","u":"https://scroll.in/latest/1091949/ahead-of-assam-polls-ai-generated-disinformation-targeted-muslims-state-congress-chief-study","d":"2026-04-07","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-04-07","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-04-05","organization":"Foundation Diaspora in Action for Human Rights and Democracy","aiSystem":"AI-generated disinformation","affectedGroup":"Muslims","action":"Generated and disseminated disinformation targeting Muslims","consequence":"Dehumanisation, disenfranchisement, displacement, and erasure from cultural memory"}},{"i":"R1820","k":"India","p":"India","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.3  Employment displacement / quality harm"],"t":"Claude plugin threatens indian its back office of the world model","s":"The new plugins are designed to automate precisely the high-volume, repetitive knowledge work that has been the bread and butter of Indian IT. This article was originally published in Rest of World, which covers technology’s impact outside the West. On February 4, the $300 billion Indian IT sector faced a moment of reckoning. The country’s benchmark IT stocks index slumped nearly 6%, reacting to Anthropic’s release of its Claude Cowork agentic plugin. The new plugins are designed to automate precisely the high-volume, repetitive knowledge work that has been the bread and butter of Indian IT: contract reviews, regulatory compliance tracking, and sales forecasting, among other things.","u":"https://scroll.in/article/1091073/claude-plugin-threatens-indian-its-back-office-of-the-world-model","d":"2026-03-10","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2026-03-10","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-02-04","organization":"Anthropic","aiSystem":"Claude plugin","affectedGroup":"Indian IT sector","action":"released the new plugins","consequence":"reacting to the release, Indian IT sector's benchmark index slumped nearly 6%"}},{"i":"R1821","k":"India","p":"India","b":["Data"],"h":["4.3  Data rights / consent violation"],"t":"In india ai deepfakes of the deceased are a lively business","s":"Self-taught small creators are using AI tools to recreate the dead for special events. This article was originally published in Rest of World, which covers technology’s impact outside the West. When the lights dimmed at Jaideep Sharma’s wedding reception in the north Indian city of Ajmer, guests expected to see a cheesy montage of the young couple in various attractive locations. Instead, they saw Sharma’s father – dead for more than a year – on the screen, smiling and blessing the newlyweds. The video was created using artificial intelligence by a local creator Sharma found on Instagram. Using pictures of Sharma’s father, the creator produced a minute-long video in about a week, and charged about Rs 50,000, Sharma told Rest of World It was worth it, he said.","u":"https://scroll.in/article/1091074/in-india-ai-deepfakes-of-the-deceased-are-a-lively-business","d":"2026-03-03","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-03-03","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-03-03","organization":"Self-taught small creators","aiSystem":"AI tools for creating deepfakes","affectedGroup":"Guests at Jaideep Sharma's wedding reception","action":"Created a deepfake video of deceased person","consequence":"Exposure to misleading content"}},{"i":"R1822","k":"India","p":"India","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Why experts say ai tool to detect bangladeshis will be open to misuse","s":"They warned it could lead to more harassment of working-class migrants from Bengal and the North East. In 1997, Babar Ali Mandal migrated from Nadia district in West Bengal to Mumbai in search of work. In the three decades since, he has made Mumbai his home. He runs a small workshop to make footwear in Govandi, a slum cluster located in the east of the Maharashtra capital. In his free time, he often scrolls through social media sites. What he sees is distressing. “A lot of people from my state are being targeted for speaking Bengali,” he said. The fear of being labelled Bangladeshi has intensified in the last one year, he added.","u":"https://scroll.in/article/1090325/why-experts-say-ai-tool-to-detect-bangladeshis-will-be-open-to-misuse","d":"2026-01-29","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2026-01-29","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-01-29","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"AI tool to detect Bangladeshis","affectedGroup":"Working-class migrants from Bengal and the North East","action":"AI tool flagged individuals for potential Bangladeshi identity","consequence":"Increased harassment and targeting of migrants"}},{"i":"R1824","k":"India","p":"India","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"Ai generated images fuelling hate against muslims in india says us thinktank report","s":"Visuals with exaggerated features and Muslim stereotypes are being used to illustrate or bolster Hindutva conspiracy theories. A heavily-bearded Muslim man smiles wickedly at the terrified woman next to him. A muscular man in a saffron dhoti walks in the rain carrying a young woman wearing a headscarf. A man in a skullcap wields a knife below a sign that reads “Abdul beef shop” while cows weep outside. Images like these generated by artificial intelligence tools, featuring stereotypical Muslim men with exaggerated evil features and sexualised Muslim women, are being widely circulated on major social media platforms in India.","u":"https://scroll.in/article/1087778/ai-generated-images-fuelling-hate-against-muslims-in-india-says-us-thinktank-report","d":"2025-10-18","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-10-18","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-10-18","organization":"Major social media platforms in India","aiSystem":"AI-generated images","affectedGroup":"People exposed to misleading content","action":"Generated and circulated AI-generated images","consequence":"Exposure to misleading content"}},{"i":"R1825","k":"India","p":"India","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Indias embrace of dangerous facial recognition technology is great for ai terrible for privacy","s":"Disregard for privacy concerns and weak legal protections create an environment conducive to unregulated technological experimentation. In February, India, along with France, co-hosted the AI Action Summit held in Paris. At the end, it was announced that the next edition will be held in India. In its naming, priorities, and focus, the summit witnessed a clear shift from “safety” to “innovation” as the principal theme in artificial intelligence discourse. This move aligns with India’s lax regulatory stance on AI governance, even in high-risk areas like healthcare and surveillance-driven technologies such as facial recognition technology In the upcoming summit, this shift will enable the Indian government to steer discussions toward innovation, investment and accessibility while avoiding scrutiny over its weak legal protections, which create an environment conducive to unregulated…","u":"https://scroll.in/article/1084395/indias-embrace-of-dangerous-facial-recognition-technology-is-great-for-ai-terrible-for-privacy","d":"2025-08-04","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-08-04","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-08-04","organization":"Indian government","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"Individuals","action":"Steer discussions toward innovation, investment and accessibility while avoiding scrutiny over weak legal protections","consequence":"Create an environment conducive to unregulated technological experimentation"}},{"i":"R1826","k":"India","p":"India","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.5  Human computer interaction harm"],"t":"Dear chatgpt am i having a panic attack ai is bridging mental health gaps but not without risks","s":"Young Indians are turning to artificial intelligence-driven tools for support. Experts and tech founders say these help but can’t replace human therapists. During a stressful internship early this year, 21-year-old Keshav* was struggling with unsettling thoughts. “One day, on the way home from work, I saw a dead rat and instantly wanted to pick it up and eat it,” he said. “I’m a vegetarian and have never had meat in my life.” After struggling with similar thoughts a few more times, Keshav spoke to a therapist. Then he entered a query into ChatGPT, a “chatbot” powered by artificial intelligence that is designed to simulate human conversations.","u":"https://scroll.in/article/1084587/dear-chatgpt-am-i-having-a-panic-attack-ai-is-bridging-mental-health-gaps-but-not-without-risks","d":"2025-07-19","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-07-19","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-07-19","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"ChatGPT (a generative AI system)","affectedGroup":"Young Indians","action":"entered a query into ChatGPT","consequence":"exposed to misleading content"}},{"i":"R1827","k":"India","p":"India","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.2  Land use and siting harm"],"t":"Indias data centre push runs into farmers resistance","s":"Google, Microsoft, and Amazon are each developing at least one big multibillion-dollar data centre project in India. This article was originally published in Rest of World, which covers technology’s impact outside the West. India is aggressively courting US big tech to set up data centres in the country. But this push is colliding with a very different reality on the ground. In February, the Indian government announced a 20-year tax holiday for foreign cloud service companies using India-based data centres to serve global customers. The move is aimed at positioning India as a global hub for artificial intelligence infrastructure, and encouraging companies like Google, Microsoft, and Amazon to boost their commitments.","u":"https://scroll.in/article/1092311/indias-data-centre-push-runs-into-farmers-resistance","d":"2025-05-09","e":"Acute and chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-05-09","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-02","organization":"Indian government","aiSystem":"Artificial intelligence infrastructure","affectedGroup":"Foreign cloud service companies using India-based data centres to serve global customers","action":"Announced a 20-year tax holiday","consequence":"Encouraging companies to boost their commitments"}},{"i":"R1829","k":"India","p":"India","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Indian courts have no jurisdiction in ani copyright case openai to delhi hc","s":"The company said it could not delete the news agency’s content from ChatGPT as this would violate its legal obligations in the United States. Microsoft-backed artificial intelligence company OpenAI has told the Delhi High Court that Indian courts have no jurisdiction in a copyright case filed by news agency Asian News International, reported Reuters on Thursday. This is because OpenAI does not have a physical presence or servers in India, said the company in an 86-page court filing on January 10, according to the news agency. ANI filed the lawsuit in November, accusing OpenAI of using its content without permission to train its ChatGPT service.","u":"https://scroll.in/latest/1078290/indian-courts-have-no-jurisdiction-in-ani-copyright-case-openai-to-delhi-hc","d":"2025-01-23","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-01-23","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-01-10","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"OpenAI's ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Asian News International (ANI)","action":"Used ANI's content without permission","consequence":"Accused of training ChatGPT service"}},{"i":"R1830","k":"India","p":"India","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.3  Employment displacement / quality harm"],"t":"Indias semiconductor ambitions could risk worker safety","s":"The history of health risks in chip manufacturing poses challenges for the country given the state of its occupational health and safety laws. India has grand plans for its semi-conductor industry. The country is a relatively new entrant to the world of chip manufacturing, dominated by Taiwan, South Korea, and the US. Over the last few years, India has undertaken an unprecedented push for investments in the field. “Our dream is that every device in the world will have an Indian-made chip ,” said Prime Minister Narendra Modi at an event in September. In recent years, New Delhi has invested Rs 76,000 crore into the sector as part of the India Semi-Conductor Mission, providing huge subsidies to companies that plan to manufacture chips in India and hopes to see the industry’s value grow from $29 billion in 2023, to nearly four times its size, to $109 billion, by 2030.","u":"https://scroll.in/article/1076669/indias-semiconductor-ambitions-could-risk-worker-safety","d":"2024-12-18","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2024-12-18","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-12-18","organization":"India","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"Workers in the semiconductor industry","action":"Undertake an unprecedented push for investments in the field","consequence":"Risk of worker safety due to inadequate occupational health and safety laws"}},{"i":"R1831","k":"India","p":"India","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Delhi hc seeks openais response to ani suit accusing chatgpt of copyright violation","s":"The news agency alleged that the United States-based artificial intelligence company used its content without permission to train its chatbot. The Delhi High Court on Tuesday summoned United States-based artificial intelligence company OpenAI in a suit filed by news agency Asian News International, which accused the firm of using its content without permission to train and operate its chatbot ChatGPT, reported Bar and Bench. The news agency has sought damages of Rs 2 crore , as well as directions to stop OpenAI from storing or using its work, according to The Indian Express OpenAI, however, told the court that after ANI issued it a legal notice in September, the company blocklisted the news agency’s domain, which effectively meant that it was not using material from ANI to train the chatbot.","u":"https://scroll.in/latest/1075830/delhi-hc-seeks-openais-response-to-ani-suit-accusing-chatgpt-of-copyright-violation","d":"2024-11-20","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-11-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-11-20","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Asian News International","action":"Used content without permission to train and operate the chatbot","consequence":"Blocked domain, prevented from using content to train the chatbot"}},{"i":"R1832","k":"India","p":"India","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Bjp shared ai generated audio clips targeting opposition leaders in maharashtra says fact checker","s":"Hours before the Assembly polls, the party claimed that the ‘recordings’ were evidence of Maha Vikas Aghadi leaders’ involvement in an alleged Bitcoin scam. Audio clips shared by the Bharatiya Janata Party on its X account targeting Opposition leaders in Maharashtra, hours before voting for the state Assembly polls began, were generated by Artificial Intelligence tools, fact-checking organisation BOOM has said. The Hindutva party claimed that the “leaked recordings” were evidence that implicated Nationalist Congress Party (Sharadchandra Pawar) leader Supriya Sule, Congress leader Nana Patole, Indian Police Service officer Amitabh Gupta and Gaurav Mehta, who works with an audit firm, in misappropriating cryptocurrency to fund the state elections.","u":"https://scroll.in/latest/1075880/bjp-shared-ai-generated-audio-clips-targeting-opposition-leaders-in-maharashtra-says-fact-checker","d":"2024-11-20","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-11-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-11-20","organization":"Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP)","aiSystem":"AI-generated audio clips","affectedGroup":"Opposition leaders in Maharashtra","action":"Shared audio clips targeting opposition leaders","consequence":"Exposure to misleading content"}},{"i":"R1836","k":"India","p":"India","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Explained should india be worried about malicious ai tools influencing politics","s":"Deepfakes like the supposedly manipulated audio that put a Tamil Nadu leader in spot recently may become common, damaging the integrity of political processes. Last week, Tamil Nadu Finance Minister Palanivel Thiaga Rajan claimed that viral audio clips of him purportedly accusing leaders of the ruling Dravida Munnetra Kazhagam of corruption and praising the Bharatiya Janata Party were fabricated with the help of advanced technology such as artificial intelligence, bringing to fore concerns about the potential misuse of such tools in politics. AI tools, especially deepfake, are being increasingly used around the world to spread misinformation and influence politics.","u":"https://scroll.in/article/1048562/explained-should-india-be-worried-about-malicious-ai-tools-influencing-politics","d":"2023-05-05","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-05-05","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-05-05","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"Artificial Intelligence (AI), Deepfake technology","affectedGroup":"Politicians, voters, political processes","action":"Used to spread misinformation and influence politics","consequence":"Damaging the integrity of political processes, spreading false accusations"}},{"i":"R1839","k":"India","p":"India","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.1  Data labor exploitation"],"t":"For workers in developing countries artificial intelligence is both a boon and a bane","s":"Lax labour regulations and low wages are the norm for data annotation workers in poorer nations. Dinesh Raj, who lives in the south Indian city of Salem, treasures his account on microwork website Amazon Mechanical Turk, even if competition for data annotation tasks on the crowdsourced platform is high, and the pay is low. The 30-year-old, who has an engineering degree, has struggled to find a well-paid job, and relies on the platform for much of his income, which can vary every day. “I work at night, when there are more jobs from US clients,” said Raj, who has done tasks on Amazon MTurk for about four years. “Of 10 tasks I do, only two may get approved, so I have to do more tasks to make $10-$30 a day.","u":"https://scroll.in/article/1045661/for-workers-in-developing-countries-artificial-intelligence-is-both-a-boon-and-a-bane","d":"2023-03-25","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-03-25","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-03-25","organization":"Amazon Mechanical Turk","aiSystem":"Data annotation process on Amazon Mechanical Turk","affectedGroup":"Data annotation workers in poorer nations, particularly in India","action":"Workers perform data annotation tasks on the platform","consequence":"Workers' personal information is collected and potentially misused"}},{"i":"R1840","k":"India","p":"India","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"In india fears grow that facial recognition systems may used to target minorities","s":"As the country seeks to build one of the world’s largest face databases, activists fear bias against vulnerable groups. On a hot May afternoon in India, when Hyderabad activist SQ Masood stepped out to get some paperwork done, he did not expect the police to stop and question him. Nor did he expect them to photograph him without consent. At the time of the incident, Masood was passing through Shahran Market. It is an area inhabited by a number of Muslim residents in Hyderabad, the capital of Telangana. His father-in-law was also with him. Profiled picture “There were eight to 10 police officers there randomly stopping people and questioning them.","u":"https://scroll.in/article/1006615/in-india-fears-grow-that-facial-recognition-systems-may-used-to-target-minorities","d":"2021-10-02","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-10-02","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-05","organization":"Police authorities","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"Muslim residents","action":"Randomly stopped and questioned people","consequence":"Exposure to surveillance and tracking"}},{"i":"R1841","k":"India","p":"India","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Facial recognition technology isnt wholly accurate at reading indian faces find researchers","s":"A research paper audited four software tools and found they failed far more in identifying Indian women than Indian men. In 2018, researchers from the United States conducted a study which found that facial recognition technology worked profoundly differently on different demographic groups. When the software saw “lighter-skinned” men, it correctly identified their gender 99% of the time. But when it encountered “darker-skinned” women, the accuracy dropped precipitously to 35%. Although the research was ground-breaking, it did have a chink. It tested the facial recognition technologies with a database of photographs of parliamentarians from Rwanda, Senegal, South Africa, Finland, Iceland and Sweden – in other words, mostly Black or White faces.","u":"https://scroll.in/magazine/1001836/facial-recognition-technology-isnt-wholly-accurate-at-reading-indian-faces-find-researchers","d":"2021-08-06","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-08-06","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2018","organization":"Researchers from the United States","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"Darker-skinned women","action":"Facial recognition technology incorrectly identified their gender","consequence":"Privacy risks due to inaccurate identification"}},{"i":"R1842","k":"India","p":"India","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"A lawsuit against facial recognition in telangana could set a precedent for the rest of india","s":"As facial recognition systems get deployed nationwide, digital rights activists warn about the infringement of privacy. It was lockdown in the Indian city of Hyderabad when activist SQ Masood was stopped on the street by police who asked him to remove his facial mask and then took his picture, giving no reason and ignoring his objections. Worried about how the photographs would be used, Masood sent a legal notice to the city’s police chief. But after receiving no response, he filed suit last month over Telangana state’s use of facial recognition systems – the first such case in India. “Being Muslim and having worked with minority groups that are frequently targeted by the police, I am concerned that my photo could be matched wrongly and that I could be harassed,” Masood, 38, told the Thomson Reuters Foundation.","u":"https://scroll.in/article/1015798/a-lawsuit-against-facial-recognition-in-telangana-could-set-a-precedent-for-the-rest-of-india","d":"2021-01-25","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-01-25","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-01-25","organization":"Telangana state authorities","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"Individuals, particularly Muslims and minority groups","action":"Facial recognition systems were used to take pictures of people without their consent","consequence":"Potential misuse of photos leading to harassment"}},{"i":"R1870","k":"India","p":"India","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"Voice accent technology call center white american","s":"A Silicon Valley startup offers voice-altering tech to call center workers around the world: ‘Yes, this is wrong … but a lot of things exist in the world’. “Hi, good morning. I’m calling in from Bangalore, India.” I’m talking on speakerphone to a man with an obvious Indian accent. He pauses. “Now I have enabled the accent translation,” he says. It’s the same person, but he sounds completely different: loud and slightly nasal, impossible to distinguish from the accents of my friends in Brooklyn. Only after he had spoken a few more sentences did I notice a hint of the software changing his voice: it rendered the word “technology” with an unnatural cadence and stress on the wrong syllable.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2022/aug/23/voice-accent-technology-call-center-white-american","d":"2022-08-24","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2022-08-24","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2022-08-23","organization":"Silicon Valley startup","aiSystem":"Voice-accent altering technology","affectedGroup":"Call center workers","action":"Enabled accent translation feature","consequence":"Misleading representation of accent change"}},{"i":"R1922","k":"India","p":"India","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"China using ai disrupt elections","s":"Beijing did a test run in Taiwan using AI-generated content to influence voters away from a pro-sovereignty candidate. China will attempt to disrupt elections in the US, South Korea and India this year with artificial intelligence-generated content after making a dry run with the presidential poll in Taiwan, Microsoft has warned. The US tech firm said it expected Chinese state-backed cyber groups to target high-profile elections in 2024, with North Korea also involved, according to a report by the company’s threat intelligence team published on Friday. “As populations in India, South Korea and the United States head to the polls, we are likely to see Chinese cyber and influence actors, and to some extent North Korean cyber actors, work toward targeting these elections,” the report reads.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2024/apr/05/china-using-ai-disrupt-elections","d":"2024-04-05","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2024-04-05","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-04-05","organization":"Chinese state-backed cyber groups, North Korea","aiSystem":"Artificial intelligence-generated content","affectedGroup":"Voters in US, South Korea, and India","action":"Generate and disseminate AI-generated content to influence voter preferences","consequence":"Disruption of elections and potential manipulation of voting outcomes"}},{"i":"R1294","k":"India","p":"India","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"What are digital arrests the newest deepfake tool used by cybercriminals","s":"Scammers are increasingly using deepfake tech to fraud people. Experts say awareness is key to prevent online scam. An Indian textile baron has revealed that he was duped out of 70 million rupees ($833,000) by online scammers impersonating federal investigators and even the Supreme Court chief justice. The fraudsters posing as officers from India’s Central Bureau of Investigation (CBI) called SP Oswal, chairman and managing director of the textile manufacturer Vardhman, on August 28 and accused him of money laundering. For the next two days, Oswal was under digital surveillance as he was ordered to keep Skype open on his phone 24/7 during which he was interrogated and threatened with arrest.","u":"https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2024/10/11/what-are-digital-arrests-the-newest-deepfake-tool-used-by-cybercriminals","d":"2024-09-11","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-09-11","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-08-28","organization":"Online scammers","aiSystem":"Deepfake technology","affectedGroup":"Individuals","action":"Impersonating federal investigators and the Supreme Court chief justice","consequence":"Financial loss and psychological distress"}},{"i":"R1300","k":"India","p":"India","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Deepfake democracy behind the ai trickery shaping indias 2024 elections","s":"As India prepares for the world's largest elections, parties are turning to AI for novel - and dangerous - strategies. New Delhi, India — As voters queued up early morning on November 30 last year to vote in legislative elections to choose the next government of the southern Indian state of Telangana, a seven-second clip started going viral on social media. Posted on X by the Congress party, which is in opposition nationally, and was in the state at the time, it showed KT Rama Rao, a leader of the Bharat Rashtra Samiti that was ruling the state, calling on people to vote in favour of the Congress. The Congress shared it widely on a range of WhatsApp groups “operated unofficially” by the party, according to a senior leader who requested anonymity.","u":"https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2024/2/20/deepfake-democracy-behind-the-ai-trickery-shaping-indias-2024-elections","d":"2024-02-20","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-02-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"November 30, 2023","organization":"Congress party","aiSystem":"Deepfake technology","affectedGroup":"Voters in Telangana state","action":"Generated and distributed misleading video content","consequence":"Spread false information leading to potential voter manipulation"}},{"i":"R1301","k":"India","p":"India","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.2  Allocative harm"],"t":"In india an algorithm declares them dead they have to prove theyre","s":"Haryana state ended old age and widow pensions for thousands of claimants because they were dead. But they weren't. This story was produced with support from the Pulitzer Center’s AI Accountability Network. Rohtak and New Delhi, India: Dhuli Chand was 102 years old on September 8, 2022, when he led a wedding procession in Rohtak, a district town in the north Indian state of Haryana. As is customary in north Indian weddings, he sat on a chariot in his wedding finery, wearing garlands of Indian rupee notes, while a band played celebratory music and family members and villagers accompanied him. But instead of a bride, Chand was on his way to meet government officials.","u":"https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2024/1/25/in-india-an-algorithm-declares-them-dead-they-have-to-prove-theyre","d":"2024-01-25","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-01-25","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2022-09-08","organization":"Government of Haryana","aiSystem":"Automated eligibility verification system","affectedGroup":"Old age and widow pension claimants","action":"Automatically flagged claimants as deceased","consequence":"Denied access to benefits"}},{"i":"R1302","k":"India","p":"India","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.2  Allocative harm"],"t":"How an algorithm denied food to thousands of poor in indias telangana","s":"It adopted AI in welfare schemes to weed out ineligible ones, but has wrongfully removed thousands of legitimate ones. This story was produced with support from the Pulitzer Center’s AI Accountability Network. Hyderabad and New Delhi, India – Bismillah Bee can’t conceive of owning a car. The 67-year-old widow and 12 members of her family live in a cramped three-room house in an urban slum in Hyderabad, the capital of the Indian state of Telangana. Since her rickshaw puller husband’s death two years ago of mouth cancer, Bee makes a living by peeling garlic for a local business. But an algorithmic system, which the Telangana government deploys to digitally profile its more than 30 million residents, tagged Bee’s husband as a car owner in 2021, when he was still alive.","u":"https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2024/1/24/how-an-algorithm-denied-food-to-thousands-of-poor-in-indias-telangana","d":"2024-01-24","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-01-24","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021","organization":"Telangana government","aiSystem":"algorithmic system deployed by Telangana government","affectedGroup":"Bismillah Bee and her family","action":"tagged Bee's husband as a car owner","consequence":"wrongfully removed from welfare schemes"}},{"i":"R1313","k":"India","p":"India","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"India surveillance hotspot telangana facial recognition court lawsuit privacy","s":"Suit claims facial recognition is unconstitutional in Telangana, the state using the most facial recognition systems. Lawsuit challenges facial recognition as unconstitutional in Telangana, the state using the most facial recognition systems. It was lockdown in the Indian city of Hyderabad when activist S Q Masood was stopped on the street by police who asked him to remove his face mask and then took his picture, giving no reason and ignoring his objections. Worried about how the photographs would be used, Masood sent a legal notice to the city’s police chief. But after receiving no response, he filed suit last month over Telangana state’s use of facial recognition systems – the first such case in India.","u":"https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2022/1/20/india-surveillance-hotspot-telangana-facial-recognition-court-lawsuit-privacy","d":"2021-01-20","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-01-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-01-20","organization":"Telangana state authorities","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"Individuals, particularly activists and residents of Hyderabad","action":"Facial recognition technology was used to identify and photograph individuals without their consent","consequence":"Exposure to surveillance and potential misuse of personal data"}},{"i":"R1838","k":"India","p":"India","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.2  Allocative harm"],"t":"Punjab high court turns to chatgpt for view on bail in murder case","s":"‘What is the jurisprudence on bail when the assailants assaulted with cruelty?’, the court asked the artificial intelligence chat bot. In a first in India, the Punjab and Haryana High Court on Monday passed an order which included responses of artificial intelligence bot ChatGPT, reported The Indian Express. The court used the response to get ChatGPT’s view on granting bail to a man accused of assault by cruelty. Justice Anoop Chitkara clarified in his order that the response from ChatGPT was only meant to present a broader picture of the case, not as an opinion on the merits of the matter. ChatGPT has been created by US-based artificial intelligence company OpenAI and was launched in November.","u":"https://scroll.in/latest/1046405/punjab-high-court-turns-to-chatgpt-for-view-on-bail-in-murder-case","d":"2023-03-28","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-03-28","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-03-28","organization":"Punjab and Haryana High Court","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Man accused of assault by cruelty","action":"Used ChatGPT to seek a view on granting bail","consequence":"Received a response from ChatGPT, which was not considered as an opinion on the merits of the case"}},{"i":"R2023","k":"India","p":"India","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"Mumbai datacentres coal air pollution","s":"As Mumbai sees increased energy demand from new datacenters, particularly from Amazon, the filthiest neighbourhood in one of India’s largest cities must keep its major coal plants. Each day, Kiran Kasbe drives a rickshaw taxi through his home neighbourhood of Mahul on Mumbai’s eastern seafront, down streets lined with stalls selling tomatoes, bottle gourds and aubergines–and, frequently, through thick smog. Earlier this year, doctors found three tumours in his 54-year-old mother’s brain. It’s not clear exactly what caused her cancer. But people who live near coal plants are much more likely to develop the illness, studies show, and the residents of Mahul live a few hundred metres down the road from one.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2025/nov/24/mumbai-datacentres-coal-air-pollution","d":"2025-11-24","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-11-24","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-11-24","organization":"Amazon","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"Residents of Mahul, particularly Kiran Kasbe's mother","action":"Coal plants emitting pollutants","consequence":"Increased likelihood of developing cancer"}},{"i":"R2222","k":"India","p":"India","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"Voice accent technology call center white american","s":"A Silicon Valley startup offers voice-altering tech to call center workers around the world: ‘Yes, this is wrong … but a lot of things exist in the world’. “Hi, good morning. I’m calling in from Bangalore, India.” I’m talking on speakerphone to a man with an obvious Indian accent. He pauses. “Now I have enabled the accent translation,” he says. It’s the same person, but he sounds completely different: loud and slightly nasal, impossible to distinguish from the accents of my friends in Brooklyn. Only after he had spoken a few more sentences did I notice a hint of the software changing his voice: it rendered the word “technology” with an unnatural cadence and stress on the wrong syllable.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2022/aug/23/voice-accent-technology-call-center-white-american","d":"2022-08-24","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2022-08-24","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2022-08-23","organization":"Silicon Valley startup","aiSystem":"Voice-accent altering technology","affectedGroup":"Call center workers","action":"Enabled accent translation feature","consequence":"Misleading representation of accent change"}},{"i":"R2274","k":"India","p":"India","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"China using ai disrupt elections","s":"Beijing did a test run in Taiwan using AI-generated content to influence voters away from a pro-sovereignty candidate. China will attempt to disrupt elections in the US, South Korea and India this year with artificial intelligence-generated content after making a dry run with the presidential poll in Taiwan, Microsoft has warned. The US tech firm said it expected Chinese state-backed cyber groups to target high-profile elections in 2024, with North Korea also involved, according to a report by the company’s threat intelligence team published on Friday. “As populations in India, South Korea and the United States head to the polls, we are likely to see Chinese cyber and influence actors, and to some extent North Korean cyber actors, work toward targeting these elections,” the report reads.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2024/apr/05/china-using-ai-disrupt-elections","d":"2024-04-05","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2024-04-05","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-04-05","organization":"Chinese state-backed cyber groups, North Korea","aiSystem":"Artificial intelligence-generated content","affectedGroup":"Voters in US, South Korea, and India","action":"Generate and disseminate AI-generated content to influence voter preferences","consequence":"Disruption of elections and potential manipulation of voting outcomes"}},{"i":"R2375","k":"India","p":"India","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"Mumbai datacentres coal air pollution","s":"As Mumbai sees increased energy demand from new datacenters, particularly from Amazon, the filthiest neighbourhood in one of India’s largest cities must keep its major coal plants. Each day, Kiran Kasbe drives a rickshaw taxi through his home neighbourhood of Mahul on Mumbai’s eastern seafront, down streets lined with stalls selling tomatoes, bottle gourds and aubergines–and, frequently, through thick smog. Earlier this year, doctors found three tumours in his 54-year-old mother’s brain. It’s not clear exactly what caused her cancer. But people who live near coal plants are much more likely to develop the illness, studies show, and the residents of Mahul live a few hundred metres down the road from one.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2025/nov/24/mumbai-datacentres-coal-air-pollution","d":"2025-11-24","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-11-24","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-11-24","organization":"Amazon","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"Residents of Mahul, particularly Kiran Kasbe's mother","action":"Coal plants emitting pollutants","consequence":"Increased likelihood of developing cancer"}},{"i":"R2706","k":"India","p":"India","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.3  Employment displacement / quality harm"],"t":"Generative ai advertising oreos cadbury watermarking","s":"AI is selling you cookies now. Do you really want to know if AI is selling you something? Creating an ad can be tricky and costly, and more brands see generative AI as a means to make that process less painful. Reuters reported large advertising agencies like WPP and multinational corporations like Unilever look to generative AI to cut marketing costs while making more ads. WPP clients Nestlé and Mondelez, makers of Oreo and Cadbury, used OpenAI’s DALL-E 2 to make ads. One ad for Cadbury ran in India with an AI-generated video of the Bollywood actor Shah Rukh Khan inviting pedestrians to shop at stores. WPP’s CEO told Reuters savings from generative AI can be “10 to 20 times.”","u":"https://www.theverge.com/2023/8/18/23837273/generative-ai-advertising-oreos-cadbury-watermarking","d":"2023-08-18","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-08-18","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-08-18","organization":"WPP, Unilever, Nestlé, Mondelez","aiSystem":"Generative AI","affectedGroup":"Consumers, pedestrians, shoppers","action":"Generated AI-generated video","consequence":"Exposure to misleading content"}},{"i":"R2930","k":"India","p":"India","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Indian elections ai deepfakes","s":"India’s elections are a glimpse of the AI-driven future of democracy. Politicians are using audio and video deepfakes of themselves to reach voters—who may have no idea they’ve been talking to a clone. On a stifling April afternoon in Ajmer, in the Indian state of Rajasthan, local politician Shakti Singh Rathore sat down in front of a greenscreen to shoot a short video. He looked nervous. It was his first time being cloned. Wearing a crisp white shirt and a ceremonial saffron scarf bearing a lotus flower—the logo of the BJP, the country’s ruling party—Rathore pressed his palms together and greeted his audience in Hindi.","u":"https://www.wired.com/story/indian-elections-ai-deepfakes/","d":"2024-05-20","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-05-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-04","organization":"Politicians in India","aiSystem":"Audio and video deepfakes","affectedGroup":"Voters","action":"Generated and distributed deepfake videos of themselves","consequence":"May lead to misleading communication and voter confusion"}},{"i":"R643","k":"Indonesia","p":"Indonesia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Indonesian deepfakes impersonate former health ministers promote dubious cures","s":"In violation of Indonesian food and drug regulations, these deepfakes disparage medical professionals and advise patients to stop taking prescribed medications while exaggerating claims of efficacy of herbal supplements. First of 2 parts One quiet afternoon in October 2024, my 72-year-old mother approached me, her smartphone in hand. “Is this true?” she asked, her voice tinged with concern. She turned the screen toward me, revealing a YouTube ad video featuring a familiar face: Dr. Terawan Agus Putranto, a well-known former Indonesian health minister who served from 2019 to 2020. In the video, Dr. Terawan appeared to endorse a miraculous cure for joint pain.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/newsbreak/in-depth/indonesian-deepfakes-impersonate-former-health-ministers-promote-dubious-cures/","d":"2025-01-17","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-01-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-10","organization":"Unknown organization producing deepfakes","aiSystem":"AI-generated deepfake videos","affectedGroup":"General public, particularly elderly users","action":"AI-generated deepfake videos impersonating Dr. Terawan Agus Putranto, a former Indonesian health minister, endorsing dubious herbal cures and advising against prescribed medications","consequence":"Dissemination of false information leading to potential health risks and misinformation"}},{"i":"R649","k":"Indonesia","p":"Indonesia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Southeast asia voters grapple with artificial intelligence","s":"We shine a spotlight of how AI, social media, and electoral politics have already become enmeshed and entrenched in recent elections in Malaysia and Indonesia. Those who watched the video may be forgiven for thinking ghosts are real. Before their eyes was the familiar face of the late former Indonesian dictator Suharto , seated at a desk against a yellow background, wearing a traditional black kopiah hat and a batik shirt. Flanked by the flags of Indonesia and his party, Golkar, Suharto urged his listeners to vote for Golkar representatives in the upcoming elections. There was just one problem: the video came out in January 2024, a month before the presidential election that had as its frontrunner, Suharto’s former son-in-law and general, Prabowo Subianto, and Suharto died 16 years ago, in January 2008.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/newsbreak/in-depth/southeast-asia-voters-grapple-with-artificial-intelligence/","d":"2024-12-30","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-12-30","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-01-01","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"AI-generated video","affectedGroup":"Electoral voters in Indonesia","action":"AI-generated video featuring a deceased political figure urging votes","consequence":"Misleading information leading to potential voter confusion"}},{"i":"R732","k":"Indonesia","p":"Indonesia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Lessons philippines how generative ai was used indonesia election 2024","s":"Generative AI helped Prabowo Subianto, poised to become Indonesia’s next president, rebrand from infamous military strongman to a cute Gen Z icon. MANILA, Philippines – Earlier this month, on Wednesday, February 14, Indonesia elected Prabowo Subianto as their next president. For older Indonesians, they remember Prabowo for his human rights abuses under the brutal Suharto dictatorship, which ended only 25 years ago. But for younger Indonesian voters, they know Prabowo as a “cuddly grandpa.” For his 2024 campaign, Prabowo greatly benefited from a major rebrand, thanks to generative AI (genAI). A cute, chubby-cheeked cartoon version of Prabowo, generated using text-to-image genAI tool Midjourney, is often shown dancing in viral videos across social media.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/features/lessons-philippines-how-generative-ai-was-used-indonesia-election-2024/","d":"2024-02-27","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-02-27","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-02-14","organization":"Prabowo Subianto's 2024 presidential campaign team","aiSystem":"text-to-image generative AI tool Midjourney","affectedGroup":"Younger Indonesian voters","action":"Generated a cute, chubby-cheeked cartoon version of Prabowo","consequence":"Rebranded Prabowo from a military strongman to a 'cuddly grandpa' image"}},{"i":"R734","k":"Indonesia","p":"Indonesia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Generative ai faces major test indonesia largest election since boom","s":"From AI avatars made using Midjourney to speech generators that use data from social media and news websites, the Indonesian elections on February 14 offer a glimpse of generative AI’s use in campaigns. Fika Juliana Putri, a 19-year-old shopkeeper in East Jakarta, plans to vote in Indonesia’s presidential election next week for a once-feared former special forces commander. She likes him, she says, because he’s cuddly. A doe-eyed cartoon version of Gen. Prabowo Subianto – produced using generative AI – is emblazoned on billboards across Indonesia. It’s reproduced on sweatshirts and stickers, and featured prominently on #Prabowo-tagged posts that have some 19 billion views on TikTok.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/features/generative-ai-faces-major-test-indonesia-largest-election-since-boom/","d":"2024-02-09","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-02-09","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-02-14","organization":"Various social media platforms, news websites, and possibly Midjourney","aiSystem":"Generative AI","affectedGroup":"General public, particularly young voters in Indonesia","action":"Emblazoning a generative AI-created image of Gen. Prabowo Subianto on billboards and merchandise","consequence":"Potential misrepresentation and manipulation of public opinion through AI-generated content"}},{"i":"R639","k":"Indonesia","p":"Indonesia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Who are circulating health related deepfakes indonesia","s":"AI-manipulated fakes exploiting the likeness of former Indonesian health ministers promote fraudulent hypertension and diabetes cures. “Do not use Metformin or insulin to treat diabetes—it will kill you.” Seated in front of the red and white flag, the man wearing a black suit and red tie looking sharply at the camera is former Indonesian health minister Dr. Terawan Agus Putranto. “The root cause of diabetes has nothing to do with carbohydrates, sugar, poor diet, age, or genetics,” the figure of “Terawan” in the video appeared to say. Claiming that “none of the options available to the corrupt pharmaceutical industry” can address this supposed real “root cause,” the fake Terawan then describes a “revolutionary solution” supposedly invented by Asian scientists, alleging it can eliminate the disease within three days.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/newsbreak/in-depth/who-are-circulating-health-related-deepfakes-indonesia/","d":"2025-01-19","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-01-19","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-01-19","organization":"Unknown (no specific organization mentioned)","aiSystem":"AI-generated deepfake videos","affectedGroup":"General public, particularly those seeking information about diabetes treatment","action":"AI-generated videos portraying former Indonesian health minister Dr. Terawan Agus Putranto promoting fraudulent hypertension and diabetes cures","consequence":"Promotion of false health advice leading to potential harm to individuals"}},{"i":"R2988","k":"Indonesia","p":"Indonesia","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"Chatgpt non english languages ai revolution","s":"AI chatbots are less fluent in languages other than English, threatening to amplify existing bias in global commerce and innovation. Computer scientist Pascale Fung can imagine a rosy future in which polyglot AI helpers like ChatGPT bridge language barriers. In that world, Indonesian store owners fluent only in local dialects might reach new shoppers by listing their products online in English. “It can open opportunities,” Fung says—then pauses. She’s spotted the bias in her vision of a more interconnected future: The AI-aided shopping would be one-sided, because few Americans would bother to use AI translation to help research products advertised in Indonesian.","u":"https://www.wired.com/story/chatgpt-non-english-languages-ai-revolution/","d":"2023-05-31","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-05-31","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-05-31","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"AI chatbots","affectedGroup":"Indonesian store owners, American consumers","action":"AI chatbots assist in translating advertisements","consequence":"One-sided communication, limited access to information"}},{"i":"R1107","k":"Iran","p":"Iran","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Cyber irans ai powered surveillance state","s":"In Iran, the state is using AI and facial recognition technology to enforce draconian laws. On January 31, a court in Iran handed out a combined sentence of 10 years to a couple who danced outside of Azadi Tower in Tehran, Iran. A film of the brief dance went viral on Instagram and Twitter. They’re 21 and 22 years old. The woman was not wearing a hijab. The long sentence for a viral post is part of a pattern in Iran. In response to protests, the Iranian government is using technology and violence to suppress its people. Iran is a pioneer in the use of new technologies like AI and facial recognition to suppress dissent and enforce the will of the state.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/cyber-irans-ai-powered-surveillance-state/","d":"2023-02-02","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-02-02","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-01-31","organization":"Iranian_government","aiSystem":"AI_and_facial_recognition_technology","affectedGroup":"dancers","action":"AI-assisted_facial_recognition_identification_and_classification","consequence":"long_sentence_for_viral_post"}},{"i":"R1016","k":"Iran","p":"Iran","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Openai russia china malicious cyber activities","s":"OpenAI shut down accounts affiliated with multiple “state-affiliated threat actors.” Microsoft said that no “significant” cyber campaigns were carried out. OpenAI shut down multiple accounts affiliated with the governments of China, Russia, Iran, and North Korea, which it said were attempting to use its AI chatbot services “in support of malicious cyber activities,” the company announced in a blog post on Wednesday. The announcement came as a result of OpenAI’s collaboration with Microsoft Threat Intelligence. Microsoft stated in its blog post that it had not found evidence of these actors having carried out any significant cyberattacks, but that much of its findings were “representative of an adversary exploring the use cases of a new technology.”","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/openai-russia-china-malicious-cyber-activities/","d":"2024-02-15","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-02-15","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-02-15","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"AI chatbot services","affectedGroup":"Multiple state-affiliated threat actors","action":"Shut down accounts","consequence":"Suspected involvement in malicious cyber activities"}},{"i":"R827","k":"Iran","p":"Iran","b":["Deployment Context / Weapons"],"h":["5.4  Supply chain / investor complicity harm"],"t":"Portland sightline ai surveillance drones israel","s":"Video processing firm Sightline Intelligence, which claims its AI can separate civilians from militants, faces protests at home. A company in Portland, Oregon, that specializes in AI targeting for drones has made significant shipments of materials to military contractors in Israel, according to cargo data reviewed by The Intercept. The shipments raise the possibility that a boutique Pacific Northwest tech firm has helped the Israeli military attack people in places like Gaza, Lebanon, and Iran, among others. Sightline Intelligence, a firm focused on AI video processing, has made at least 10 shipments of hardware to the Israeli weapons giant Elbit Systems since 2024, according to investigators with the Movement Research Unit , the group that originally obtained the documents.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2026/05/05/portland-sightline-ai-surveillance-drones-israel/","d":"2026-05-05","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-05-05","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-01-01","organization":"Sightline Intelligence (video processing firm)","aiSystem":"AI video processing technology","affectedGroup":"People in Gaza, Lebanon, and Iran","action":"Provided AI technology to Elbit Systems for military use","consequence":"Potential civilian casualties"}},{"i":"R597","k":"Iran","p":"Iran","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Video airstrike iran ai generated","s":"While Israel and Iran have been exchanging airstrikes since June 13, AI detection tool Hive Moderation identified the viral video as 93% likely to contain AI-generated or deepfake content. Claim: A video shows airstrikes hitting Iran, causing buildings to catch fire. Rating: FALSE Why we fact-checked this: The video posted on Facebook has garnered 9.9 million views, 39,000 reactions, and 4,600 shares as of writing. The Facebook account that posted it has 28,000 followers and identifies itself as coming from the Philippines. The video shows an aerial view of an area in Iran with several buildings on fire following missile strikes.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/newsbreak/fact-check/video-airstrike-iran-ai-generated/","d":"2025-06-17","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-06-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-06-17","organization":"Facebook","aiSystem":"Hive Moderation AI detection tool","affectedGroup":"Users of Facebook","action":"AI detection tool flagged a video as containing AI-generated or deepfake content","consequence":"Misleading claim about real-world events leading to public confusion"}},{"i":"R363","k":"Iran","p":"Iran","b":["Deployment Context / Weapons"],"h":["5.2  Military decision support harm"],"t":"Artificial intelligence quitters","s":"On Saturday, the United States and Israeli governments unleashed stealth bombers, drones, and missiles on Iran, citing a rationale of preventing Iran from developing and deploying catastrophic nuclear weapons. “They’ve rejected every opportunity to renounce their nuclear ambitions, and we can’t take it anymore,” President Donald Trump would later say in an 6-minute address to […]. “They’ve rejected every opportunity to renounce their nuclear ambitions, and we can’t take it anymore,” President Donald Trump would later say in an 6-minute address to the nation. But in their quest to prevent Iran from developing an advanced weapon, the US and Israel were also deploying one of their own: artificial intelligence.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2026/03/artificial-intelligence-quitters/","d":"2026-03-05","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-03-05","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-03-05","organization":"United States government, Israeli government","aiSystem":"artificial intelligence","affectedGroup":"Iranian civilians","action":"deployed artificial intelligence to assess and execute military strikes","consequence":"casualties among Iranian civilians"}},{"i":"R466","k":"Iran","p":"Iran","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Iran ai memes reaching people winning propaganda war","s":"Iran is now ‘using popular culture against the No.1 pop culture country, the United States,’ a scholar notes. A Lego-style Iranian military commander raps over a gangster beat : “Our inbox is flooded with Americans saying they don’t watch the news. They listen to our songs instead since your media is full of shit.” This is the opening line of an AI-generated video which is part of Iran’s meme campaign – built around Lego-style animation and rap soundtracks, which have accumulated billions of views online. The line captures the strange reality of contemporary politics: news is often most effectively disseminated not through journalism but humor, memes and entertainment.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/features/iran-ai-memes-reaching-people-winning-propaganda-war/","d":"2026-04-20","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-04-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-04-20","organization":"Iranian authorities","aiSystem":"AI-generated content","affectedGroup":"general public, especially those in the United States","action":"AI-generated meme campaign featuring Lego-style animation and rap soundtracks","consequence":"Dissemination of propaganda through popular culture, undermining traditional media"}},{"i":"R467","k":"Iran","p":"Iran","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Iran disinformation propaganda us war ai lego memes","s":"What to know as Iran uses memes, AI, and fake personas to shape how the war is seen online. As the conflict between Iran, the United States, and Israel intensifies, the battlefield has expanded far beyond physical borders, and as expected, into the digital world. Along with threats to US company-owned data centers and other tech infrastructure in the Middle East, Iran and its supporters have deployed a sophisticated online influence campaign designed to sow confusion, boost its own military image, and weaken the public’s trust in Western leadership. Disinformation as part of asymmetrical warfare As we’ve previously discussed, Iran’s targeting of data centers , and its closure of the Strait of Hormuz are part of its asymmetrical warfare strategy.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/iran-disinformation-propaganda-us-war-ai-lego-memes/","d":"2026-04-11","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-04-11","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-04-11","organization":"Iran and its supporters","aiSystem":"AI-generated memes and fake personas","affectedGroup":"Public opinion in the West","action":"Deployed a sophisticated online influence campaign","consequence":"Sow confusion, boost military image, weaken trust in Western leadership"}},{"i":"R484","k":"Iran","p":"Iran","b":["Deployment Context / Weapons"],"h":["5.2  Military decision support harm"],"t":"Anthropic artificial intelligence bombers suicide drones used iran strikes","s":"US Central Command releases photographs showing Tomahawk missiles, F-18 and F-35 fighter jets alongside details of the strikes on Iran as part of Operation Epic Fury. The United States unleashed an array of weaponry against Iranian targets on Saturday, February 28, including Tomahawk cruise missiles, stealth fighters, and for the first time in combat, low-cost one-way attack drones modeled after Iranian designs. US Central Command released photographs showing Tomahawk missiles, F-18 and F-35 fighter jets alongside details of the strikes on Iran as part of Operation Epic Fury. Must Read Iranian leader Khamenei killed in air strikes as US, Israel launch attacks Artificial intelligence The Pentagon used artificial intelligence services from Anthropic, including its Claude tools, during its attack on Iran, according to a source familiar with the situation.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/world/us-canada/anthropic-artificial-intelligence-bombers-suicide-drones-used-iran-strikes/","d":"2026-03-02","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-03-02","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-02-28","organization":"US Central Command, United States","aiSystem":"Anthropic AI services, including Claude tools","affectedGroup":"Iranian targets","action":"Launched an array of weaponry against Iranian targets","consequence":"Low-cost one-way attack drones modeled after Iranian designs were used for the first time in combat"}},{"i":"R1642","k":"Iran","p":"Iran","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"2024 05 29 israel foresees seven more months of war meta removes ai generated influence campaigns","s":"Israel probably won’t be able to defeat Hamas before the end of the year, its national security adviser said, underscoring the difficulty the country faces in achieving its stated aim of crushing the Islamist group. Meta Platforms removed hundreds of Facebook accounts associated with covert influence campaigns from China, Israel, Iran, Russia and other countries, some of which used artificial intelligence tools to generate disinformation, according to the company’s quarterly threat report. President Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva relocated Brazil’s ambassador from Israel to another post after months of diplomatic tensions over the country’s military campaign in the Gaza Strip.","u":"https://www.dailymaverick.co.za/article/2024-05-29-israel-foresees-seven-more-months-of-war-meta-removes-ai-generated-influence-campaigns/","d":"2024-05-22","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-05-22","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-05-22","organization":"Meta Platforms","aiSystem":"Artificial intelligence tools","affectedGroup":"Facebook users","action":"Removed accounts associated with covert influence campaigns","consequence":"Exposure to disinformation"}},{"i":"R1717","k":"Iran","p":"Iran","b":["Deployment Context / Weapons"],"h":["5.1  Autonomous weapons / lethal targeting harm"],"t":"Elon musk grok ai iran missiles pentagon b2997321","s":"Top defense official says data centers powering trillionaire’s chatbot are critical to national security. Donald Trump’s administration turned to Elon Musk’s Grok chatbot to launch thousands of missiles in Iran , according to a top defense official. In a sworn statement defending the trillionaire from a lawsuit alleging xAI data centers are illegally polluting Black communities , the Pentagon’s artificial intelligence chief said the chatbot’s continued operation is “a matter of paramount national security” — and was used to fire more than “2,000 munitions at 2,000 distinct targets within 96 hours.” Grok, a generative artificial intelligence chatbot developed by xAI, is among four AI models “currently capable of supporting national security applications,” according to Cameron Stanley, the Pentagon’s chief digital and artificial intelligence officer.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/us-politics/elon-musk-grok-ai-iran-missiles-pentagon-b2997321.html","d":"2026-06-17","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-06-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-06-17","organization":"Donald Trump's administration, Pentagon","aiSystem":"Grok, a generative artificial intelligence chatbot","affectedGroup":"Iran","action":"used Grok to launch thousands of missiles","consequence":"caused damage to 2,000 distinct targets within 96 hours"}},{"i":"R1782","k":"Iran","p":"Iran","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Chatgpt hack ai feminists openai microsoft b2496310","s":"Iranian hackers used artificial intelligence tools to ‘lure prominent feminists’. Hackers in Iran have used the popular AI tool ChatGPT to launch cyber attacks against feminists, researchers have revealed. It is one of several incidents of state-backed actors using the technology in hacking campaigns, with the app’s creator OpenAI also naming groups linked to China, North Korea and Russia. A report published on Wednesday said hackers were honing their skills and tricking their targets by using generative artificial intelligence tools like ChatGPT, which draw on massive amounts of text to generate human-sounding responses.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/tech/chatgpt-hack-ai-feminists-openai-microsoft-b2496310.html","d":"2024-02-14","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-02-14","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-02-14","organization":"Iranian hackers","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"prominent feminists","action":"used generative artificial intelligence tools to lure targets","consequence":"tricked their targets into falling for cyber attacks"}},{"i":"R1807","k":"Iran","p":"Iran","b":["Deployment Context / Weapons"],"h":["5.2  Military decision support harm"],"t":"Deadly iran school strike casts shadow over pentagons ai targeting push","s":"A Ukrainian drone developer says the Minab strike exposed a familiar danger of semi-autonomous warfare. KYIV, Ukraine — On the first day of the U.S.-Iran war, a Tomahawk cruise missile struck Shajareh Tayyebeh elementary school in Minab, southern Iran. At least 168 people were killed — more than 100 of them under the age of 12, according to UN and Iranian officials. The school building sat fewer than 100 yards from a long-time Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps naval installation and was previously located within the IRGC compound perimeter until a wall appeared between 2013 and 2016, according to an analysis of satellite imagery by Amnesty International By the time the U.S. and Israel launched their first strikes on Feb. 28, the school had been established several years prior.","u":"https://www.militarytimes.com/news/your-military/2026/03/24/deadly-iran-school-strike-casts-shadow-over-pentagons-ai-targeting-push/","d":"2026-03-24","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2026-03-24","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2020-02-28","organization":"U.S. military and Israeli forces","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"Children under 12 and civilians near the school","action":"Launched Tomahawk cruise missiles targeting Shajareh Tayyebeh elementary school","consequence":"Casualties including children and civilian deaths"}},{"i":"R1814","k":"Iran","p":"Iran","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"The conflict involving Iran is revealing new lessons about how artificial intelligence is …","s":"The conflict involving Iran is revealing new lessons about how artificial intelligence is being weaponized in modern warfare and statecraft. WASHINGTON -- From viral Lego-style videos and AI-generated hip-hop tracks to fabricated battlefield imagery, the war in Iran is revealing how artificial intelligence (AI) is being weaponized in modern warfare and statecraft. To discuss this rapid shift, RFE/RL spoke with Max Lesser, senior analyst on emerging threats at the Foundation for Defense of Democracies (FDD) Center on Cyber and Technology Innovation. RFE/RL: You have tracked Iranian influence operations closely. How sophisticated has Iran become online?","u":"https://www.rferl.org/a/lego-hip-hop-deepfakes-iran-ai-propaganda/33756098.html","d":"2026-05-13","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-05-13","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-05-13","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"AI-generated deepfake technology","affectedGroup":"People exposed to misleading content","action":"Generated and distributed false videos and audio","consequence":"Exposure to misleading information"}}]);
